INSERT INTO _s463c VALUES
(4287,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,1,48,1,$n$*Then stood up Elias the prophet as fire, and his word burned like a lamp.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:1). *In those days came John the Baptist, preaching in the wilderness of Judaea,* (Matthew 3:1). Sirach opens his praise of Elijah with fire: he stood up as fire and his word burned like a lamp. Matthew opens John's ministry in the wilderness, and the fire is already there in what he says, *O generation of vipers* and *he shall baptize you with the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Ghost), and with fire* (Matthew 3:7, 11). The angel had told Zacharias his son would go before Yahuah (God) in the spirit and power of Elias (Luke 1:17), and Yahusha (Jesus) later says it plainly, *this is Elias, which was for to come* (Matthew 11:14). The prophet who stood up as fire has stood up again in the wilderness of Judaea.$n$,$n$Then stood up Elias the prophet as fire, and his word burned like a lamp.$n$,true),
(4288,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,10,48,10,$n$*Who were ordained for reproofs in their times, to pacify the wrath of the judgment of Yahuah (God), before it brake forth into fury, and to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:10). *For this is he that was spoken of by the prophet Esaias, saying, The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of Yahuah (Lord), make his paths straight.* (Matthew 3:3). Sirach is drawing on Malachi, *he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children* (Malachi 4:6), and then he says what Malachi's Elijah is for: to restore the tribes of Jacob. Not Yahudah (Judah) only, the tribes. That is the errand John steps into when he stands in the wilderness as the voice Isaiah heard, preparing the way of Yahuah (Lord). Preparing the way is the beginning of the Elijah work; the end of it is the scattered tribes of Jacob brought home, and the one whose way John prepared is the one who gathers them.$n$,$n$Who were ordained for reproofs in their times, to pacify the wrath of the judgment of Yahuah (God), before it brake forth into fury, and to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.$n$,true),
(4290,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,12,48,12,$n$*Elias it was, who was covered with a whirlwind: and Eliseus was filled with his spirit: while he lived, he was not moved with the presence of any prince, neither could any bring him into subjection.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:12). *Then cometh Yahusha (Jesus) from Galilee to Jordan unto John, to be baptized of him.* (Matthew 3:13). The whirlwind took Elijah at the Jordan, and Elisha came back across the same river carrying his spirit (2 Kings 2:8–15). Sirach adds that no prince could move Elijah while he lived. Now Yahusha (Jesus) comes from Galilee to the Jordan, to a man no prince could move either, the John who would shortly tell Herod his marriage was unlawful. At that river the heavens open and the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) comes down on him (Matthew 3:16). The Jordan is where the prophetic spirit passed from Elijah to Elisha, and it is where the Spirit rests on the one Elijah's successor was sent to announce.$n$,$n$Elias it was, who was covered with a whirlwind: and Eliseus was filled with his spirit: while he lived, he was not moved with the presence of any prince, neither could any bring him into subjection.$n$,true),
(4318,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,13,2,13,$n$*He professeth to have the knowledge of Yahuah (God): and he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:13). *And when the tempter came to him, he said, If thou be the Son of Elohim (God), command that these stones be made bread.* (Matthew 4:3). The ungodly in Wisdom of Solomon fasten on one claim, that the righteous man calls himself the child of Yahuah (God), and they set out to test it: *if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:18). The tempter in the wilderness uses the same handle, if you are the Son of Elohim (God), prove it, command the stones. The test comes back at the cross in the same words, *If thou be the Son of Elohim (God), come down from the cross* (Matthew 27:40). The claim of sonship is what the ungodly cannot leave alone, and the devil was the first to pick it up.$n$,$n$He professeth to have the knowledge of Yahuah (God): and he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4319,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,16,2,16,$n$*We are esteemed of him as counterfeits: he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness: he pronounceth the end of the just to be blessed, and makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:16). *And saith unto him, If thou be the Son of Elohim (God), cast thyself down: for it is written, He shall give his angels charge concerning thee: and in their hands they shall bear thee up, lest at any time thou dash thy foot against a stone.* (Matthew 4:6). The ungodly resent one thing above the rest: the righteous man makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father. In the wilderness the tempter picks up the same grievance and turns it into a dare — if you are the Son of Elohim (God), prove it, throw yourself down and let the angels catch you. The claim of sonship is treated as something to be tested by a display of rescue. Yahusha (Jesus) did not take the dare, because sonship is shown by doing the Father's will, not by forcing the Father's hand.$n$,$n$We are esteemed of him as counterfeits: he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness: he pronounceth the end of the just to be blessed, and makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.$n$,true),
(4320,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,18,2,18,$n$*For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:18). *And saith unto him, If thou be the Son of Elohim (God), cast thyself down: for it is written, He shall give his angels charge concerning thee: and in their hands they shall bear thee up, lest at any time thou dash thy foot against a stone.* (Matthew 4:6). Wisdom puts the test in the mouths of the ungodly: if he really is the son of Yahuah (God), then his Father will have to rescue him. The devil carries the same test into the wilderness almost word for word — if thou be the Son of Elohim (God), cast thyself down, and let the angels bear thee up. He even cuts a line out of Psalm 91 to make the dare sound like scripture. The writer of Wisdom had heard this voice centuries before; the gospel shows whose voice it is.$n$,$n$For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.$n$,true),
(4321,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,20,2,20,$n$*Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:20). *Then the devil leaveth him, and, behold, angels came and ministered unto him.* (Matthew 4:11). The plot against the righteous man in Wisdom ends in a death sentence; the plot against Yahusha (Jesus) in the wilderness ends with the devil leaving and angels ministering to him. The two are not in conflict. The wilderness was the opening move, not the last one — the enemy withdrew to come back through other hands. Wisdom names the whole arc from reproof to shameful death; Matthew 4 is where that arc begins in the gospel, and it does not end until the cross.$n$,$n$Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.$n$,true),
(4326,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,1,3,1,$n$*But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1). *Blessed are they which are persecuted for righteousness’ sake: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 5:10). Wisdom's third chapter answers its second. The ungodly tortured and condemned the righteous, and here is where the righteous ended up: in the hand of Yahuah (God), where no torment can reach them. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same to those persecuted for righteousness' sake — the kingdom is theirs. The persecution lands on the body and goes no further. What Wisdom promised the just man, the King promised everyone who would be hated for walking as he walked.$n$,$n$But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.$n$,true),
(4327,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,9,3,9,$n$*They that put their trust in him shall understand the truth: and such as be faithful in love shall abide with him: for grace and mercy is to his saints, and he has care for his elect.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:9). *Rejoice, and be exceeding glad: for great is your reward in heaven: for so persecuted they the prophets which were before you.* (Matthew 5:12). Wisdom closes its word to the persecuted righteous with what they have coming: understanding of the truth, abiding with him, grace and mercy to his saints, and his care over his elect. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same to his disciples — rejoice, for great is your reward in heaven, for so they persecuted the prophets before you. The disciples are being added to the line of the prophets who were hated for speaking the Father's word, and the care and reward Wisdom promised the elect is laid up for them in the same place.$n$,$n$They that put their trust in him shall understand the truth: and such as be faithful in love shall abide with him: for grace and mercy is to his saints, and he has care for his elect.$n$,true),
(4336,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,9,23,9,$n$*Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.* (Ecclesiasticus 23:9). *Again, ye have heard that it hath been said by them of old time, Thou shalt not forswear thyself, but shalt perform unto Yahuah (Lord) thine oaths:* (Matthew 5:33). Sirach tells his son not to let his mouth grow used to swearing, nor to make a habit of naming the Holy One. Yahusha (Jesus) begins from what they had heard said of old — do not forswear yourself, perform your oaths to Yahuah (Lord) — and goes on to say swear not at all, let your yes be yes and your no be no. He is not loosening the Torah on oaths; he is cutting away the habit Sirach warned of, the casual oath that made the name common and the word cheap. Speech that is true needs no oath to prop it up.$n$,$n$Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.$n$,true),
(4337,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,10,23,10,$n$*For as a servant that is continually beaten shall not be without a blue mark: so he that sweareth and nameth Yahuah (God) continually shall not be faultless.* (Ecclesiasticus 23:10). *But I say unto you, Swear not at all; neither by heaven; for it is Elohim's (God’s) throne:* (Matthew 5:34). Sirach says a servant who is beaten every day is never without a bruise, and a man who swears and names Yahuah (God) continually is never clean of fault — the habit leaves its mark. Yahusha (Jesus) says swear not at all, not even by heaven, because heaven is the throne of Elohim (God). To swear by heaven lightly is to drag the throne into common talk. The mark Sirach describes is what casual swearing does to a man, and the command in Matthew is how it stops.$n$,$n$For as a servant that is continually beaten shall not be without a blue mark: so he that sweareth and nameth Yahuah (God) continually shall not be faultless.$n$,true),
(4338,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,11,23,11,$n$*A man that useth much swearing shall be filled with iniquity, and the plague shall never depart from his house: if he shall offend, his sin shall be upon him: and if he acknowledge not his sin, he makes a double offence: and if he swear in vain, he shall not be innocent, but his house shall be full of calamities.* (Ecclesiasticus 23:11). *But let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay: for whatsoever is more than these cometh of evil.* (Matthew 5:37). Sirach traces what much swearing does to a man: he is filled with iniquity, the plague does not leave his house, and every oath sworn in vain adds to the account. Yahusha (Jesus) names the root in one line — whatever is more than yes and no comes of evil. The iniquity Sirach saw filling the swearer's house and the evil Yahusha (Jesus) names at the source are the same thing seen from two ends. Plain speech, yes and no, is the way out of both.$n$,$n$A man that useth much swearing shall be filled with iniquity, and the plague shall never depart from his house: if he shall offend, his sin shall be upon him: and if he acknowledge not his sin, he makes a double offence: and if he swear in vain, he shall not be innocent, but his house shall be full of calamities.$n$,true),
(4342,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',12,19,12,19,$n$*But by such works have you taught your people that the just man should be merciful, and have made your children to be of a good hope that you give repentance for sins.* (Wisdom of Solomon 12:19). *But I say unto you, Love your enemies, bless them that curse you, do good to them that hate you, and pray for them which despitefully use you, and persecute you;* (Matthew 5:44). Solomon says the Father's dealings with Yashar'el's (Israel's) enemies were themselves the lesson: by such works he taught his people that the just man should be merciful, and gave his children good hope that he gives repentance for sins. The King teaches the same lesson from the same source. Love your enemies, bless those who curse you, pray for those who persecute you, and the reason follows in the next verse: so that you may be children of your Father, who does exactly that. The mercy the children show is learned from watching the Father show it.$n$,$n$But by such works have you taught your people that the just man should be merciful, and have made your children to be of a good hope that you give repentance for sins.$n$,true),
(4343,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',12,20,12,20,$n$*For if you did punish the enemies of your children, and the condemned to death, with such deliberation, giving them time and place, by which they might be delivered from their malice:* (Wisdom of Solomon 12:20). *That ye may be the children of your Father which is in heaven: for he maketh his sun to rise on the evil and on the good, and sendeth rain on the just and on the unjust.* (Matthew 5:45). Even the enemies of his children, even those already condemned to death, he punished with deliberation, giving them time and place to be delivered from their malice. The King points at the sky for the same pattern: the Father makes his sun rise on the evil and on the good and sends rain on the just and on the unjust. The wicked are still being given daylight and harvest, which is time and place to turn. Children who love their enemies in this age are copying a Father who has been doing it since before the land was given.$n$,$n$For if you did punish the enemies of your children, and the condemned to death, with such deliberation, giving them time and place, by which they might be delivered from their malice:$n$,true),
(4345,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,6,35,6,$n$*The offering of the righteous makes the altar fat, and the sweet savour thereof is before the Most High.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:6). *That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.* (Matthew 6:4). Sirach says the offering of the righteous makes the altar fat and its sweet savour rises before the Most High. Yahusha (Jesus) says alms given in secret are seen by the Father who sees in secret, and he rewards them openly. The altar is the same in both: it is the Most High who receives, and he receives what is righteous, not what is displayed. An offering made for men to see never reaches that altar; the one made where only he looks is the sweet savour.$n$,$n$The offering of the righteous makes the altar fat, and the sweet savour thereof is before the Most High.$n$,true),
(4346,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,7,35,7,$n$*The sacrifice of a just man is acceptable. and the memorial thereof shall never be forgotten.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:7). *That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.* (Matthew 6:4). Sirach says the sacrifice of a just man is accepted and its memorial is never forgotten. Yahusha (Jesus) says alms given in secret are seen by the Father, who repays openly. The reward of being seen by men is spent the moment it is received; the memorial Sirach speaks of is kept by the Most High, who does not forget. The just man's offering goes into a record that outlasts him.$n$,$n$The sacrifice of a just man is acceptable. and the memorial thereof shall never be forgotten.$n$,true),
(4347,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,2,28,2,$n$*Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you, so shall your sins also be forgiven when you prayest.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:2). *And forgive us our debts, as we forgive our debtors.* (Matthew 6:12). Sirach says forgive your neighbour the hurt he did you, and then your own sins will be forgiven when you pray. That is the petition Yahusha (Jesus) taught his disciples to pray, forgive us our debts as we forgive our debtors, and the condition is the same in both. Pardon received and pardon extended are named in one breath, in the same act of prayer. The disciples' prayer was not a new idea in Yashar'el (Israel); the wise had been teaching sons to pray this way for generations.$n$,$n$Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you, so shall your sins also be forgiven when you prayest.$n$,true),
(4349,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,4,28,4,$n$*He sheweth no mercy to a man, which is like himself: and does he ask forgiveness of his own sins?* (Ecclesiasticus 28:4). *But if ye forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses.* (Matthew 6:15). Sirach presses the question down to the heart: a man shows no mercy to another man, one made like himself, and then asks forgiveness for his own sins? Yahusha (Jesus) answers it in the Sermon: if you forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive yours. The brother who is like you is the test. The heart that withholds mercy from him has no standing to ask it from the Father, and the wise in Yashar'el (Israel) had been saying so long before the disciples' prayer was taught.$n$,$n$He sheweth no mercy to a man, which is like himself: and does he ask forgiveness of his own sins?$n$,true),
(4351,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,10,4,10,$n$*Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10). *But lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt, and where thieves do not break through nor steal:* (Matthew 6:20). Tobit tells his son that alms deliver from death and keep a man out of the darkness; the open hand protects the giver in the day of necessity. Yahusha (Jesus) says to lay up treasure in heaven, where moth and rust do not corrupt and thieves do not break through. Both are talking about what cannot be lost. Tobit names the deliverance the open hand brings; the gospel names the storehouse the open hand fills, and nothing gets in to spoil it.$n$,$n$Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.$n$,true),
(4352,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',29,11,29,11,$n$*Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High, and it shall bring you more profit than gold.* (Ecclesiasticus 29:11). *But lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt, and where thieves do not break through nor steal:* (Matthew 6:20). Sirach says lay up your treasure the way the commandments of the Most High direct, and it will profit you more than gold. Yahusha (Jesus) says lay up for yourselves treasure in heaven, where moth and rust and thieves cannot reach. The two are the same counsel: the hand that opens to the brother and the poor because the commandment says so is laying up the treasure the King means. The treasure in heaven is kept by keeping the commandments on earth.$n$,$n$Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High, and it shall bring you more profit than gold.$n$,true),
(4355,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,8,35,8,$n$*Give Yahuah (God) his honour with a good eye, and diminish not the firstfruits of yours hands.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:8). *The light of the body is the eye: if therefore thine eye be single, thy whole body shall be full of light.* (Matthew 6:22). Sirach tells the worshipper to give Yahuah (God) his honour with a good eye and not to cut short the firstfruits of his hands. The good eye is an old Hebrew way of speaking of generosity, the eye that is not grudging and does not measure what it gives. Yahusha (Jesus) speaks of the single eye that fills the whole body with light, and the verses around it are about treasure and about mammon. A good eye in giving and a single eye in the body are the same eye; it looks at Yahuah (God) and his honour, and the hand follows where it looks.$n$,$n$Give Yahuah (God) his honour with a good eye, and diminish not the firstfruits of yours hands.$n$,true),
(4356,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',14,8,14,8,$n$*The envious man has a wicked eye; he turns away his face, and despiseth men.* (Ecclesiasticus 14:8). *But if thine eye be evil, thy whole body shall be full of darkness. If therefore the light that is in thee be darkness, how great is that darkness!* (Matthew 6:23). Sirach calls envy a wicked eye and says the man who has it turns away his face and despises men. Yahusha (Jesus) says the evil eye fills the whole body with darkness. In the Hebrew idiom the evil eye is not a vague picture of bad vision; it is the stingy, envious heart that closes its hand and looks away. The King is speaking the idiom Sirach spoke, and the darkness he names is the darkness of the closed hand.$n$,$n$The envious man has a wicked eye; he turns away his face, and despiseth men.$n$,true),
(4357,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',14,9,14,9,$n$*A covetous man’s eye is not satisfied with his portion; and the iniquity of the wicked drieth up his soul.* (Ecclesiasticus 14:9). *But if thine eye be evil, thy whole body shall be full of darkness. If therefore the light that is in thee be darkness, how great is that darkness!* (Matthew 6:23). Sirach says the covetous eye is never satisfied with its portion, and that the iniquity dries the soul up. Yahusha (Jesus) says the evil eye leaves the whole body full of darkness. A dried-up soul and a darkened body are two ways of describing one man — the grasping one who never has enough and cannot see by his own light.$n$,$n$A covetous man’s eye is not satisfied with his portion; and the iniquity of the wicked drieth up his soul.$n$,true),
(4358,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',14,10,14,10,$n$*A wicked eye envies his bread, and he is a niggard at his table.* (Ecclesiasticus 14:10). *But if thine eye be evil, thy whole body shall be full of darkness. If therefore the light that is in thee be darkness, how great is that darkness!* (Matthew 6:23). Sirach brings the wicked eye to the table: the host who begrudges his own bread even as he serves it. Yahusha (Jesus) asks how great the darkness is when the light itself has gone dark. Bread can cross a table with no blessing in it. The niggard's whole hospitality is darkness, however much he puts out, because the eye behind it is closed.$n$,$n$A wicked eye envies his bread, and he is a niggard at his table.$n$,true),
(4359,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,5,31,5,$n$*He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:5). *No man can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon.* (Matthew 6:24). Sirach says the man who loves gold will not be justified. Yahusha (Jesus) says why: no man can serve two masters, and you cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon. The man whose heart holds to gold has already given his service to another master, and he cannot stand justified before a Father whose service is the whole heart. The gold-lover and the mammon-server are the same man seen from two sides.$n$,$n$He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof.$n$,true),
(4360,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,6,31,6,$n$*Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:6). *No man can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon.* (Matthew 6:24). Sirach says gold has ruined many, and their destruction was right there with it. Yahusha (Jesus) sets the choice before his disciples: Elohim (God) or mammon, one master or the other, never both. The disciple who chooses mammon is walking toward the ruin Sirach had already watched overtake many. The wise saw where gold leads; the King made the fork in the road plain.$n$,$n$Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present.$n$,true),
(4361,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,7,31,7,$n$*It is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it, and every fool shall be taken with it.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:7). *No man can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon.* (Matthew 6:24). Sirach speaks of men who sacrifice to gold. He names it as a thing worshipped, an altar men bring offerings to, and he says it trips every fool who goes near it. That is why Yahusha (Jesus) can set mammon across from Elohim (God) as a rival master: mammon in that sentence is not a neutral tool but a master that takes those who serve it, the very stumblingblock Sirach saw men sacrificing to. You cannot keep an altar to both.$n$,$n$It is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it, and every fool shall be taken with it.$n$,true),
(4362,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,15,5,15,$n$*But the righteous live for evermore; their reward also is with Yahuah (God), and the care of them is with the Most High.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:15). *Behold the fowls of the air: for they sow not, neither do they reap, nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feedeth them. Are ye not much better than they?* (Matthew 6:26). The care of the righteous is with the Most High. That is the ground the King stands on when he tells his disciples to look at the birds: they neither sow nor reap nor fill barns, and the heavenly Father feeds them, and you are worth more than they. Taking no anxious thought is not a technique; it is what follows when you know whose care you are already in. Solomon named the care, and the King named the birds so his hearers could see it with their eyes.$n$,$n$But the righteous live for evermore; their reward also is with Yahuah (God), and the care of them is with the Most High.$n$,true),
(4363,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,16,5,16,$n$*Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God): for with his right hand shall he cover them, and with his arm shall he protect them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:16). *But seek ye first the kingdom of Elohim (God), and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.* (Matthew 6:33). The kingdom and the crown come from the hand of Yahuah (God), and his right hand covers those who receive them. The King tells his disciples to seek that kingdom first and let the rest be added. Food and clothing are the small things the covering hand already holds for the one who is reaching for the kingdom itself; the same arm that protects the righteous in Wisdom is the one the King promises will add all these things.$n$,$n$Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God): for with his right hand shall he cover them, and with his arm shall he protect them.$n$,true),
(4364,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',11,14,11,14,$n$*Prosperity and adversity, life and death, poverty and riches, come of Yahuah (God).* (Ecclesiasticus 11:14). *But seek ye first the kingdom of Elohim (God), and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.* (Matthew 6:33). Sirach says both halves come from Yahuah (God) — prosperity and adversity, life and death, poverty and riches. That is the ground under Yahusha's (Jesus's) seek first: if the Father administers both, the citizen does not have to administer either, and can turn toward the kingdom and his righteousness with both hands. The adding comes from the same hand the adversity does.$n$,$n$Prosperity and adversity, life and death, poverty and riches, come of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4368,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,15,4,15,$n$*Do that to no man which you hatest: drink not wine to make you drunken: neither let drunkenness go with you in your journey.* (Tobit 4:15). *Therefore all things whatsoever ye would that men should do to you, do ye even so to them: for this is the law and the prophets.* (Matthew 7:12). Here is the golden rule in the Hebrew library, in its negative form, from a father to his son generations before the Sermon on the Mount. Yahusha (Jesus) states it positively and then says what it is: *this is the law and the prophets.* He was not bringing a kinder ethic to replace a Torah too hard to keep; he was naming the heart of the Torah that Tobit had already handed to his son. The teaching that the King invented a new religion in place of the law collapses against this one verse in Tobit.$n$,$n$Do that to no man which you hatest: drink not wine to make you drunken: neither let drunkenness go with you in your journey.$n$,true),
(4369,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,30,27,30,$n$*Malice and wrath, even these are abominations; and the sinful man shall have them both.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:30). *Judge not, that ye be not judged.* (Matthew 7:1). Sirach calls malice and wrath abominations and says the sinful man carries both. Yahusha (Jesus) says judge not, that you be not judged, and the next verse tells why: the measure you use is the measure that comes back on you. A man carrying malice cannot judge his brother rightly, because the malice is in the judgment, and the judgment rebounds. Sirach names the heart condition that has to be cleared before any brother can be weighed.$n$,$n$Malice and wrath, even these are abominations; and the sinful man shall have them both.$n$,true),
(4370,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,1,28,1,$n$*He that revengeth shall find vengeance from Yahuah (God), and he will surely keep his sins in remembrance.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:1). *For with what judgment ye judge, ye shall be judged: and with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you again.* (Matthew 7:2). Sirach says the man who takes revenge will get vengeance from Yahuah (God), and his own sins will be kept in remembrance. Yahusha (Jesus) says it as a rule of measure: with what judgment you judge, you will be judged, and with what measure you mete it will be measured back. The standard a man uses on his brother is the standard the Father uses on him. Sirach had it in the Hebrew library long before the Sermon on the Mount: the revenger writes his own sentence.$n$,$n$He that revengeth shall find vengeance from Yahuah (God), and he will surely keep his sins in remembrance.$n$,true),
(4371,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,5,28,5,$n$*If he that is but flesh nourish hatred, who will intreat for pardon of his sins?* (Ecclesiasticus 28:5). *Thou hypocrite, first cast out the beam out of thine own eye; and then shalt thou see clearly to cast out the mote out of thy brother’s eye.* (Matthew 7:5). Sirach asks who will plead for the pardon of a man who is only flesh and yet nurses hatred. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the man with the beam in his own eye to get it out first, and then he will see clearly to help his brother with the mote. The two belong together: the one nursing hatred cannot ask the Father's pardon for himself, and so he cannot rightly help a brother with his fault either. The beam comes out first, and the hatred is the beam.$n$,$n$If he that is but flesh nourish hatred, who will intreat for pardon of his sins?$n$,true),
(4372,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,7,22,7,$n$*Whoso teaches a fool is as one that glueth a potsherd together, and as he that waketh one from a sound sleep.* (Ecclesiasticus 22:7). *Give not that which is holy unto the dogs, neither cast ye your pearls before swine, lest they trample them under their feet, and turn again and rend you.* (Matthew 7:6). Sirach says teaching a fool is like gluing a broken pot back together, or waking a man out of a dead sleep — the effort does not hold. Yahusha (Jesus) says it as a command: do not give what is holy to dogs or cast pearls before swine, because they will trample them and turn and rend you. Both know that some hearers cannot receive, and that the holy thing is wasted and the teacher is harmed when it is pressed on them. The proclamation goes out broadly, but the pursuit ends where the rejection lands.$n$,$n$Whoso teaches a fool is as one that glueth a potsherd together, and as he that waketh one from a sound sleep.$n$,true),
(4373,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,8,22,8,$n$*He that telleth a tale to a fool speaks to one in a slumber: when he has told his tale, he will say, What is the matter?* (Ecclesiasticus 22:8). *Give not that which is holy unto the dogs, neither cast ye your pearls before swine, lest they trample them under their feet, and turn again and rend you.* (Matthew 7:6). Sirach says telling a tale to a fool is talking to a man asleep — when you have finished he asks what you were saying. Yahusha (Jesus) sharpens that into a command about the holy things: do not cast pearls before swine, who will trample them and turn on you. The wisdom book had been teaching discernment of the hearer for centuries; the gospel makes it a rule for the citizens of the kingdom. We speak the truth wherever the Father sends us, and when it is refused we shake the dust off and go on.$n$,$n$He that telleth a tale to a fool speaks to one in a slumber: when he has told his tale, he will say, What is the matter?$n$,true),
(4374,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,12,6,12,$n$*Wisdom is glorious, and never fadeth away: yes, she is easily seen of them that love her, and found of such as seek her.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:12). *Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you:* (Matthew 7:7). Wisdom is found of such as seek her. The King's three verbs, ask, seek, knock, carry the same promise: it will be given, you will find, it will be opened. Solomon adds that she is easily seen of them that love her, which means the seeking is not a long climb but a turning toward what is already in view. The Father gives the good gift to those who ask, and Luke names the gift as the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) (Luke 11:13), which is the spirit of wisdom Solomon himself prayed for.$n$,$n$Wisdom is glorious, and never fadeth away: yes, she is easily seen of them that love her, and found of such as seek her.$n$,true),
(4375,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,13,6,13,$n$*She preventeth them that desire her, in making herself first known to them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:13). *Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you:* (Matthew 7:7). Preventeth in the old sense means goes before. Wisdom does not wait to be caught; she goes out ahead of those who desire her and makes herself known first. That is what the King's ask, seek, knock rests on: the disciple is not climbing up to a Father who has to be found, he is knocking on a door the Father is already standing behind. The asking is answered, the seeking finds, the knocking opens, because the one being sought came out to meet the seeker before the seeker set out.$n$,$n$She preventeth them that desire her, in making herself first known to them.$n$,true),
(4379,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,6,27,6,$n$*The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:6). *Ye shall know them by their fruits. Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?* (Matthew 7:16). Sirach says the fruit tells you whether the tree was dressed, and a man's speech tells you what is in his heart. Yahusha (Jesus) gives the same test for recognizing false prophets: you will know them by their fruits, because thorns do not yield grapes. The test was not new when he gave it; the wise in Yashar'el (Israel) had been teaching it for generations. What the gospel does is hand it to every disciple as the way to know a teacher, so that no one has to be fooled by the costume if he will look at the fruit.$n$,$n$The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.$n$,true),
(4380,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,7,27,7,$n$*Praise no man before you hear him speak; for this is the trial of men.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:7). *Wherefore by their fruits ye shall know them.* (Matthew 7:20). Sirach says do not praise a man until you have heard him speak, because that is where men are tried. Yahusha (Jesus) closes his warning about false prophets the same way: by their fruits you shall know them. A prophet's teaching is his fruit, and a teaching that voids the commandments of Yahuah (God) names the wolf no matter what signs he carries, because Deuteronomy 13 had already said the sign does not excuse the leading away. Hear him speak first; then you know what he is.$n$,$n$Praise no man before you hear him speak; for this is the trial of men.$n$,true),
(4381,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,16,22,16,$n$*As timber girt and bound together in a building cannot be loosed with shaking: so the heart that is stablished by advised counsel shall fear at no time.* (Ecclesiasticus 22:16). *Therefore whosoever heareth these sayings of mine, and doeth them, I will liken him unto a wise man, which built his house upon a rock:* (Matthew 7:24). Sirach says a heart established by sound counsel is like timber girded and bound into a building — shake it and it does not come loose, so it fears nothing. Yahusha (Jesus) says the wise man is the one who hears his sayings and does them, and he likens him to a house built on a rock. The counsel that establishes the heart is the King's sayings, and the doing of them is what binds the timber. Hearing alone leaves the building loose.$n$,$n$As timber girt and bound together in a building cannot be loosed with shaking: so the heart that is stablished by advised counsel shall fear at no time.$n$,true),
(4382,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,17,22,17,$n$*A heart settled upon a thought of understanding is as a fair plaistering on the wall of a gallery.* (Ecclesiasticus 22:17). *Therefore whosoever heareth these sayings of mine, and doeth them, I will liken him unto a wise man, which built his house upon a rock:* (Matthew 7:24). Sirach says a heart settled on a thought of understanding is like fine plaster on the wall of a gallery — finished, smooth, holding. Yahusha (Jesus) says the wise man is the one who hears his sayings and does them, and likens him to a house built on rock. The settled heart and the house on rock are the same man: his heart has come to rest on the King's words, and the building he is stands.$n$,$n$A heart settled upon a thought of understanding is as a fair plaistering on the wall of a gallery.$n$,true),
(4383,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,18,22,18,$n$*Pales set on an high place will never stand against the wind: so a fearful heart in the imagination of a fool cannot stand against any fear.* (Ecclesiasticus 22:18). *And the rain descended, and the floods came, and the winds blew, and beat upon that house; and it fell not: for it was founded upon a rock.* (Matthew 7:25). Sirach gives the opposite picture: pales set on a height will never stand against the wind, and the fearful heart of a fool cannot stand against any fear. Matthew says the rain came down, the floods came, the winds blew and beat on the house, and it did not fall because it stood on rock. The same wind that cannot move the house on the rock blows the pales flat. The difference is not the wind but the foundation, and the foundation is hearing and doing.$n$,$n$Pales set on an high place will never stand against the wind: so a fearful heart in the imagination of a fool cannot stand against any fear.$n$,true),
(4384,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *And I say unto you, That many shall come from the east and west, and shall sit down with Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, in the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 8:11). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and given back their inheritance as from the beginning, twelve tribes and not two. Yahusha (Jesus) says many will come from east and west and sit down with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom. The many from east and west are the children of those three fathers coming home from where they were scattered, which is exactly what Sirach asked for. They are not a new people put in the seat of Yashar'el (Israel); they are the tribes of Jacob taking their own seats at their fathers' table.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(4385,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,12,36,12,$n$*O Yahuah (God), have mercy upon the people that is called by your name, and upon Yashar'el (Israel), whom you have named your firstborn.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:12). *And I say unto you, That many shall come from the east and west, and shall sit down with Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, in the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 8:11). Sirach prays for mercy on the people called by the name of Yahuah (God), on Yashar'el (Israel) whom he named his firstborn. Yahusha (Jesus) says many will come from east and west and sit down with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom. The firstborn is one people, named from the beginning, and the many from east and west are that firstborn's scattered children coming to their fathers' table. The mercy Sirach asks for the firstborn is the seat Yahusha (Jesus) says they will take.$n$,$n$O Yahuah (God), have mercy upon the people that is called by your name, and upon Yashar'el (Israel), whom you have named your firstborn.$n$,true),
(4387,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,12,2,12,$n$*Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:12). *That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet, saying, Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses.* (Matthew 8:17). Wisdom of Solomon 2 is a long portrait of the righteous one hunted by the ungodly, from the lying in wait here to the shameful death they plan for him in verse 20. Isaiah painted the same man: despised, a man of sorrows, bearing our griefs and carrying our sorrows (Isaiah 53:3–4). Matthew says the healings fulfil that line; the one carrying away the sicknesses of Capernaum is the servant Isaiah saw. He is also the righteous one of Wisdom 2, and the reason the ungodly hate him is given here: his life reproves their breaking of the law. The one who bore our sicknesses was, at the same time, being watched by men who wanted him dead for being righteous.$n$,$n$Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.$n$,true),
(4388,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,18,2,18,$n$*For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:18). *That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet, saying, Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses.* (Matthew 8:17). The ungodly think the Father's help for his son would look like rescue from the hand of his enemies. Matthew shows what the help actually looked like: the son himself took our infirmities and bore our sicknesses, as Isaiah said he would. The just man who is the son of Yahuah (God) was not spared the suffering; he carried it, and carried it all through his ministry, not only on the last day. Deliverance came, but it came through the bearing and not around it.$n$,$n$For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.$n$,true),
(4393,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',14,3,14,3,$n$*But your providence, O Father, governeth it: for you have made a way in the sea, and a safe path in the waves;* (Wisdom of Solomon 14:3). *And he saith unto them, Why are ye fearful, O ye of little faith? Then he arose, and rebuked the winds and the sea; and there was a great calm.* (Matthew 8:26). The Father's providence governs the sea; he has made a way in it and a safe path in the waves. The Father governs the sea through his Word, the one who made the way in it, and on the lake of Galilee that one was asleep in the stern of a fishing boat while the disciples thought they were dying. He woke, rebuked the winds and the sea, and there was a great calm. The voice Solomon credits with governing the waters was in the boat in flesh, and the rebuke that calmed the water was the same governance Wisdom had named, exercised from a wet deck by the one who had made the way in the sea in the first place.$n$,$n$But your providence, O Father, governeth it: for you have made a way in the sea, and a safe path in the waves;$n$,true),
(4394,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',14,4,14,4,$n$*Shewing that you can save from all danger: yes, though a man went to sea without art.* (Wisdom of Solomon 14:4). *And he saith unto them, Why are ye fearful, O ye of little faith? Then he arose, and rebuked the winds and the sea; and there was a great calm.* (Matthew 8:26). Solomon says the Father can save from all danger at sea, even a man who went out without the skill to be there. The disciples were fishermen, and the storm outdid their skill anyway; their cry was Lord, save us, we perish (Matthew 8:25). He answered by asking why they were fearful and then rebuking the wind and the water into a great calm. The saving Solomon prayed about was done in front of them, by the one in the boat, and the rebuke of their little faith is that they had the governor of the sea aboard and thought they were on their own.$n$,$n$Shewing that you can save from all danger: yes, though a man went to sea without art.$n$,true),
(4399,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,1,35,1,$n$*He that keepeth the law brings offerings enough: he that takes heed to the commandment offers a peace offering.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:1). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Sirach says the man who keeps the law has already brought offerings enough, and the one who takes heed to the commandment has offered a peace offering. That is the key to what Yahusha (Jesus) meant by I will have mercy, and not sacrifice, which he took from Hosea. He was not setting mercy against the Torah; mercy is the Torah kept, and the Pharisees who kept the altar while despising the publicans and sinners at his table had kept the smaller part and dropped the greater. The commandment kept is the offering Yahuah (God) wanted all along.$n$,$n$He that keepeth the law brings offerings enough: he that takes heed to the commandment offers a peace offering.$n$,true),
(4400,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,2,35,2,$n$*He that requiteth a goodturn offers fine flour; and he that gives alms sacrificeth praise.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:2). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Sirach says that to repay a good turn is to offer fine flour and to give alms is to sacrifice praise. These are the altar's own words, fine flour and the sacrifice of praise, set on ordinary acts of kindness. When Yahusha (Jesus) told the Pharisees to go and learn what I will have mercy, and not sacrifice means, Sirach had already written out the lesson. Mercy shown is sacrifice offered, and the man who gives alms has stood at the altar whether he knows it or not.$n$,$n$He that requiteth a goodturn offers fine flour; and he that gives alms sacrificeth praise.$n$,true),
(4401,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,3,35,3,$n$*To depart from wickedness is a thing pleasing to Yahuah (God); and to forsake unrighteousness is a propitiation.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:3). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Sirach says departing from wickedness pleases Yahuah (God) and forsaking unrighteousness is a propitiation; the turning itself is the atonement. Yahusha (Jesus) says he came to call sinners to repentance, not the righteous, in the same breath as I will have mercy, and not sacrifice. The publicans at his table who turned had made the offering Sirach describes; the men standing outside objecting to the company had brought their sacrifices and left the turning undone.$n$,$n$To depart from wickedness is a thing pleasing to Yahuah (God); and to forsake unrighteousness is a propitiation.$n$,true),
(4402,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Give to the poor and turn your face from none, and the face of Yahuah (God) will not turn from you. That is the mercy Hosea asked for in place of sacrifice, the word Yahusha (Jesus) sends the Pharisees to go and learn. They were offended that he sat at table with sinners (9:11); Tobit had told his son not to turn his face from any poor man. Mercy toward the one in need is the heart of the Torah, and a system that keeps its credentials clean while turning its face away has lost the thing the commandment was for.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(4403,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,9,4,9,$n$*For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* (Tobit 4:9). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Alms laid up are a treasure against the day of need; Tobit sees the open hand as storing something before Yahuah (God). Yahusha (Jesus) tells the Pharisees to go learn Hosea's word, mercy and not sacrifice. They had stored up sacrifices and credentials; the heart of the Torah was the mercy Tobit's son was told to lay up. The treasure that counts in the day of necessity is the mercy that was done, and the King is calling a religious system back to it.$n$,$n$For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.$n$,true),
(4404,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,10,4,10,$n$*Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Tobit says alms deliver from death, and he says it in the voice of a father who has lived it. The Pharisees at Matthew's table had their sacrifices in order and their mercy withheld, and the King sent them to Hosea: mercy, and not sacrifice. Tobit had already named mercy as the thing that delivers. A religion that keeps its offerings and loses its mercy has kept the shell and lost the life.$n$,$n$Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.$n$,true),
(4405,'T','apocrypha','tobit',12,9,12,9,$n$*For alms does deliver from death, and shall purge away all sin. Those that exercise alms and righteousness shall be filled with life:* (Tobit 12:9). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Raphael's last word to Tobit and Tobias is that alms deliver from death and purge away all sin, and that those who do alms and righteousness are filled with life. This is mercy given the weight of life and death. In Matthew 9 the King forgives a paralytic (9:2) and sits at table with sinners, and when the Pharisees object he sends them to Hosea: mercy, and not sacrifice. Mercy is what the Torah asked for, mercy is what delivers, and the King and the angel are saying one thing.$n$,$n$For alms does deliver from death, and shall purge away all sin. Those that exercise alms and righteousness shall be filled with life:$n$,true),
(4406,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',12,4,12,4,$n$*Give to the godly man, and help not a sinner.* (Ecclesiasticus 12:4). *And it came to pass, as Yahusha (Jesus) sat at meat in the house, behold, many publicans and sinners came and sat down with him and his disciples.* (Matthew 9:10). Sirach says to give to the godly man and not to help a sinner, and the next verse shows what he means — bread given to the ungodly becomes strength he uses against you. Yahusha (Jesus) sat at meat with publicans and sinners. These are not two stances at war. The sinners at that table had come to him, and he was calling them to repentance, not feeding them in their sin. Sirach guards the hand; the King opens the door; and the Pharisee who turned the one into a wall against persons had misread it and could not see the other.$n$,$n$Give to the godly man, and help not a sinner.$n$,true),
(4407,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',12,5,12,5,$n$*Do well to him that is lowly, but give not to the ungodly: hold back your bread, and give it not to him, lest he overmaster you thereby: for else you shall receive twice as much evil for all the good you shall have done to him.* (Ecclesiasticus 12:5). *And when the Pharisees saw it, they said unto his disciples, Why eateth your Master with publicans and sinners?* (Matthew 9:11). Sirach says to do well to the lowly but to hold back your bread from the ungodly, because what you give him he will turn into power over you. The Pharisees asked why the Master ate with publicans and sinners, as if this verse had been broken. It had not. Sirach is warning against strengthening a man in his wickedness; Yahusha (Jesus) was eating with men who had come to hear him and were being called out of theirs. The ungodly in Sirach is the man who stays ungodly with your bread in his hand; the sinner at the King's table is the one who heard and sat down.$n$,$n$Do well to him that is lowly, but give not to the ungodly: hold back your bread, and give it not to him, lest he overmaster you thereby: for else you shall receive twice as much evil for all the good you shall have done to him.$n$,true),
(4408,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',12,7,12,7,$n$*Give to the good, and help not the sinner.* (Ecclesiasticus 12:7). *But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Matthew 9:13). Sirach says it again: give to the good, help not the sinner. Yahusha (Jesus) says he came not to call the righteous but sinners to repentance, and he sends the Pharisees to learn what mercy and not sacrifice means. The two verses meet at the word repentance. Sirach withholds help from the sinner who stays a sinner; the King calls the sinner to stop being one, and the mercy he names is the call itself. Neither verse feeds a man in his wickedness, and neither shuts the door on the man who turns.$n$,$n$Give to the good, and help not the sinner.$n$,true),
(4412,'T','apocrypha','tobit',12,8,12,8,$n$*Prayer is good with fasting and alms and righteousness. A little with righteousness is better than much with unrighteousness. It is better to give alms than to lay up gold:* (Tobit 12:8). *Then came to him the disciples of John, saying, Why do we and the Pharisees fast oft, but thy disciples fast not?* (Matthew 9:14). The angel Raphael tells Tobit and his son that prayer is good with fasting and alms and righteousness, all together, and that a little with righteousness outweighs much without it. John's disciples asked Yahusha (Jesus) why his disciples did not fast as they and the Pharisees did. He does not throw fasting out — he says the days will come when they will fast (9:15) — but he sets it where the angel set it, as one part of a life of mercy and righteousness, not a credential by itself. Fasting alone was never the discipline; fasting with prayer, alms, and righteousness is what the angel taught Tobit and what the King restores.$n$,$n$Prayer is good with fasting and alms and righteousness. A little with righteousness is better than much with unrighteousness. It is better to give alms than to lay up gold:$n$,true),
(4413,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',34,26,34,26,$n$*So is it with a man that fasteth for his sins, and goes again, and doeth the same: who will hear his prayer? or what does his humbling profit him?* (Ecclesiasticus 34:26). *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them? but the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them, and then shall they fast.* (Matthew 9:15). Sirach asks what a man's fast profits him when he fasts for his sins and then goes back and does the same; who will hear his prayer? When John's disciples asked why his disciples did not fast, Yahusha (Jesus) did not do away with fasting; he said the days would come when they would fast. But he placed it after the bridegroom is taken, in the grief of a people who have truly lost something, not as a routine kept while nothing changes. Sirach had already named the difference: a fast that turns from the sin is heard, and a fast that returns to it is noise.$n$,$n$So is it with a man that fasteth for his sins, and goes again, and doeth the same: who will hear his prayer? or what does his humbling profit him?$n$,true),
(4414,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',9,10,9,10,$n$*Forsake not an old friend; for the new is not comparable to him: a new friend is as new wine; when it is old, you shall drink it with pleasure.* (Ecclesiasticus 9:10). *Neither do men put new wine into old bottles: else the bottles break, and the wine runneth out, and the bottles perish: but they put new wine into new bottles, and both are preserved.* (Matthew 9:17). Sirach honors the old friend and the old wine; the new has to age before it is worth drinking. Yahusha (Jesus) is not quarreling with that. The question put to him in Matthew 9 was about fasting, and his answer is about the vessel: new wine poured into a brittle old skin bursts it and both are lost. The Torah is neither the wine nor the skin; it is the vineyard the wine comes from. The skin that cannot stretch is the body of added tradition, and the wine the King pours will itself grow old and be drunk with pleasure, exactly as Sirach says new wine does.$n$,$n$Forsake not an old friend; for the new is not comparable to him: a new friend is as new wine; when it is old, you shall drink it with pleasure.$n$,true),
(4415,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',38,9,38,9,$n$*My son, in your sickness be not negligent: but pray to Yahuah (God), and he will make you whole.* (Ecclesiasticus 38:9). *And, behold, a woman, which was diseased with an issue of blood twelve years, came behind him, and touched the hem of his garment:* (Matthew 9:20). Sirach tells the sick man not to be negligent but to pray to Yahuah (God), who will make him whole. The woman with the issue of blood had been sick twelve years, and she came behind Yahusha (Jesus) and touched the hem of his garment, and he turned and told her that her faith had made her whole. She was not going around Sirach's counsel; she was doing it. The one who spoke and walked with the fathers was standing in the crowd in flesh, and reaching for his garment was her prayer to Yahuah (God).$n$,$n$My son, in your sickness be not negligent: but pray to Yahuah (God), and he will make you whole.$n$,true),
(4416,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',38,12,38,12,$n$*Then give place to the physician, for Yahuah (God) has created him: let him not go from you, for you have need of him.* (Ecclesiasticus 38:12). *But Yahusha (Jesus) turned him about, and when he saw her, he said, Daughter, be of good comfort; thy faith hath made thee whole. And the woman was made whole from that hour.* (Matthew 9:22). Sirach does not slight the physician; he says Yahuah (God) created him and tells the sick man to keep him close. The woman with the issue of blood did exactly that for twelve years, and Mark 5:26 adds that she spent all she had on physicians and only grew worse. When she reached for the hem of his garment she was reaching past the physician to the one who made the physician, and Yahusha (Jesus) does not rebuke the years she spent under their care; he names her faith as what made her whole. The physician is a gift from his hand, and the healing was always his.$n$,$n$Then give place to the physician, for Yahuah (God) has created him: let him not go from you, for you have need of him.$n$,true),
(4417,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',38,14,38,14,$n$*For they shall also pray to Yahuah (God), that he would prosper that, which they give for ease and remedy to prolong life.* (Ecclesiasticus 38:14). *But when the people were put forth, he went in, and took her by the hand, and the maid arose.* (Matthew 9:25). Sirach says the physician himself prays to Yahuah (God) that the remedy in his hand would prosper, because the healing never belonged to the remedy. In the ruler's house the remedies have run out; the girl is dead, and no physician prays over a corpse. Yahusha (Jesus) walks in and takes her by the hand, and the one the physician prays to is the one standing in the room. The physician asks for the prospering; here the giver of it does the work with his own hand.$n$,$n$For they shall also pray to Yahuah (God), that he would prosper that, which they give for ease and remedy to prolong life.$n$,true),
(4418,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,13,16,13,$n$*For you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:13). *He said unto them, Give place: for the maid is not dead, but sleepeth. And they laughed him to scorn.* (Matthew 9:24). Power of life and death belongs to Yahuah (God): he leads to the gates of hell, the grave, and brings up again. In the ruler's house the King looked at a dead girl and said she was not dead but sleeping, and the mourners laughed at him. Then he took her by the hand and she arose. The one who brings up again from the gates of the grave was standing in the room in flesh, and death in his presence is sleep because he is the one who wakes it. The righteous dead are asleep the same way, and the hand that took the girl's hand is the one that will bring them up.$n$,$n$For you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.$n$,true),
(4419,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,14,16,14,$n$*A man indeed killeth through his malice: and the spirit, when it is gone forth, returns not; neither the soul received up comes again.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:14). *But when the people were put forth, he went in, and took her by the hand, and the maid arose.* (Matthew 9:25). The verse draws the line a man cannot cross: he can kill, but once the spirit has gone forth he cannot call it back. Then Yahusha (Jesus) takes a dead girl by the hand and she arises. What Wisdom of Solomon had just said belongs to Yahuah (God) alone — the power of life and death, leading down and bringing up again (16:13) — is done in a house in Capernaum by a man with his hand on a child. That is who he is: not the formless Father, but the one through whom the Father's power has always acted, now standing in flesh.$n$,$n$A man indeed killeth through his malice: and the spirit, when it is gone forth, returns not; neither the soul received up comes again.$n$,true),
(4420,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',12,31,12,31,$n$*And the lion, whom you sawest rising up out of the wood, and roaring, and speaking to the eagle, and rebuking her for her unrighteousness with all the words which you have heard; This is the anointed, which the Highest has kept for them and for their wickedness to the end: he shall reprove them, and shall upbraid them with their cruelty.* (2 Esdras 12:31–32). *And when Yahusha (Jesus) departed thence, two blind men followed him, crying, and saying, Thou Son of David, have mercy on us.* (Matthew 9:27). Ezra's lion comes up roaring out of the wood and rebukes the eagle, which he had been told is the fourth kingdom Daniel saw (2 Esdras 12:11), and the angel then names the lion: the anointed, kept by the Highest to the end. Two blind men on the road name the same figure by his line, Son of David. The anointed one Ezra saw as a lion, the blind men perceived with no eyes, and they cried out to him as present. The Messiah (Christ) kept for the end was walking past them.$n$,$n$And the lion, whom you sawest rising up out of the wood, and roaring, and speaking to the eagle, and rebuking her for her unrighteousness with all the words which you have heard;$n$,true),
(4421,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',12,32,12,32,$n$*This is the anointed, which the Highest has kept for them and for their wickedness to the end: he shall reprove them, and shall upbraid them with their cruelty.* (2 Esdras 12:32). *And when Yahusha (Jesus) departed thence, two blind men followed him, crying, and saying, Thou Son of David, have mercy on us.* (Matthew 9:27). The angel tells Ezra what the lion is: the anointed, kept by the Highest to the end, who will reprove the wicked for their cruelty. Two blind men cry to Yahusha (Jesus) as Son of David, the line the anointed king comes from. The one kept to the end to reprove the proud is the one who stops in the road for the blind who ask for mercy. The same anointed, seen by Ezra in a vision and met on a road.$n$,$n$This is the anointed, which the Highest has kept for them and for their wickedness to the end: he shall reprove them, and shall upbraid them with their cruelty.$n$,true),
(4422,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',12,34,12,34,$n$*For the rest of my people shall he deliver with mercy, those that have been pressed upon my borders, and he shall make them joyful until the coming of the day of judgment, of which I have spoken to you from the beginning.* (2 Esdras 12:34). *And when Yahusha (Jesus) departed thence, two blind men followed him, crying, and saying, Thou Son of David, have mercy on us.* (Matthew 9:27). The anointed does two things in Ezra's vision: he reproves the eagle, and he delivers the rest of the people with mercy, those pressed upon the borders, and makes them joyful until the day of judgment. The blind men ask the Son of David for exactly that, mercy. The remnant pressed at the borders are the scattered of Yashar'el (Israel), and the first work of the Messiah (Christ) toward them is mercy, as it was for the two who cried out. He saw the multitudes as sheep having no shepherd and was moved for them (Matthew 9:36).$n$,$n$For the rest of my people shall he deliver with mercy, those that have been pressed upon my borders, and he shall make them joyful until the coming of the day of judgment, of which I have spoken to you from the beginning.$n$,true),
(4423,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,28,7,28,$n$*For my son the Messiah shall be revealed with those that be with him, and they that remain shall rejoice within four hundred years.* (2 Esdras 7:28). *And when Yahusha (Jesus) departed thence, two blind men followed him, crying, and saying, Thou Son of David, have mercy on us.* (Matthew 9:27). Ezra is told that the Most High's own son, the Messiah, will be revealed, with those that are with him. Two blind men who cannot see him cry out to him on the road as the Son of David and ask for mercy. What was spoken to Ezra as something still coming, they confess as someone present. The one the Most High calls my son and the one the blind men call Son of David are the same man standing in the road.$n$,$n$For my son the Messiah shall be revealed with those that be with him, and they that remain shall rejoice within four hundred years.$n$,true),
(4429,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,35,4,35,$n$*Did not the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers, saying, How long shall I hope on this fashion? when comes the fruit of the floor of our reward?* (2 Esdras 4:35). *Then saith he unto his disciples, The harvest truly is plenteous, but the labourers are few;* (Matthew 9:37). The souls of the righteous in their chambers ask how long, and when comes the fruit of the floor, the threshing floor where the reward is given. The King looks out over the multitudes and says the harvest is plenteous and the labourers few. Both speak of one harvest. The souls are asking when the floor will be filled; the King is sending men into the field to fill it, and the next verse in 2 Esdras gives the answer the souls received, when the number of seeds is filled. The waiting dead and the working living are looking toward the same floor from opposite sides of it.$n$,$n$Did not the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers, saying, How long shall I hope on this fashion? when comes the fruit of the floor of our reward?$n$,true),
(4430,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,36,4,36,$n$*And to these things Uriel the archangel gave them answer, and said, Even when the number of seeds is filled in you: for he has weighed the world in the balance.* (2 Esdras 4:36). *Pray ye therefore Yahuah (Lord) of the harvest, that he will send forth labourers into his harvest.* (Matthew 9:38). Uriel's answer to the waiting souls is a number: the harvest comes when the number of seeds is filled, and he has weighed the world in the balance. The King's answer to a plenteous harvest with few labourers is a prayer: ask Yahuah (Lord) of the harvest to send forth labourers. The number is his and the timing is his, but the gathering is done by hands he sends, and the labourers he sends are how the seeds are filled up to the number. Esdras was shown why the end waits; the disciples were told what to do while it does.$n$,$n$And to these things Uriel the archangel gave them answer, and said, Even when the number of seeds is filled in you: for he has weighed the world in the balance.$n$,true),
(4433,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,40,13,40,$n$*Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.* (2 Esdras 13:40). *But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 10:6). Ezra is told plainly who the peaceable multitude of his vision is: the ten tribes Shalmaneser carried out of their own land and over the waters, who never came back. Those are the people the King means when he sends the twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel). A sheep is lost when it has wandered where the shepherd cannot see it, and the northern house had been lost in another land for seven centuries by the time Yahusha (Jesus) spoke these words. The mission of the twelve was aimed at the same body Ezra was shown.$n$,$n$Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.$n$,true),
(4434,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,41,13,41,$n$*But they took this counsel among themselves, that they would leave the multitude of the heathen, and go forth into a further country, where never mankind dwelt, That they might there keep their statutes, which they never kept in their own land.* (2 Esdras 13:41–42). *But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 10:6). The tribes did not simply vanish into the heathen. Ezra is told they took counsel to leave the multitude and go further out, into a country where no one lived, so that they could keep the statutes they had never kept in their own land. The carrying away became, for a remnant of them, a going out to keep Torah. Centuries later the King sends the twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), the same body, in the lands it went out to. The commandments they carried into the far country are what the sheep are being called back to, and Yahusha (Jesus) sends his messengers after them rather than waiting for them to find the way home alone.$n$,$n$But they took this counsel among themselves, that they would leave the multitude of the heathen, and go forth into a further country, where never mankind dwelt,$n$,true),
(4435,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,47,13,47,$n$*The Highest shall stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through: therefore sawest you the multitude with peace.* (2 Esdras 13:47). *But when they persecute you in this city, flee ye into another: for verily I say unto you, Ye shall not have gone over the cities of Yashar'el (Israel), till the Son of Adam be come.* (Matthew 10:23). The Most High stayed the river once so the tribes could cross out, and Ezra is promised he will stay it again so they can cross back, in the latter time. That return is the end of the story the vision tells. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the twelve they will not finish going over the cities of Yashar'el (Israel) before the Son of Adam comes; the work he sends them on is not completed in their lifetime but runs until his coming. Set together, the two say the same thing: the going after the lost sheep and the gathering of the ten tribes are one work, begun with the sending and finished when the Highest opens the way home and the King returns.$n$,$n$The Highest shall stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through: therefore sawest you the multitude with peace.$n$,true),
(4436,'T','apocrypha','tobit',13,5,13,5,$n$*And he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us.* (Tobit 13:5). *But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 10:6). Tobit lays out the whole order in one breath: Yahuah (God) scourges his people for their iniquities, has mercy again, and gathers them out of all the nations he scattered them into. The scourge was the scattering. The mercy is the gathering. When Yahusha (Jesus) sends the twelve, he points them at the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), the very people Tobit said would be gathered, and the sending is that mercy beginning to move. The gathering Tobit prayed for as a thing still to come is the mission the King commissions.$n$,$n$And he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us.$n$,true),
(4437,'T','apocrypha','tobit',13,3,13,3,$n$*Confess him before the Gentiles, you children of Yashar'el (Israel): for he has scattered us among them. There declare his greatness, and extol him before all the living: for he is our Elohim (God), and he is Yahuah (God) our Father for ever.* (Tobit 13:3–4). *Whosoever therefore shall confess me before men, him will I confess also before my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 10:32). Tobit sings this from the land of the captivity, and the instruction is not to hide among the Gentiles but to confess Yahuah (God) in front of them, to declare his greatness in the very places he scattered his children into. Yahusha (Jesus) is sending the twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) in this same chapter, and he gives them the same charge with a promise attached: confess me before men, and I will confess you before my Father. The scattered confess him where they were scattered, and the King names them before the Father. The confession Tobit called for and the confession Yahusha (Jesus) rewards are one confession, made by the same scattered people in the same territories.$n$,$n$Confess him before the Gentiles, you children of Yashar'el (Israel): for he has scattered us among them.$n$,true),
(4438,'T','apocrypha','tobit',14,5,14,5,$n$*And that again Yahuah (God) will have mercy on them, and bring them again into the land, where they shall build a temple, but not like to the first, until the time of that age be fulfilled; and afterward they shall return from all places of their captivity, and build up Jerusalem gloriously, and the house of Yahuah (God) shall be built in it for ever with a glorious building, as the prophets have spoken thereof.* (Tobit 14:5). *But when they persecute you in this city, flee ye into another: for verily I say unto you, Ye shall not have gone over the cities of Yashar'el (Israel), till the Son of Adam be come.* (Matthew 10:23). Tobit, dying, tells his son there will be two returns, not one. First a return to the land and a temple not like the first, which is the house Zerubbabel, Ezra, and Nehemiah raised after Babylon, a return of Yahudah (Judah) only. Then, after the time of that age is fulfilled, a return from all places of the captivity, Jerusalem built gloriously and the house of Yahuah (God) built in it for ever, as the prophets spoke. That second return has not happened. Between the two stands the work Yahusha (Jesus) sends the twelve into, and he tells them it will not be finished before the Son of Adam comes. The mission to the cities of Yashar'el (Israel) runs through this age toward the return Tobit saw at the end of it.$n$,$n$And that again Yahuah (God) will have mercy on them, and bring them again into the land, where they shall build a temple, but not like to the first, until the time of that age be fulfilled; and afterward they shall return from all places of their captivity, and build up Jerusalem gloriously, and the house of Yahuah (God) shall be built in it for ever with a glorious building, as the prophets have spoken thereof.$n$,true),
(4442,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',2,1,2,1,$n$*My son, if you come to serve Yahuah (God), prepare your soul for temptation.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:1). *And ye shall be hated of all men for my name’s sake: but he that endureth to the end shall be saved.* (Matthew 10:22). Sirach opens his second chapter by telling the one who comes to serve Yahuah (God) to expect temptation and get his soul ready for it, and the next line says how: set your heart aright, and constantly endure. Yahusha (Jesus) sends the twelve out with the same preparation. They will be hated for his name, and the one who endures to the end is the one who is saved. Neither voice promises the servant an easy road; both tie the outcome to endurance. The disciple's endurance is not something new the gospel invented; it is what serving Yahuah (God) has always asked, and Sirach had been saying so for generations before the twelve were sent.$n$,$n$My son, if you come to serve Yahuah (God), prepare your soul for temptation.$n$,true),
(4443,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',2,5,2,5,$n$*For gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:5). *And ye shall be hated of all men for my name’s sake: but he that endureth to the end shall be saved.* (Matthew 10:22). Sirach says the furnace is where acceptable men are proved, the way fire proves gold. Yahusha (Jesus) sends the twelve into that furnace with his eyes open: hated of all men for his name, and saved by enduring to the end. The hatred is not a sign the mission failed; it is the fire doing what fire does, and the disciple who comes through it is shown to be what Sirach calls acceptable. Adversity in the service of Yahuah (God) is the trial, not the verdict.$n$,$n$For gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.$n$,true),
(4444,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',2,18,2,18,$n$*Saying, We will fall into the hands of Yahuah (God), and not into the hands of men: for as his majesty is, so is his mercy.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:18). *And fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.* (Matthew 10:28). Those who fear Yahuah (God), Sirach says, would rather fall into his hands than into the hands of men, because his mercy is as great as his majesty. Yahusha (Jesus) gives the twelve the same choice with the stakes spelled out: men can kill the body and no more, while Yahuah (God) is able to destroy both soul and body in hell, so fear him and not them. Both verses move the fear off men and onto Yahuah (God), and both find that the safer place. The one whose hands hold the final judgment is the same one whose mercy matches his majesty, which is why falling into his hands is the choice of the one who fears him.$n$,$n$Saying, We will fall into the hands of Yahuah (God), and not into the hands of men: for as his majesty is, so is his mercy.$n$,true),
(4445,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,1,3,1,$n$*But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1). *And fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.* (Matthew 10:28). Men can kill the body. Wisdom says what they cannot touch: the souls of the righteous, which are in the hand of Yahuah (God). Yahusha (Jesus) tells his disciples the same thing before sending them out among wolves — do not fear the ones who can kill the body but cannot kill the soul. The persecutor's reach stops where the Father's hand begins. The righteous who died under the ungodly in Wisdom 2 were not lost; they were kept.$n$,$n$But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.$n$,true),
(4446,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,5,3,5,$n$*And having been a little chastised, they shall be greatly rewarded: for Yahuah (God) proved them, and found them worthy for himself.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:5). *And ye shall be hated of all men for my name’s sake: but he that endureth to the end shall be saved.* (Matthew 10:22). Wisdom calls the suffering of the righteous a proving: Yahuah (God) chastens a little, tests the metal, and finds them worthy of himself. Yahusha (Jesus) tells his disciples the same thing from the other side — you will be hated of all men for my name's sake, and the one who endures to the end will be saved. The hatred is the furnace; the enduring is what the furnace proves. The reward is great because the trial was real, and the Father is the one who did the proving.$n$,$n$And having been a little chastised, they shall be greatly rewarded: for Yahuah (God) proved them, and found them worthy for himself.$n$,true),
(4447,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,1,5,1,$n$*Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:1). *Whosoever therefore shall confess me before men, him will I confess also before my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 10:32). Wisdom looks to the day when the righteous man, whose labours his afflicters counted as nothing, stands in front of them in great boldness. The King gives his disciples the way into that day: confess him before men now, and he will confess them before the Father then. The confession is made in the face of the same people doing the afflicting, and the boldness Solomon saw is the boldness of the one being named by the Son in front of the Father while those who despised him look on.$n$,$n$Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.$n$,true),
(4458,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,33,4,33,$n$*Then I answered and said, How, and when shall these things come to pass? wherefore are our years few and evil?* (2 Esdras 4:33). *And said unto him, Art thou he that should come, or do we look for another?* (Matthew 11:3). Esdras, shown the end and the threshing, asks the question anyone in distress asks: how, and when, and why are our years few and evil. John, in Herod's prison, sends to ask Yahusha (Jesus), art thou he that should come, or do we look for another. John had preached the axe at the root and the fan in the hand, and the fire had not fallen while he sat in chains. Neither question is unbelief. Both are the faithful asking the one who knows the times why the times are not yet what he said they would be.$n$,$n$Then I answered and said, How, and when shall these things come to pass? wherefore are our years few and evil?$n$,true),
(4459,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,34,4,34,$n$*And he answered me, saying, Do not you hasten above the most Highest: for your haste is in vain to be above him, for you have much exceeded.* (2 Esdras 4:34). *And blessed is he, whosoever shall not be offended in me.* (Matthew 11:6). The angel's answer to Esdras is not a date; it is, do not hasten above the Most High, the times are his and your hurry cannot move them. The King's answer to John is not a date either; after naming the works being done he adds, blessed is he who is not offended in me. Both answers ask the same thing of the one waiting: that he not stumble over the gap between what was promised and what he can see from where he sits. The blessing is on the one who trusts the timing of Yahuah (God) when the timing is the hardest part.$n$,$n$And he answered me, saying, Do not you hasten above the most Highest: for your haste is in vain to be above him, for you have much exceeded.$n$,true),
(4460,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,35,4,35,$n$*Did not the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers, saying, How long shall I hope on this fashion? when comes the fruit of the floor of our reward?* (2 Esdras 4:35). *And blessed is he, whosoever shall not be offended in me.* (Matthew 11:6). The righteous dead ask from their chambers how long they must hope this way. John saw the same souls under the altar asking how long, O Yahuah (Lord), and they were told to rest a little while longer (Revelation 6:9–11). The King's word to John the Baptist in his prison is the word for everyone who waits: blessed is he who is not offended in me. The dead wait in their chambers, the living wait in their chains, and the blessing is on both for not letting the length of the wait turn into offense at the one who set its length.$n$,$n$Did not the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers, saying, How long shall I hope on this fashion? when comes the fruit of the floor of our reward?$n$,true),
(4464,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,10,48,10,$n$*Who were ordained for reproofs in their times, to pacify the wrath of the judgment of Yahuah (God), before it brake forth into fury, and to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:10). *And if ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come.* (Matthew 11:14). When Yahusha (Jesus) says John is the Elias who was to come, he is pointing at a job description everyone in the crowd knew. Sirach had written it out: Elijah is ordained to reprove, to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob. All of Jacob, all twelve tribes, the scattered house of Yashar'el (Israel) and the house of Yahudah (Judah) together. John began that work at the Jordan; the full restoring of the tribes waits on the great and dreadful day Malachi set it beside (Malachi 4:5). *If ye will receive it* is the hard part, because receiving it means receiving that the gathering of a scattered people is what Elijah comes to do.$n$,$n$Who were ordained for reproofs in their times, to pacify the wrath of the judgment of Yahuah (God), before it brake forth into fury, and to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.$n$,true),
(4465,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',2,58,2,58,$n$*Elias for being zealous and fervent for the law was taken up into heaven.* (1 Maccabees 2:58). *And if ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come.* (Matthew 11:14). Mattathias remembers Elijah (Elias) for one thing: he was zealous and fervent for the law, and he was taken up into heaven. That is how the faithful in Yashar'el (Israel) remembered him, as the man who stood for the commandments of Yahuah (God) against a kingdom that had traded them for Baal. When Yahusha (Jesus) says of John, this is Elijah (Elias) which was for to come, the likeness is that same zeal: John in the wilderness calling the leadership a generation of vipers, John in prison for telling Herod his marriage was unlawful. Elijah's zeal for the law is what Malachi promised would come before the great day, and it came first in John.$n$,$n$Elias for being zealous and fervent for the law was taken up into heaven.$n$,true),
(4469,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,12,2,12,$n$*Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:12). *The Son of Adam came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children.* (Matthew 11:19). The system faulted John for fasting and Yahusha (Jesus) for eating, which tells you the complaints were not the reason. Wisdom of Solomon gave the reason long before: the righteous one is clean contrary to our doings and upbraids us with our offending the law, so let us lie in wait for him. The glutton and winebibber charge is a cover for the grievance the ungodly admit among themselves, that his life exposes their departure from the Torah. *Wisdom is justified of her children* because wisdom's own book had already written the accusers' script.$n$,$n$Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.$n$,true),
(4470,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,15,2,15,$n$*He is grievous to us even to behold: for his life is not like other men’s, his ways are of another fashion.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:15). *The Son of Adam came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children.* (Matthew 11:19). The ungodly in Wisdom cannot even stand to look at the righteous man, because his life is cut to another pattern than theirs. That is the complaint behind the slander in Matthew: the Son of Adam sat at table with publicans and sinners, and the religious system of his day could only read a life of another fashion as gluttony and drunkenness. The charge changes its words from one age to the next, but it is the same offense — a righteous life reproves the ones watching it, and they answer by calling it unrighteous. Wisdom is justified of her children all the same.$n$,$n$He is grievous to us even to behold: for his life is not like other men’s, his ways are of another fashion.$n$,true),
(4471,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,20,2,20,$n$*Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:20). *The Son of Adam came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children.* (Matthew 11:19). The ungodly in Wisdom finish where they were always headed: condemn him to a shameful death. In Matthew 11 they are only at the beginning — calling him a glutton and a winebibber for sitting with sinners. The road runs from there to Matthew 12, where his works are credited to Beelzebub, and on to the trial and the cross in Matthew 26 and 27. A system that cannot deny what it sees moves from slander to false accusation to execution. Wisdom had named the end of that road before the gospel showed its first step.$n$,$n$Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.$n$,true),
(4472,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,19,24,19,$n$*Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:19). *The Son of Adam came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children.* (Matthew 11:19). Wisdom calls out: come to me, all you who desire me, and fill yourselves with my fruits. Yahusha (Jesus) came eating and drinking, and the ones who came to him were the publicans and the sinners, while the system called him a glutton and a winebibber — and then he says wisdom is justified of her children. The children of wisdom are the ones who heard the call and came to be filled. The invitation Sirach wrote down is the one the Son of Adam spoke in Galilee, and his table was where the filling happened.$n$,$n$Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.$n$,true),
(4473,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',3,36,3,36,$n$*He has found out all the way of knowledge, and has given it to Jacob his servant, and to Yashar'el (Israel) his beloved.* (Baruch 3:36). *The Son of Adam came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children.* (Matthew 11:19). Baruch says the whole way of knowledge was found out by Elohim (God) and then given, not to the world at large but to Jacob his servant and to Yashar'el (Israel) his beloved. Wisdom has a people she was given to. Yahusha (Jesus), reproached for eating with publicans and sinners, says wisdom is justified of her children: the ones who recognize her are her own. The children of wisdom in Matthew 11 are the covenant seed Baruch names, the people the knowledge was given to, and when the one who gave it stood among them in flesh they knew his voice even when the leaders did not. Her children are the proof she was given to Jacob.$n$,$n$He has found out all the way of knowledge, and has given it to Jacob his servant, and to Yashar'el (Israel) his beloved.$n$,true),
(4474,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',4,1,4,1,$n$*This is the book of the commandments of Yahuah (God), and the law that endureth for ever: all they that keep it shall come to life; but such as leave it shall die.* (Baruch 4:1). *The Son of Adam came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children.* (Matthew 11:19). Baruch names what the wisdom given to Jacob is: the book of the commandments of Yahuah (God), the law that endures for ever, which gives life to those who keep it and death to those who leave it. Wisdom is not a mood or a philosophy; she is the Torah. So when Yahusha (Jesus) says wisdom is justified of her children, her children are the ones who keep the commandments. John says the same thing from the other side: *He that saith, I know him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar, and the truth is not in him* (1 John 2:4). The law that endureth for ever has not been set aside by the one who spoke it at Sinai and walked in Galilee; wisdom's children are known by keeping it.$n$,$n$This is the book of the commandments of Yahuah (God), and the law that endureth for ever: all they that keep it shall come to life; but such as leave it shall die.$n$,true),
(4475,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,5,6,5,$n$*Horribly and speedily shall he come upon you: for a sharp judgment shall be to them that be in high places.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:5). *Woe unto thee, Chorazin! woe unto thee, Bethsaida! for if the mighty works, which were done in you, had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes.* (Matthew 11:21). A sharp judgment belongs to those in high places. Chorazin and Bethsaida were set in a high place: the mighty works were done in their streets, and they did not repent. Tyre and Sidon, cities the prophets thundered against, would have been in sackcloth long ago if they had seen the same works. The more that is shown to a city or a man, the sharper the reckoning, and Solomon had written that rule before the King applied it to the towns he had been healing in.$n$,$n$Horribly and speedily shall he come upon you: for a sharp judgment shall be to them that be in high places.$n$,true),
(4476,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,6,6,6,$n$*For mercy will soon pardon the meanest: but mighty men shall be mightily tormented.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:6). *But I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon at the day of judgment, than for you.* (Matthew 11:22). Mercy soon pardons the lowly; the mighty are mightily tormented. The King says it over the towns that saw his works: more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon at the day of judgment than for you. The judgment is real and it has degrees. Those who were shown little answer for little; those who were shown the kingdom working in their own streets answer for that. It is not one flat torment for everyone and it is not nothing; it is weighed.$n$,$n$For mercy will soon pardon the meanest: but mighty men shall be mightily tormented.$n$,true),
(4477,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,8,6,8,$n$*But a sore trial shall come upon the mighty.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:8). *And thou, Capernaum, which art exalted unto heaven, shalt be brought down to hell: for if the mighty works, which have been done in thee, had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.* (Matthew 11:23). A sore trial comes upon the mighty. Capernaum was mighty in the one way that counts in this chapter: it was exalted unto heaven, the King's own city where he lived and healed and taught. The sore trial for a city like that is to be brought down to hell, the grave, the place of the dead, lower than Sodom. The hell here is the pit the city goes down into, not the endless fire the pulpit reads into the word. Sodom would have stood if it had seen what Capernaum saw.$n$,$n$But a sore trial shall come upon the mighty.$n$,true),
(4480,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',10,7,10,7,$n$*Of whose wickedness even to this day the waste land that smoketh is a testimony, and plants bearing fruit that never come to ripeness: and a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an unbelieving soul.* (Wisdom of Solomon 10:7). *But I say unto you, That it shall be more tolerable for the land of Sodom in the day of judgment, than for thee.* (Matthew 11:24). The waste land still smoking, the fruit that never ripens, the pillar of salt standing as a monument of an unbelieving soul: Sodom was still preaching in Solomon's day. The King uses that monument as the floor. If the works done in Capernaum had been done in Sodom, Sodom would still be standing, and so it will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom in the day of judgment than for the town that saw the kingdom at work in its own streets and did not turn. The smoking land is the witness against the unbelieving soul; the city with the mighty works has a sharper witness against it.$n$,$n$Of whose wickedness even to this day the waste land that smoketh is a testimony, and plants bearing fruit that never come to ripeness: and a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an unbelieving soul.$n$,true),
(4485,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,18,3,18,$n$*The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).* (Ecclesiasticus 3:18). *At that time Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, I thank thee, O Father, Yahuah (Lord) of heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.* (Matthew 11:25). Sirach says favour before Yahuah (God) runs toward the one who humbles himself, and the greater he is, the lower he should go. Yahusha (Jesus) thanks the Father for the same thing working: these things hidden from the wise and prudent and revealed unto babes. The wise and prudent are the ones who did not humble themselves, who came to the Father with their standing in hand, and the revelation went past them to the ones with nothing to defend. Sirach names the principle; the prayer in Matthew 11 shows it happening.$n$,$n$The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4486,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,19,3,19,$n$*Many are in high place, and of renown: but mysteries are revealed to the meek.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:19). *At that time Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, I thank thee, O Father, Yahuah (Lord) of heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.* (Matthew 11:25). Sirach says it as plainly as it can be said: many are in high place and of renown, but mysteries are revealed to the meek. Yahusha (Jesus) prays the same pairing to the Father, hidden from the wise and prudent, revealed unto babes. The meek in Sirach and the babes in Matthew are the same people, the ones with nothing to defend when the Father speaks, and the high and renowned are the same people as the wise and prudent, the ones whose standing fills the space where the hearing would be. The two verses line up in their shape, high against low, hidden against revealed, and they name one rule of how Yahuah (God) gives understanding.$n$,$n$Many are in high place, and of renown: but mysteries are revealed to the meek.$n$,true),
(4487,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',7,7,7,7,$n$*Wherefore I prayed, and understanding was given me: I called upon Yahuah (God), and the spirit of wisdom came to me.* (Wisdom of Solomon 7:7). *At that time Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, I thank thee, O Father, Yahuah (Lord) of heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.* (Matthew 11:25). Solomon prayed, and understanding was given him; he called upon Yahuah (God), and the spirit of wisdom came. The King thanks the Father that these things were hidden from the wise and prudent and revealed unto babes. The two agree on where understanding comes from: it is given. Solomon asked and it was given him; the babes had nothing in hand and it was revealed to them; the wise and prudent, sure they already had it, were passed over.$n$,$n$Wherefore I prayed, and understanding was given me: I called upon Yahuah (God), and the spirit of wisdom came to me.$n$,true),
(4488,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',51,23,51,23,$n$*Draw near to me, you unlearned, and dwell in the house of learning.* (Ecclesiasticus 51:23). *Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.* (Matthew 11:28). Sirach ends his book with an invitation: draw near, you unlearned, and dwell in the house of learning. Yahusha (Jesus) issues the same one, come unto me, all you that labour and are heavy laden. Sirach was pointing people toward Wisdom; the one who speaks in Matthew is the Wisdom who stood beside the Maker before the world was (Ecclesiasticus 24:9; Proverbs 8:22–30), now speaking in his own voice. The load his hearers carried was the burden the scribes bound on them, *heavy burdens and grievous to be borne* (Matthew 23:4), not the Torah itself. The rest he offers is in the house of learning, not outside it.$n$,$n$Draw near to me, you unlearned, and dwell in the house of learning.$n$,true),
(4489,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',51,26,51,26,$n$*Put your neck under the yoke, and let your soul receive instruction: she is hard at hand to find.* (Ecclesiasticus 51:26). *Take my yoke upon you, and learn of me; for I am meek and lowly in heart: and ye shall find rest unto your souls.* (Matthew 11:29). Sirach's yoke is instruction, and he tells the soul to receive it, so the rest he found was not rest from the yoke but rest under it. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same thing in the same order: take my yoke, learn of me, and you will find rest for your souls. He is quoting Jeremiah, *ask for the old paths, where is the good way, and walk therein, and ye shall find rest for your souls* (Jeremiah 6:16). His yoke is not the absence of a yoke; it is the old path, his Father's commandments as he teaches and walks them, set in place of the heavy burdens the scribes had bound on men's shoulders (Matthew 23:4). Sirach put his neck under it and called it rest.$n$,$n$Put your neck under the yoke, and let your soul receive instruction: she is hard at hand to find.$n$,true),
(4490,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',51,27,51,27,$n$*Behold with your eyes, how that I have but little labour, and have gotten to me much rest.* (Ecclesiasticus 51:27). *For my yoke is easy, and my burden is light.* (Matthew 11:30). Sirach is not theorizing; he points to his own life and says look, little labour, much rest, and his neck is still under the yoke when he says it. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same of his yoke: easy, and the burden light. Neither man is describing a life without commandments. Moses said the commandment *is not hidden from thee, neither is it far off* (Deuteronomy 30:11), and John says *his commandments are not grievous* (1 John 5:3). The heavy thing was never the Torah; it was what men added to it, and the one who gave it carries it lightly.$n$,$n$Behold with your eyes, how that I have but little labour, and have gotten to me much rest.$n$,true),
(4491,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',6,28,6,28,$n$*For at the last you shall find her rest, and that shall be turned to your joy.* (Ecclesiasticus 6:28). *Take my yoke upon you, and learn of me; for I am meek and lowly in heart: and ye shall find rest unto your souls.* (Matthew 11:29). Sirach's sixth chapter is about putting the neck under wisdom's yoke; her fetters look like bondage at first, and at the last you find her rest, and the rest is turned to joy. Yahusha (Jesus) offers his yoke in the same words: take it, learn of me, and ye shall find rest unto your souls. The yoke is not the opposite of the rest; it is the road to it, in Sirach and in the Messiah alike. The pulpit offers a rest with no yoke in it, and the other side of the aisle calls the yoke a burden; both have lost what Sirach and the Messiah say in the same breath, that the rest is found at the end of wearing it.$n$,$n$For at the last you shall find her rest, and that shall be turned to your joy.$n$,true),
(4492,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,19,24,19,$n$*Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:19). *Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.* (Matthew 11:28). Wisdom has been calling this way all through Ecclesiasticus, and here she says it outright: come to me, all of you who want me, and be filled. When Yahusha (Jesus) stood in Galilee and said *come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden*, it was not a new voice. It was the same one, the Wisdom who sat beside the Maker in Ecclesiasticus 24, now speaking in flesh, and the ones he calls are the ones Sirach's Wisdom had already called, the weary who want her and have not yet been fed.$n$,$n$Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.$n$,true),
(4495,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',2,41,2,41,$n$*At that time therefore they decreed, saying, Whoever shall come to make battle with us on the sabbath day, we will fight against him; neither will we die all, as our brothers that were murdered in the secret places.* (1 Maccabees 2:41). *How much then is a man better than a sheep? Wherefore it is lawful to do well on the sabbath days.* (Matthew 12:12). After a thousand of their brothers were slaughtered in the caves rather than lift a hand on the sabbath, Mattathias and his men decreed that they would fight whoever came against them on that day. They were not setting the sabbath aside; they were refusing to let the heathen use the sabbath to root the people who kept it out of the earth. Yahusha (Jesus) makes the same judgment when he asks whether a man is worth more than a sheep pulled from a pit on the sabbath and answers that it is lawful to do well on the sabbath days. The sabbath was given for life; to preserve life on it is to keep it, not to break it.$n$,$n$At that time therefore they decreed, saying, Whoever shall come to make battle with us on the sabbath day, we will fight against him; neither will we die all, as our brothers that were murdered in the secret places.$n$,true),
(4496,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,1,35,1,$n$*He that keepeth the law brings offerings enough: he that takes heed to the commandment offers a peace offering.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:1). *But if ye had known what this meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice, ye would not have condemned the guiltless.* (Matthew 12:7). Sirach says keeping the law is bringing offerings enough, and heeding the commandment is itself a peace offering. Yahusha (Jesus) quotes Hosea, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice, against men who condemned the guiltless for picking grain on the Sabbath. Nothing in either verse loosens the Sabbath or the altar. What both say is that the man who holds the form of the commandment while condemning the hungry has not kept the commandment at all, and his sacrifice counts for nothing, while the one who shows mercy has brought the offering.$n$,$n$He that keepeth the law brings offerings enough: he that takes heed to the commandment offers a peace offering.$n$,true),
(4497,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',34,18,34,18,$n$*He that sacrificeth of a thing wrongfully gotten, his offering is ridiculous; and the gifts of unjust men are not accepted.* (Ecclesiasticus 34:18). *But if ye had known what this meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice, ye would not have condemned the guiltless.* (Matthew 12:7). Sirach says the offering of a thing wrongfully gotten is laughable before Yahuah (God), and the gifts of unjust men are not received. Yahusha (Jesus) told the Pharisees that if they had understood I will have mercy, and not sacrifice, they would not have condemned his hungry disciples for plucking grain on the Sabbath. Both lines say the same thing about the altar: it does not wash the hands that bring to it. A sacrifice offered while mercy is withheld from the guiltless is the unjust man's gift, and Yahuah (God) does not take it.$n$,$n$He that sacrificeth of a thing wrongfully gotten, his offering is ridiculous; and the gifts of unjust men are not accepted.$n$,true),
(4498,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,10,4,10,$n$*Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10). *But if ye had known what this meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice, ye would not have condemned the guiltless.* (Matthew 12:7). Tobit gives mercy its full weight: alms deliver from death and keep a man from the darkness. Mercy is not the soft side of the Torah, something to be set aside when the rules get strict. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same to the Pharisees on the Sabbath: if you had known what Hosea meant by mercy and not sacrifice, you would not have condemned the guiltless. The system that condemned hungry disciples had lost the thing Tobit's household had kept, and the King names the loss in Hosea's own words.$n$,$n$Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.$n$,true),
(4499,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,1,5,1,$n$*Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:1). *Then the Pharisees went out, and held a council against him, how they might destroy him.* (Matthew 12:14). The council that went out to plan his destruction was the system rendering its verdict on the righteous man. It had started by faulting his disciples, moved to calling him a drunkard and a friend of sinners, and now it was planning his death. Wisdom had already written the end of that story: the one the system condemned stands in great boldness before the faces of those who condemned him. The third day was the first installment of that standing, and the day of judgment is the rest of it, when the verdict rendered in this age is reversed in front of the men who rendered it.$n$,$n$Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.$n$,true),
(4500,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,4,5,4,$n$*We fools accounted his life madness, and his end to be without honour:* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:4). *Then the Pharisees went out, and held a council against him, how they might destroy him.* (Matthew 12:14). The men who held a council to destroy him accounted his life madness; before long they will say he casts out devils by Beelzebub. Wisdom puts their own confession in their mouths at the judgment: we fools, we accounted his life madness and his end without honour. The council of Matthew 12:14 is the first step of that story, and the verse that follows in Wisdom is its last, when they see him numbered among the children of Yahuah (God). The same reversal waits for everyone the system counts a fool for walking in his commandments now.$n$,$n$We fools accounted his life madness, and his end to be without honour:$n$,true),
(4505,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',4,1,4,1,$n$*My son, defraud not the poor of his living, and make not the needy eyes to wait long.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:1). *A bruised reed shall he not break, and smoking flax shall he not quench, till he send forth judgment unto victory.* (Matthew 12:20). Sirach tells his son not to defraud the poor and not to make the needy eyes wait long; the next line adds not to make a hungry soul sorrowful or provoke a man in his distress. That is a description of handling the bruised reed without breaking it and the smoking flax without quenching it, which is what Isaiah said of the Messiah and what Matthew says Yahusha (Jesus) fulfilled. The disposition Sirach asks of a son is the disposition the Son carried. A system that sorts the bruised and the smoldering into categories and dismisses them has lost both the counsel and the Messiah who kept it.$n$,$n$My son, defraud not the poor of his living, and make not the needy eyes to wait long.$n$,true),
(4506,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',4,4,4,4,$n$*Reject not the supplication of the afflicted; neither turn away your face from a poor man.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:4). *A bruised reed shall he not break, and smoking flax shall he not quench, till he send forth judgment unto victory.* (Matthew 12:20). Sirach is describing a face that stays turned toward the one in need, and Matthew 12:20 describes the Messiah's: a bruised reed he will not break, smoking flax he will not quench. The supplication of the afflicted is the bruised reed, and the poor man at the door is the smoking flax, easy to snap and easy to snuff. What Sirach asked of every son of Yashar'el (Israel), Yahusha (Jesus) did. A religious system that turns its face from the afflicted has abandoned the counsel and parted ways with the Messiah in the same motion.$n$,$n$Reject not the supplication of the afflicted; neither turn away your face from a poor man.$n$,true),
(4507,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,13,35,14,$n$*He will not despise the supplication of the fatherless; nor the widow, when she poureth out her complaint. Do not the tears run down the widow’s cheeks? and is not her cry against him that causeth them to fall?* (Ecclesiasticus 35:14–15). *A bruised reed shall he not break, and smoking flax shall he not quench, till he send forth judgment unto victory.* (Matthew 12:20). Sirach says Yahuah (God) does not despise the fatherless when they plead or the widow when she pours out her complaint. Matthew is quoting Isaiah about the servant who will not break a bruised reed or put out a smoking wick, and he says it of Yahusha (Jesus) in the same chapter where the Pharisees are plotting to destroy him. The ones who cause the tears and the one who hears them stand side by side. The widow's cry Sirach speaks of is heard by the same one who would not break a reed already bent.$n$,$n$He will not despise the supplication of the fatherless; nor the widow, when she poureth out her complaint.$n$,true),
(4513,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',12,10,12,10,$n$*But executing your judgments upon them by little and little, you gavest them place of repentance, not being ignorant that they were a naughty generation, and that their malice was bred in them, and that their cogitation would never be changed.* (Wisdom of Solomon 12:10). *And whosoever speaketh a word against the Son of Adam, it shall be forgiven him: but whosoever speaketh against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit), it shall not be forgiven him, neither in this world, neither in the world to come.* (Matthew 12:32). Yahuah (God) executed his judgments on the Canaanites little by little and gave them place of repentance, knowing all the while that their malice was bred in them and their cogitation would never be changed; the next verse calls them a cursed seed from the beginning. The King speaks of a sin that will not be forgiven in this world or the one to come, the word spoken against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) by men who had just watched the Spirit cast out devils and called it Beelzebub. Both passages describe a disposition that does not change: room to repent is given, and the heart that was never going to turn uses the room to harden. He said of such hearers that they believe not because they are not of his sheep (John 10:26).$n$,$n$But executing your judgments upon them by little and little, you gavest them place of repentance, not being ignorant that they were a naughty generation, and that their malice was bred in them, and that their cogitation would never be changed.$n$,true),
(4514,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,6,27,6,$n$*The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:6). *O generation of vipers, how can ye, being evil, speak good things? for out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh.* (Matthew 12:34). Sirach puts the two halves side by side: the fruit shows how the tree was kept, and what a man says shows the conceit of his heart. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same two things in one breath to the Pharisees, that a tree is known by its fruit and that the mouth speaks out of the abundance of the heart. Words are fruit. Bad fruit means a bad tree, and bad speech means a bad heart, and no amount of religious credential changes what the mouth gives away.$n$,$n$The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.$n$,true),
(4515,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,7,27,7,$n$*Praise no man before you hear him speak; for this is the trial of men.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:7). *But I say unto you, That every idle word that men shall speak, they shall give account thereof in the day of judgment.* (Matthew 12:36). Sirach says a man is tried when he speaks, so hear him before you praise him. Yahusha (Jesus) carries the same trial all the way to the end: every idle word men speak will be given account of in the day of judgment. What Sirach says a wise listener does now, the Judge will do then. The words are the evidence of the heart, and the heart is what the judgment weighs.$n$,$n$Praise no man before you hear him speak; for this is the trial of men.$n$,true),
(4516,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',1,11,1,11,$n$*Therefore beware of murmuring, which is unprofitable; and refrain your tongue from backbiting: for there is no word so secret, that shall go for nought: and the mouth that belieth slayeth the soul.* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:11). *But I say unto you, That every idle word that men shall speak, they shall give account thereof in the day of judgment.* (Matthew 12:36). Wisdom of Solomon says no word is so secret that it goes for nothing, and that a lying mouth kills the soul that owns it. Yahusha (Jesus) says every idle word will be accounted for in the day of judgment, and the next verse says what the accounting looks like: *by thy words thou shalt be justified, and by thy words thou shalt be condemned* (Matthew 12:37). Murmuring and backbiting feel like small, private sins. Both books say there is nothing private about them; the ear of jealousy hears everything, and the words are kept for the day.$n$,$n$Therefore beware of murmuring, which is unprofitable; and refrain your tongue from backbiting: for there is no word so secret, that shall go for nought: and the mouth that belieth slayeth the soul.$n$,true),
(4517,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,13,16,13,$n$*For you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:13). *For as Jonas was three days and three nights in the whale’s belly; so shall the Son of Adam be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.* (Matthew 12:40). The Father holds both doors: he leads down to the gates of hell and he brings up again. Yahusha (Jesus) gave that generation one sign, the sign of Jonah — three days and three nights in the heart of the earth, and then up. The resurrection was not a new idea arriving in Galilee; the library already knew that the one who has power of life and death brings up from the grave, and the King walked that road in his own body so the sign would stand in front of everyone.$n$,$n$For you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.$n$,true),
(4520,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,48,7,48,$n$*O you Adam, what have you done? for though it was you that sinned, you are not fallen alone, but we all that come of you.* (2 Esdras 7:48). *Then goeth he, and taketh with himself seven other spirits more wicked than himself, and they enter in and dwell there: and the last state of that man is worse than the first. Even so shall it be also unto this wicked generation.* (Matthew 12:45). Ezra cries out to Adam that his fall did not stay with him; everyone who comes of him fell with him, carrying the grain of evil seed sown in Adam's heart from the beginning (2 Esdras 4:30). Yahusha (Jesus) describes a house swept clean of its unclean spirit but left empty, so that the spirit returns with seven worse and the last state is worse than the first. A son of Adam cleaned out but not given a new heart is that empty house; the inherited pattern comes back to fill what was only swept. The remedy is not a cleaner house but a different heart in it (Ezekiel 36:26).$n$,$n$O you Adam, what have you done? for though it was you that sinned, you are not fallen alone, but we all that come of you.$n$,true),
(4522,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,13,2,13,$n$*He professeth to have the knowledge of Yahuah (God): and he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:13). *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* (Matthew 12:50). The righteous one in Wisdom of Solomon calls himself the child of Yahuah (God), and two verses later the ungodly say why they believe him enough to hate him: his life is not like other men's, his ways are of another fashion. Yahusha (Jesus) names the family of the Father the same way, whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother, sister, mother. The child of Yahuah (God) is known by a life that does the Father's will; that is how the Father's children are recognised. The ungodly recognised it too, and it was the recognising that made them lie in wait.$n$,$n$He professeth to have the knowledge of Yahuah (God): and he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4523,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,18,2,18,$n$*For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:18). *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* (Matthew 12:50). The ungodly have a test for who is the son of Yahuah (God): will the Father step in and deliver him? Yahusha (Jesus) gives the Father's own test for who belongs to his family: whoever does the will of my Father in heaven. One measures sonship by rescue, the other by obedience. Wisdom shows the just man already living by the second standard before the first is ever applied to him, and that is exactly why his life was grievous to them.$n$,$n$For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.$n$,true),
(4524,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',4,10,4,10,$n$*Be as a father to the fatherless, and instead of an husband to their mother: so shall you be as the son of the Most High, and he shall love you more than your mother does.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:10). *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* (Matthew 12:50). Sirach says the man who fathers the fatherless and stands in for the widow's husband shall be as the son of the Most High, loved by him more than a mother loves. Yahusha (Jesus) says whoever does the will of his Father is his brother, sister, and mother. In both, the family of Elohim (God) is known by the doing of the Father's will, which in Sirach's example is the Torah's own care for the orphan and widow. The doing does not manufacture the sonship; it shows it, the way a son is known by bearing his father's likeness, and that is why Yahusha (Jesus) names his kin by it.$n$,$n$Be as a father to the fatherless, and instead of an husband to their mother: so shall you be as the son of the Most High, and he shall love you more than your mother does.$n$,true),
(4527,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,9,5,9,$n$*And salt waters shall be found in the sweet, and all friends shall destroy one another; then shall wit hide itself, and understanding withdraw itself into his secret chamber, And shall be sought of many, and yet not be found: then shall unrighteousness and incontinency be multiplied upon earth.* (2 Esdras 5:9–10). *He answered and said unto them, Because it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it is not given.* (Matthew 13:11). Esdras is shown a day when understanding withdraws into its secret chamber and is sought by many and not found. The King tells his disciples why he speaks in parables: to you it is given to know the mysteries of the kingdom, to them it is not given. Understanding does not vanish from the earth; it withdraws from those who would not have it and is given to those who hear. The many who seek and do not find in Esdras are the ones who, in Matthew, hear and do not understand, because what is withdrawn from one is a gift to the other, and the hearing reveals which is which.$n$,$n$And salt waters shall be found in the sweet, and all friends shall destroy one another; then shall wit hide itself, and understanding withdraw itself into his secret chamber,$n$,true),
(4528,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',1,4,1,4,$n$*For into a malicious soul wisdom shall not enter; nor dwell in the body that is subject to sin.* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:4). *Therefore speak I to them in parables: because they seeing see not; and hearing they hear not, neither do they understand.* (Matthew 13:13). Wisdom will not move into a soul that is set against her. Yahusha (Jesus) speaks in parables for the same reason he gives in the next lines, quoting Isaiah: *this people's heart is waxed gross, and their ears are dull of hearing, and their eyes they have closed* (Matthew 13:15). The parable did not shut them out; the closed eyes were their own doing, and wisdom does not force the door. In the same breath he tells the disciples their eyes are blessed because they see (Matthew 13:16). The teaching was one teaching; what differed was the soul it met.$n$,$n$For into a malicious soul wisdom shall not enter; nor dwell in the body that is subject to sin.$n$,true),
(4529,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',39,6,39,6,$n$*When the great Yahuah (God) will, he shall be filled with the spirit of understanding: he shall pour out wise sentences, and give thanks to Yahuah (God) in his prayer.* (Ecclesiasticus 39:6). *But blessed are your eyes, for they see: and your ears, for they hear.* (Matthew 13:16). Sirach says the scribe is filled with the spirit of understanding when the great Yahuah (God) wills it; the man does not work it up out of his own study, he is filled. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the disciples their eyes and ears are blessed, and the verse before explains why: *it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom* (Matthew 13:11). The seeing and the hearing are given the same way the scribe's understanding is given. The hand that fills the scribe is the hand that opens the disciple's eyes, and both of them answer with thanks.$n$,$n$When the great Yahuah (God) will, he shall be filled with the spirit of understanding: he shall pour out wise sentences, and give thanks to Yahuah (God) in his prayer.$n$,true),
(4532,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,30,4,30,$n$*For the grain of evil seed has been sown in the heart of Adam from the beginning, and how much ungodliness has it brought up to this time? and how much shall it yet bring forth until the time of threshing come?* (2 Esdras 4:30). *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* (Matthew 13:24). Uriel says a grain of evil seed was sown in the heart of Adam from the beginning, that it has brought up ungodliness ever since, and that it will go on bearing until the time of threshing. Yahusha (Jesus) opens his parable with a man sowing good seed in his field, and the verses that follow tell of the enemy's seed and the harvest when the two are separated. Esdras and the King both speak of seed, of two kinds of seed in one ground, and of a threshing that ends the matter. The evil seed is not the last word in either book; the threshing is.$n$,$n$For the grain of evil seed has been sown in the heart of Adam from the beginning, and how much ungodliness has it brought up to this time? and how much shall it yet bring forth until the time of threshing come?$n$,true),
(4533,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',3,21,3,21,$n$*For the first Adam bearing a wicked heart transgressed, and was overcome; and so be all they that are born of him.* (2 Esdras 3:21). *But while men slept, his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat, and went his way.* (Matthew 13:25). Esdras says the first Adam bore a wicked heart, transgressed, and was overcome, and so are all who are born of him. The parable says that while men slept an enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat. These are two different facts about one field. The sons of Adam carry the wicked heart, and the law was given to bring fruit out of them in spite of it; the tares are another seed, planted by an enemy, not born of Adam at all. The wheat is wounded, the tares are not wheat, and the harvest will tell them apart.$n$,$n$For the first Adam bearing a wicked heart transgressed, and was overcome; and so be all they that are born of him.$n$,true),
(4534,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,6,8,6,$n$*O Yahuah (God), if you suffer not your servant, that we may pray before you, and you give us seed to our heart, and culture to our understanding, that there may come fruit of it; how shall each man live that is corrupt, who bears the place of a man?* (2 Esdras 8:6). *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* (Matthew 13:24). Ezra prays for seed to the heart and culture to the understanding, so that fruit may come, because a corrupt man cannot live any other way. The kingdom parable answers that prayer with a sower: a man goes out and sows good seed in his field. The seed to the heart Ezra begs for is what the sower is sowing; the fruit Ezra hopes for is what the good ground brings forth (Matthew 13:23). The prayer and the parable meet in the same field.$n$,$n$O Yahuah (God), if you suffer not your servant, that we may pray before you, and you give us seed to our heart, and culture to our understanding, that there may come fruit of it; how shall each man live that is corrupt, who bears the place of a man?$n$,true),
(4543,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,7,3,7,$n$*And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:7). *Then shall the righteous shine forth as the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Who hath ears to hear, let him hear.* (Matthew 13:43). Wisdom puts the shining of the righteous at a set time — the time of their visitation — and sets it in a harvest scene: sparks running among the stubble. Yahusha (Jesus) puts it at the same moment in the parable of the tares: when the field is reaped and the tares are bundled for the fire, then shall the righteous shine forth as the sun. The stubble burning and the sparks flying are the same picture as the tares burning and the wheat shining. The shining is not yet; it waits on the harvest.$n$,$n$And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.$n$,true),
(4546,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,22,6,22,$n$*As for wisdom, what she is, and how she came up, I will tell you, and will not hide mysteries from you: but will seek her out from the beginning of her nativity, and bring the knowledge of her into light, and will not pass over the truth.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:22). *That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, saying, I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter things which have been kept secret from the foundation of the world.* (Matthew 13:35). Solomon promises to tell what wisdom is and how she came up, to hide no mysteries, to trace her from the beginning of her nativity and bring the knowledge of her into light. The King opens his mouth in parables to utter things kept secret from the foundation of the world. Both are teachers who refuse to pass over the truth; both are bringing into the light what had been hidden, and the parables, which the crowd heard as stories, were that light being given to the disciples who asked what they meant.$n$,$n$As for wisdom, what she is, and how she came up, I will tell you, and will not hide mysteries from you: but will seek her out from the beginning of her nativity, and bring the knowledge of her into light, and will not pass over the truth.$n$,true),
(4547,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',6,26,6,26,$n$*And the men that are received shall see it, who have not tasted death from their birth: and the heart of the inhabitants shall be changed, and turned into another meaning.* (2 Esdras 6:26). *That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, saying, I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter things which have been kept secret from the foundation of the world.* (Matthew 13:35). Among the tokens of the end Ezra is told that the heart of the inhabitants will be changed and turned into another meaning, and that men who have not tasted death will see it. When Yahusha (Jesus) teaches in parables he is uttering what had been kept secret from the foundation of the world, and a parable only opens to a heart that has been turned toward its meaning; to everyone else it stays a story about seed and soil. Ezra goes on to hear that the truth so long without fruit shall be declared (2 Esdras 6:28). The parables are that declaring begun, and the changed heart is the one that hears them.$n$,$n$And the men that are received shall see it, who have not tasted death from their birth: and the heart of the inhabitants shall be changed, and turned into another meaning.$n$,true),
(4548,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',20,30,20,30,$n$*Wisdom that is hid, and treasure that is hoarded up, what profit is in them both?* (Ecclesiasticus 20:30). *Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto treasure hid in a field; the which when a man hath found, he hideth, and for joy thereof goeth and selleth all that he hath, and buyeth that field.* (Matthew 13:44). Sirach asks what good hidden wisdom or hoarded treasure does anyone — none, until it is found and brought out. Yahusha (Jesus) tells of treasure hidden in a field, and of the man who finds it and sells everything he has to buy the field, because treasure found is worth all of it. The man who buys the field is the one who paid the whole price: the Father, through the Son, finding his own scattered through the field of the world and giving everything to obtain them. The point Sirach made holds either way — hidden treasure profits no one until someone finds it, and finding it is worth everything.$n$,$n$Wisdom that is hid, and treasure that is hoarded up, what profit is in them both?$n$,true),
(4549,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',6,14,6,14,$n$*A faithfull friend is a strong defence: and he that has found such an one has found a treasure.* (Ecclesiasticus 6:14). *Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto a merchant man, seeking goodly pearls:* (Matthew 13:45). Sirach says a faithful friend is a treasure, and the next verse says nothing countervails him; there is no price you could set against him. Yahusha (Jesus) says the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking goodly pearls who finds one worth everything he owns and sells all to have it. The merchant is Yahuah (God), and the pearl is the seed of promise — always the few, scattered like gold through the dust of the earth, and worth to him everything he has. Sirach knew that kind of worth in a friend; the Father knows it in his own, and when he finds them he does not haggle.$n$,$n$A faithfull friend is a strong defence: and he that has found such an one has found a treasure.$n$,true),
(4550,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',7,8,7,8,$n$*I preferred her before sceptres and thrones, and esteemed riches nothing in comparison of her.* (Wisdom of Solomon 7:8). *Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto a merchant man, seeking goodly pearls:* (Matthew 13:45). A king with a sceptre and a throne in hand says he preferred wisdom before both of them and counted riches nothing beside her. The merchant in the parable does the same arithmetic over one pearl: he sells all he has to get it. That merchant is Yahuah (God), and the pearl of great price is the seed of promise, the few he has always called his treasure, found scattered in the nations like gold in the dust of the earth. Solomon weighed sceptres against wisdom and they came up light; the Father weighs everything against his own and pays it all.$n$,$n$I preferred her before sceptres and thrones, and esteemed riches nothing in comparison of her.$n$,true),
(4551,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',7,14,7,14,$n$*For she is a treasure to men that never fails: which they that use become the friends of Yahuah (God), being commended for the gifts that come from learning.* (Wisdom of Solomon 7:14). *Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto treasure hid in a field; the which when a man hath found, he hideth, and for joy thereof goeth and selleth all that he hath, and buyeth that field.* (Matthew 13:44). Wisdom is a treasure to men that never fails, and those who use her become the friends of Yahuah (God). The King says the kingdom of heaven is like treasure hid in a field, found by a man who hides it again, sells all he has for joy, and buys the field. The man who buys the field is Yahuah (God), and the treasure hid in it is the seed of promise, the few scattered through the field of the world like gold in the dust of the earth; he gives everything to have the field so that he has them. The one who learns wisdom becomes his friend; the one he buys the field for was his treasure before anyone dug.$n$,$n$For she is a treasure to men that never fails: which they that use become the friends of Yahuah (God), being commended for the gifts that come from learning.$n$,true),
(4553,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,3,8,3,$n$*There be many created, but few shall be saved.* (2 Esdras 8:3). *So shall it be at the end of the world: the angels shall come forth, and sever the wicked from among the just,* (Matthew 13:49). Ezra is told that many are created but few saved, after the likeness of much clay and little gold (2 Esdras 8:2). Yahusha (Jesus) puts the sorting at the end of the world, when the angels sever the wicked from among the just as a net full of every kind is sorted on the shore. The net gathers all; the shore keeps few. The few saved are what remains when that severing is done.$n$,$n$There be many created, but few shall be saved.$n$,true),
(4555,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,8,3,8,$n$*They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:8). *So shall it be at the end of the world: the angels shall come forth, and sever the wicked from among the just,* (Matthew 13:49). Wisdom says the righteous will judge the nations and have dominion over the people. Yahusha (Jesus) says what has to happen first: at the end of the age the angels come and sever the wicked from among the just. The sorting comes before the ruling. Once the wicked are taken out from among them, the just who are left are the ones Wisdom sees judging the nations — a kingdom of priests to the peoples left alive after that day, under an Elohim (God) who reigns for ever.$n$,$n$They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.$n$,true),
(4556,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,24,2,24,$n$*Nevertheless through envy of the devil came death into the world: and they that do hold of his side do find it.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:24). *The enemy that sowed them is the devil; the harvest is the end of the world; and the reapers are the angels.* (Matthew 13:39). Wisdom names where death came from — the envy of the devil — and who finds it: they that hold of his side. Yahusha (Jesus) names the same enemy as the one who sowed the tares in the field, and in the verse before he calls the tares the children of the wicked one. The serpent in the garden, the watchers who corrupted the earth, the sower of tares in the field — it is one enemy doing one work across the whole story. Those who are of his side come to his end, because the seed shows at the harvest.$n$,$n$Nevertheless through envy of the devil came death into the world: and they that do hold of his side do find it.$n$,true),
(4559,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,1,48,1,$n$*Then stood up Elias the prophet as fire, and his word burned like a lamp.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:1). *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3). Elijah's fire burned hottest at the king's court. He told Ahab to his face that the house of Ahab had forsaken the commandments of Yahuah (God), and Jezebel sought his life for it. John carried the same fire to Herod's court and said *it is not lawful for thee to have her* (Matthew 14:4), the Torah spoken against the king's own bed (Leviticus 18:16). Herod answered the prophet the way Ahab's house had answered Elijah: the king turned on him and a queen wanted him dead; this time the prophet was caught and bound. The word that burns like a lamp is not dimmed for royalty, and royalty does not forgive it.$n$,$n$Then stood up Elias the prophet as fire, and his word burned like a lamp.$n$,true),
(4560,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,2,48,2,$n$*He brought a sore famine upon them, and by his zeal he diminished their number.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:2). *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3). Elijah's zeal against Ahab's idolatry shut up heaven; *there shall not be dew nor rain these years, but according to my word* (1 Kings 17:1), and the famine thinned the land. John's zeal against Herod's unlawful marriage brought no famine; it brought a prison cell and, before long, his head on a platter. The zeal is the same zeal, a prophet unwilling to let a king break the Torah in peace. Sirach records what it cost Ahab's people; Matthew records what it cost the prophet. Neither man softened the word to spare himself the consequences.$n$,$n$He brought a sore famine upon them, and by his zeal he diminished their number.$n$,true),
(4561,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,10,48,10,$n$*Who were ordained for reproofs in their times, to pacify the wrath of the judgment of Yahuah (God), before it brake forth into fury, and to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:10). *And when he would have put him to death, he feared the multitude, because they counted him as a prophet.* (Matthew 14:5). Herod would have killed John and did not, because the people counted him a prophet. They were right, and more right than they knew. Sirach describes the prophet they were looking at: ordained for reproofs in his time, to hold back the wrath of judgment before it broke out, to turn the heart of the father to the son, to restore the tribes of Jacob. John reproved a king, called a nation to turn, and prepared the way for the one who gathers Jacob. Herod feared the crowd and not the Torah, and the crowd, for once, had the better instinct.$n$,$n$Who were ordained for reproofs in their times, to pacify the wrath of the judgment of Yahuah (God), before it brake forth into fury, and to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.$n$,true),
(4562,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',48,11,48,11,$n$*Blessed are they that saw you, and slept in love; for we shall surely live.* (Ecclesiasticus 48:11). *And his disciples came, and took up the body, and buried it, and went and told Yahusha (Jesus).* (Matthew 14:12). Sirach blesses the ones who saw Elijah and then fell asleep in love, and gives the reason in one line: for we shall surely live. Death is a sleep for the righteous, and the sleeper will wake. John's disciples saw the Elijah of their day, and when Herod took his head they took up his body and buried him, and then they went and told Yahusha (Jesus). They carried the news of the prophet's death to the one who holds the life Sirach was sure of. They had seen Elijah, they buried him in love, and they walked straight to the resurrection.$n$,$n$Blessed are they that saw you, and slept in love; for we shall surely live.$n$,true),
(4563,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',1,19,1,19,$n$*Then had I pity upon your mournings, and gave you manna to eat; so you did eat angels’ bread.* (2 Esdras 1:19). *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13). In 2 Esdras Yahuah (God) reminds the people that when they murmured in the wilderness he pitied them and fed them with manna, angels' bread. In Matthew the King goes by ship into a desert place and the people follow on foot out of the cities, and the verses that follow say he was moved with compassion and fed them there. The same pity, the same wilderness, the same bread given to a multitude that had nothing. The one who fed the fathers by the hand of Moses fed the children with his own hands by the sea of Galilee.$n$,$n$Then had I pity upon your mournings, and gave you manna to eat; so you did eat angels’ bread.$n$,true),
(4564,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,20,16,20,$n$*Instead of which you feddest your own people with angels’ food, and did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour, able to content every man’s delight, and agreeing to every taste.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:20). *And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* (Matthew 14:19). Bread from heaven, prepared without their labour, was how the Father fed his own people in the wilderness. On the grass in a desert place Yahusha (Jesus) looks up to heaven, blesses, breaks, and the loaves go out through the disciples' hands to a multitude that brought nothing but its hunger. The same hand that sent the manna is feeding Yashar'el (Israel) again, and John's account of this same meal ends with him saying who the bread is: *I am the bread of life* (John 6:35).$n$,$n$Instead of which you feddest your own people with angels’ food, and did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour, able to content every man’s delight, and agreeing to every taste.$n$,true),
(4565,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,26,16,26,$n$*That your children, O Yahuah (God), whom you lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:26). *And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* (Matthew 14:19). The manna was a lesson, and Wisdom of Solomon says what the lesson was: it is not the harvest that keeps a man alive but the word of Yahuah (God). When the King blesses five loaves and feeds a multitude, the loaves are the smaller thing; the disciples who hand them out have been receiving his word all along, and in John's telling of this same meal he says outright that his words are spirit and life (John 6:63). The bread points past itself to the word that preserves those who trust him.$n$,$n$That your children, O Yahuah (God), whom you lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.$n$,true),
(4566,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,19,24,19,$n$*Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:19). *And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the fragments that remained twelve baskets full.* (Matthew 14:20). Wisdom says come and fill yourselves with my fruits, and in the desert place Yahusha (Jesus) did exactly that: the multitude came to him, they ate, and they were filled. He is the Wisdom of Ecclesiasticus 24 feeding her children with her own hand, and the invitation of Matthew 11:28, *come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden*, is the same mouth speaking. Twelve baskets were left over, one for every tribe of Yashar'el (Israel), and the leftover says the feeding was never meant for that hillside alone. The scattered children of Wisdom will be gathered and fed the same way.$n$,$n$Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.$n$,true),
(4567,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',43,23,43,23,$n$*By his counsel he appeaseth the deep, and planteth islands in it.* (Ecclesiasticus 43:23). *And in the fourth watch of the night Yahusha (Jesus) went unto them, walking on the sea.* (Matthew 14:25). Sirach praises the Maker who stills the deep by his counsel and sets islands in it, and Job says of the same one that he *alone spreadeth out the heavens, and treadeth upon the waves of the sea* (Job 9:8). In the fourth watch of the night the disciples watch a man do what Job said belongs to Elohim (God) alone. The one whose counsel appeases the deep is walking across it. The one who spoke the sea into its bounds came in flesh and treads on his own work.$n$,$n$By his counsel he appeaseth the deep, and planteth islands in it.$n$,true),
(4568,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',43,25,43,25,$n$*For in it be strange and wondrous works, variety of all kinds of beasts and whales created.* (Ecclesiasticus 43:25). *And in the fourth watch of the night Yahusha (Jesus) went unto them, walking on the sea.* (Matthew 14:25). Sirach looks at the sea as a storehouse of the Creator's wonders, every beast and whale in it created, and in the next line he says *by his word all things consist* (Ecclesiasticus 43:26). The disciples saw a figure on the water and took it for a spirit. What they saw was the Creator of the sea walking on his own creation, and when he spoke he gave them his name, *it is I; be not afraid* (Matthew 14:27). The word that holds the sea together was the word speaking to them from on top of it.$n$,$n$For in it be strange and wondrous works, variety of all kinds of beasts and whales created.$n$,true),
(4569,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',14,3,14,3,$n$*But your providence, O Father, governeth it: for you have made a way in the sea, and a safe path in the waves;* (Wisdom of Solomon 14:3). *And in the fourth watch of the night Yahusha (Jesus) went unto them, walking on the sea.* (Matthew 14:25). The Father has made a way in the sea and a safe path in the waves; the psalmist says the same, that his way is in the sea and his path in the great waters (Psalm 77:19). In the fourth watch the King walks that way, on the water, toward a boat full of men being tossed by it. The Formed one walks the path the Father's providence made, and when Peter steps out and sinks, the hand that catches him (Matthew 14:31) is the safe path in the waves Solomon prayed about. The way in the sea was never a figure of speech; a man walked it.$n$,$n$But your providence, O Father, governeth it: for you have made a way in the sea, and a safe path in the waves;$n$,true),
(4570,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',18,15,18,15,$n$*Your Almighty word leaped down from heaven out of your royal throne, as a fierce man of war into the midst of a land of destruction,* (Wisdom of Solomon 18:15). *But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.* (Matthew 14:27). The almighty word leaped down from the throne into Egypt's night as a man of war. In the fourth watch of another night the disciples see a figure on the sea and are terrified, and the Word speaks: it is I; be not afraid. The one who came down from the Father's throne on Passover night is the same one walking the dark water, the Word who was with Elohim (God) and was made flesh (John 1:1, 14). Wisdom of Solomon shows the Word as one who comes down and acts; Matthew shows him in flesh naming himself.$n$,$n$Your Almighty word leaped down from heaven out of your royal throne, as a fierce man of war into the midst of a land of destruction,$n$,true),
(4571,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,3,24,3,$n$*I came out of the mouth of the Most High, and covered the earth as a cloud.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:3). *But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.* (Matthew 14:27). Wisdom says she came out of the mouth of the Most High and covered the earth as a cloud. The one who came out of the Father's mouth is the Word the Father spoke — the Formed one beside the formless Maker — and the prophets knew him as the one who rides the clouds. On the water Yahusha (Jesus) says to the frightened men, it is I, the same words he uses when he says that before Abraham was, I am. The Wisdom that came out of the mouth of the Most High is the one walking on the sea, and he tells them not to be afraid.$n$,$n$I came out of the mouth of the Most High, and covered the earth as a cloud.$n$,true),
(4572,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',9,1,9,1,$n$*O Yahuah (God) of my fathers, and Yahuah (God) of mercy, who have made all things with your word,* (Wisdom of Solomon 9:1). *But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.* (Matthew 14:27). Solomon prays to the Elohim (God) of his fathers who made all things with his word. John says the same of the Word: all things were made by him, and without him was not any thing made that was made (John 1:3). In the fourth watch the disciples see a figure walking on the sea and cry out, and he answers with the name the Formed one spoke from the bush: it is I, be not afraid. The one walking on the water is the Word the sea was made with, come in flesh and standing on his own handiwork. The Father made all things with his word; here the word is walking toward the boat.$n$,$n$O Yahuah (God) of my fathers, and Yahuah (God) of mercy, who have made all things with your word,$n$,true),
(4573,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,12,2,12,$n$*Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:12). *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3). John upbraided Herod with his offending the law, *it is not lawful for thee to have her* (Matthew 14:4), and Herod laid hold on him and bound him. That is Wisdom of Solomon 2:12 acted out at a king's court: the righteous one is clean contrary to what the king is doing, his word reproves the king's bed, and so the king and the woman lie in wait for him. The same reflex shows up wherever a righteous man reproves a system that has left the Torah, in the Pharisees who take counsel to destroy Yahusha (Jesus) (Matthew 12:14) and in the chief priests who do the same (Matthew 26:3–4). Herod is one more instance of a very old disposition.$n$,$n$Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.$n$,true),
(4574,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,19,2,19,$n$*Let us examine him with despitefulness and torture, that we may know his meekness, and prove his patience.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:19). *And when he would have put him to death, he feared the multitude, because they counted him as a prophet.* (Matthew 14:5). Wisdom shows the heart of the ungodly toward the righteous: torment him and see how long his meekness holds. Herod carried that heart toward John; he would have put him to death already, and only his fear of the crowd held his hand. The restraint was politics, not mercy, and a birthday oath later removed it. Isaiah's sheep dumb before her shearers, John in Herod's prison, Yahusha (Jesus) before Pilate — the ungodly keep running the same test on the righteous, and the righteous keep answering it the same way.$n$,$n$Let us examine him with despitefulness and torture, that we may know his meekness, and prove his patience.$n$,true),
(4575,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,20,2,20,$n$*Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:20). *And he sent, and beheaded John in the prison.* (Matthew 14:10). A shameful death is not the honored end of a long life; it is a death arranged by men who want the righteous man gone. John's beheading in Herod's prison, on the strength of a rash birthday oath, is exactly the death Wisdom describes. The ungodly did not wait for the Father's verdict on his words; they gave their own. The prophets who upbraided the nation with the Torah died this way before him, and the King tells that whole history over in Matthew 23.$n$,$n$Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.$n$,true),
(4576,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,1,3,1,$n$*Hear me your father, O children, and do thereafter, that you may be safe.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:1). *For Elohim (God) commanded, saying, Honour thy father and mother: and, He that curseth father or mother, let him die the death.* (Matthew 15:4). Sirach begins his teaching on parents by speaking as a father: hear me, children, and do what I say, so that you may be safe. The whole chapter rests on the fifth commandment, the one Yahusha (Jesus) quotes back to the Pharisees in Matthew 15:4, honour thy father and mother, with the death penalty for cursing them attached. The tradition he is exposing in the verses that follow let a man vow to the altar what his aged parents needed and call himself free of them. Sirach had tied the children's safety to hearing and doing their father's word; the tradition untied it, and the Messiah names what Elohim (God) commanded to tie it back.$n$,$n$Hear me your father, O children, and do thereafter, that you may be safe.$n$,true),
(4577,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,8,3,8,$n$*Honour your father and mother both in word and deed, that a blessing may come upon you from them.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:8). *For Elohim (God) commanded, saying, Honour thy father and mother: and, He that curseth father or mother, let him die the death.* (Matthew 15:4). Honour your parents both in word and deed, Sirach says, and the two words are the whole argument. The corban tradition Yahusha (Jesus) exposes in Matthew 15 kept the word and dropped the deed: a man could speak a vow that sounded devout while the support his father and mother needed was cut off by it. The Messiah answers with what Elohim (God) commanded, honour thy father and mother, with no room left for honour that is spoken and not done. Sirach had closed that gap generations before by naming both halves, and a tradition that keeps one half has kept neither.$n$,$n$Honour your father and mother both in word and deed, that a blessing may come upon you from them.$n$,true),
(4578,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',7,27,7,27,$n$*Honour your father with your whole heart, and forget not the sorrows of your mother.* (Ecclesiasticus 7:27). *For Elohim (God) commanded, saying, Honour thy father and mother: and, He that curseth father or mother, let him die the death.* (Matthew 15:4). Sirach says honour your father with your whole heart, and he adds the mother's sorrows so the son remembers what he cost her. Matthew 15:4 is the commandment behind it, honour thy father and mother, quoted by Yahusha (Jesus) against a tradition that let a son divide his heart, a vow to the altar in one half and nothing left for his parents in the other. Whole heart is Sirach's word against that division. Later in the same chapter the Messiah says what defiles comes out of the heart (vv.18–20), and the corban device is the proof: an honour split by a vow was defiled before the vow was spoken.$n$,$n$Honour your father with your whole heart, and forget not the sorrows of your mother.$n$,true),
(4579,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,16,3,16,$n$*He that forsaketh his father is as a blasphemer; and he that angereth his mother is cursed: of Yahuah (God).* (Ecclesiasticus 3:16). *And honour not his father or his mother, he shall be free. Thus have ye made the commandment of Elohim (God) of none effect by your tradition.* (Matthew 15:6). Sirach does not treat neglect of parents as a small fault. To forsake a father is as blasphemy; to anger a mother is to be cursed of Yahuah (God). That is the weight behind Matthew 15:6, where a tradition lets a son honour not his father or his mother and call himself free, and the Messiah says the commandment of Elohim (God) has been made of none effect. What the tradition filed under devotion, Sirach had filed under blasphemy and curse. Yahusha (Jesus) is not inventing a harsher reading; he is reading the commandment the way the fathers already read it.$n$,$n$He that forsaketh his father is as a blasphemer; and he that angereth his mother is cursed: of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4580,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,3,4,3,$n$*And when he had called him, he said, My son, when I am dead, bury me; and despise not your mother, but honour her all the days of your life, and do that which shall please her, and grieve her not.* (Tobit 4:3). *For Elohim (God) commanded, saying, Honour thy father and mother: and, He that curseth father or mother, let him die the death.* (Matthew 15:4). A dying father in exile tells his son: honour your mother all the days of her life, please her, grieve her not. That is the fifth commandment lived out to the end of a parent's days, in a household far from the land. Yahusha (Jesus) quotes the same commandment against a tradition that let a man say *It is a gift* and so be freed from supporting his parents (15:5–6). Tobit shows what the commandment looks like when it is kept; the tradition shows what it looks like when a religious system finds a way around it, and it is the system that is being answered, not the people who were taught it.$n$,$n$And when he had called him, he said, My son, when I am dead, bury me; and despise not your mother, but honour her all the days of your life, and do that which shall please her, and grieve her not.$n$,true),
(4581,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',15,11,15,11,$n$*Say not you, It is through Yahuah (God) that I fell away: for you oughtest not to do the things that he hates.* (Ecclesiasticus 15:11). *Not that which goeth into the mouth defileth a man; but that which cometh out of the mouth, this defileth a man.* (Matthew 15:11). Sirach forbids a man to say he fell away through Yahuah (God); the things he hates are not his doing. Yahusha (Jesus) says defilement does not come in through the mouth but out of it — the source is the man's own heart. The two verses fence off the same lie from opposite sides: the Father does not cause the falling, and bread eaten with unwashed hands does not cause the defilement. What a man does and says comes from inside him, and that is where the Torah has always located it.$n$,$n$Say not you, It is through Yahuah (God) that I fell away: for you oughtest not to do the things that he hates.$n$,true),
(4582,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',15,20,15,20,$n$*He has commanded no man to do wickedly, neither has he given any man licence to sin.* (Ecclesiasticus 15:20). *But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.* (Matthew 15:18). Sirach says plainly that Yahuah (God) has commanded no man to do wickedly and given no man licence to sin. Yahusha (Jesus) says what defiles a man comes out of his own heart. Put the two together and the source of sin is found — not the Father, not the bread, not the unwashed hands the Pharisees fussed over, but the heart. Any teaching that gives licence to sin is giving what Yahuah (God) never gave.$n$,$n$He has commanded no man to do wickedly, neither has he given any man licence to sin.$n$,true),
(4583,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,6,27,6,$n$*The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:6). *But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.* (Matthew 15:18). The fruit shows the tree, Sirach says, and a man's speech shows what his heart has conceived. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same when the Pharisees fuss over unwashed hands: what defiles a man comes out of his mouth, and it comes from the heart. The heart is the source. Bread eaten with unwashed hands does not defile the man; what defiles him is what his own heart conceives and his mouth lets out, and the good tree bringing forth good fruit in Matthew 7:17 is the same picture. Watch what comes out, and you have seen what is inside.$n$,$n$The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.$n$,true),
(4584,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',1,11,1,11,$n$*Therefore beware of murmuring, which is unprofitable; and refrain your tongue from backbiting: for there is no word so secret, that shall go for nought: and the mouth that belieth slayeth the soul.* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:11). *For out of the heart proceed evil thoughts, murders, adulteries, fornications, thefts, false witness, blasphemies:* (Matthew 15:19). Wisdom of Solomon warns about the mouth: murmuring, backbiting, the lying mouth that kills the soul. Yahusha (Jesus) traces the mouth back to its source, *those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart* (Matthew 15:18), and then lists what comes out of it, false witness and blasphemies among them. The two lists overlap because they are describing one thing from two ends: the heart is where the evil starts, and the tongue is where it shows. That is why the promise of the new covenant is a heart with the Torah written on it (Jeremiah 31:33), because a clean mouth needs a clean spring.$n$,$n$Therefore beware of murmuring, which is unprofitable; and refrain your tongue from backbiting: for there is no word so secret, that shall go for nought: and the mouth that belieth slayeth the soul.$n$,true),
(4585,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,1,36,1,$n$*Have mercy upon us, O Yahuah (God) of all, and behold us:* (Ecclesiasticus 36:1). *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24). Sirach opens his great prayer with a cry for mercy to the Elohim (God) of all, and the prayer goes on to ask that all the tribes of Jacob be gathered and inherited as from the beginning. Yahusha (Jesus) says he was not sent but to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel). The one being asked for mercy in Sirach's prayer is the one who answered it in person, sent to the very scattered house the prayer was about. Behold us, Sirach prays; the Shepherd came looking for the sheep.$n$,$n$Have mercy upon us, O Yahuah (God) of all, and behold us:$n$,true),
(4586,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24). Sirach prays for the gathering of all the tribes of Jacob, back into the inheritance as from the beginning. Yahusha (Jesus) says he was sent to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel). The prayer and the mission have the same object: not Yahudah (Judah) alone, who had a temple and a land, but the whole house that had been scattered and lost among the nations. The prophets promised it, Sirach prayed it, and the King came for it.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(4587,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,40,13,40,$n$*Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.* (2 Esdras 13:40). *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24). When the woman of Canaan pressed him, Yahusha (Jesus) named the scope of his sending: the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) — and she, who heard the Shepherd and would not be turned away, was one of them. Ezra had already been shown who that house is, the ten tribes Shalmaneser led away captive over the waters into another land, who never returned. The lost sheep were not a figure of speech; they were a people with a date, a captor, and a direction of travel, the same carrying away the book of Kings records (2 Kings 17:6). The King was sent to them, and the prophets who promised their gathering (Jeremiah 31; Ezekiel 37) were speaking of the same people.$n$,$n$Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.$n$,true),
(4588,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,48,13,48,$n$*But those that be left behind of your people are they that are found within my borders.* (2 Esdras 13:48). *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24). The vision does not forget the ones who never left. After the ten tribes come home, Ezra is told that those left behind of his people are the ones found within the borders of the land, the remnant of Yahudah (Judah) that stayed. When Yahusha (Jesus) says he was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), his sending reaches both of these: the multitudes of Galilee and Yahudah (Judah) he walked among inside the borders, and the scattered house beyond the waters his apostles would be sent after. The house of Yashar'el (Israel) is both of them, the ones who stayed and the ones who were carried, and the King's mission is to the whole house.$n$,$n$But those that be left behind of your people are they that are found within my borders.$n$,true),
(4589,'T','apocrypha','tobit',13,5,13,5,$n$*And he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us.* (Tobit 13:5). *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24). Tobit names who gets gathered and from where: the people Yahuah (God) scourged and scattered, brought back out of all the nations. Ezekiel had said the same, *I will take the children of Yashar'el (Israel) from among the heathen, whither they be gone, and will gather them on every side* (Ezekiel 37:21). When Yahusha (Jesus) says he is not sent but to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), he is stating the scope of that gathering, not narrowing his mercy. He said it to the woman of Canaan, and she was not turned away; the sheep hear the Shepherd's voice wherever the scattering has carried them, and the hearing shows who they are. The mission to the lost sheep is the mercy Tobit said would follow the scourge.$n$,$n$And he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us.$n$,true),
(4601,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,18,2,18,$n$*For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:18). *And Simon Peter answered and said, Thou art the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).* (Matthew 16:16). The same words come from two mouths. The ungodly in Wisdom say it in mockery — if the just man is the son of Yahuah (God), let him be delivered. Peter says it in confession — thou art the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God). What the wicked used as a taunt against the righteous man is the title the Father reveals to Peter at the turning point of the gospel. The just man of Wisdom is the Son Peter confessed, and the plot to test him never changed who he was.$n$,$n$For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.$n$,true),
(4606,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,12,2,12,$n$*Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:12). *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21). The ungodly in Wisdom of Solomon 2 begin by lying in wait and end by resolving, *let us condemn him with a shameful death* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:20). Yahusha (Jesus) tells his disciples the same ending in advance, suffering at the hands of the elders, chief priests and scribes, and death in Jerusalem. The religious leadership of the day fills the role Wisdom wrote for the ungodly, a system that could not bear a righteous man whose life reproved it. But he adds a line Wisdom's plotters never saw coming, raised again the third day, and Wisdom itself knew it: the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Elohim (God) (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1).$n$,$n$Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.$n$,true),
(4607,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,16,2,16,$n$*We are esteemed of him as counterfeits: he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness: he pronounceth the end of the just to be blessed, and makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:16). *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21). The men who esteemed themselves righteous felt judged by a man who would not walk their way and who called Yahuah (God) his father. Wisdom puts the grievance in their own mouths; Matthew names who held it in Yahusha's (Jesus') day — the elders, the chief priests and the scribes, the men who held the seats of religion in Jerusalem. He told his disciples plainly that he must suffer at their hands and be killed, because a life that counts their ways as filthiness is an indictment they could not leave standing. It was the seat of the system that rose against him, not the people sitting under it.$n$,$n$We are esteemed of him as counterfeits: he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness: he pronounceth the end of the just to be blessed, and makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.$n$,true),
(4608,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,20,2,20,$n$*Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:20). *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21). Wisdom says the ungodly will condemn the righteous man to a shameful death. Yahusha (Jesus) tells his disciples he must suffer and be killed, and later he names how: delivered to be mocked, scourged and crucified (Matthew 20:19). The cross was the shameful death — the Torah itself says *he that is hanged is accursed of Elohim (God)* (Deuteronomy 21:23), so the men who hung him there meant it as the Father's own rejection of him. Isaiah's servant, Wisdom's just man and the passion the King announced all meet at that tree. And he named the third day in the same breath, which is the answer to the whole plot.$n$,$n$Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.$n$,true),
(4609,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,1,3,1,$n$*But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1). *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21). Wisdom 2 ends with the just man condemned to a shameful death; Wisdom 3 opens with his soul in the hand of Yahuah (God), untouched by torment. Yahusha (Jesus) tells his disciples both halves in one sentence — he must be killed, and be raised again the third day. The being killed is the shameful death; the being raised is the hand of the Father. Wisdom had already laid the two chapters side by side, and the gospel shows the one man who walked through both.$n$,$n$But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.$n$,true),
(4610,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,1,5,1,$n$*Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:1). *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21). Here the King lays out both halves at once: he will suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, be killed, and be raised the third day. Solomon saw the second half from far off, the righteous man standing in boldness before the very men who afflicted him and made no account of his labours. The third day is where that standing begins, and the King finishes the thought a few verses later, when the Son of Adam comes in the glory of his Father to reward every man according to his works (Matthew 16:27). The vindication does not make him righteous; it shows in public what was true all along.$n$,$n$Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.$n$,true),
(4611,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',1,13,1,13,$n$*For Yahuah (God) made not death: neither has he pleasure in the destruction of the living.* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:13). *And I say also unto thee, That thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18). Wisdom of Solomon says it flatly: Yahuah (God) did not make death. It came in through the envy of the devil (Wisdom of Solomon 2:24) and through the sin in the garden; it is an intruder in his creation, not his design. That is why Yahusha (Jesus) can say the gates of hell, the gates of the grave, will not prevail against the assembly he builds. A thing the Father did not make has no permanent claim on what he did make. The grave's gates held every son of Adam until the one who said this walked out through them on the third day, and because he did, the gates will not hold his people either.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) made not death: neither has he pleasure in the destruction of the living.$n$,true),
(4612,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,13,16,13,$n$*For you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:13). *And I say also unto thee, That thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18). The gates of hell have no final say, because the one who leads down to those gates is the same one who brings up again. That is what stands behind the promise to Peter: the assembly is built on the King who would be raised the third day (he says so plainly at verse 21), and the gates that closed on him could not hold him, so they will not prevail against what he builds. The same chapter opens with the sign of Jonah (verse 4); the whole chapter is carrying this one truth, that the grave is a door the Father opens.$n$,$n$For you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.$n$,true),
(4614,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,35,4,35,$n$*Did not the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers, saying, How long shall I hope on this fashion? when comes the fruit of the floor of our reward?* (2 Esdras 4:35). *And I say also unto thee, That thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18). The righteous dead are in chambers, Esdras is told, asking when the floor of their reward will come. They are not in torment and they are not in heaven; they are resting and waiting, and later in the book the chambers deliver up the souls committed to them at the resurrection (2 Esdras 7:32). That is what the King means when he says the gates of hell shall not prevail against his assembly. Sheol is a holding place with gates, and the gates do not hold his people past the day he calls them out. The chambers keep the righteous; they do not keep them forever.$n$,$n$Did not the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers, saying, How long shall I hope on this fashion? when comes the fruit of the floor of our reward?$n$,true),
(4615,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',16,12,16,12,$n$*As his mercy is great, so is his correction also: he judges a man according to his works* (Ecclesiasticus 16:12). *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27). Sirach holds mercy and correction in the same hand and says the measure of both is a man's works. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same about his own coming in his Father's glory: every man rewarded according to his works. Grace is the door home, not the cancelling of the reckoning. The judgment weighs the fruit, and the fruit comes from a heart that either received the new heart Ezekiel promised or did not.$n$,$n$As his mercy is great, so is his correction also: he judges a man according to his works$n$,true),
(4616,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',16,14,16,14,$n$*Make way for every work of mercy: for every man shall find according to his works.* (Ecclesiasticus 16:14). *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27). Sirach ties the two halves together: make way for every work of mercy, because every man shall find according to his works. Yahusha (Jesus) says the finding happens when the Son of Adam comes in his Father's glory. Mercy done now is what is found then. The door home is opened by grace; what is weighed at the judgment is what the one who came home did after he walked in.$n$,$n$Make way for every work of mercy: for every man shall find according to his works.$n$,true),
(4617,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,19,35,19,$n$*Till he have rendered to every man according to his deeds, and to the works of men according to their devices; till he have judged the cause of his people, and made them to rejoice in his mercy.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:19). *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27). Sirach says Yahuah (God) will not rest until he has rendered to every man according to his deeds and judged the cause of his people and made them rejoice in his mercy. Yahusha (Jesus) says the Son of Adam will come in the glory of his Father and reward every man according to his works. The two halves Sirach holds together are the two halves the King names: a reckoning by works for every man, and vindication and joy for his own people. Reward comes at his coming and it comes by deeds, and those who are his rejoice in mercy on that day rather than in a reckoning they skipped.$n$,$n$Till he have rendered to every man according to his deeds, and to the works of men according to their devices; till he have judged the cause of his people, and made them to rejoice in his mercy.$n$,true),
(4618,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,32,7,32,$n$*And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.* (2 Esdras 7:32). *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27). Ezra is shown the order: first the earth gives back those asleep in her and the secret places deliver the souls committed to them, then the Most High appears on the seat of judgment, and the work follows and the reward is shown (2 Esdras 7:33–35). Yahusha (Jesus) gives the same order in one sentence: the Son of Adam comes in his Father's glory, and then he rewards every man according to his works. Resurrection first, then the reward by works. The righteous who sleep are not being rewarded somewhere now; they rest in the places committed to them until the earth restores them, and the reckoning comes after.$n$,$n$And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.$n$,true),
(4619,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,35,7,35,$n$*And the work shall follow, and the reward shall be shewed, and the good deeds shall be of force, and wicked deeds shall bear no rule.* (2 Esdras 7:35). *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27). Ezra is told what follows the judgment seat: the work follows the man, the reward is shown, and good deeds are of force while wicked deeds bear no rule. Yahusha (Jesus) says it as plainly: when the Son of Adam comes in his Father's glory, he rewards every man according to his works. This is not a man earning his place; it is a man's deeds going with him into the day and being weighed there. The works that follow are the fruit of a heart that kept what it was given, and those with no such fruit are told to depart (Matthew 7:23).$n$,$n$And the work shall follow, and the reward shall be shewed, and the good deeds shall be of force, and wicked deeds shall bear no rule.$n$,true),
(4687,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',19,13,19,13,$n$*Admonish a friend, it may be he has not done it: and if he have done it, that he do it no more.* (Ecclesiasticus 19:13). *Moreover if thy brother shall trespass against thee, go and tell him his fault between thee and him alone: if he shall hear thee, thou hast gained thy brother.* (Matthew 18:15). Sirach's counsel is to go to the friend first, because he may not have done it at all, and if he has, so that he will do it no more. Yahusha (Jesus) makes that the first step when a brother trespasses: go to him alone, tell him his fault, and if he hears you the brother is gained. Both aim at restoration, not at shame or gossip — the private word comes before anything else, and the Torah had already told a man to rebuke his neighbour rather than carry a grudge against him in his heart.$n$,$n$Admonish a friend, it may be he has not done it: and if he have done it, that he do it no more.$n$,true),
(4688,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',19,17,19,17,$n$*Admonish your neighbour before you threaten him; and not being angry, give place to the law of the Most High.* (Ecclesiasticus 19:17). *Moreover if thy brother shall trespass against thee, go and tell him his fault between thee and him alone: if he shall hear thee, thou hast gained thy brother.* (Matthew 18:15). Sirach sets the order and the temper together: admonish before you threaten, and do it without anger, letting the law of the Most High govern the matter rather than your own heat. That is the spirit of the first step Yahusha (Jesus) gives — go to the brother alone and tell him his fault, which is admonition, not threat. The goal is to gain the brother, and a man cannot gain his brother while he is angry at him. The rebuke works when the Torah, not the rebuker's temper, is what it stands on.$n$,$n$Admonish your neighbour before you threaten him; and not being angry, give place to the law of the Most High.$n$,true),
(4689,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',19,15,19,15,$n$*Admonish a friend: for many times it is a slander, and believe not every tale.* (Ecclesiasticus 19:15). *But if he will not hear thee, then take with thee one or two more, that in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established.* (Matthew 18:16). Sirach warns that many a report is slander and that not every tale should be believed — so go and admonish the friend rather than take the story as settled. Yahusha (Jesus) builds the same safeguard into the second step: if the brother will not hear, bring one or two more, so that every word is established in the mouth of two or three witnesses. The witnesses protect the accused from a false charge and the accuser from his own inflated one. What Sirach counseled as wisdom, the King set as procedure, and both rest on the Torah's rule that one witness is never enough.$n$,$n$Admonish a friend: for many times it is a slander, and believe not every tale.$n$,true),
(4690,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',20,2,20,2,$n$*It is much better to reprove, than to be angry secretly: and he that confesseth his fault shall be preserved from hurt.* (Ecclesiasticus 20:2). *And if he shall neglect to hear them, tell it unto the church: but if he neglect to hear the church, let him be unto thee as an heathen man and a publican.* (Matthew 18:17). Sirach says open reproof is far better than secret anger, and the man who confesses his fault when reproved is preserved from hurt. That is what the third step in Matthew is for: when the private word and the witnesses have both been refused, the matter is told to the assembly in the open, where it can still be confessed and the brother still preserved. Secret anger restores no one. Open reproof, met with confession, does — and only when even the assembly is refused does the brother put himself outside.$n$,$n$It is much better to reprove, than to be angry secretly: and he that confesseth his fault shall be preserved from hurt.$n$,true),
(4691,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,30,27,30,$n$*Malice and wrath, even these are abominations; and the sinful man shall have them both.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:30). *Then came Peter to him, and said, Adonai (Lord), how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?* (Matthew 18:21). Sirach calls malice and wrath abominations, the portion of the sinful man. Peter comes to Yahusha (Jesus) still counting, asking how many times he has to forgive his brother, seven? A man who is counting offences is still holding them, and what he is holding is the malice Sirach names. The King's answer pushes past the count altogether, because the heart he is forming in his disciples is the new heart of Ezekiel 36:26, the one the Spirit moves to walk in the statutes, and that heart does not keep a tally against a brother.$n$,$n$Malice and wrath, even these are abominations; and the sinful man shall have them both.$n$,true),
(4692,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,2,28,2,$n$*Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you, so shall your sins also be forgiven when you prayest.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:2). *Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto him, I say not unto thee, Until seven times: but, Until seventy times seven.* (Matthew 18:22). Sirach says forgive your neighbour, and your own sins will be forgiven when you pray. Peter asks how many times, and Yahusha (Jesus) answers seventy times seven, then tells the parable of the servant who was forgiven a fortune and would not forgive a few pence, and closes it with the warning that the heavenly Father will do the same to anyone who does not forgive his brother from the heart. The Lord's Prayer had already made the same condition, forgive and be forgiven, forgive not and be not forgiven (Matthew 6:14–15). Sirach said it in one line; the King said it in a prayer, a number, and a story, and it is one teaching.$n$,$n$Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you, so shall your sins also be forgiven when you prayest.$n$,true),
(4693,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,3,28,3,$n$*One man bears hatred against another, and does he seek pardon from Yahuah (God)?* (Ecclesiasticus 28:3). *Shouldest not thou also have had compassion on thy fellowservant, even as I had pity on thee?* (Matthew 18:33). Sirach asks the plain question: a man holds hatred against another man, and he goes looking for pardon from Yahuah (God)? The parable in Matthew 18 puts that man in a story. He has just been forgiven everything he owed, and he takes his fellow servant by the throat over a trifle, and his lord asks him the very question Sirach asked, should you not have had compassion as I had pity on you? The pardon he had received was taken back. Mercy received has to become mercy given, or it does not stay.$n$,$n$One man bears hatred against another, and does he seek pardon from Yahuah (God)?$n$,true),
(4694,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,7,28,7,$n$*Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour: remember the covenant of the Highest, and wink at ignorance.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:7). *So likewise shall my heavenly Father do also unto you, if ye from your hearts forgive not every one his brother their trespasses.* (Matthew 18:35). Sirach says two things in one line and means them as one: remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour; remember the covenant of the Highest, and overlook ignorance. Yahusha (Jesus) ends the parable of the unforgiving servant on the same note, that the heavenly Father will deal the same way with anyone who does not forgive his brother from the heart. Remembering the Torah and forgiving the brother are not two different religions; the law written on the heart (Jeremiah 31:33) is what produces a heart without malice. And the parable is plain that the forgiveness a man received can be taken back if he will not extend it; the door home stays a door, and the man who bears malice against his brother has walked away from it.$n$,$n$Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour: remember the covenant of the Highest, and wink at ignorance.$n$,true),
(4696,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,18,3,18,$n$*The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).* (Ecclesiasticus 3:18). *And said, Verily I say unto you, Except ye be converted, and become as little children, ye shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 18:3). The greater you are, the more humble yourself, Sirach says, and Yahusha (Jesus) makes it the condition of entry: except you be converted and become as little children, you do not enter the kingdom at all. The disciples had just asked who is greatest, and the answer turned their measure upside down, because in the kingdom greatness is measured by how low a man will go, not how high he is set. Sirach was teaching it at a father's table long before the disciples argued about it on the road. The one who humbles himself as a little child is the greatest in that kingdom, which is exactly the favour Sirach said the humble find.$n$,$n$The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4697,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',10,14,10,14,$n$*Yahuah (God) has cast down the thrones of proud princes, and set up the meek in their stead.* (Ecclesiasticus 10:14). *Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 18:4). Sirach says Yahuah (God) has already done it: the proud cast off their thrones, the meek set in their place. Yahusha (Jesus) says the one who humbles himself as a little child is the greatest in the kingdom. It is the same hand moving in the same direction. The assembly is told to walk now by the measure the Father will judge by on that day.$n$,$n$Yahuah (God) has cast down the thrones of proud princes, and set up the meek in their stead.$n$,true),
(4698,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',10,7,10,7,$n$*Pride is hateful before Yahuah (God) and man: and by both does one commit iniquity.* (Ecclesiasticus 10:7). *At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* (Matthew 18:1). Sirach names pride as hateful before Yahuah (God) and man both, the root from which iniquity grows on either side. The disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus) asking who would be greatest in the kingdom; Mark records that they had been disputing it on the road. The question itself was the disposition Sirach warned against, and the King answered it by setting a child in their midst. The kingdom does not rank the way the kingdoms of the earth do.$n$,$n$Pride is hateful before Yahuah (God) and man: and by both does one commit iniquity.$n$,true),
(4701,'T','apocrypha','tobit',8,6,8,6,$n$*You madest Adam, and gavest him Eve his wife for an helper and stay: of them came mankind: you have said, It is not good that man should be alone; let us make to him an aid like to himself.* (Tobit 8:6). *Wherefore they are no more twain, but one flesh. What therefore Elohim (God) hath joined together, let not man put asunder.* (Matthew 19:6). On his wedding night Tobias prays the creation back over his marriage: you made Adam and gave him Eve for a helper, and from them came mankind. When the Pharisees pressed Yahusha (Jesus) about divorce he went to the same place, to the beginning, and said that what Elohim (God) has joined no man may put asunder. The library and the gospel stand on the same ground: marriage is the Father's joining, not a human arrangement to be undone at preference. Malachi says he hates putting away (Malachi 2:16), and Tobias's prayer shows a young man who already knew it.$n$,$n$You madest Adam, and gavest him Eve his wife for an helper and stay: of them came mankind: you have said, It is not good that man should be alone; let us make to him an aid like to himself.$n$,true),
(4702,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,14,3,14,$n$*And blessed is the eunuch, which with his hands has wrought no iniquity, nor imagined wicked things against Yahuah (God): for to him shall be given the special gift of faith, and an inheritance in the temple of Yahuah (God) more acceptable to his mind.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:14). *For there are some eunuchs, which were so born from their mother’s womb: and there are some eunuchs, which were made eunuchs of men: and there be eunuchs, which have made themselves eunuchs for the kingdom of heaven’s sake. He that is able to receive it, let him receive it.* (Matthew 19:12). Wisdom blesses the eunuch who has kept his hands clean and promises him the special gift of faith and an inheritance in the house of Yahuah (God). Isaiah had said it first — *even unto them will I give in mine house and within my walls a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters* (Isaiah 56:5). Yahusha (Jesus) speaks of eunuchs who have made themselves so for the kingdom of heaven's sake, and adds: he that is able to receive it, let him receive it. All three treat it as a gift received, not a rule imposed, which is the word both the system that made celibacy a requirement for its clergy and the system that treats the unmarried as half a life managed to miss. The inheritance is for the one to whom it is given.$n$,$n$And blessed is the eunuch, which with his hands has wrought no iniquity, nor imagined wicked things against Yahuah (God): for to him shall be given the special gift of faith, and an inheritance in the temple of Yahuah (God) more acceptable to his mind.$n$,true),
(4703,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',7,23,7,23,$n$*Have you children? instruct them, and bow down their neck from their youth.* (Ecclesiasticus 7:23). *Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.* (Matthew 19:13). Sirach puts children on the list of a man's responsibilities: if you have them, instruct them and bend their necks from their youth. The parents in Matthew 19:13 are doing that when they bring their little ones to Yahusha (Jesus) for his hands and his prayer; it is the Torah's own charge to teach the children carried out in the plainest way, bringing them to the one who teaches. The disciples rebuked the parents, and the Messiah rebuked the disciples, because the bringing was right. Instruction begins with whom you bring your children to.$n$,$n$Have you children? instruct them, and bow down their neck from their youth.$n$,true),
(4704,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,1,3,1,$n$*Hear me your father, O children, and do thereafter, that you may be safe.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:1). *But Yahusha (Jesus) said, Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 19:14). Sirach's picture of safety is children who hear their father and do what he says. Yahusha (Jesus) says the kingdom of heaven is of such, and he says it while his own disciples are forbidding the little ones to come. The kingdom belongs to children not because children are innocent but because a child hears his father's voice and comes when he is called; that is the disposition the kingdom is made of. Hearing is how the Father's children are shown to be his, and the one who hears and does thereafter is the one Sirach says will be safe.$n$,$n$Hear me your father, O children, and do thereafter, that you may be safe.$n$,true),
(4705,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,3,4,3,$n$*And when he had called him, he said, My son, when I am dead, bury me; and despise not your mother, but honour her all the days of your life, and do that which shall please her, and grieve her not.* (Tobit 4:3). *And he laid his hands on them, and departed thence.* (Matthew 19:15). Tobit calls his son to him before he dies and hands him the commandments — honour your mother, bury me, walk uprightly — which is a father's blessing in the old form. Parents brought their children to Yahusha (Jesus) and he laid his hands on them. The library shows a father passing covenant life to the next generation; the gospel shows the King receiving the children their parents bring to him. The two belong together at the household, where the covenant has always been handed down.$n$,$n$And when he had called him, he said, My son, when I am dead, bury me; and despise not your mother, but honour her all the days of your life, and do that which shall please her, and grieve her not.$n$,true),
(4706,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,11,17,11,$n$*Beside this he gave them knowledge, and the law of life for an heritage.* (Ecclesiasticus 17:11). *And he said unto him, Why callest thou me good? there is none good but one, that is, Elohim (God): but if thou wilt enter into life, keep the commandments.* (Matthew 19:17). Sirach calls the law a law of life, given for an heritage along with knowledge. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the young man that if he would enter into life he should keep the commandments. The law of life and entering into life are the same road. Moses set life and good before Yashar'el (Israel) in the commandments, Sirach calls that inheritance by its name, and the King does not amend it when the question is put to him directly.$n$,$n$Beside this he gave them knowledge, and the law of life for an heritage.$n$,true),
(4707,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',29,1,29,1,$n$*He that is merciful will lend to his neighbour; and he that strengtheneth his hand keepeth the commandments.* (Ecclesiasticus 29:1). *And he said unto him, Why callest thou me good? there is none good but one, that is, Elohim (God): but if thou wilt enter into life, keep the commandments.* (Matthew 19:17). Sirach says the merciful man lends to his neighbour, and the one who strengthens his hand keeps the commandments; mercy and commandment are one act. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the young man who asks about life to keep the commandments, and when the young man says he has kept them all, the King tests him with the poor: sell and give. The test did not change the subject. Mercy to the neighbour is what keeping the commandments looks like with a full purse in hand, and the young man's grief showed where his keeping had stopped. Sirach would have told him the same thing.$n$,$n$He that is merciful will lend to his neighbour; and he that strengtheneth his hand keepeth the commandments.$n$,true),
(4708,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',29,11,29,11,$n$*Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High, and it shall bring you more profit than gold.* (Ecclesiasticus 29:11). *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If thou wilt be perfect, go and sell that thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.* (Matthew 19:21). Sirach says lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High, and it will outprofit gold. Yahusha (Jesus) says it to the young man who has kept the commandments from his youth: sell, give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. The King's word was a test for one heart, not a command that every disciple become penniless; it found the place where this man's love of neighbour stopped, which was his own possessions. Giving to the poor was never new; the Torah had always commanded it (Deuteronomy 15:7–8), and Sirach is only saying that treasure laid up that way is the kind that lasts.$n$,$n$Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High, and it shall bring you more profit than gold.$n$,true),
(4709,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,9,4,9,$n$*For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* (Tobit 4:9). *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If thou wilt be perfect, go and sell that thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.* (Matthew 19:21). The young ruler asked what he still lacked, and the King told him: sell, give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven. Tobit had told his son the same thing long before — alms are a good treasure laid up against the day of necessity. The Torah already commanded the open hand to the poor brother, and the library carried it forward as treasure laid up. Yahusha (Jesus) was not raising the bar on the young man; he was reading him the library, and the young man went away sorrowful because he could not walk it.$n$,$n$For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.$n$,true),
(4718,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That ye which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and inherited as from the beginning. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the twelve that in the regeneration, when the Son of Adam sits on the throne of his glory, they will sit on twelve thrones judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel). Twelve thrones for twelve tribes means the twelve tribes are there to be judged: gathered, whole, as from the beginning. What Sirach asked for in prayer the King states as the shape of the kingdom when it comes.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(4719,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,8,3,8,$n$*They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:8). *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That ye which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28). Wisdom says the righteous shall judge the nations; Yahusha (Jesus) tells the twelve they shall sit on twelve thrones judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel). These are two views of one kingdom. The gathered twelve tribes sit under the apostles' thrones, and that gathered people is the kingdom of priests the Father promised at Sinai — *ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests* (Exodus 19:6) — who judge and teach the nations left alive in the reign. The priesthood toward the nations begins when the kingdom is set up, not before. Wisdom saw the outer ring; the King named the inner one.$n$,$n$They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.$n$,true),
(4721,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,8,24,8,$n$*So the Creator of all things gave me a commandment, and he that made me caused my tabernacle to rest, and said, Let your dwelling be in Jacob, and your inheritance in Yashar'el (Israel).* (Ecclesiasticus 24:8). *For the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.* (Matthew 20:1). Wisdom was commanded by the Creator to pitch her tent in Jacob and take her inheritance in Yashar'el (Israel). Isaiah had already said that the vineyard of Yahuah (God) is the house of Yashar'el (Israel), and Jeremiah mourned the pastors who destroyed it. When Yahusha (Jesus) opens the parable with a householder hiring labourers into his vineyard, that is the vineyard he means — the covenant people the Father planted and in whom Wisdom made her dwelling. The householder is the Father, and the labourers are the ones he calls into it through the hours of the day.$n$,$n$So the Creator of all things gave me a commandment, and he that made me caused my tabernacle to rest, and said, Let your dwelling be in Jacob, and your inheritance in Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,true),
(4722,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,12,24,12,$n$*And I took root in an honourable people, even in the portion of the inheritance of Yahuah (God).* (Ecclesiasticus 24:12). *For the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.* (Matthew 20:1). Wisdom says she took root in an honourable people, in the portion that is Yahuah's (God's) own inheritance. That people is the vineyard of the parable — the householder's own ground, into which he goes out to hire labourers through the day. The vineyard is Yashar'el (Israel), and so are the labourers called into it, whether called early or late: the covenant line from Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and the children of that line who grew up scattered among the nations and are being called home. The honourable people Wisdom rooted in is the inheritance the Father is still gathering.$n$,$n$And I took root in an honourable people, even in the portion of the inheritance of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4723,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',33,13,33,13,$n$*As the clay is in the potter’s hand, to fashion it at his pleasure: so man is in the hand of him that made him, to render to them as liketh him best.* (Ecclesiasticus 33:13). *Is it not lawful for me to do what I will with mine own? Is thine eye evil, because I am good?* (Matthew 20:15). Sirach says man is in the hand of his Maker as clay is in the hand of the potter, to render to each as seems best to him, and the verse before it says he has blessed and exalted some and brought others low. The householder in the parable asks the grumbling labourers the same thing: is it not lawful for me to do what I will with my own? The one who made the clay owes it no explanation for how he shapes it. An eye that goes evil because the Maker has been good to another has forgotten whose hand it is in.$n$,$n$As the clay is in the potter’s hand, to fashion it at his pleasure: so man is in the hand of him that made him, to render to them as liketh him best.$n$,true),
(4724,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,23,5,23,$n$*And said, O Yahuah (God) that bearest rule, of every wood of the earth, and of all the trees thereof, you have chosen you one only vine:* (2 Esdras 5:23). *For the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.* (Matthew 20:1). Ezra, praying, names what the Most High did with the whole earth: out of every wood and every tree he chose one vine. When Yahusha (Jesus) opens the parable of the labourers, the householder's vineyard is not a new field; it is that one vine, the vineyard Isaiah had already named as the house of Yashar'el (Israel) (Isaiah 5:7). Labourers are hired into it at different hours, but the vineyard itself was chosen long before the morning began. Whoever is brought in at the eleventh hour is brought into that same vineyard, not into a different one planted beside it.$n$,$n$And said, O Yahuah (God) that bearest rule, of every wood of the earth, and of all the trees thereof, you have chosen you one only vine:$n$,true),
(4725,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',9,22,9,22,$n$*Let the multitude perish then, which was born in vain; and let my grape be kept, and my plant; for with great labour have I made it perfect.* (2 Esdras 9:22). *So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen.* (Matthew 20:16). The Most High tells Ezra he will let the multitude born in vain perish and keep his grape and his plant, made perfect with great labour. The parable ends the same way: many called, few chosen. The grape kept out of the cluster is the few; the great labour is the Father's long keeping of his seed through all the scattering until the gathering. He does not lose what he laboured over, and he does not keep what he never planted.$n$,$n$Let the multitude perish then, which was born in vain; and let my grape be kept, and my plant; for with great labour have I made it perfect.$n$,true),
(4728,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,1,8,1,$n$*And he answered me, saying, The most High has made this world for many, but the world to come for few.* (2 Esdras 8:1). *So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen.* (Matthew 20:16). Ezra asks why so many are made for so little, and the answer is plain: this world was made for many, the world to come for few. Yahusha (Jesus) closes the parable of the labourers with the same count: many are called, few chosen. The call goes out broadly because the sheep are scattered among many and no one can tell them by sight; the few are the ones who hear and come in and bear fruit. The many and the few are not two peoples set against each other; they are the whole field and the harvest that comes out of it.$n$,$n$And he answered me, saying, The most High has made this world for many, but the world to come for few.$n$,true),
(4729,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,3,8,3,$n$*There be many created, but few shall be saved.* (2 Esdras 8:3). *So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen.* (Matthew 20:16). The angel gives Ezra the plainest form of it: many created, few saved. Yahusha (Jesus) says many called, few chosen. The few are not those who saved themselves by effort, nor a number chosen apart from how they walked; they are the ones whose names were written from the foundation of the world (Revelation 13:8), who hear because they are his sheep, and whose walk bears the fruit that keeps a name from being blotted out (Revelation 3:5). Being created does not make a man one of the few, and neither does being called. Hearing and keeping do, and those come from what he already was.$n$,$n$There be many created, but few shall be saved.$n$,true),
(4730,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,1,3,1,$n$*But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1). *But Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, Ye know not what ye ask. Are ye able to drink of the cup that I shall drink of, and to be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with? They say unto him, We are able.* (Matthew 20:22). James and John asked for thrones and were handed a cup. Yahusha (Jesus) asked whether they could drink what he was about to drink, and they said they could, not knowing what they were agreeing to. James was put to the sword by Herod (Acts 12:2); John outlived them all and was on Patmos for the testimony. The cup was real suffering, and Wisdom tells where it ends: the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there no torment touches them. James drank the cup to the bottom and lies down in that hand, kept and asleep until the Father raises him; the sword reached his body and could not follow him there.$n$,$n$But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.$n$,true),
(4731,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,1,5,1,$n$*Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:1). *And he saith unto them, Ye shall drink indeed of my cup, and be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with: but to sit on my right hand, and on my left, is not mine to give, but it shall be given to them for whom it is prepared of my Father.* (Matthew 20:23). The sons of Zebedee asked for the seats; the King promised them the cup. They would drink what he drank and be baptized with what he was baptized with, and the seats would be the Father's to assign. Wisdom puts the order the same way: the righteous man's labours are made no account of first, and the standing in great boldness comes after. The cup is the afflicting; the boldness before the afflicters is what follows it, and who sits where on that day is in the Father's hand, not seized along the road.$n$,$n$Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.$n$,true),
(4732,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,15,5,15,$n$*But the righteous live for evermore; their reward also is with Yahuah (God), and the care of them is with the Most High.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:15). *And he saith unto them, Ye shall drink indeed of my cup, and be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with: but to sit on my right hand, and on my left, is not mine to give, but it shall be given to them for whom it is prepared of my Father.* (Matthew 20:23). The righteous live for evermore and their reward is with Yahuah (God). It is kept in his hand, which is what the King tells the sons of Zebedee when they ask for the seats: to sit at his right hand and his left is not his to give, it is given to those for whom it is prepared of his Father. The reward is real and it is the Father's to give. It is not seized by flesh and it is not handed out regardless of the walk; it is held for the righteous by the one who sees their labours when no one else does.$n$,$n$But the righteous live for evermore; their reward also is with Yahuah (God), and the care of them is with the Most High.$n$,true),
(4733,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',7,37,7,37,$n$*But I, as my brothers, offer up my body and life for the laws of our fathers, beseeching Yahuah (God) that he would speedily be merciful to our nation; and that you by torments and plagues may confess, that he alone is Yahuah (God);* (2 Maccabees 7:37). *But Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, Ye know not what ye ask. Are ye able to drink of the cup that I shall drink of, and to be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with? They say unto him, We are able.* (Matthew 20:22). The youngest brother offers his body and life for the laws of his fathers and prays that in him and his brothers the wrath on the nation would cease (2 Maccabees 7:38). When James and John ask for the chief seats, Yahusha (Jesus) asks whether they can drink the cup he is about to drink, and they say they are able. The brothers under Antiochus had drunk a cup like that, giving their lives for the Torah and for mercy on their people. Only the King's cup carries the remission of sins; his death is the one no martyr could die. But the cost of following him is real, and the seven brothers show what the sons of Zebedee were agreeing to, James by the sword (Acts 12:2) and John in exile on Patmos (Revelation 1:9).$n$,$n$But I, as my brothers, offer up my body and life for the laws of our fathers, beseeching Yahuah (God) that he would speedily be merciful to our nation; and that you by torments and plagues may confess, that he alone is Yahuah (God);$n$,true),
(4738,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,18,3,18,$n$*The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).* (Ecclesiasticus 3:18). *But it shall not be so among you: but whosoever will be great among you, let him be your minister;* (Matthew 20:26). The kingdoms of the world run on domination; Yahusha (Jesus) has just said so of the princes of the Gentiles, and then he says it shall not be so among you. Whoever will be great among you, let him be your minister, the one who waits the table. Sirach had set the same direction centuries earlier: the greater you are, the more humble yourself, and favour before Yahuah (God) follows the humbling. In the kingdom greatness flows downward into service, the exact reverse of the kingdoms around it, and the Messiah is not introducing that reversal; he is restoring what the fathers had already taught and then living it out with a towel.$n$,$n$The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4744,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,7,10,7,$n$*Therefore they bare branches, and fair boughs, and palms also, and sang psalms to him that had given them good success in cleansing his place.* (2 Maccabees 10:7). *And a very great multitude spread their garments in the way; others cut down branches from the trees, and strawed them in the way.* (Matthew 21:8). When the temple was cleansed, the people carried branches and fair boughs and palms and sang psalms to the one who had given them success in cleansing his place, keeping eight days with gladness like the feast of tabernacles (2 Maccabees 10:6). When Yahusha (Jesus) rode into Jerusalem, the multitude cut down branches and strewed them in the way and cried Hosanna from Psalm 118, and the next thing he did was walk into the temple and cleanse it. Branches, psalms, and a cleansed temple belong together in both books. The people at the gate were doing what their fathers had done after Judas's victory, whether they knew it or not, and the King they welcomed went straight to his Father's house as the Maccabees had gone to theirs.$n$,$n$Therefore they bare branches, and fair boughs, and palms also, and sang psalms to him that had given them good success in cleansing his place.$n$,true),
(4745,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,14,36,14,$n$*Fill Sion with your unspeakable oracles, and your people with your glory:* (Ecclesiasticus 36:14). *And the multitudes that went before, and that followed, cried, saying, Hosanna to the Son of David: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of Yahuah (Lord); Hosanna in the highest.* (Matthew 21:9). Sirach prays for Sion to be filled with the oracles of Yahuah (God) and his people with his glory, in the same prayer that asks for the tribes to be gathered and for mercy on Jerusalem. When Yahusha (Jesus) rode into the city the multitudes cried Hosanna to the Son of David and Blessed is he that cometh in the name of Yahuah (Lord), and the next verse says all the city was moved. The glory Sirach asked to fill Sion came through its gate on a colt. The city was moved and did not know by whom; the prayer was being answered in its streets.$n$,$n$Fill Sion with your unspeakable oracles, and your people with your glory:$n$,true),
(4746,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',4,43,4,43,$n$*Who cleansed the sanctuary, and bare out the defiled stones into an unclean place.* (1 Maccabees 4:43). *And Yahusha (Jesus) went into the temple of Elohim (God), and cast out all them that sold and bought in the temple, and overthrew the tables of the moneychangers, and the seats of them that sold doves,* (Matthew 21:12). The priests Judas chose carried the defiled stones out of the sanctuary to an unclean place. Yahusha (Jesus) went into the temple of Elohim (God) and carried out the sellers, the buyers, the tables, and the seats. What defiled the house in Judas's day was an idol the Greeks had set on the altar; what defiled it in the King's day was trade the priesthood had set in the court of prayer. Both were put out. Cleansing the sanctuary is what faithful men do when the house of Yahuah (God) has been made to serve something other than him.$n$,$n$Who cleansed the sanctuary, and bare out the defiled stones into an unclean place.$n$,true),
(4747,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,3,10,3,$n$*And having cleansed the temple they made another altar, and striking stones they took fire out of them, and offered a sacrifice after two years, and set forth incense, and lights, and shewbread.* (2 Maccabees 10:3). *And Yahusha (Jesus) went into the temple of Elohim (God), and cast out all them that sold and bought in the temple, and overthrew the tables of the moneychangers, and the seats of them that sold doves,* (Matthew 21:12). Judas and his men cleansed what Antiochus had defiled: they pulled down the heathen altars, built a new altar, struck fresh fire from stones, and brought back the sacrifice, the incense, the lamps, and the shewbread after two years without them. Yahusha (Jesus) also walked into the temple and cleansed it, but the defilement he drove out had not come from a foreign king. It came from the house's own leadership, who had let the courts become a market, and he answered them with Jeremiah's words: *My house shall be called the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves* (Matthew 21:13; Jeremiah 7:11). The Maccabees cleansed the temple of a defilement pressed on it from outside; the King cleansed it of the one that grew up inside, in the very priesthood their victory had restored. Both cleansings say the same thing about whose house it is.$n$,$n$And having cleansed the temple they made another altar, and striking stones they took fire out of them, and offered a sacrifice after two years, and set forth incense, and lights, and shewbread.$n$,true),
(4748,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,12,35,12,$n$*Do not think to corrupt with gifts; for such he will not receive: and trust not to unrighteous sacrifices; for Yahuah (God) is judge, and with him is no respect of persons.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:12). *And said unto them, It is written, My house shall be called the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves.* (Matthew 21:13). Sirach says do not think to corrupt Yahuah (God) with gifts, for he will not take them, and do not trust in unrighteous sacrifices, for he is judge and shows no favour to persons. Yahusha (Jesus) walked into the temple courts and found the house of prayer turned into a den of thieves; the gifts and the sacrifices had become a trade. The leaders who ran that trade trusted that the sacrifices would still cover them. Sirach had already told them the judge does not take bribes, and the King overturning the tables was the judge saying it in person.$n$,$n$Do not think to corrupt with gifts; for such he will not receive: and trust not to unrighteous sacrifices; for Yahuah (God) is judge, and with him is no respect of persons.$n$,true),
(4749,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',4,46,4,46,$n$*And laid up the stones in the mountain of the temple in a convenient place, until there should come a prophet to shew what should be done with them.* (1 Maccabees 4:46). *And said unto them, It is written, My house shall be called the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves.* (Matthew 21:13). Judas's priests did not know what to do with the stones of the profaned altar, so they laid them up on the temple mount until a prophet should come to say. Yahusha (Jesus) came into that temple as the prophet the crowds had just named him, the prophet of Nazareth (Matthew 21:11), and the word he gave about the house was from the prophets already written: it is written, my house shall be called the house of prayer. The Maccabees waited for a prophet to tell them what the house required. When he came, he told them, and what it required was that it be a house of prayer and not a den of thieves.$n$,$n$And laid up the stones in the mountain of the temple in a convenient place, until there should come a prophet to shew what should be done with them.$n$,true),
(4750,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',4,4,4,4,$n$*For though they flourish in branches for a time; yet standing not last, they shall be shaken with the wind, and through the force of winds they shall be rooted out.* (Wisdom of Solomon 4:4). *And when he saw a fig tree in the way, he came to it, and found nothing thereon, but leaves only, and said unto it, Let no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever. And presently the fig tree withered away.* (Matthew 21:19). Wisdom describes the brood of the ungodly as branches that flourish for a while with nothing under them; the wind shakes them and finally roots them out. Yahusha (Jesus) came to a fig tree in full leaf, found no fruit, and it withered. Leaves without fruit is the same picture as branches without roots — a show of life with nothing feeding it. The tree stands for the religious leadership of that day, which had the look of the covenant and none of its fruit; it does not stand for the people of Yashar'el (Israel) as a whole. What flourishes without a root does not stand when the wind comes.$n$,$n$For though they flourish in branches for a time; yet standing not last, they shall be shaken with the wind, and through the force of winds they shall be rooted out.$n$,true),
(4751,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',4,5,4,5,$n$*The imperfect branches shall be broken off, their fruit unprofitable, not ripe to eat, yes, meet for nothing.* (Wisdom of Solomon 4:5). *And when he saw a fig tree in the way, he came to it, and found nothing thereon, but leaves only, and said unto it, Let no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever. And presently the fig tree withered away.* (Matthew 21:19). Wisdom is describing branches that flourish for a season, bear nothing worth eating, and are broken off. The fig tree the King met on the road the morning after he cleansed the Temple was all leaves and no fruit, and he spoke its end on the spot. The tree was not the people of Yashar'el (Israel); it was the leadership standing in that Temple with every show of foliage and nothing to feed anyone, the same men he answers with seven woes in Matthew 23 before the week is out. The test he had already given his disciples was fruit, not leaves (Matthew 7:16), and Solomon had written the same verdict long before.$n$,$n$The imperfect branches shall be broken off, their fruit unprofitable, not ripe to eat, yes, meet for nothing.$n$,true),
(4752,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,25,23,25,$n$*Her children shall not take root, and her branches shall bring forth no fruit.* (Ecclesiasticus 23:25). *And when he saw a fig tree in the way, he came to it, and found nothing thereon, but leaves only, and said unto it, Let no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever. And presently the fig tree withered away.* (Matthew 21:19). Sirach says of the adulterous woman that her children will not take root and her branches will bring forth no fruit — what grows from unfaithfulness has nothing underneath it, and nothing comes of it. Yahusha (Jesus) came to the fig tree, found leaves and no fruit, and spoke the same sentence over it: let no fruit grow on you from now on. The tree stands for the leadership that held the vineyard in trust and gave the Father leaves instead of fruit, as the parable of the husbandmen later in the same chapter says plainly. No root, no fruit, and then no fruit forever — the judgment reads the same in both books.$n$,$n$Her children shall not take root, and her branches shall bring forth no fruit.$n$,true),
(4754,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,24,17,24,$n$*But to them that repent, he granted them return, and comforted those that failed in patience.* (Ecclesiasticus 17:24). *He answered and said, I will not: but afterward he repented, and went.* (Matthew 21:29). Sirach says Yahuah (God) grants return to them that repent and comforts those who failed in patience. The first son in the parable said I will not, then repented and went — and he is the one who did the father's will. The publicans and harlots Yahusha (Jesus) names a few lines later walked the same road: a no at first, and then a return. Return is granted to the one who turns, not to the one who said the right word and stayed where he was.$n$,$n$But to them that repent, he granted them return, and comforted those that failed in patience.$n$,true),
(4755,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',1,28,1,28,$n$*Distrust not the fear of Yahuah (God) when you are poor: and come not to him with a double heart.* (Ecclesiasticus 1:28). *And he came to the second, and said likewise. And he answered and said, I go, sir: and went not.* (Matthew 21:30). The second son in the parable says yes with his mouth and no with his feet. Sirach had already named that man: the one who comes to Yahuah (God) with a double heart, one heart for what he says and another for what he does. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the parable to the chief priests and elders, who had answered the Father's call through John with the words of obedience and none of the walking, and the double heart is what he is exposing. A yes that does not go is not a weaker obedience; it is the thing Sirach warns against, and the Father does not receive it.$n$,$n$Distrust not the fear of Yahuah (God) when you are poor: and come not to him with a double heart.$n$,true),
(4756,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',1,29,1,29,$n$*Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men, and take good heed what you speak.* (Ecclesiasticus 1:29). *And he came to the second, and said likewise. And he answered and said, I go, sir: and went not.* (Matthew 21:30). Sirach's warning is against being a hypocrite in the sight of men, and against careless speech, and the second son in the parable fails both at once. He speaks the right words, I go, sir, where men can hear them, and then does not go. Yahusha (Jesus) is telling the parable to the chief priests and elders, who had given the Father the public yes and withheld the obedience, which is hypocrisy as Sirach defines it: a performance for the watching crowd with nothing behind it. Take good heed what you speak, Sirach says, because the Father holds a man to what his mouth promised.$n$,$n$Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men, and take good heed what you speak.$n$,true),
(4757,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',21,1,21,1,$n$*My son, have you sinned? do so no more, but ask pardon for your former sins.* (Ecclesiasticus 21:1). *For John came unto you in the way of righteousness, and ye believed him not: but the publicans and the harlots believed him: and ye, when ye had seen it, repented not afterward, that ye might believe him.* (Matthew 21:32). Sirach opens the chapter with the whole of repentance in one line: stop doing it, and ask pardon for what is past. That is what John came preaching in the wilderness, and Yahusha (Jesus) names who actually did it — the publicans and the harlots believed him and turned, while the chief priests and elders saw it and repented not. The ones with the least standing walked Sirach's counsel; the ones holding the office refused it. Sonship is shown by the turning, not by the title.$n$,$n$My son, have you sinned? do so no more, but ask pardon for your former sins.$n$,true),
(4758,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',9,11,9,11,$n$*And they that have loathed my law, while they had yet liberty, and, when as yet place of repentance was open to them, understood not, but despised it;* (2 Esdras 9:11). *For John came unto you in the way of righteousness, and ye believed him not: but the publicans and the harlots believed him: and ye, when ye had seen it, repented not afterward, that ye might believe him.* (Matthew 21:32). The Most High describes those who loathed his law while they still had liberty, while a place of repentance stood open to them, and despised it. The chief priests and elders are told the same about themselves: John came in the way of righteousness, which is the law's own way, and they did not believe him, and even after watching publicans and harlots repent they did not repent afterward. The open place of repentance was John at the Jordan; they walked past it. Ezra's lament and the King's rebuke describe the same men in the same posture.$n$,$n$And they that have loathed my law, while they had yet liberty, and, when as yet place of repentance was open to them, understood not, but despised it;$n$,true),
(4759,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',1,32,1,32,$n$*I sent to you my servants the prophets, whom you have taken and slain, and torn their bodies in pieces, whose blood I will require of your hands, says Yahuah (God).* (2 Esdras 1:32). *And the husbandmen took his servants, and beat one, and killed another, and stoned another.* (Matthew 21:35). Yahuah (God) says he sent his servants the prophets and the people took them, slew them, and tore their bodies in pieces, and he will require their blood. In the parable the husbandmen take the owner's servants and beat one, kill another, and stone another. The servants are the prophets, the husbandmen are the men set over the vineyard, and the owner who sent them is going to require the blood. The parable is not a new story; the King is telling the leadership of his day the history already written against their fathers, and daring them to finish it.$n$,$n$I sent to you my servants the prophets, whom you have taken and slain, and torn their bodies in pieces, whose blood I will require of your hands, says Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4760,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',1,33,1,33,$n$*Thus says the Almighty Yahuah (God), Your house is desolate, I will cast you out as the wind does stubble.* (2 Esdras 1:33). *And they caught him, and cast him out of the vineyard, and slew him.* (Matthew 21:39). The sentence in 2 Esdras follows directly on the slaying of the prophets: your house is desolate, I will cast you out as the wind does stubble. The parable follows the same order. After the servants are beaten and killed the son is sent, and the husbandmen cast him out of the vineyard and slay him, and the King asks his hearers what the owner will do to them. The desolate house is the answer. Killing the prophets brought the sentence near; killing the son brought it down, and within a generation not one stone of that house was left on another.$n$,$n$Thus says the Almighty Yahuah (God), Your house is desolate, I will cast you out as the wind does stubble.$n$,true),
(4762,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *Therefore say I unto you, The kingdom of Elohim (God) shall be taken from you, and given to a nation bringing forth the fruits thereof.* (Matthew 21:43). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and inherited as from the beginning. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the chief priests the kingdom will be taken from them and given to a nation bringing forth its fruits; a nation, one, not the nations. That one nation is the gathered tribes of Jacob bearing fruit, the very people Sirach prayed for. It is not the church put in the place of Yashar'el (Israel), and it is not whoever keeps Torah regardless of seed; it is Jacob's own tribes, gathered home and bearing the fruit their leaders in that day would not bring.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(4763,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',2,38,2,38,$n$*Arise up and stand, behold the number of those that be sealed in the feast of Yahuah (God);* (2 Esdras 2:38). *Go ye therefore into the highways, and as many as ye shall find, bid to the marriage.* (Matthew 22:9). Esdras is told to stand and look at the number of those sealed in the feast of Yahuah (God). In the parable the first guests would not come, so the king sends his servants into the highways to bid as many as they find to the marriage. The ones found on the highways are not strangers to the king; they are the scattered children whose number he already knows, sealed before the servants ever went out. The feast of Yahuah (God) in 2 Esdras and the marriage in Matthew are the same table, and the guests are gathered in from wherever they were scattered.$n$,$n$Arise up and stand, behold the number of those that be sealed in the feast of Yahuah (God);$n$,true),
(4764,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',2,42,2,42,$n$*I Esdras saw upon the mount Sion a great people, whom I could not number, and they all praised Yahuah (God) with songs.* (2 Esdras 2:42). *So those servants went out into the highways, and gathered together all as many as they found, both bad and good: and the wedding was furnished with guests.* (Matthew 22:10). Esdras sees on mount Sion a great people he could not number, all praising Yahuah (God) with songs. The parable ends with the wedding furnished with guests, gathered from the highways, both bad and good. John saw the same crowd in Revelation 7, a great multitude no man could number, out of all nations. Esdras saw the gathered on the mountain, the parable shows the gathering on the roads, and the next verse in each shows the King coming among them to see who is wearing what.$n$,$n$I Esdras saw upon the mount Sion a great people, whom I could not number, and they all praised Yahuah (God) with songs.$n$,true),
(4765,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',2,43,2,43,$n$*And in the midst of them there was a young man of a high stature, taller than all the rest, and upon every one of their heads he set crowns, and was more exalted; which I marvelled at greatly.* (2 Esdras 2:43). *And when the king came in to see the guests, he saw there a man which had not on a wedding garment:* (Matthew 22:11). In the midst of the gathered crowd on mount Sion a young man taller than all the rest sets a crown on every head, and the angel tells Esdras a few verses on that this is the Son of Elohim (God) whom they confessed in the world. In the parable the king comes in to see the guests and finds a man with no wedding garment. Both scenes have the King walking among the gathered, and in both what he does depends on what they are wearing; in 2 Esdras the verses before say the gathered have received glorious garments, and these receive crowns. The man without the garment at the marriage receives nothing and is put out. The garment is the righteousness of the saints, and the King himself inspects it.$n$,$n$And in the midst of them there was a young man of a high stature, taller than all the rest, and upon every one of their heads he set crowns, and was more exalted; which I marvelled at greatly.$n$,true),
(4766,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,16,5,16,$n$*Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God): for with his right hand shall he cover them, and with his arm shall he protect them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:16). *And he saith unto him, Friend, how camest thou in hither not having a wedding garment? And he was speechless.* (Matthew 22:12). The guest came in without a wedding garment and had nothing to say. Wisdom shows what the garment was for: those who receive a glorious kingdom and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God) receive them clothed. The fine linen of the bride is the righteousness of saints (Revelation 19:8), granted to her and then worn, and the man at the feast had neither put it on nor walked in it. The crown is given from the Father's hand to those who came dressed for the feast; the one who came in his own clothes receives the silence and the door.$n$,$n$Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God): for with his right hand shall he cover them, and with his arm shall he protect them.$n$,true),
(4767,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,3,17,3,$n$*He endued them with strength by themselves, and made them according to his image,* (Ecclesiasticus 17:3). *And he saith unto them, Whose is this image and superscription?* (Matthew 22:20). Sirach says the Maker made men according to his image, with strength of their own, carrying Genesis 1 forward. Yahusha (Jesus) held up the coin and asked whose image and superscription it bore. The coin bears Caesar's image and goes back to Caesar; the man bears the Father's image and belongs to the Father. The question about the tribute was a question about ownership, and Sirach had already said who stamped the man.$n$,$n$He endued them with strength by themselves, and made them according to his image,$n$,true),
(4768,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,11,17,11,$n$*Beside this he gave them knowledge, and the law of life for an heritage.* (Ecclesiasticus 17:11). *They say unto him, Cæsar’s. Then saith he unto them, Render therefore unto Cæsar the things which are Cæsar’s; and unto Elohim (God) the things that are Elohim's (God’s).* (Matthew 22:21). Sirach says the law of life was given as an heritage — set on the people along with the image. Yahusha (Jesus) says to render to Elohim (God) the things that are Elohim's (God's). What is Elohim's (God's) is what he put on the man: his image, and the law he gave for the man's inheritance. Rendering it back is not a burden laid on a free man; it is the heir keeping his inheritance.$n$,$n$Beside this he gave them knowledge, and the law of life for an heritage.$n$,true),
(4769,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,23,2,23,$n$*For Yahuah (God) created man to be immortal, and made him to be an image of his own eternity.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:23). *And he saith unto them, Whose is this image and superscription?* (Matthew 22:20). Yahusha (Jesus) asked whose image was on the coin, and the answer settled where the coin belonged. Wisdom tells us whose image is on the man: Yahuah (God) made him an image of his own eternity. Caesar's stamp is on the money, so the money goes back to Caesar; the Father's stamp is on the one holding it, so the one holding it goes back to the Father. The coin is perishable and so is its owner. The man was made for immortality, and the image he bears tells him whose he is.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) created man to be immortal, and made him to be an image of his own eternity.$n$,true),
(4770,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',9,1,9,1,$n$*O Yahuah (God) of my fathers, and Yahuah (God) of mercy, who have made all things with your word,* (Wisdom of Solomon 9:1). *They say unto him, Cæsar’s. Then saith he unto them, Render therefore unto Cæsar the things which are Cæsar’s; and unto Elohim (God) the things that are Elohim's (God’s).* (Matthew 22:21). Caesar's face on the coin makes the coin Caesar's. Solomon's prayer tells you what makes everything else the Father's: he made all things with his word, and the next verse adds that he ordained man through his wisdom to rule over what was made. So when the King says render unto Elohim (God) the things that are Elohim's (God's), the things in question are all things, man first, because man carries the Maker's image the way the denarius carries Caesar's. The Word the Father made all things with is the one standing in the court holding the coin and asking whose image is on it.$n$,$n$O Yahuah (God) of my fathers, and Yahuah (God) of mercy, who have made all things with your word,$n$,true),
(4771,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',7,9,7,9,$n$*And when he was at the last gasp, he said, You like a fury takest us out of this present life, but the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.* (2 Maccabees 7:9). *Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Ye do err, not knowing the scriptures, nor the power of Elohim (God).* (Matthew 22:29). The second of the seven brothers, dying under Antiochus for refusing to break the Torah, said with his last breath that the King of the world would raise him up to everlasting life. He did not hold a philosophy about the afterlife; he held a hope in the resurrection, and he paid for it with his body. When the Sadducees, who said there is no resurrection (Acts 23:8), came to trap Yahusha (Jesus), he told them they erred because they knew neither the scriptures nor the power of Elohim (God). The brothers in 2 Maccabees 7 knew both. The resurrection the King defended in the temple was the resurrection a family had already confessed under torture, for his laws.$n$,$n$And when he was at the last gasp, he said, You like a fury takest us out of this present life, but the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.$n$,true),
(4772,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',7,23,7,23,$n$*But doubtless the Creator of the world, who formed the generation of man, and found out the beginning of all things, will also of his own mercy give you breath and life again, as you now regard not your own selves for his laws’ sake.* (2 Maccabees 7:23). *I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob? Elohim (God) is not the Elohim (God) of the dead, but of the living.* (Matthew 22:32). The mother tells her sons where life comes from: she did not give them breath, the Creator did, and the one who formed man at the beginning can give breath and life again. That is her whole comfort as they die. Yahusha (Jesus) makes the same argument from the same ground: Elohim (God) names himself the Elohim (God) of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and he is not the Elohim (God) of the dead but of the living, so the fathers must rise. Neither the mother nor the King speaks of souls already in heaven; both look to the Creator giving breath again to those who sleep. The seven sons and their mother lie down in that hope, waiting with the fathers for the day their breath is given back.$n$,$n$But doubtless the Creator of the world, who formed the generation of man, and found out the beginning of all things, will also of his own mercy give you breath and life again, as you now regard not your own selves for his laws’ sake.$n$,true),
(4774,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,1,3,1,$n$*But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1). *I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob? Elohim (God) is not the Elohim (God) of the dead, but of the living.* (Matthew 22:32). Read this verse with the rest of its chapter and it is not souls flying off to heaven. The righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God) — kept, at rest, waiting; a few verses on, *in the time of their visitation they shall shine* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:7), and that visitation has not come yet. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob: Elohim (God) is not the Elohim (God) of the dead but of the living. The fathers are asleep and held, and the Father counts them living because he is going to raise them. That is why Paul can tell the grieving not to sorrow as those who have no hope (1 Thessalonians 4:13), and it is the only reason.$n$,$n$But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.$n$,true),
(4775,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',7,30,7,30,$n$*Love him that made you with all your strength, and forsake not his ministers.* (Ecclesiasticus 7:30). *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Thou shalt love Yahuah Elohayka (the Lord thy God) with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind.* (Matthew 22:37). Sirach is carrying the Shema forward: love him that made you with all your strength, the strength Deuteronomy 6:5 names and that Yahusha (Jesus) renders heart, soul, and mind when he gives the great commandment in Matthew 22:37. The Messiah is not replacing the commandments with love; he is quoting the commandment, the same one Sirach's readers recited twice a day. Sirach's next clause keeps love and Torah together, forsake not his ministers, and the verses around it give the priests their portion as it is commanded; loving Yahuah (God) with all your strength shows up as keeping what he commanded. That is the love written on the heart the prophets promised, not a feeling that excuses the walk.$n$,$n$Love him that made you with all your strength, and forsake not his ministers.$n$,true),
(4776,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,14,17,14,$n$*And he said to them, Beware of all unrighteousness; and he gave every man commandment concerning his neighbour.* (Ecclesiasticus 17:14). *And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.* (Matthew 22:39). Sirach says the commandment concerning the neighbour was given to every man, in the same breath as the warning against all unrighteousness — this is Sinai, where their eyes saw his glory and their ears heard his voice. When Yahusha (Jesus) names the second great commandment he is quoting the Torah, the commandment Sirach is describing. The King added nothing. He put his finger on what was given at the beginning.$n$,$n$And he said to them, Beware of all unrighteousness; and he gave every man commandment concerning his neighbour.$n$,true),
(4777,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,2,28,2,$n$*Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you, so shall your sins also be forgiven when you prayest.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:2). *And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.* (Matthew 22:39). Sirach says forgive your neighbour the hurt he did you, and your own sins will be forgiven. Yahusha (Jesus) names the second great commandment as love your neighbour as yourself, quoting Leviticus 19:18, and that verse in the Torah begins by forbidding vengeance and grudges before it ever says love. Forgiving the hurt is what loving the neighbour looks like when he has wronged you. Sirach is not adding to the commandment; he is reading it, and the King's answer to the lawyer stands on the same verse.$n$,$n$Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you, so shall your sins also be forgiven when you prayest.$n$,true),
(4778,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,7,28,7,$n$*Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour: remember the covenant of the Highest, and wink at ignorance.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:7). *On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets.* (Matthew 22:40). Sirach binds the commandments and the covenant together with bearing no malice and overlooking ignorance, all in one breath. When Yahusha (Jesus) says all the law and the prophets hang on loving Yahuah (God) and loving your neighbour, he is doing the same thing, not the opposite: he is saying what the whole Torah is for, not cutting it down to two lines. A man cannot hang a coat on a peg and then throw the coat away. Remember the commandments, and the love of the neighbour is what keeping them looks like; the heart Jeremiah 31:33 and Ezekiel 36:26–27 promise is the heart that does both at once.$n$,$n$Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour: remember the covenant of the Highest, and wink at ignorance.$n$,true),
(4779,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,18,6,18,$n$*And love is the keeping of her laws; and the giving heed to her laws is the assurance of incorruption;* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:18). *On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets.* (Matthew 22:40). Love is the keeping of her laws. The King hangs all the law and the prophets on two commandments, love of Yahuah (God) and love of neighbour, and the pulpit has used that sentence for centuries to say love replaced the law. Solomon says the opposite: love is what keeping the law looks like, and heed to her laws is the assurance of incorruption. John says it the same way, that this is the love of Elohim (God), that we keep his commandments (1 John 5:3). The two commandments are not a shorter list; they are the heart the whole Torah hangs from, the heart Ezekiel promised would be new so the statutes could be walked (Ezekiel 36:27).$n$,$n$And love is the keeping of her laws; and the giving heed to her laws is the assurance of incorruption;$n$,true),
(4785,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',10,14,10,14,$n$*Yahuah (God) has cast down the thrones of proud princes, and set up the meek in their stead.* (Ecclesiasticus 10:14). *And whosoever shall exalt himself shall be abased; and he that shall humble himself shall be exalted.* (Matthew 23:12). Sirach says the throne itself changes hands: the proud prince loses it and the meek receive it. Yahusha (Jesus) states the rule behind the history — exalt yourself and you are abased, humble yourself and you are exalted — and he says it in the middle of his rebuke of the scribes and Pharisees who loved the chief seats. This is not piety literature sitting beside the gospel; it is the same voice. What the Father does to kingdoms he does to men.$n$,$n$Yahuah (God) has cast down the thrones of proud princes, and set up the meek in their stead.$n$,true),
(4786,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,18,3,18,$n$*The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).* (Ecclesiasticus 3:18). *But he that is greatest among you shall be your servant.* (Matthew 23:11). Sirach's counsel runs opposite to how religious status works: the greater you are, the more humble yourself. In Matthew 23 Yahusha (Jesus) is describing a leadership that had reversed it, loving the chief seats and the greetings and the titles, and in the middle of the woes he states the kingdom's rule: he that is greatest among you shall be your servant. Any system that climbs as it grows holy has walked Sirach backward. The favour before Yahuah (God) that Sirach promises goes to the one who goes down, and the Messiah says the same of greatness.$n$,$n$The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4787,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',7,4,7,4,$n$*Seek not of Yahuah (God) preeminence, neither of the king the seat of honour.* (Ecclesiasticus 7:4). *And love the uppermost rooms at feasts, and the chief seats in the synagogues,* (Matthew 23:6). Sirach tells his son not to ask Yahuah (God) for preeminence or the king for the seat of honour. Matthew 23:6 describes a religious leadership that loved exactly those things, the uppermost rooms at feasts and the chief seats in the synagogues. The counsel and the woe are the same measure applied twice, once as a warning and once as a diagnosis, and the seat of honour is the tell in both. A system that wants the chief seat has made its religion a way of climbing, which is what Sirach said not to do with it.$n$,$n$Seek not of Yahuah (God) preeminence, neither of the king the seat of honour.$n$,true),
(4788,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',32,1,32,1,$n$*If you be made the master of a feast, lift not thyself up, but be among them as one of the rest; take diligent care for them, and so sit down.* (Ecclesiasticus 32:1). *And love the uppermost rooms at feasts, and the chief seats in the synagogues,* (Matthew 23:6). Sirach's rule for the master of a feast is to be among the guests as one of the rest and to take diligent care for them before he sits down. Yahusha (Jesus) names the scribes and Pharisees by the opposite habit: they love the uppermost rooms at feasts and the chief seats in the synagogues. Sirach put the care of the company first and the seat last; the leaders of that day had put the seat first and the company nowhere. The one who washed his disciples' feet on the night he was betrayed was keeping Sirach's rule to the letter.$n$,$n$If you be made the master of a feast, lift not thyself up, but be among them as one of the rest; take diligent care for them, and so sit down.$n$,true),
(4792,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',6,4,6,4,$n$*Because, being ministers of his kingdom, you have not judged aright, nor kept the law, nor walked after the counsel of Yahuah (God);* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:4). *But woe unto you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! for ye shut up the kingdom of heaven against men: for ye neither go in yourselves, neither suffer ye them that are entering to go in.* (Matthew 23:13). Wisdom addresses men who hold office as ministers of his kingdom and have not judged aright, nor kept the law, nor walked after his counsel. The King addresses the same office in the Temple court: men who hold the keys of the kingdom of heaven, do not go in themselves, and shut the door on those who are entering. The test is the one John gives plainly, that he who says he knows him and keeps not his commandments is a liar (1 John 2:4), and a ministry that sets aside the law it was given to keep fails it in whatever century it stands. The woe is on the office and the system; the men in it had the lie handed to them, and the people under them are the ones being shut out.$n$,$n$Because, being ministers of his kingdom, you have not judged aright, nor kept the law, nor walked after the counsel of Yahuah (God);$n$,true),
(4793,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,9,23,9,$n$*Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.* (Ecclesiasticus 23:9). *Woe unto you, ye blind guides, which say, Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gold of the temple, he is a debtor!* (Matthew 23:16). Sirach's counsel is simple: do not get your mouth used to swearing or to naming the Holy One. The tradition Yahusha (Jesus) is answering had gone the other way — it had built grades of oath, where swearing by the temple meant nothing but swearing by the gold of the temple bound you. The Torah knows no such grades; every vow binds and every oath counts. Sirach stood with the Torah on this, and the blind guides are the office that built the ladder of evasions, not the people who were taught to climb it.$n$,$n$Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.$n$,true),
(4794,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,11,23,11,$n$*A man that useth much swearing shall be filled with iniquity, and the plague shall never depart from his house: if he shall offend, his sin shall be upon him: and if he acknowledge not his sin, he makes a double offence: and if he swear in vain, he shall not be innocent, but his house shall be full of calamities.* (Ecclesiasticus 23:11). *And he that shall swear by heaven, sweareth by the throne of Elohim (God), and by him that sitteth thereon.* (Matthew 23:22). Sirach says the man who swears much is filled with iniquity and his house is full of calamities — he takes every oath seriously, because every one of them counts. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same against the tradition that had sorted oaths into binding and empty: to swear by heaven is to swear by the throne of Elohim (God) and by him who sits on it, so there is no empty oath. Sirach and Yahusha (Jesus) stand together on the Torah here; the grading of oaths was the invention of the system, not of Moses.$n$,$n$A man that useth much swearing shall be filled with iniquity, and the plague shall never depart from his house: if he shall offend, his sin shall be upon him: and if he acknowledge not his sin, he makes a double offence: and if he swear in vain, he shall not be innocent, but his house shall be full of calamities.$n$,true),
(4795,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,14,27,14,$n$*The talk of him that sweareth much makes the hair stand upright; and their brawls make one stop his ears.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:14). *And, Whosoever shall swear by the altar, it is nothing; but whosoever sweareth by the gift that is upon it, he is guilty.* (Matthew 23:18). Sirach says the talk of a man who swears constantly makes the hair stand on end, and their quarrels make you cover your ears. Yahusha (Jesus) met a whole religion of that: a system that had ruled an oath by the altar was nothing but an oath by the gift on the altar was binding. That is the much swearing Sirach warned of, dressed up as law, a maze of oaths that let a man say anything and be bound by none of it. The teaching that hedges every oath is what the King names blind, and his own word on the matter was let your yes be yes.$n$,$n$The talk of him that sweareth much makes the hair stand upright; and their brawls make one stop his ears.$n$,true),
(4796,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',1,28,1,28,$n$*Distrust not the fear of Yahuah (God) when you are poor: and come not to him with a double heart.* (Ecclesiasticus 1:28). *Woe unto you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! for ye make clean the outside of the cup and of the platter, but within they are full of extortion and excess.* (Matthew 23:25). A double heart is a clean outside and a different inside. Sirach warns the one who comes to Yahuah (God) not to come that way; Yahusha (Jesus) names the religious leadership of his day as having done exactly that, the cup scrubbed where men can see and full of extortion and excess where only Elohim (God) sees. The two verses are the same diagnosis from two directions, the counsel before the fault and the woe after it. Yahuah (God) has always looked on the heart, so a system that polishes the outside and leaves the inside alone has misjudged who is doing the inspecting.$n$,$n$Distrust not the fear of Yahuah (God) when you are poor: and come not to him with a double heart.$n$,true),
(4797,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',1,29,1,29,$n$*Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men, and take good heed what you speak.* (Ecclesiasticus 1:29). *Even so ye also outwardly appear righteous unto men, but within ye are full of hypocrisy and iniquity.* (Matthew 23:28). The word Yahusha (Jesus) uses, hypocrite, is the word for a stage actor, a man playing a part for an audience, and Sirach had warned against that very thing two centuries earlier: be not an hypocrite in the sight of men. Matthew 23:28 is where the seven woes land, outward righteousness for men to see, hypocrisy and iniquity underneath. The counsel in Sirach and the woe in Matthew describe one man, the one whose righteousness is aimed at human eyes. Yahuah (God) is not in the audience for that performance; he is reading the inside.$n$,$n$Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men, and take good heed what you speak.$n$,true),
(4800,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,8,5,8,$n$*What has pride profited us? or what good has riches with our vaunting brought us?* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:8). *Woe unto you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! for ye make clean the outside of the cup and of the platter, but within they are full of extortion and excess.* (Matthew 23:25). The pride and the riches were real; so was the vaunting. What Wisdom records is their worth on the day, which is nothing. The cup the King points at in the Temple court is the same lesson in a dish: clean on the outside where men can see it, extortion and excess on the inside where only the judgment will look. What has pride profited us is the question the inside of that cup will have to answer when the day of the righteous turns it over.$n$,$n$What has pride profited us? or what good has riches with our vaunting brought us?$n$,true),
(4803,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',1,30,1,30,$n$*I gathered you together, as a hen gathers her chickens under her wings: but now, what shall I do to you? I will cast you out from my face.* (2 Esdras 1:30). *O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, thou that killest the prophets, and stonest them which are sent unto thee, how often would I have gathered thy children together, even as a hen gathereth her chickens under her wings, and ye would not!* (Matthew 23:37). The voice in 2 Esdras is Yahuah (God) speaking to Yashar'el (Israel): I gathered you as a hen gathers her chickens under her wings, and you would not stay gathered. In the temple Yahusha (Jesus) says the same words over Jerusalem in the first person, how often would I have gathered thy children together, even as a hen gathereth her chickens under her wings, and ye would not. He is not borrowing a figure of speech; he is the one who did the gathering the figure describes, the one who spoke to the fathers and now stands in the city that killed the prophets he sent. The hen is the same hen, the children are the same children, and the refusal is the same refusal.$n$,$n$I gathered you together, as a hen gathers her chickens under her wings: but now, what shall I do to you? I will cast you out from my face.$n$,true),
(4804,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',1,32,1,32,$n$*I sent to you my servants the prophets, whom you have taken and slain, and torn their bodies in pieces, whose blood I will require of your hands, says Yahuah (God).* (2 Esdras 1:32). *Wherefore, behold, I send unto you prophets, and wise men, and scribes: and some of them ye shall kill and crucify; and some of them shall ye scourge in your synagogues, and persecute them from city to city:* (Matthew 23:34). The pattern in 2 Esdras is past tense: I sent my servants the prophets, you slew them, I will require their blood. In Matthew 23 the King turns it to the future: behold, I send unto you prophets, and wise men, and scribes, and some of them ye shall kill. He is the sender in both. The same voice that sent Jeremiah and Zechariah to be rejected now sends apostles to be scourged in the synagogues and chased from city to city, and the next verses say the blood of all of them, from Abel onward, will be required of that generation. What Yahuah (God) promised through Esdras, Yahusha (Jesus) pronounces in the temple.$n$,$n$I sent to you my servants the prophets, whom you have taken and slain, and torn their bodies in pieces, whose blood I will require of your hands, says Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(4805,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',1,33,1,33,$n$*Thus says the Almighty Yahuah (God), Your house is desolate, I will cast you out as the wind does stubble.* (2 Esdras 1:33). *Behold, your house is left unto you desolate.* (Matthew 23:38). The words are nearly the same in both books. Yahuah (God) Almighty says through Esdras, your house is desolate; Yahusha (Jesus) says in the temple, behold, your house is left unto you desolate, and walks out of it. Jeremiah had said it of the first house before Babylon burned it, and Ezekiel watched the glory leave it. Now the one whose glory it was leaves the second house on his own feet, and the house was desolate from that hour whatever the priests kept doing in it. The leadership that killed the prophets lost the house; the children the hen would have gathered are still being gathered.$n$,$n$Thus says the Almighty Yahuah (God), Your house is desolate, I will cast you out as the wind does stubble.$n$,true),
(4815,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,1,5,1,$n$*Nevertheless as concerning the tokens, behold, the days shall come, that they which dwell upon earth shall be taken in a great number, and the way of truth shall be hidden, and the land shall be barren of faith.* (2 Esdras 5:1). *And then shall many be offended, and shall betray one another, and shall hate one another.* (Matthew 24:10). The first sign Esdras is given of the end is not war or famine but this: the way of truth hidden and the land barren of faith. The King gives the same sign in the same order in Matthew 24, many offended, betraying one another, hating one another, and two verses on, because lawlessness abounds the love of many grows cold. Faith does not die by itself; it dies when the way of truth, which is the law of Yahuah (God), is hidden from the people, and lawlessness fills the place it left. Esdras saw the land barren; the King explains what made it so.$n$,$n$Nevertheless as concerning the tokens, behold, the days shall come, that they which dwell upon earth shall be taken in a great number, and the way of truth shall be hidden, and the land shall be barren of faith.$n$,true),
(4816,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,4,5,4,$n$*But if the Most High grant you to live, you shall see after the third trumpet that the sun shall suddenly shine again in the night, and the moon thrice in the day:* (2 Esdras 5:4). *Immediately after the tribulation of those days shall the sun be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light, and the stars shall fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens shall be shaken:* (Matthew 24:29). Esdras is told that those who live to see it will see the sun shine in the night and the moon by day, after the third trumpet. The King says that immediately after the tribulation of those days the sun will be darkened, the moon will not give her light, and the stars will fall. The prophets had said the same of the day of Yahuah (LORD): Isaiah, Joel, Amos, Zephaniah. In both books the disordered heavens come after the trouble, not before it, and in both they are seen by people still standing on the earth. A teaching that has his people carried away before those days has to set 2 Esdras 5 and Matthew 24 both aside to stand.$n$,$n$But if the Most High grant you to live, you shall see after the third trumpet that the sun shall suddenly shine again in the night, and the moon thrice in the day:$n$,true),
(4817,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,9,5,9,$n$*And salt waters shall be found in the sweet, and all friends shall destroy one another; then shall wit hide itself, and understanding withdraw itself into his secret chamber,* (2 Esdras 5:9). *And then shall many be offended, and shall betray one another, and shall hate one another.* (Matthew 24:10). Among the signs Esdras is given is that all friends shall destroy one another. The King names the same thing among his: many shall be offended, and shall betray one another, and shall hate one another. The pressure of the last days does not only come from outside; it turns friend on friend and brother on brother inside the house, and Paul tasted it when Demas forsook him and no man stood with him at his first answer. Esdras and Matthew both put the betrayal of friends on the list of signs, so the reader will not mistake it for proof that the way was wrong.$n$,$n$And salt waters shall be found in the sweet, and all friends shall destroy one another; then shall wit hide itself, and understanding withdraw itself into his secret chamber,$n$,true),
(4818,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,32,13,32,$n$*And the time shall be when these things shall come to pass, and the signs shall happen which I shewed you before, and then shall my Son be declared, whom you sawest as a man ascending.* (2 Esdras 13:32). *And then shall appear the sign of the Son of Adam in heaven: and then shall all the tribes of the earth mourn, and they shall see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.* (Matthew 24:30). Ezra sees a man come up out of the sea and is told that when the signs have happened, the Most High's Son will be declared. Yahusha (Jesus) sets the same order on the Mount of Olives: after the tribulation the sign of the Son of Adam appears in heaven and all the tribes of the earth see him coming in the clouds with power and great glory. The signs come first, then the Son is shown openly to all. Ezra was given the sequence; Matthew names the one it is about.$n$,$n$And the time shall be when these things shall come to pass, and the signs shall happen which I shewed you before, and then shall my Son be declared, whom you sawest as a man ascending.$n$,true),
(4819,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,39,13,39,$n$*And whereas you sawest that he gathered another peaceable multitude to him; Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.* (2 Esdras 13:39–40). *And he shall send his angels with a great sound of a trumpet, and they shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from one end of heaven to the other.* (Matthew 24:31). Ezra asks about the peaceable multitude the man from the sea gathers to himself, and the Most High tells him outright who they are: the ten tribes carried out of their own land by Salmanasar of Assyria and taken over the waters into another land. The elect gathered from the four winds at the sound of the trumpet are that multitude. The gathering Yahusha (Jesus) describes is not the assembling of whoever has decided to believe; it is the homecoming of a scattered people the Most High kept, named here by tribe. Ezra tells us whom the angels are sent to gather.$n$,$n$And whereas you sawest that he gathered another peaceable multitude to him;$n$,true),
(4820,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',11,26,11,26,$n$*For it is an easy thing to Yahuah (God) in the day of death to reward a man according to his ways.* (Ecclesiasticus 11:26). *Blessed is that servant, whom his lord when he cometh shall find so doing.* (Matthew 24:46). Sirach says the reward according to a man's ways is an easy thing for Yahuah (God) at the day of death. Yahusha (Jesus) blesses the servant found so doing when the lord returns — found at the work, not found having once begun it. The reward follows the ways walked through the whole delay. The day of death and the day of the lord's coming come to the same servant, and what he is found doing is the whole question.$n$,$n$For it is an easy thing to Yahuah (God) in the day of death to reward a man according to his ways.$n$,true),
(4821,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',16,14,16,14,$n$*Make way for every work of mercy: for every man shall find according to his works.* (Ecclesiasticus 16:14). *Verily I say unto you, That he shall make him ruler over all his goods.* (Matthew 24:47). Sirach says every man shall find according to his works. The faithful and wise servant of Matthew 24 found more — ruler over all his goods — because he was found faithful with the household he had. The one-time confession that the pulpits treat as the whole account is not what either verse describes. The finding is according to the walking, and the servant is rewarded for what he was found doing through the long wait.$n$,$n$Make way for every work of mercy: for every man shall find according to his works.$n$,true),
(4822,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,4,27,4,$n$*As when one sifteth with a sieve, the refuse remains; so the filth of man in his talk.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:4). *And shall cut him asunder, and appoint him his portion with the hypocrites: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.* (Matthew 24:51). Shake a sieve and what stays behind is the refuse; so a man's talk shows what is left in him once everything else falls through. The servant in Matthew 24 was trusted with his lord's household, and his talk and his conduct while the lord delayed showed what he was, so that when the lord came he was cut asunder and given his portion with the hypocrites. That is a sifting of the household, not of strangers; the one being sifted had been given a stewardship. Ezekiel says the same about the righteous man who turns from his righteousness, that none of his righteousness will be remembered (Ezekiel 18:24), and John the Baptist saw the fan in the Messiah's hand purging his own floor (Matthew 3:12). The sieve finds the refuse among the servants.$n$,$n$As when one sifteth with a sieve, the refuse remains; so the filth of man in his talk.$n$,true),
(4823,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,7,3,7,$n$*And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:7). *Blessed is that servant, whom his lord when he cometh shall find so doing.* (Matthew 24:46). Wisdom sets the shining of the righteous at the time of their visitation — the day the Master comes to look in on his house. Yahusha (Jesus) describes that same day from the servant's side: blessed is that servant whom his lord, when he comes, finds so doing. The visitation is the coming. The servant found at his work when the Master arrives is the one who shines, which is what Daniel saw as well — *they that be wise shall shine as the brightness of the firmament* (Daniel 12:3).$n$,$n$And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.$n$,true),
(4828,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,7,3,7,$n$*And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:7). *When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* (Matthew 25:31). The time of the righteous' visitation in Wisdom is the day Yahusha (Jesus) describes at the opening of the sheep and the goats: the Son of Adam coming in his glory with all the holy angels, and sitting on the throne of his glory. That is the visitation. The righteous who were killed in the sight of men shine on that day and not before, the way Daniel saw it — *they that be wise shall shine as the brightness of the firmament* (Daniel 12:3). The throne and the shining arrive together.$n$,$n$And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.$n$,true),
(4829,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,8,3,8,$n$*They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:8). *And before him shall be gathered all nations: and he shall separate them one from another, as a shepherd divideth his sheep from the goats:* (Matthew 25:32). Before the righteous judge the nations there is a gathering and a sorting. Yahusha (Jesus) says all nations are gathered before him and he separates them as a shepherd divides sheep from goats — the seed brought out of every nation and sifted, the way Ezekiel saw it pass under the rod. Then the sifted remnant, his brethren of Matthew 25:40, does what Wisdom says: judges the nations and has dominion over the people. Daniel saw the same order — *judgment was given to the saints of the El Elyon (most High); and the time came that the saints possessed the kingdom* (Daniel 7:22). The gathering comes first, the reign after.$n$,$n$They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.$n$,true),
(4830,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,5,5,5,$n$*How is he numbered among the children of Yahuah (God), and his lot is among the saints!* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:5). *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, ye blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34). At the judgment his afflicters discover that the man they despised is numbered among the children of Yahuah (God) and has his lot among the saints. The King says where that lot came from: a kingdom prepared for the blessed from the foundation of the world. It was not assigned on the day; it was prepared before there was a world, and the day only reveals whose it was. Names were written in the Lamb's book from the foundation of the world (Revelation 13:8), and on that day the sheep hear the Shepherd say come, because they were his before they ever heard him.$n$,$n$How is he numbered among the children of Yahuah (God), and his lot is among the saints!$n$,true),
(4831,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,1,5,1,$n$*Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:1). *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, ye cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41). Wisdom and the King are describing the same day from two sides. On that day the righteous stand in great boldness, and the next verse in Wisdom has their afflicters troubled with terrible fear and amazed at a salvation they never looked for, which is exactly the astonishment of those on the left hand asking when they ever saw him hungry or thirsty (Matthew 25:44). Notice what the King says about the fire: it was prepared for the devil and his angels, the fallen ones Jude and Peter say are kept in chains for judgment (Jude 6; 2 Peter 2:4), and John sees the devil himself cast into that lake of fire (Revelation 20:10). The goats are sent toward a place that was not made for them. They are the unfruitful among the gathered, sifted before the reign, not the nations being judged for entry.$n$,$n$Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.$n$,true),
(4832,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',11,21,11,21,$n$*Marvel not at the works of sinners; but trust in Yahuah (God), and abide in your labour: for it is an easy thing in the sight of Yahuah (God) on the sudden to make a poor man rich.* (Ecclesiasticus 11:21). *His lord said unto him, Well done, thou good and faithful servant: thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord.* (Matthew 25:21). Sirach tells the labourer not to marvel at how sinners get on, but to trust Yahuah (God) and stay at the work, because he can make a poor man rich in a moment. The faithful servant of the parable did exactly that — did not look sideways, did not stop, traded what he was given — and heard well done. The commendation is for the faithfulness, not the size of the return; the servant with two talents heard the same words as the one with five.$n$,$n$Marvel not at the works of sinners; but trust in Yahuah (God), and abide in your labour: for it is an easy thing in the sight of Yahuah (God) on the sudden to make a poor man rich.$n$,true),
(4833,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,1,22,1,$n$*A slothful man is compared to a filthy stone, and every one will hiss him out to his disgrace.* (Ecclesiasticus 22:1). *His lord answered and said unto him, Thou wicked and slothful servant, thou knewest that I reap where I sowed not, and gather where I have not strawed:* (Matthew 25:26). Sirach's picture is blunt: a slothful man is a filthy stone, and everyone hisses him out to his disgrace. The lord in the parable is just as blunt with the servant who buried his talent — wicked and slothful, he calls him, because the servant knew what his master expected and did nothing. The fault in both is the same: not wickedness done, but the work left undone. Sloth is treated as disgrace in the wisdom book and as judgment in the parable, and it is the same sin in both.$n$,$n$A slothful man is compared to a filthy stone, and every one will hiss him out to his disgrace.$n$,true),
(4834,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',33,28,33,28,$n$*Set him to work, as is fit for him: if he be not obedient, put on more heavy fetters.* (Ecclesiasticus 33:28). *Thou oughtest therefore to have put my money to the exchangers, and then at my coming I should have received mine own with usury.* (Matthew 25:27). Sirach's counsel for a servant is to set him to work fit for him, and if he will not obey, to bind him tighter; idleness, the verse before says, teaches much evil. The master in the parable judges his servant by the same measure. The servant was not condemned for losing the money; he was condemned for doing nothing with it, when even handing it to the exchangers would have been work enough. The one who was given a charge and buried it is the one Sirach's heavier fetters were written for.$n$,$n$Set him to work, as is fit for him: if he be not obedient, put on more heavy fetters.$n$,true),
(4835,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',51,30,51,30,$n$*Work your work betimes, and in his time he will give you your reward.* (Ecclesiasticus 51:30). *His lord said unto him, Well done, good and faithful servant; thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord.* (Matthew 25:23). Sirach's last word is a word about timing: do your work early, and the reward comes in his time, not yours. The faithful servant in the parable did exactly that; he worked while the master was away and was paid when the master came back, rule over many things and entry into his lord's joy. The reward is not handed out along the way. Yahusha (Jesus) says *the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works* (Matthew 16:27), and the reigning with him that follows the first resurrection is that reward (Revelation 20:4–6). The work is now; the wages are at his coming.$n$,$n$Work your work betimes, and in his time he will give you your reward.$n$,true),
(4836,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,1,8,1,$n$*And he answered me, saying, The most High has made this world for many, but the world to come for few.* (2 Esdras 8:1). *And while they went to buy, the bridegroom came; and they that were ready went in with him to the marriage: and the door was shut.* (Matthew 25:10). Ten virgins went out to meet the bridegroom, and all ten were called to the wedding; only those who were ready went in before the door was shut. That is Ezra's answer told as a story: this world is made for many, the world to come for few. The five who found the door shut were not strangers; they were virgins with lamps who had gone out to meet him. The few are those found ready when he comes, which is why readiness matters more than having once set out.$n$,$n$And he answered me, saying, The most High has made this world for many, but the world to come for few.$n$,true),
(4837,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,3,8,3,$n$*There be many created, but few shall be saved.* (2 Esdras 8:3). *But he answered and said, Verily I say unto you, I know you not.* (Matthew 25:12). Many created, few saved: Ezra receives it as the hard rule of the age. The foolish virgins knock at the shut door and hear I know you not, the same word spoken to those who say Lord, Lord and work iniquity (Matthew 7:23). They were among the called and they were not among the few. Ezra's few saved and the bridegroom's I know you not are the two ends of one sentence.$n$,$n$There be many created, but few shall be saved.$n$,true),
(4838,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,35,7,35,$n$*And the work shall follow, and the reward shall be shewed, and the good deeds shall be of force, and wicked deeds shall bear no rule.* (2 Esdras 7:35). *And cast ye the unprofitable servant into outer darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.* (Matthew 25:30). Ezra is told that at the judgment the work follows and the reward is shown, that good deeds are of force and wicked ones bear no rule. The servant in the parable was given a talent and called a servant; he belonged to the house. What condemned him was not that he was never the master's, but that his work did not follow, and so he is cast into the outer darkness. The warning here is to those inside the household whose deeds bore no fruit, which is why it is so sharp.$n$,$n$And the work shall follow, and the reward shall be shewed, and the good deeds shall be of force, and wicked deeds shall bear no rule.$n$,true),
(4840,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,16,4,16,$n$*Give of your bread to the hungry, and of your garments to them that are naked; and according to your abundance give alms: and let not your eye be envious, when you give alms.* (Tobit 4:16). *For I was an hungred, and ye gave me meat: I was thirsty, and ye gave me drink: I was a stranger, and ye took me in:* (Matthew 25:35). Bread to the hungry, garments to the naked, alms according to abundance, no envious eye — Tobit gives his son two of the very things the King names at the judgment: *I was an hungred, and ye gave me meat ... naked, and ye clothed me.* Isaiah had said the same, to deal bread to the hungry and cover the naked. This is not a charity program for the world in general; the King says it is mercy done to his brethren, and it is the measure by which the sheep are told from the goats. The library had been teaching that measure in the exile household all along.$n$,$n$Give of your bread to the hungry, and of your garments to them that are naked; and according to your abundance give alms: and let not your eye be envious, when you give alms.$n$,true),
(4841,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',7,32,7,32,$n$*And stretch yours hand to the poor, that your blessing may be perfected.* (Ecclesiasticus 7:32). *Naked, and ye clothed me: I was sick, and ye visited me: I was in prison, and ye came unto me.* (Matthew 25:36). Sirach says the hand stretched to the poor is what perfects the blessing — the blessing is not finished until it has gone out the door to someone in need. Yahusha (Jesus) sets the same thing as the measure at his judgment seat: the naked clothed, the sick visited, the prisoner visited, and he counts it as done to himself. Sirach keeps going in the next verses — be with them that weep, be not slow to visit the sick — and the list reads like the King's own. This is the fruit of the new heart Ezekiel promised, a heart that keeps his judgments because it has been made able to; a confession that never stretches out its hand has not yet been perfected into a blessing.$n$,$n$And stretch yours hand to the poor, that your blessing may be perfected.$n$,true),
(4842,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',29,9,29,9,$n$*Help the poor for the commandment’s sake, and turn him not away because of his poverty.* (Ecclesiasticus 29:9). *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40). Sirach says help the poor because the commandment says so, and do not turn him away because he is poor. At the judgment the King says that whatever was done to the least of his brethren was done to him. The mercy Sirach commands is not a charity program; it is the commandment kept, and the King counts it as done to himself because the least of these are his own kin, his brethren, the household of Yashar'el (Israel) scattered and poor. Help the poor for the commandment's sake and you have served the King without knowing his face.$n$,$n$Help the poor for the commandment’s sake, and turn him not away because of his poverty.$n$,true),
(4843,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',4,1,4,1,$n$*My son, defraud not the poor of his living, and make not the needy eyes to wait long.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:1). *For I was an hungred, and ye gave me meat: I was thirsty, and ye gave me drink: I was a stranger, and ye took me in:* (Matthew 25:35). Sirach's command not to defraud the poor and not to keep needy eyes waiting is what the sheep in Matthew 25 are found to have done: I was hungry and you gave me meat, thirsty and you gave me drink, a stranger and you took me in. The judgment does not ask whether a man knew the right words; it asks what he did when the needy eyes were on him, and Sirach's chapter goes on to name the failure the goats are judged for, turn not away your eye from the needy (verse 5). This is not earning anything; it is what covenant life with Yahuah (God) looks like from the outside. The King recognizes his own by it because it was always his way.$n$,$n$My son, defraud not the poor of his living, and make not the needy eyes to wait long.$n$,true),
(4844,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40). Tobit promises that the face of Yahuah (God) stays turned toward the man who does not turn his face from the poor. Yahusha (Jesus) says it more sharply: what you did to the least of my brethren you did to me. The giver meets the King in the one he feeds, and the King's face toward him is the Father's face toward him. The same thing was said to Saul on the Damascus road, that to persecute the brethren was to persecute him (Acts 9:4–5). It is personal: mercy toward the King's brethren is mercy the King himself receives.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(4855,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',1,63,1,63,$n$*Wherefore they chose rather to die, that they might not be defiled with meats, and that they might not profane the holy covenant: so then they died.* (1 Maccabees 1:63). *For this is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many for the remission of sins.* (Matthew 26:28). Under Antiochus the faithful in Yashar'el (Israel) chose death over unclean meat because eating it would profane the holy covenant, and so they died. At the table Yahusha (Jesus) names his own blood the blood of the new covenant, shed for many. The covenant the Maccabean faithful died for and the covenant he died to seal are not two covenants with two laws; Jeremiah says the new covenant is the law written in the inward parts and on the heart, the same law those men would not break. They gave their lives rather than profane it; he gave his to write it where it could not be taken from them.$n$,$n$Wherefore they chose rather to die, that they might not be defiled with meats, and that they might not profane the holy covenant: so then they died.$n$,true),
(4856,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',2,50,2,50,$n$*Now therefore, my sons, be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.* (1 Maccabees 2:50). *For this is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many for the remission of sins.* (Matthew 26:28). Mattathias, dying, charges his sons to be zealous for the law and to give their lives for the covenant of their fathers. The King does both. His zeal for his Father's house and his Father's commandments runs through the gospels, and at the table he gives his life, his blood of the new covenant shed for many. The covenant of the fathers and the new covenant are one covenant; what is new in Jeremiah 31 is where the law is written, on the heart, not whether it stands. So the zeal Mattathias asked of his sons is not a different thing from what Yahusha (Jesus) poured out; it is the same love of the same law, carried to the same end.$n$,$n$Now therefore, my sons, be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.$n$,true),
(4857,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',7,36,7,36,$n$*For our brothers, who now have suffered a short pain, are dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life: but you, through the judgment of Yahuah (God), shall receive just punishment for your pride.* (2 Maccabees 7:36). *For this is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many for the remission of sins.* (Matthew 26:28). The youngest brother says his brothers are dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life. They died for his laws, and their deaths are held inside a covenant that promises them life again. At the last supper Yahusha (Jesus) names his blood as the blood of the new covenant, shed for many for the remission of sins. The covenant the martyrs died under and the covenant the King's blood seals are not two covenants; the brothers trusted a promise of everlasting life, and the King's death and third-day rising are where that promise was made good. Their short pain and his cup end in the same resurrection, and the Torah they died for is the Torah the new covenant writes on the heart (Jeremiah 31:33).$n$,$n$For our brothers, who now have suffered a short pain, are dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life: but you, through the judgment of Yahuah (God), shall receive just punishment for your pride.$n$,true),
(4864,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',6,1,6,1,$n$*Instead of a friend become not an enemy; for thereby you shall inherit an ill name, shame, and reproach: even so shall a sinner that has a double tongue.* (Ecclesiasticus 6:1). *Then Judas, which betrayed him, answered and said, Master, is it I? He said unto him, Thou hast said.* (Matthew 26:25). Sirach warns against two things in one verse, the friend who turns enemy and the sinner with a double tongue, and Judas is both at the table. He had already sold his Master for thirty pieces of silver, and he asks, Master, is it I?, with the bargain in his pocket; that is the double tongue, one voice at the supper and another with the priests. Yahusha (Jesus) answers, thou hast said, and lets the man's own mouth carry the verdict. The betrayal served the Father's purpose and was Judas's own choice at the same time; the devil put it in his heart, John says, and Judas walked it out, and Sirach's warning stands over him as the shame and reproach of a friend who became an enemy.$n$,$n$Instead of a friend become not an enemy; for thereby you shall inherit an ill name, shame, and reproach: even so shall a sinner that has a double tongue.$n$,true),
(4865,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',12,16,12,16,$n$*An enemy speaks sweetly with his lips, but in his heart he imagineth how to throw you into a pit: he will weep with his eyes, but if he find opportunity, he will not be satisfied with blood.* (Ecclesiasticus 12:16). *And forthwith he came to Yahusha (Jesus), and said, Hail, master; and kissed him.* (Matthew 26:49). Sirach draws the enemy who speaks sweetly with his lips while planning the pit, who weeps with his eyes and is not satisfied with blood. Judas came with Hail, master, and a kiss. The sweet lips and the pit were never far apart in the Hebrew library, and Yahusha (Jesus) knew the pattern before the kiss landed. The betrayer is the old enemy of the proverbs with a face.$n$,$n$An enemy speaks sweetly with his lips, but in his heart he imagineth how to throw you into a pit: he will weep with his eyes, but if he find opportunity, he will not be satisfied with blood.$n$,true),
(4866,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',37,2,37,2,$n$*Is it not a grief to death, when a companion and friend is turned to an enemy?* (Ecclesiasticus 37:2). *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Friend, wherefore art thou come? Then came they, and laid hands on Yahusha (Jesus), and took him.* (Matthew 26:50). Sirach asks whether it is not a grief unto death when a companion and friend turns enemy. In the garden Yahusha (Jesus) called Judas Friend as the kiss was given and the hands were laid on him. He had just said his soul was exceeding sorrowful, even unto death, which is Sirach's own measure of it. The friend who had eaten his bread lifted up his heel against him, and the King named him Friend to the last; that is the grief Sirach meant.$n$,$n$Is it not a grief to death, when a companion and friend is turned to an enemy?$n$,true),
(4867,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',2,1,2,1,$n$*My son, if you come to serve Yahuah (God), prepare your soul for temptation.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:1). *Watch and pray, that ye enter not into temptation: the spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak.* (Matthew 26:41). In Gethsemane Yahusha (Jesus) tells the disciples to watch and pray so they do not enter into temptation, and they fall asleep. Sirach had said what they needed to hear before they ever came to the garden: if you come to serve Yahuah (God), prepare your soul for temptation. The flesh is weak, the Messiah says; what Sirach adds is that a servant can get his soul ready for that weakness instead of being surprised by it. The disciples were willing in spirit and unprepared in soul, and the night proved Sirach right.$n$,$n$My son, if you come to serve Yahuah (God), prepare your soul for temptation.$n$,true),
(4868,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',2,5,2,5,$n$*For gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:5). *And Peter remembered the word of Yahusha (Jesus), which said unto him, Before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice. And he went out, and wept bitterly.* (Matthew 26:75). Peter went into the furnace that night and came out weeping. He had denied his Master three times, the cock crowed, the word came back to him, and he went out and wept bitterly. Sirach says acceptable men are tried in the furnace of adversity the way gold is tried in the fire, and the bitter weeping is the fire working. Caiaphas had rent his garments a few verses earlier (v.65); Peter rent his heart, which is the tearing Yahuah (God) asks for, and it carried him to the restoration by the shore in John 21. A disciple can fail, and a failed disciple is neither finished nor safe; he repents, and the furnace is what shows whether he is gold.$n$,$n$For gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.$n$,true),
(4881,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,30,7,30,$n$*After these years shall my son Messiah (Christ) die, and all men that have life. And the world shall be turned into the old silence seven days, like as in the former judgments: so that no man shall remain.* (2 Esdras 7:29–30). *Now from the sixth hour there was darkness over all the land unto the ninth hour.* (Matthew 27:45). Ezra is told that the Messiah will die, and that the world will then be turned back into the old silence, as in the former judgments, before the raising up that follows (2 Esdras 7:31–32). When Yahusha (Jesus) hung on the tree, darkness lay over all the land from the sixth hour to the ninth, as if creation had drawn back toward its first dark. The three hours were not the seven days; they were a small showing of them, the world going quiet at the death of the Messiah (Christ) before the graves gave up their dead (Matthew 27:52). Amos had said *I will cause the sun to go down at noon, and I will darken the earth in the clear day* (Amos 8:9); Ezra was told the Messiah's death brings the old silence; the cross carried both for three hours.$n$,$n$And the world shall be turned into the old silence seven days, like as in the former judgments: so that no man shall remain.$n$,true),
(4882,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,33,7,33,$n$*And the Most High shall appear upon the seat of judgment, and misery shall pass away, and the long suffering shall have an end:* (2 Esdras 7:33). *And, behold, the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom; and the earth did quake, and the rocks rent;* (Matthew 27:51). In Ezra's order, once the earth has given back her dead the Most High appears upon the seat of judgment, and misery and long suffering come to an end. At the moment Yahusha (Jesus) died the veil of the temple tore from top to bottom, the earth quaked, the rocks split, and the graves were opened (Matthew 27:52). The signs that belong to the day of the Most High's appearing broke through for a moment at the cross. It was not that day, but it carried the marks of it, the way a first shaking tells of the one still to come (Haggai 2:6–7).$n$,$n$And the Most High shall appear upon the seat of judgment, and misery shall pass away, and the long suffering shall have an end:$n$,true),
(4883,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,32,7,32,$n$*And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.* (2 Esdras 7:32). *And the graves were opened; and many bodies of the saints which slept arose,* (Matthew 27:52). Ezra is told that the earth will restore those asleep in her, that the dust will give back those that dwell in silence. At the cross the earth quaked and the graves were opened, and many bodies of the saints that slept arose. The earth was doing in small measure what Ezra was told she will do fully: giving back what was sleeping in her. The saints who came out of their graves that day are a first showing of the resurrection the fathers are still waiting for, the first resurrection in which the faithful who fell asleep are raised (Revelation 20:4–6; Hebrews 11:39–40).$n$,$n$And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.$n$,true),
(4890,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,7,3,7,$n$*And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:7). *He is not here: for he is risen, as he said. Come, see the place where Yahusha (Lord) lay.* (Matthew 28:6). The angel at the tomb announced the first man to pass through Wisdom's time of visitation: He is not here, for he is risen. The righteous who are held in the Father's hand shine when he visits them, and the empty tomb is the firstfruits of that day — *Messiah (Christ) the firstfruits; afterward they that are Messiah's (Christ's) at his coming* (1 Corinthians 15:23). Daniel saw the rest of them still sleeping in the dust until they awake and shine (Daniel 12:2–3). One has risen; the others rest and wait for the same visitation.$n$,$n$And in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.$n$,true),
(4891,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,8,3,8,$n$*They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:8). *He is not here: for he is risen, as he said. Come, see the place where Yahusha (Lord) lay.* (Matthew 28:6). Wisdom sees the righteous judging the nations and ruling the people under an Elohim (God) who reigns for ever — the reign John saw on the earth, where *they lived and reigned with Messiah (Christ) a thousand years* (Revelation 20:4). That reign needs risen saints, and the empty tomb is where the rising began: He is not here, for he is risen. The first to rise is the one who will seat the rest. This is not souls escaping to heaven while the earth burns; it is the dead raised to rule on the earth with their King, the way Daniel said the saints of the most High would take the kingdom.$n$,$n$They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people, and their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.$n$,true),
(4892,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,15,5,15,$n$*But the righteous live for evermore; their reward also is with Yahuah (God), and the care of them is with the Most High.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:15). *And as they went to tell his disciples, behold, Yahusha (Jesus) met them, saying, All hail. And they came and held him by the feet, and worshipped him.* (Matthew 28:9). The righteous live for evermore. The women at the tomb found out what that looks like: they held him by the feet. He had a body they could grip, flesh and bones he would show the disciples that evening (Luke 24:39), and that risen body is the firstfruits of what Wisdom promises the righteous. The righteous dead are not disembodied souls already in heaven; they are asleep, their reward kept with Yahuah (God), waiting for the day the one who rose first raises them (1 Thessalonians 4:16). The care of them is with the Most High the whole time they wait.$n$,$n$But the righteous live for evermore; their reward also is with Yahuah (God), and the care of them is with the Most High.$n$,true),
(4893,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,16,5,16,$n$*Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God): for with his right hand shall he cover them, and with his arm shall he protect them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:16). *And as they went to tell his disciples, behold, Yahusha (Jesus) met them, saying, All hail. And they came and held him by the feet, and worshipped him.* (Matthew 28:9). The women held the risen King by the feet and worshipped him. He is the first of the righteous to receive the glorious kingdom from the Father's hand, and the crown Wisdom speaks of is the one Paul expects at his appearing (2 Timothy 4:8) and the one the King promises to the faithful unto death (Revelation 2:10). The right hand that covers the righteous is the hand he will name a few verses later when he says all power is given unto him in heaven and in earth (Matthew 28:18). What the women touched that morning was the first of the resurrected righteous, crowned.$n$,$n$Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God): for with his right hand shall he cover them, and with his arm shall he protect them.$n$,true),
(4903,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,40,13,40,$n$*Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.* (2 Esdras 13:40). *Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit):* (Matthew 28:19). Set these two side by side and the Great Commission stops reading as a change of audience. Ezra was shown the ten tribes carried over the waters into another land, and that land is among the nations; the scattered house lives there, mixed in and unrecognizable, the people Hosea called not my people (Hosea 1:9). So the King who said he was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) now sends his disciples to all nations, because that is where the sheep are. The scope widened to every nation because the flock is scattered through every nation, not because the flock was replaced. We do not know those sheep by sight, so the word goes out broadly, and the ones who hear are revealed to be his (John 10:27).$n$,$n$Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.$n$,true),
(4904,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,47,13,47,$n$*The Highest shall stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through: therefore sawest you the multitude with peace.* (2 Esdras 13:47). *Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit):* (Matthew 28:19). Ezra sees the ten tribes come back through the stream the Most High stays for them; the peaceable multitude of his vision is the scattered house coming home, and it is the Highest who does the gathering. Yahusha (Jesus) sends his disciples to all nations, and these two verses explain each other: the disciples go out into the nations because that is where the tribes were carried, and the Father brings them back. Isaiah had already said he would set his hand the second time to recover the remnant of his people from the four corners of the earth (Isaiah 11:11–12); James wrote to the twelve tribes scattered abroad (James 1:1), and Peter to the strangers scattered through the provinces (1 Peter 1:1). The proclamation to all nations is that second recovery going out to find them.$n$,$n$The Highest shall stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through: therefore sawest you the multitude with peace.$n$,true),
(7835,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',9,31,9,31,$n$*For, behold, I sow my law in you, and it shall bring fruit in you, and you shall be honoured in it for ever.* (2 Esdras 9:31). *And these are they which are sown on good ground; such as hear the word, and receive it, and bring forth fruit, some thirtyfold, some sixty, and some an hundred.* (Mark 4:20). The law is spoken of here as seed: I sow my law in you, and it shall bring fruit in you. The good ground in the parable is those who hear the word, receive it, and bring forth fruit, thirty, sixty, a hundredfold. The word sown in Mark is the law sown in Ezra, and the fruit is the same fruit, a people keeping what was put in them. Ezra goes on to say that the fathers who received it and kept it not perished (2 Esdras 9:32–33), which is the other soils of the parable.$n$,$n$For, behold, I sow my law in you, and it shall bring fruit in you, and you shall be honoured in it for ever.$n$,true),
(7837,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',4,30,4,30,$n$*For the grain of evil seed has been sown in the heart of Adam from the beginning, and how much ungodliness has it brought up to this time? and how much shall it yet bring forth until the time of threshing come?* (2 Esdras 4:30). *And these are they by the way side, where the word is sown; but when they have heard, Satan cometh immediately, and taketh away the word that was sown in their hearts.* (Mark 4:15). The evil seed in the heart of Adam has been bearing ungodliness since the beginning, Uriel says, and will until the threshing. In the parable of the sower the word is sown and Satan comes at once and takes it out of the heart before it can root. The heart is the ground in both; in one the evil seed is sown into it, in the other the good seed is stolen out of it. Mark's four soils are the field Esdras was shown, and the adversary works the same ground the same way until the harvest.$n$,$n$For the grain of evil seed has been sown in the heart of Adam from the beginning, and how much ungodliness has it brought up to this time? and how much shall it yet bring forth until the time of threshing come?$n$,true),
(7849,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,9,3,9,$n$*For the blessing of the father establishes the houses of children; but the curse of the mother rooteth out foundations.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:9). *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.* (Mark 7:13). Sirach says a father's blessing establishes the houses of children and a mother's curse roots out foundations, so honour toward parents is not a courtesy; it is what a house stands on. Yahusha (Jesus) says the tradition that voided the fifth commandment made the word of Elohim (God) of none effect, and Sirach tells you what that costs: the blessing forfeited and the foundation uprooted. A tradition that teaches sons to withhold from their parents is pulling the house down on them while calling it devotion. The Messiah names the voiding; Sirach had already named the price.$n$,$n$For the blessing of the father establishes the houses of children; but the curse of the mother rooteth out foundations.$n$,true),
(7850,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,8,3,8,$n$*Honour your father and mother both in word and deed, that a blessing may come upon you from them.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:8). *And ye suffer him no more to do ought for his father or his mother;* (Mark 7:12). Mark 7:12 is the deed being forbidden: once the vow was spoken, the tradition suffered a man no more to do anything for his father or his mother. Sirach had said honour is both in word and deed, and the corban ruling kept the word, a vow formula that profited the system, and outlawed the deed. Measured by Sirach's own standard, the tradition did not merely fall short of the commandment; it subtracted half of it and called the remainder righteousness. Yahusha (Jesus) is not bringing a new standard against the elders, only the old one they had cut in two.$n$,$n$Honour your father and mother both in word and deed, that a blessing may come upon you from them.$n$,true),
(7851,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',3,7,3,7,$n$*He that fears Yahuah (God) will honour his father, and will do service to his parents, as to his masters.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:7). *For Moses said, Honour thy father and thy mother; and, Whoso curseth father or mother, let him die the death:* (Mark 7:10). Sirach binds honouring one's father to the fear of Yahuah (God): the man who fears him will serve his parents the way a servant serves his masters. Yahusha (Jesus) quotes the commandment behind that, for Moses said, honour thy father and thy mother, and then shows how the corban tradition severed what Sirach had joined, letting a man withhold from his parents what he vowed to the altar and call it devotion. A fear of Yahuah (God) that excuses a man from his parents is not the fear Sirach knew. The tradition had split the two apart; the Messiah puts Moses back on the table.$n$,$n$He that fears Yahuah (God) will honour his father, and will do service to his parents, as to his masters.$n$,true),
(7859,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,4,3,4,$n$*For though they be punished in the sight of men, yet is their hope full of immortality.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:4). *And as touching the dead, that they rise: have ye not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush Elohim (God) spake unto him, saying, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob?* (Mark 12:26). Wisdom admits the plain fact: the righteous are punished in the sight of men, and men watch them die. Then it names what the watchers cannot see — a hope full of immortality. Yahusha (Jesus) puts the same hope on the record of Moses: at the bush, generations after the patriarchs were buried, Elohim (God) still called himself the Elohim (God) of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. Their death was in the sight of men; their life is in his sight. The resurrection is the shape that hope takes when it is kept.$n$,$n$For though they be punished in the sight of men, yet is their hope full of immortality.$n$,true),
(7860,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,1,3,1,$n$*But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1). *He is not the Elohim (God) of the dead, but the Elohim (God) of the living: ye therefore do greatly err.* (Mark 12:27). The Sadducees denied the resurrection, and Yahusha (Jesus) answered them from the bush: Elohim (God) named himself the Elohim (God) of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob long after they were buried, and he is not the Elohim (God) of the dead. Wisdom explains how the fathers can be counted living while they lie in the ground — their souls are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and no torment touches them there. They rest, held against the day he raises them. To deny that is to err greatly, as he told the Sadducees to their faces.$n$,$n$But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.$n$,true),
(7861,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,29,5,29,$n$*And they which did gainsay your promises, and believed not your covenants, have trodden them down.* (2 Esdras 5:29). *What shall therefore the lord of the vineyard do? he will come and destroy the husbandmen, and will give the vineyard unto others.* (Mark 12:9). In Ezra's prayer the ones who trod the people down are the ones who gainsaid the promises and did not believe the covenants, the many nations the one people had been given over to (2 Esdras 5:28). In the parable the same unbelief shows up inside the hedge: the husbandmen entrusted with the vineyard beat the servants and kill the heir, acting as though the owner's word about returning were empty. Ezra asks why the Most High lets his people be trodden down; the parable answers with what the lord of the vineyard will do when he comes. Those who tread down what the covenants cover, whether from outside the hedge or from within it, meet the same owner.$n$,$n$And they which did gainsay your promises, and believed not your covenants, have trodden them down.$n$,true),
(7862,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,27,5,27,$n$*And among all the multitudes of people you have gotten you one people: and to this people, whom you lovedst, you gavest a law that is approved of all.* (2 Esdras 5:27). *What shall therefore the lord of the vineyard do? he will come and destroy the husbandmen, and will give the vineyard unto others.* (Mark 12:9). Ezra names what the vineyard is before the parable ever has to explain it: one people gotten out of all the multitudes, loved, and given a law. The lord of the vineyard in the parable owns it because he chose it; the husbandmen only hold it. When they beat his servants and kill his heir, what he takes from them is the stewardship, and that is what he gives to others. The one people the Most High loved and gave his law to is still his vineyard; it was never the keepers' to lose.$n$,$n$And among all the multitudes of people you have gotten you one people: and to this people, whom you lovedst, you gavest a law that is approved of all.$n$,true),
(7863,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,23,5,23,$n$*And said, O Yahuah (God) that bearest rule, of every wood of the earth, and of all the trees thereof, you have chosen you one only vine:* (2 Esdras 5:23). *And he began to speak unto them by parables. A certain man planted a vineyard, and set an hedge about it, and digged a place for the winefat, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country.* (Mark 12:1). The vineyard of the parable is not a figure Yahusha (Jesus) invents on the spot; Ezra had already prayed it back to the Most High, who out of all the trees of the earth chose one only vine. In the parable that chosen vine is hedged, given a winefat and a tower, and let out to husbandmen while the owner goes into a far country. Everything the husbandmen later do, they do to a vineyard that was never theirs. The one vine remains the owner's choice; the keepers were only ever entrusted with it.$n$,$n$And said, O Yahuah (God) that bearest rule, of every wood of the earth, and of all the trees thereof, you have chosen you one only vine:$n$,true),
(7864,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,7,31,7,$n$*It is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it, and every fool shall be taken with it.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:7). *It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of Elohim (God).* (Mark 10:25). Sirach says gold is a stumblingblock to those who sacrifice to it, and every fool is taken by it, taken as a bird is taken in a snare. Yahusha (Jesus) says the rich man passes into the kingdom about as easily as a camel through a needle's eye. The two sayings are one picture: a man held by what he sacrifices to cannot get through the narrow door, because the thing holding him will not fit. It is not the gold that stops at the needle's eye; it is the man who will not let go of it.$n$,$n$It is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it, and every fool shall be taken with it.$n$,true),
(7865,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,6,31,6,$n$*Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:6). *And the disciples were astonished at his words. But Yahusha (Jesus) answereth again, and saith unto them, Children, how hard is it for them that trust in riches to enter into the kingdom of Elohim (God)!* (Mark 10:24). Sirach does not say gold is evil; he says it has ruined many, and that their destruction was standing right beside them while they held it. Yahusha (Jesus) puts his finger on the same spot: not the riches, but the trusting in them. The hand that closes around gold is the hand that cannot take hold of the kingdom, and the disciples were astonished because the whole world around them measured blessing by that very gold.$n$,$n$Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present.$n$,true),
(7866,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,5,31,5,$n$*He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:5). *And Yahusha (Jesus) looked round about, and saith unto his disciples, How hardly shall they that have riches enter into the kingdom of Elohim (God)!* (Mark 10:23). Sirach says the man who loves gold will not be justified. Yahusha (Jesus) looks round at his disciples after the rich young man walks away and says how hard it is for those who have riches to enter the kingdom of Elohim (God). It is the same man in both verses: the one Sirach says will not be justified is the one the King says hardly enters. Not because riches are a sin, but because gold tends to own the man who has it, and a man owned by gold does not come in.$n$,$n$He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof.$n$,true),
(7867,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,8,31,8,$n$*Blessed is the rich that is found without blemish, and has not gone after gold.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:8). *Then Yahusha (Jesus) beholding him loved him, and said unto him, One thing thou lackest: go thy way, sell whatsoever thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come, take up the cross, and follow me.* (Mark 10:21). Sirach calls the rich man blessed who is found without blemish and has not gone after gold, and the next verse asks, who is he? as if such a man were rare enough to go looking for. Yahusha (Jesus) looked at a rich young man, loved him, and put Sirach's question to him in the flesh: sell what you have, give to the poor, and come. The man went away grieved, because his great possessions had him. The blessing Sirach names was offered to him on the road and he could not take it, because he had gone after gold and the gold had gone after him.$n$,$n$Blessed is the rich that is found without blemish, and has not gone after gold.$n$,true),
(7871,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',7,36,7,36,$n$*For our brothers, who now have suffered a short pain, are dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life: but you, through the judgment of Yahuah (God), shall receive just punishment for your pride.* (2 Maccabees 7:36). *And he said unto them, This is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many.* (Mark 14:24). Dying under Antiochus, the youngest brother names where his brothers now lie: dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life. They were faithful to the covenant unto death, and the covenant holds them. At the table Yahusha (Jesus) lifts the cup and calls it his blood of the new covenant, shed for many. The martyrs of 2 Maccabees 7 died trusting a covenant of everlasting life whose promise had not yet been made good; Mark 14:24 is the King's own blood put to that same covenant. The many it is shed for include the seven who went before it, and their everlasting life waits on the resurrection the King's own rising opened.$n$,$n$For our brothers, who now have suffered a short pain, are dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life: but you, through the judgment of Yahuah (God), shall receive just punishment for your pride.$n$,true),
(7872,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',2,50,2,50,$n$*Now therefore, my sons, be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.* (1 Maccabees 2:50). *And he said unto them, This is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many.* (Mark 14:24). A dying father tells his sons to give their lives for the covenant of their fathers, and the sons do it, one after another, through the rest of the book. Mark records the King doing the same thing with his own blood, the blood of the new covenant, shed for many. The many he died for are the people whose fathers cut that covenant at Sinai and whose sons died for it under Antiochus. Mattathias asked his sons for what Yahusha (Jesus) gave, and the covenant both were given for is the same covenant, now written on the heart.$n$,$n$Now therefore, my sons, be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.$n$,true),
(7880,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.* (Mark 13:27). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and given back their inheritance as from the beginning. Yahusha (Jesus) says the Son of Adam will send his angels and gather his elect from the four winds, from one end of the earth to the other. The four winds are where the tribes were scattered; the elect gathered out of them are the tribes Sirach prayed home. The prayer names who; the gospel names how and when.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(7881,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',5,5,5,5,$n$*Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and look about toward the east, and behold your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the remembrance of Yahuah (God).* (Baruch 5:5). *And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.* (Mark 13:27). Baruch 4:37 brought the children from east to west; here they come from west to east. Between the two verses the gathering comes from every direction. Mark 13:27 says it the same way: the Son of Adam sends his angels to gather his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth. Jerusalem's children in Baruch and the elect in Mark are one people, the scattered seed, brought home by the word of the Holy One and the hand of the Son of Adam at the end of the age. Jerusalem is told to stand on high and watch, because the homecoming is something she will see.$n$,$n$Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and look about toward the east, and behold your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the remembrance of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(7883,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,47,13,47,$n$*The Highest shall stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through: therefore sawest you the multitude with peace.* (2 Esdras 13:47). *And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.* (Mark 13:27). The ten tribes did not find their own way home in Ezra's vision. The Highest stayed the springs of the stream for them, as he had once held the waters when they went out, and the multitude came through in peace. Mark 13:27 is the same homecoming seen from the gospel side: when the Son of Adam comes, he sends his angels to gather his elect from the four winds, from one end of the earth to the other. The gathering is his act in both books. The scattered seed is brought back by the hand of the Most High, not by its own contriving, and the elect of Mark 13 are the people Ezra watched cross the stream.$n$,$n$The Highest shall stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through: therefore sawest you the multitude with peace.$n$,true),
(7885,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,39,13,39,$n$*And whereas you sawest that he gathered another peaceable multitude to him; Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.* (2 Esdras 13:39–40). *And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.* (Mark 13:27). The man who comes up from the sea gathers a peaceable multitude to himself, and Ezra is told in the next breath who they are: the ten tribes Salmanasar of Assyria carried away captive over the waters into another land. When Yahusha (Jesus) says the angels will gather his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth, that is the multitude he means. The elect are not a people assembled out of whoever chose to join; they are the scattered seed of Yashar'el (Israel) brought home from where they were carried. Ezra puts a name on the ones the four winds give back.$n$,$n$And whereas you sawest that he gathered another peaceable multitude to him;$n$,true),
(7888,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *He hath holpen his servant Yashar'el (Israel), in remembrance of his mercy;* (Luke 1:54). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and inherited as from the beginning. Mary, carrying the child, sings that he has helped his servant Yashar'el (Israel) in remembrance of his mercy. The help she sings is the beginning of the answer to Sirach's prayer: mercy remembered toward the whole servant Yashar'el (Israel), all the tribes, not a figure of speech for some other people. The gathering was being set in motion in her womb.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(7889,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,12,36,12,$n$*O Yahuah (God), have mercy upon the people that is called by your name, and upon Yashar'el (Israel), whom you have named your firstborn.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:12). *He hath holpen his servant Yashar'el (Israel), in remembrance of his mercy;* (Luke 1:54). Sirach asks mercy for the people called by the name of Yahuah (God), for Yashar'el (Israel) his firstborn. Mary sings that he has helped his servant Yashar'el (Israel) in remembrance of his mercy. The mercy prayed for and the mercy remembered fall on the same people, the firstborn son, the servant, and both are tied to the gathering: Sirach's verse before asks for all the tribes of Jacob, and Mary's next lines name the oath sworn to Abraham. The firstborn is remembered, not replaced.$n$,$n$O Yahuah (God), have mercy upon the people that is called by your name, and upon Yashar'el (Israel), whom you have named your firstborn.$n$,true),
(7890,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',4,36,4,36,$n$*O Jerusalem, look about you toward the east, and behold the joy that comes to you from Yahuah (God).* (Baruch 4:36). *As he spake to our fathers, to Abraham, and to his seed for ever.* (Luke 1:55). Jerusalem is told to turn east, the direction her children were carried, and watch the joy coming back to her from Yahuah (God); the next verse says what the joy is, her sons gathered from east to west. Mary ends her song on the ground that joy stands on: Yahuah (God) spoke to the fathers, to Abraham and to his seed for ever. The gathering is how the oath to Abraham is kept. Baruch's song and Mary's are singing one thing, a seed promised to the fathers, scattered, and now being brought home because he remembers what he said.$n$,$n$O Jerusalem, look about you toward the east, and behold the joy that comes to you from Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(7891,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',4,37,4,37,$n$*Lo, your sons come, whom you sentest away, they come gathered together from the east to the west by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the glory of Yahuah (God).* (Baruch 4:37). *As he spake to our fathers, to Abraham, and to his seed for ever.* (Luke 1:55). Here is what Jerusalem sees when she looks east: her sons, the ones she sent away, coming gathered from east to west by the word of the Holy One. Mary's song ends with the seed those sons belong to; Yahuah (God) spoke to our fathers, to Abraham and to his seed for ever. The sons gathered in Baruch are Abraham's seed, scattered by exile and brought back because the oath was for ever. The Magnificat names the promise; Baruch names its fulfillment walking home.$n$,$n$Lo, your sons come, whom you sentest away, they come gathered together from the east to the west by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the glory of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(7895,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',5,7,5,7,$n$*For Yahuah (God) has appointed that every high hill, and banks of long continuance, should be cast down, and valleys filled up, to make even the ground, that Yashar'el (Israel) may go safely in the glory of Yahuah (God),* (Baruch 5:7). *Every valley shall be filled, and every mountain and hill shall be brought low; and the crooked shall be made straight, and the rough ways shall be made smooth;* (Luke 3:5). Baruch gives the reason the hills come down and the valleys fill: so that Yashar'el (Israel) may walk home safely in the glory of Yahuah (God). The leveled road is a road for the gathering. John the Baptist preached from Isaiah 40, every valley filled, every mountain brought low, the rough ways made smooth, and Luke tells us who was walking that road: the people coming out to him to be baptized, the house of Yashar'el (Israel) being called back. Baruch read Isaiah's highway as the road the scattered seed comes home on; John stood at the head of that road and opened it.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) has appointed that every high hill, and banks of long continuance, should be cast down, and valleys filled up, to make even the ground, that Yashar'el (Israel) may go safely in the glory of Yahuah (God),$n$,true),
(7896,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',5,9,5,9,$n$*For Yahuah (God) shall lead Yashar'el (Israel) with joy in the light of his glory with the mercy and righteousness that comes from him.* (Baruch 5:9). *And all flesh shall see the salvation of Elohim (God).* (Luke 3:6). Baruch ends his gathering song with Yahuah (God) himself leading Yashar'el (Israel) home with joy, in the light of his glory, in mercy and righteousness. Luke ends his quotation of Isaiah 40 with all flesh seeing the salvation of Elohim (God). The two endings belong together: the road is leveled so Yashar'el (Israel) can walk it, and when Yahuah (God) leads them home in his glory the whole earth watches. The salvation all flesh sees is the gathering of the scattered seed; the nations see it done, and Yashar'el (Israel) walks it.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) shall lead Yashar'el (Israel) with joy in the light of his glory with the mercy and righteousness that comes from him.$n$,true),
(7909,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',9,33,9,33,$n$*Yet they that received it perished, because they kept not the thing that was sown in them.* (2 Esdras 9:33). *But that on the good ground are they, which in an honest and good heart, having heard the word, keep it, and bring forth fruit with patience.* (Luke 8:15). Ezra says why the fathers perished: they received what was sown and did not keep it. The good ground in the parable is the opposite in every word, an honest and good heart that hears the word, keeps it, and brings forth fruit with patience. Receiving is not the dividing line; keeping is. The fruit is the keeping, and the perishing is the not keeping.$n$,$n$Yet they that received it perished, because they kept not the thing that was sown in them.$n$,true),
(7910,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,41,8,41,$n$*For as the husbandman sows much seed upon the ground, and planteth many trees, and yet the thing that is sown good in his season comes not up, neither does all that is planted take root: even so is it of them that are sown in the world; they shall not all be saved.* (2 Esdras 8:41). *They on the rock are they, which, when they hear, receive the word with joy; and these have no root, which for a while believe, and in time of temptation fall away.* (Luke 8:13). Ezra is told the husbandman's experience: much seed sown, many trees planted, and not all of it comes up or takes root. Yahusha (Jesus) names the rocky ground the same way: hearers who receive the word with joy, have no root, believe for a while, and fall away when tried. Springing up is not the same as taking root. Ezra's not all saved and the parable's fall away are the same plant dying for the same reason.$n$,$n$For as the husbandman sows much seed upon the ground, and planteth many trees, and yet the thing that is sown good in his season comes not up, neither does all that is planted take root: even so is it of them that are sown in the world; they shall not all be saved.$n$,true),
(7911,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',9,31,9,31,$n$*For, behold, I sow my law in you, and it shall bring fruit in you, and you shall be honoured in it for ever.* (2 Esdras 9:31). *Now the parable is this: The seed is the word of Elohim (God).* (Luke 8:11). When Yahusha (Jesus) explains the parable, he says the seed is the word of Elohim (God). Ezra had already heard the Most High say what that seed is: I sow my law in you. The law is the word sown, and it is sown into the hearer to bring fruit there, not handed to him as a weight to carry. There is no gap between the word of Elohim (God) in the gospel and the law in Ezra; they are one seed.$n$,$n$For, behold, I sow my law in you, and it shall bring fruit in you, and you shall be honoured in it for ever.$n$,true),
(7912,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,18,22,18,$n$*Pales set on an high place will never stand against the wind: so a fearful heart in the imagination of a fool cannot stand against any fear.* (Ecclesiasticus 22:18). *But he that heareth, and doeth not, is like a man that without a foundation built an house upon the earth; against which the stream did beat vehemently, and immediately it fell; and the ruin of that house was great.* (Luke 6:49). Sirach says pales set on a high place never stand against the wind, and the fool's fearful heart stands against nothing. Luke names the fool: he heard and did not do, and built his house on the earth with no foundation, and when the stream beat on it the house fell at once and the ruin was great. Hearing without doing is the house without a foundation. The wind finds it, and there is nothing underneath to hold.$n$,$n$Pales set on an high place will never stand against the wind: so a fearful heart in the imagination of a fool cannot stand against any fear.$n$,true),
(7913,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',22,16,22,16,$n$*As timber girt and bound together in a building cannot be loosed with shaking: so the heart that is stablished by advised counsel shall fear at no time.* (Ecclesiasticus 22:16). *He is like a man which built an house, and digged deep, and laid the foundation on a rock: and when the flood arose, the stream beat vehemently upon that house, and could not shake it: for it was founded upon a rock.* (Luke 6:48). Sirach's picture is timber bound together in a building that cannot be loosed with shaking; the heart established by counsel is like that. Luke adds a detail Matthew does not: the wise builder dug deep and laid his foundation on a rock, and when the flood beat on the house it could not shake it. The digging deep is the doing — the hearer who does the sayings is driving his foundation down to rock. That is how a heart is established so that it fears at no time.$n$,$n$As timber girt and bound together in a building cannot be loosed with shaking: so the heart that is stablished by advised counsel shall fear at no time.$n$,true),
(7915,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,6,27,6,$n$*The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:6). *For a good tree bringeth not forth corrupt fruit; neither doth a corrupt tree bring forth good fruit.* (Luke 6:43). Sirach says the fruit tells whether the tree was dressed and the speech tells what the heart has conceived. Luke's record of Yahusha (Jesus) holds both together: a good tree does not bear corrupt fruit, a corrupt tree does not bear good, and two verses later, of the abundance of the heart his mouth speaks. The fruit is the tree's utterance and the mouth is the heart's. The wise had named the test long before; the King made it the way his disciples would know one another and know a teacher.$n$,$n$The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed; so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.$n$,true),
(7918,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',21,15,21,15,$n$*If a skilful man hear a wise word, he will commend it, and add to it: but as soon as one of no understanding heareth it, it displeaseth him, and he casteth it behind his back.* (Ecclesiasticus 21:15). *Woe unto you, lawyers! for ye have taken away the key of knowledge: ye entered not in yourselves, and them that were entering in ye hindered.* (Luke 11:52). Sirach describes two hearers: the skilful man hears a wise word and commends it and adds to it, while the man of no understanding is displeased by it and throws it behind his back. The lawyers in Luke are the second man — they heard the Word, refused it, and then took the key away so that those entering were hindered too. Casting the word behind your back is bad enough; shutting the door from the outside against others is what draws the woe. The system that does this is what Yahusha (Jesus) names; the people kept from the door are the ones he came for.$n$,$n$If a skilful man hear a wise word, he will commend it, and add to it: but as soon as one of no understanding heareth it, it displeaseth him, and he casteth it behind his back.$n$,true),
(7919,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',21,14,21,14,$n$*The inner parts of a fool are like a broken vessel, and he will hold no knowledge as long as he lives.* (Ecclesiasticus 21:14). *Woe unto you, lawyers! for ye have taken away the key of knowledge: ye entered not in yourselves, and them that were entering in ye hindered.* (Luke 11:52). Sirach pictures the fool's inward parts as a broken vessel: pour knowledge in and it runs out, so he holds none his whole life. Yahusha (Jesus) says the lawyers had taken away the key of knowledge — they had not entered themselves and were hindering those who were entering. A man who holds no knowledge cannot open the door for anyone else. The key that was taken away is the Torah read plainly, and the fences the office built around it are what kept the people outside; the woe is on the office that did it, not on the people it shut out.$n$,$n$The inner parts of a fool are like a broken vessel, and he will hold no knowledge as long as he lives.$n$,true),
(7920,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',10,12,10,12,$n$*The beginning of pride is when one departs from Yahuah (God), and his heart is turned away from his Maker.* (Ecclesiasticus 10:12). *And he said, Woe unto you also, ye lawyers! for ye lade men with burdens grievous to be borne, and ye yourselves touch not the burdens with one of your fingers.* (Luke 11:46). Sirach locates the beginning of pride in a heart turned away from its Maker. The lawyers Yahusha (Jesus) rebukes have built a weight of rules on top of the Torah and will not lift a finger under it themselves; the hand that adds to the commandment has already turned from the one who gave it. Voiding the commandment by addition is as much a departure as voiding it by subtraction, and both wear the robe of the law while they do it.$n$,$n$The beginning of pride is when one departs from Yahuah (God), and his heart is turned away from his Maker.$n$,true),
(7921,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',10,13,10,13,$n$*For pride is the beginning of sin, and he that has it shall pour out abomination: and therefore Yahuah (God) brought upon them strange calamities, and overthrew them utterly.* (Ecclesiasticus 10:13). *Woe unto you, Pharisees! for ye love the uppermost seats in the synagogues, and greetings in the markets.* (Luke 11:43). Sirach traces sin to pride, and the overthrow of nations to the abomination pride pours out. The woe Yahusha (Jesus) pronounces falls on the love of the uppermost seats and the greetings in the markets — pride in a religious dress. The library had named the disease centuries before the Pharisee's table; the King names it at the table. The woe is on the man who loves the seat; the love of the uppermost place is the pride Sirach said sin begins in.$n$,$n$For pride is the beginning of sin, and he that has it shall pour out abomination: and therefore Yahuah (God) brought upon them strange calamities, and overthrew them utterly.$n$,true),
(7922,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,24,2,24,$n$*Nevertheless through envy of the devil came death into the world: and they that do hold of his side do find it.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:24). *And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.* (Luke 10:18). The one whose envy let death into the world is the one Yahusha (Jesus) saw fall like lightning. When the seventy came back rejoicing that the devils were subject to them, he told them what they had been watching: the enemy who brought death in was being thrown down, and in the next verse he gives them power to tread on serpents and scorpions and over all the power of the enemy. Wisdom names the door death came through; Luke shows the one who opened it losing his footing.$n$,$n$Nevertheless through envy of the devil came death into the world: and they that do hold of his side do find it.$n$,true),
(7925,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',2,31,2,31,$n$*Remember your children that sleep, for I shall bring them out of the sides of the earth, and shew mercy to them: for I am merciful, says Yahuah (God) Almighty.* (2 Esdras 2:31). *For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found. And they began to be merry.* (Luke 15:24). Yahuah (God) tells the mother to remember her children that sleep, because he will bring them out of the sides of the earth and show them mercy. The father in the parable says of his returned son, this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found. The son came out of a far country, and the children in 2 Esdras come out of the sides of the earth, and in both the mercy belongs to the father and the merriment follows the return. The ones who sleep are brought out because he is merciful; that is the resurrection hope, and the prodigal's homecoming is a picture of it, the lost child of the house gathered home and counted alive.$n$,$n$Remember your children that sleep, for I shall bring them out of the sides of the earth, and shew mercy to them: for I am merciful, says Yahuah (God) Almighty.$n$,true),
(7926,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,40,13,40,$n$*Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.* (2 Esdras 13:40). *And when he cometh home, he calleth together his friends and neighbours, saying unto them, Rejoice with me; for I have found my sheep which was lost.* (Luke 15:6). The sheep in the parable was lost, and the shepherd went after it until he found it, then called his neighbours to rejoice. Ezra was told who the lost are: the ten tribes carried over the waters into another land, where they dwelt until the latter time, *and now when they shall begin to come* (2 Esdras 13:46). The joy Yahusha (Jesus) describes is the joy of that homecoming. A lost sheep is still the shepherd's sheep the whole time it is lost; the ten tribes were still the house of Yashar'el (Israel) in the far country, and the finding is the gathering the prophets promised.$n$,$n$Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land in the time of Osea the king, whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive, and he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.$n$,true),
(7927,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,13,18,13,$n$*The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:13). *And when he hath found it, he layeth it on his shoulders, rejoicing.* (Luke 15:5). Sirach says the mercy of Yahuah (God) does what a shepherd does: it reproves, it nurtures, it teaches, and it brings the flock back again. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the same thing as a story — one sheep lost, the shepherd going after it until he finds it, and the sheep carried home on his shoulders with rejoicing. The bringing again is the gathering of the lost, and the mercy that gathers is wider than the mercy of man, which reaches only as far as his neighbour.$n$,$n$The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.$n$,true),
(7928,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *And they shall come from the east, and from the west, and from the north, and from the south, and shall sit down in the kingdom of Elohim (God).* (Luke 13:29). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and inherited as from the beginning. Yahusha (Jesus) says they will come from east, west, north and south and sit down in the kingdom of Elohim (God). Four directions is where the tribes went; four directions is where they come back from. The ones sitting down are the tribes Sirach prayed for, the firstborn his next verse names, not a substitute people set at their table.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(7929,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',5,5,5,5,$n$*Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and look about toward the east, and behold your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the remembrance of Yahuah (God). For they departed from you on foot, and were led away of their enemies: but Yahuah (God) brings them to you exalted with glory, as children of the kingdom.* (Baruch 5:5–6). *And they shall come from the east, and from the west, and from the north, and from the south, and shall sit down in the kingdom of Elohim (God).* (Luke 13:29). Jerusalem is told to stand up high and watch her children come home from the west to the east, and the next verse says who they are: the ones who left her on foot, led away by their enemies, now brought back by Yahuah (God) exalted with glory as children of the kingdom. Yahusha (Jesus) says they shall come from the east and the west and the north and the south and sit down in the kingdom of Elohim (God). The ones who come from every quarter in Luke 13 are the same children of the kingdom Baruch watches return, not strangers streaming in, but Jerusalem's own sons who were led away and are being brought home.$n$,$n$Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and look about toward the east, and behold your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the remembrance of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(7933,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',29,11,29,11,$n$*Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High, and it shall bring you more profit than gold.* (Ecclesiasticus 29:11). *Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.* (Luke 12:33). Sirach says lay up your treasure the way the commandments of the Most High direct, and it will profit you more than gold; two verses earlier he said help the poor for the commandment's sake. Yahusha (Jesus) says sell, give alms, and provide yourselves bags that do not wear out, a treasure in the heavens that does not fail. The treasure of the man who opens his hand because the commandment says so is laid up where gold cannot go and thieves cannot follow. That is why it outlasts gold.$n$,$n$Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High, and it shall bring you more profit than gold.$n$,true),
(7934,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,10,4,10,$n$*Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10). *Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.* (Luke 12:33). Tobit says alms deliver from death and keep the giver out of darkness; the open hand protects the one who opens it. Yahusha (Jesus) says to sell, give alms, and have a treasure in the heavens that does not fail, where no thief approaches and no moth corrupts. Both are saying that what is given away is the one thing that cannot be taken away. The library names the deliverance; the gospel names the storehouse no thief can reach.$n$,$n$Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.$n$,true),
(7935,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,9,4,9,$n$*For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* (Tobit 4:9). *Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.* (Luke 12:33). Tobit's charge to give alms comes with a reason: the giver is laying up a good treasure against the day of necessity. Yahusha (Jesus) says the same thing with a picture — sell, give alms, and you have bags that do not wear out, a treasure in the heavens that does not fail. What is given now is what is stored for then. The library names the treasure the open hand lays up; the gospel names where it is kept and why no thief or moth can reach it.$n$,$n$For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.$n$,true),
(7936,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',11,18,11,18,$n$*There is that waxeth rich by his wariness and pinching, and this is the portion of his reward:* (Ecclesiasticus 11:18). *But Elohim (God) said unto him, Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee: then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided?* (Luke 12:20). Sirach describes the man who grows rich by wariness and pinching and says, dryly, that this is the portion of his reward — the heap is all he gets. Yahusha (Jesus) gives the same man his night: the soul is required, and the goods pass to someone else. Hoarding is not a sin with no consequence. The consequence is that the hoard was the whole reward.$n$,$n$There is that waxeth rich by his wariness and pinching, and this is the portion of his reward:$n$,true),
(7937,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',11,19,11,19,$n$*Whereas he says, I have found rest, and now will eat continually of my goods; and yet he knoweth not what time shall come upon him, and that he must leave those things to others, and die.* (Ecclesiasticus 11:19). *And I will say to my soul, Soul, thou hast much goods laid up for many years; take thine ease, eat, drink, and be merry.* (Luke 12:19). Sirach writes the rich fool's speech before the parable: I have found rest, I will eat continually of my goods. Yahusha (Jesus) puts nearly the same words in his mouth — take thine ease, eat, drink, and be merry. Sirach had also named the two things the man never reckons with: he does not know what time is coming on him, and he must leave it all to others and die. The barns come down the same night.$n$,$n$Whereas he says, I have found rest, and now will eat continually of my goods; and yet he knoweth not what time shall come upon him, and that he must leave those things to others, and die.$n$,true),
(7939,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,12,36,12,$n$*O Yahuah (God), have mercy upon the people that is called by your name, and upon Yashar'el (Israel), whom you have named your firstborn.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:12). *For the Son of Adam is come to seek and to save that which was lost.* (Luke 19:10). Sirach asks mercy for Yashar'el (Israel), the firstborn called by the name of Yahuah (God). Yahusha (Jesus) says the Son of Adam came to seek and to save that which was lost. The firstborn had been scattered until he was lost, Hosea's not my people, and the mercy Sirach prayed for arrived as a Shepherd out looking for him. The one sought is the firstborn; the seeking is the mercy.$n$,$n$O Yahuah (God), have mercy upon the people that is called by your name, and upon Yashar'el (Israel), whom you have named your firstborn.$n$,true),
(7940,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *For the Son of Adam is come to seek and to save that which was lost.* (Luke 19:10). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and inherited as from the beginning. Yahusha (Jesus) says the Son of Adam came to seek and to save that which was lost. What was lost was the house, the tribes scattered until they forgot who they were, and seeking them is the gathering Sirach prayed for. He said it in the house of Zacchaeus, a son of Abraham his neighbours had written off, which is what a found tribe looks like one man at a time.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(7945,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,32,7,32,$n$*And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.* (2 Esdras 7:32). *And he said unto him, If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead.* (Luke 16:31). Ezra names where the dead wait: asleep in the earth, in the dust, in secret places that hold the souls committed to them until the day they are delivered. When Abraham tells the rich man that his brothers would not be persuaded even if one rose from the dead, he is speaking from inside that same expectation: the dead are held, and one coming back from among them is a thing that could happen. What the brothers have now is Moses and the prophets. If those are not heard, a voice come back from the secret places will not be heard either.$n$,$n$And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.$n$,true),
(7949,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,7,31,7,$n$*It is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it, and every fool shall be taken with it.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:7). *No servant can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon.* (Luke 16:13). Sirach calls gold a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it. He is describing worship, an offering brought and a devotion given, and he says that devotion takes its devotee. Yahusha (Jesus) names the same thing when he calls mammon a master. To serve mammon is to sacrifice to the stumblingblock; the servant does not own it, it owns him, and Sirach had already said where that ends.$n$,$n$It is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it, and every fool shall be taken with it.$n$,true),
(7950,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,6,31,6,$n$*Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:6). *No servant can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon.* (Luke 16:13). Sirach had already watched gold ruin many before Yahusha (Jesus) said no servant can serve two masters. The ruin Sirach names is what happens to the man who takes mammon for a master; it does not share him, it destroys him, and the destruction is already present while he thinks he is being served. The steward in the parable just before this verse was called wise because he treated money as a thing that perishes and used it, instead of bowing to it.$n$,$n$Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present.$n$,true),
(7951,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,5,31,5,$n$*He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:5). *No servant can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon.* (Luke 16:13). Sirach says the man who loves gold will not be justified. Luke sets the King's verdict at the end of the parable of the unjust steward, a story about using mammon shrewdly before the books are called, and then closes it: you cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon. The divided heart that holds to gold has chosen its master, and Sirach already said where that leaves it. The setting is different from the Sermon on the Mount, but the verdict is the same, and it was the wisdom of Yashar'el (Israel) before the King spoke it.$n$,$n$He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof.$n$,true),
(7952,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',5,1,5,1,$n$*Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.* (Wisdom of Solomon 5:1). *Ought not Messiah (Christ) to have suffered these things, and to enter into his glory?* (Luke 24:26). On the road to Emmaus the King asks the two disciples whether the Messiah (Christ) ought not to have suffered these things and then enter his glory. Wisdom had already written the glory half: the righteous man, condemned in chapter 2 to a shameful death, now standing in great boldness before the faces of the men who did it. Suffering first, then the standing, is the shape he then shows them in Moses and all the prophets, and the Hebrew library had the same shape in Solomon's hand long before Luke wrote it down.$n$,$n$Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.$n$,true),
(7953,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,20,2,20,$n$*Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:20). *Ought not Messiah (Christ) to have suffered these things, and to enter into his glory?* (Luke 24:26). On the road to Emmaus Yahusha (Jesus) asked the slow of heart why they were surprised. Ought not Messiah (Christ) to have suffered? The suffering was written — in Moses, in the prophets, and here in Wisdom, where the just man who calls Yahuah (God) his father is sentenced to a shameful death by the men who lay in wait for him. The disciples had read the glory and skipped the suffering. The whole library had both halves on the page, and the King opened their eyes to read them together.$n$,$n$Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.$n$,true),
(7960,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,24,5,24,$n$*And of all lands of the whole world you have chosen you one pit: and of all the flowers thereof one lily:* (2 Esdras 5:24). *He shall come and destroy these husbandmen, and shall give the vineyard to others. And when they heard it, they said, Elohim (God) forbid.* (Luke 20:16). Ezra's prayer counts the Most High's choices one by one: one vine, one pit, one lily, one river, one city, one dove, one sheep, and at last one people (2 Esdras 5:23–27). When the parable ends with the vineyard given to others, those who heard it cried out against it, as though the chosen vine itself were being handed to a different people. But it is the husbandmen who are destroyed, not the vineyard. What passes to others is the keeping of the one vine; the vine the Most High chose out of all the lands of the whole world remains the one he chose.$n$,$n$And of all lands of the whole world you have chosen you one pit: and of all the flowers thereof one lily:$n$,true),
(7961,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,27,5,27,$n$*And among all the multitudes of people you have gotten you one people: and to this people, whom you lovedst, you gavest a law that is approved of all.* (2 Esdras 5:27). *Then began he to speak to the people this parable; A certain man planted a vineyard, and let it forth to husbandmen, and went into a far country for a long time.* (Luke 20:9). The vineyard a certain man plants and lets out is the one people Ezra names, loved out of all the multitudes and given a law that is approved of all. The fruit the owner later sends his servants to collect is the fruit that law was given to bring, a people keeping what they were given. The husbandmen hold the vineyard while refusing its fruit; that is a leadership that keeps the title to the people while setting aside the law the people were planted under. The prayer and the parable agree on what the vineyard is and what it was planted for.$n$,$n$And among all the multitudes of people you have gotten you one people: and to this people, whom you lovedst, you gavest a law that is approved of all.$n$,true),
(7980,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',4,36,4,36,$n$*Then said Judas and his brothers, Behold, our enemies are discomfited: let us go up to cleanse and dedicate the sanctuary.* (1 Maccabees 4:36). *And said unto them that sold doves, Take these things hence; make not my Father’s house an house of merchandise.* (John 2:16). Judas and his brothers, with the enemy beaten back, go up to cleanse and dedicate the sanctuary the heathen had defiled. Generations later Yahusha (Jesus) walks into the same house and drives out the sellers, saying make not my Father's house an house of merchandise. The Maccabees cleansed what Antiochus had polluted with his idol; the King cleansed what the priests had polluted with their market. It is the same motion, the house of Yahuah (God) purged of what does not belong in it, and the second time it is done by the Son whose Father's house it is.$n$,$n$Then said Judas and his brothers, Behold, our enemies are discomfited: let us go up to cleanse and dedicate the sanctuary.$n$,true),
(7983,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,21,24,21,$n$*They that eat me shall yet be hungry, and they that drink me shall yet be thirsty.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:21). *But whosoever drinketh of the water that I shall give him shall never thirst; but the water that I shall give him shall be in him a well of water springing up into everlasting life.* (John 4:14). Wisdom tells the truth about herself: the one who drinks of her will be thirsty again, and the student goes back to the well as long as he lives. The woman at the well was told of water that would end that going back, because the well itself would be set inside the one who drank it. Yahusha (Jesus) is not the abolition of the wisdom that left the drinker thirsty; he is what that wisdom was drawing the thirsty toward, the Word the Torah kept sending a man back to, now a spring in him that does not run dry.$n$,$n$They that eat me shall yet be hungry, and they that drink me shall yet be thirsty.$n$,true),
(7985,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',3,37,3,37,$n$*Afterward did he shew himself upon earth, and conversed with men.* (Baruch 3:37). *And no man hath ascended up to heaven, but he that came down from heaven, even the Son of Adam which is in heaven.* (John 3:13). Baruch closes his search for wisdom with a sentence most Bibles no longer carry: afterward he shewed himself upon earth, and conversed with men. The one who alone knows the way of knowledge came down and walked among men. John 3:13 is the same event from the inside; the Son of Adam who came down from heaven is the only one who was ever up there. Baruch said the descent would come; Nicodemus was sitting across from it. The wisdom no man could fetch down from the clouds was in the room, talking with him.$n$,$n$Afterward did he shew himself upon earth, and conversed with men.$n$,true),
(7986,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',3,30,3,30,$n$*Who has gone over the sea, and found her, and will bring her for pure gold?* (Baruch 3:30). *And no man hath ascended up to heaven, but he that came down from heaven, even the Son of Adam which is in heaven.* (John 3:13). Baruch piles up the impossibility. No one has gone up to heaven to bring wisdom down, and no one has crossed the sea to find her and buy her with pure gold; *No man knoweth her way, nor thinketh of her path* (Baruch 3:31). Yahusha (Jesus) tells Nicodemus that no man has ascended to heaven but the one who came down from it. The reaching Baruch rules out, upward or outward, is answered from the other direction. The Son of Adam did not have to search for wisdom over the sea, because he came down from where she dwells, and he sat in front of Nicodemus as the thing no gold could buy.$n$,$n$Who has gone over the sea, and found her, and will bring her for pure gold?$n$,true),
(7987,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',3,29,3,29,$n$*Who has gone up into heaven, and taken her, and brought her down from the clouds?* (Baruch 3:29). *And no man hath ascended up to heaven, but he that came down from heaven, even the Son of Adam which is in heaven.* (John 3:13). Baruch asks a question meant to have no answer: who has gone up into heaven and taken wisdom and brought her down? No one, is the expected reply; wisdom is beyond the reach of any son of Adam. Then Yahusha (Jesus) tells Nicodemus that no man has ascended to heaven except the one who came down from it, the Son of Adam. He is the answer Baruch's question was waiting for. The wisdom no man could climb to fetch came down herself, in flesh; Wisdom of Solomon 9 and Ecclesiasticus 24 had already set her beside the Maker, and John 3:13 names the one who was there and came down.$n$,$n$Who has gone up into heaven, and taken her, and brought her down from the clouds?$n$,true),
(7994,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',1,19,1,19,$n$*Then had I pity upon your mournings, and gave you manna to eat; so you did eat angels’ bread.* (2 Esdras 1:19). *For the bread of Elohim (God) is he which cometh down from heaven, and giveth life unto the world.* (John 6:33). The manna was angels' bread, 2 Esdras says, given out of the Father's pity. In John, Yahusha (Jesus) tells the crowd that the bread of Elohim (God) is he which comes down from heaven and gives life to the world, and a few verses on he says it outright, I am the bread of life. The fathers ate angels' bread in the wilderness and died; the bread the Father sends now is a person, and the life he gives does not end at the edge of the wilderness. The manna was real bread and a real sign, and the one it signified stood in the synagogue at Capernaum and said so.$n$,$n$Then had I pity upon your mournings, and gave you manna to eat; so you did eat angels’ bread.$n$,true),
(7995,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,26,16,26,$n$*That your children, O Yahuah (God), whom you lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:26). *Then Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Moses gave you not that bread from heaven; but my Father giveth you the true bread from heaven.* (John 6:32). The manna was teaching that it is the word of Yahuah (God), not the harvest, that keeps his children alive. Yahusha (Jesus) says Moses did not give the bread from heaven; the Father did, and the Father is now giving the true bread. The word that preserved the wilderness generation is standing in front of the crowd in flesh, the Word made flesh who is himself the bread. What the manna said in a picture, he says in person.$n$,$n$That your children, O Yahuah (God), whom you lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.$n$,true),
(7996,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,20,16,20,$n$*Instead of which you feddest your own people with angels’ food, and did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour, able to content every man’s delight, and agreeing to every taste.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:20). *Our fathers did eat manna in the desert; as it is written, He gave them bread from heaven to eat.* (John 6:31). The crowd at Capernaum quotes the manna back at him as the high mark of what heaven has ever given. The library agrees with them about the manna: it was angels' food, sent from heaven without their labour, fitted to every taste. Yahusha (Jesus) does not take a word of it back; he says the Father who sent that bread is now giving the true bread, and the true bread is himself (John 6:32, 35). The manna was real and it was heaven's, and it was pointing past itself to the one who came down.$n$,$n$Instead of which you feddest your own people with angels’ food, and did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour, able to content every man’s delight, and agreeing to every taste.$n$,true),
(8003,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',36,11,36,11,$n$*Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.* (Ecclesiasticus 36:11). *And not for that nation only, but that also he should gather together in one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad.* (John 11:52). Sirach prays for all the tribes of Jacob to be gathered and inherited as from the beginning. John says Yahusha (Jesus) would die not for that nation only but to gather together in one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad. The scattered children are Jacob's tribes among the nations; the one nation in the land was Yahudah (Judah). Sirach prayed for the gathering, and John names the death that pays for it; Caiaphas said more than he knew.$n$,$n$Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning.$n$,true),
(8004,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',5,9,5,9,$n$*For Yahuah (God) shall lead Yashar'el (Israel) with joy in the light of his glory with the mercy and righteousness that comes from him.* (Baruch 5:9). *And not for that nation only, but that also he should gather together in one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad.* (John 11:52). Baruch's song ends with the gathering complete: Yahuah (God) leads Yashar'el (Israel) home with joy in the light of his glory. John names what the death of Yahusha (Jesus) accomplished in the same terms, gathering into one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad. The scattered children and Yashar'el (Israel) led home are one people; John's into one is Baruch's led with joy. The mercy and righteousness that come from him in Baruch came in the flesh in John, and the gathering they promised runs through the cross.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) shall lead Yashar'el (Israel) with joy in the light of his glory with the mercy and righteousness that comes from him.$n$,true),
(8005,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',5,5,5,5,$n$*Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and look about toward the east, and behold your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the remembrance of Yahuah (God).* (Baruch 5:5). *And not for that nation only, but that also he should gather together in one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad.* (John 11:52). Baruch says it twice, once in chapter 4 and again here, so no one misses it: Jerusalem's children are gathered by the word of the Holy One, from the east and from the west. John says the death of Yahusha (Jesus) was to gather together in one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad. Scattered abroad is where Baruch's children went; gathered into one is where they are going. The homecoming Baruch sang from the captivity is the homecoming John says the cross was for.$n$,$n$Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and look about toward the east, and behold your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the remembrance of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(8006,'T','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',4,37,4,37,$n$*Lo, your sons come, whom you sentest away, they come gathered together from the east to the west by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the glory of Yahuah (God).* (Baruch 4:37). *And not for that nation only, but that also he should gather together in one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad.* (John 11:52). Baruch sees the sons Jerusalem sent away coming gathered from east to west by the word of the Holy One. John explains the death of Yahusha (Jesus) in the same terms: not for that nation only, but to gather into one the children of Elohim (God) that were scattered abroad. The scattered children are not the nations; they are Jerusalem's own sons, sent away in the scattering, the ones Hosea said would be called sons of the living Elohim (God) in the very place they were called not his people (Hosea 1:10). Baruch names the gathering by the word of the Holy One; John names the death that does the gathering.$n$,$n$Lo, your sons come, whom you sentest away, they come gathered together from the east to the west by the word of the Holy One, rejoicing in the glory of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(8009,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,32,7,32,$n$*And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.* (2 Esdras 7:32). *Martha saith unto him, I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection at the last day.* (John 11:24). Martha, grieving, says what the Hebrew library had taught her: her brother will rise again in the resurrection at the last day. Ezra was told the same thing in fuller words, that the earth will restore those asleep in her and the secret places will deliver the souls committed to them. Martha's hope is not a vague comfort; it is this promise. And the one standing in front of her is about to call Lazarus out of the grave (John 11:43–44), a first sign of the day Ezra was shown.$n$,$n$And the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence, and the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.$n$,true),
(8010,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',13,33,13,33,$n$*And when all the people hear his voice, every man shall in their own land leave the battle they have one against another.* (2 Esdras 13:33). *My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me:* (John 10:27). When the Son is declared, Ezra is told, all the people hear his voice and every man leaves his battle in his own land. Yahusha (Jesus) says his sheep hear his voice, and he knows them, and they follow him. The hearing is what shows who they are; the ones who leave their fighting when he speaks are the ones who were his. His voice does not make sheep; it calls the sheep out of the battle they were in.$n$,$n$And when all the people hear his voice, every man shall in their own land leave the battle they have one against another.$n$,true),
(8020,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,23,24,23,$n$*All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:23). *I am the vine, ye are the branches: He that abideth in me, and I in him, the same bringeth forth much fruit: for without me ye can do nothing.* (John 15:5). A few verses earlier Wisdom said she brought forth pleasant savour as the vine, and now Sirach tells us plainly what that vine is: the book of the covenant, the law Moses commanded as the inheritance of Jacob. When Yahusha (Jesus) says he is the vine and his disciples are the branches, he is not planting a new vine beside the old one. To abide in him is to abide in the Word the Father spoke, the same covenant Torah Sirach named as Wisdom. A branch cut from the vine bears nothing, and a man cut off from the Word bears nothing, because the vine and the Word are one.$n$,$n$All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.$n$,true),
(8021,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,17,24,17,$n$*As the vine brought I forth pleasant savour, and my flowers are the fruit of honour and riches.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:17). *I am the true vine, and my Father is the husbandman.* (John 15:1). In the great hymn of Wisdom she calls herself a vine, bringing forth pleasant savour, her flowers the fruit of honour and riches — the same Wisdom the Creator commanded to take root in Jacob, and whom Sirach goes on to name as the book of the covenant, the law Moses commanded. Yahusha (Jesus) says I am the true vine, and my Father is the husbandman. The Wisdom planted as a vine in Yashar'el (Israel) is the one who stood on the earth and said so; the Word that became flesh is the vine the branches abide in and bear from. The husbandman is the Father, and the vine is his Formed one.$n$,$n$As the vine brought I forth pleasant savour, and my flowers are the fruit of honour and riches.$n$,true),
(8028,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,14,18,14,$n$*He has mercy on them that receive discipline, and that diligently seek after his judgments.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:14). *He saith unto him the third time, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me? Peter was grieved because he said unto him the third time, Lovest thou me? And he said unto him, Adonai (Lord), thou knowest all things; thou knowest that I love thee. Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto him, Feed my sheep.* (John 21:17). Sirach says mercy falls on the one who receives discipline. Peter was grieved at the third asking — three questions answering three denials — and he did not turn away from it; he received it, and the mercy landed not as condemnation but as a charge to feed the sheep. That is the pattern Sirach names: the discipline is received, the judgments of Yahuah (God) are sought, and the mercy follows.$n$,$n$He has mercy on them that receive discipline, and that diligently seek after his judgments.$n$,true),
(8029,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',5,18,5,18,$n$*Up then, and eat bread, and forsake us not, as the shepherd that leaveth his flock in the hands of cruel wolves.* (2 Esdras 5:18). *So when they had dined, Yahusha (Jesus) saith to Simon Peter, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me more than these? He saith unto him, Yea, Adonai (Lord); thou knowest that I love thee. He saith unto him, Feed my lambs.* (John 21:15). Phaltiel the captain finds Ezra fasting in the field and begs him not to leave the people the way a shepherd leaves his flock to the wolves, because Yashar'el (Israel) in the land of their captivity had been committed to him. The risen Yahusha (Jesus) sets bread before Peter and then asks him three times whether he loves him, once for each denial, and every answer comes back as a charge to feed the flock. Peter had forsaken his Master on the night of the trial; now he is told plainly what love looks like in a shepherd: eat the bread set before you, and do not leave the lambs to the wolves. The picture Phaltiel used as a warning is the very failure the threefold charge turns around.$n$,$n$Up then, and eat bread, and forsake us not, as the shepherd that leaveth his flock in the hands of cruel wolves.$n$,true),
(8030,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,13,18,13,$n$*The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:13). *He saith to him again the second time, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me? He saith unto him, Yea, Adonai (Lord); thou knowest that I love thee. He saith unto him, Feed my sheep.* (John 21:16). The shepherd Sirach describes is Yahuah (God) himself — reproving, nurturing, teaching, bringing the flock back again. On the shore Yahusha (Jesus) does exactly that to Peter: the second asking is the reproof, the answer is the nurture, and the charge to feed my sheep is the bringing again. Then he hands that same work to the man he has just restored. The one who was brought back is sent to bring back the flock.$n$,$n$The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.$n$,true),
(8031,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,18,2,18,$n$*For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:18). *When Yahusha (Jesus) therefore had received the vinegar, he said, It is finished: and he bowed his head, and gave up the ghost.* (John 19:30). The ungodly set the terms: if he is the son, his Father will pull him out of their hands. On the cross the terms were met in a way they never imagined. Yahusha (Jesus) did not come down; he finished the work and gave up the ghost. The deliverance came on the third day, not by escape but by resurrection — exactly where Wisdom goes next, when the chapter turns: *But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1).$n$,$n$For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.$n$,true),
(8032,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,20,2,20,$n$*Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:20). *When the chief priests therefore and officers saw him, they cried out, saying, Crucify him, crucify him. Pilate saith unto them, Take ye him, and crucify him: for I find no fault in him.* (John 19:6). Wisdom gives the resolve; John gives the shout. Let us condemn him with a shameful death becomes Crucify him, crucify him in the mouths of the chief priests and officers. Pilate found no fault in him, and still the death was demanded — not for any crime, but because he called Yahuah (God) his father and his life exposed theirs. The crucifixion is the shameful death Wisdom said the ungodly would lay on the just man.$n$,$n$Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.$n$,true),
(8033,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,19,2,19,$n$*Let us examine him with despitefulness and torture, that we may know his meekness, and prove his patience.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:19). *Then came Yahusha (Jesus) forth, wearing the crown of thorns, and the purple robe. And Pilate saith unto them, Behold the man!* (John 19:5). Here is the program the ungodly lay out: despitefulness and torture, to see what the righteous man's meekness and patience are made of. John 19 shows it carried out to the letter — Pilate scourged him, the soldiers wove him a crown of thorns and dressed him in purple and struck him, and then he was led out before the crowd. Behold the man. He came forth meek and patient under the very test Wisdom said the ungodly would devise, and the test proved what they refused to see.$n$,$n$Let us examine him with despitefulness and torture, that we may know his meekness, and prove his patience.$n$,true),
(8034,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,16,2,16,$n$*We are esteemed of him as counterfeits: he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness: he pronounceth the end of the just to be blessed, and makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:16). *Pilate saith unto him, What is truth? And when he had said this, he went out again unto the Yahudim (Jews), and saith unto them, I find in him no fault at all.* (John 18:38). Pilate looked for a crime and found none. The men pressing for his death had no crime to show either, only what Wisdom had already named: a man whose life rebuked theirs and who made his boast that Yahuah (God) was his father. That was the whole charge — he claimed the Father as his own, and his righteousness made their religion look like counterfeit. The fault the ungodly find in the just is never a crime; it is the reproof they feel just by standing near him.$n$,$n$We are esteemed of him as counterfeits: he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness: he pronounceth the end of the just to be blessed, and makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.$n$,true),
(8035,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,13,2,13,$n$*He professeth to have the knowledge of Yahuah (God): and he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:13). *The Yahudim (Jews) answered him, We have a law, and by our law he ought to die, because he made himself the Son of Elohim (God).* (John 19:7). The charge the accusers bring to Pilate, *by our law he ought to die, because he made himself the Son of Elohim (God)*, is word for word the complaint Wisdom of Solomon put in the mouth of the ungodly against the righteous one: he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God). What the plotters of Wisdom 2 treat as the offence worth killing for is what the chief priests treat as the capital offence at the trial. They did not invent the charge; they inherited it from men who had been laying in wait for the righteous since before there was a Pilate to bring it to. And the law they appealed to was the law his life had been reproving them with.$n$,$n$He professeth to have the knowledge of Yahuah (God): and he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(8036,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,12,2,12,$n$*Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:12). *Judas then, having received a band of men and officers from the chief priests and Pharisees, cometh thither with lanterns and torches and weapons.* (John 18:3). The arrest at night is the lying in wait. Judas comes with a band of men and officers sent by the chief priests and Pharisees, with lanterns and torches and weapons, for one unarmed man in a garden. Wisdom of Solomon had already named the motive: the righteous one is clean contrary to their doings and upbraids them with their offending the law. The plot against the righteous one is not a New Testament novelty; the ungodly had been lying in wait for the just man who reproves them for as long as there have been just men, and the torches in Gethsemane are that old resolve carried out.$n$,$n$Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn, and he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law, and objecteth to our infamy the transgressings of our education.$n$,true),
(11561,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',2,24,2,24,$n$*Nevertheless through envy of the devil came death into the world: and they that do hold of his side do find it.* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:24). *By one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin* (Romans 5:12). The older witness names the deeper root: death came into the world *through envy of the devil* — for *Yahuah (God) created man to be immortal, and made him to be an image of his own eternity* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:23). The serpent of the garden is the envy behind the one man's fall; death is the intruder, not the Maker's design.$n$,$n$Nevertheless through envy of the devil came death into the world: and they that do hold of his side do find it.$n$,false),
(12154,'T','apocrypha','tobit',1,10,1,10,$n$*And when we were carried away captives to Nineve, all my brothers and those that were of my kindred did eat of the bread of the Gentiles.* (Tobit 1:10). *For one believeth that he may eat all things: another, who is weak, eateth herbs* (Romans 14:2). The weak brother who *eateth herbs* is doing in Rome what Tobit did in the captivity — refusing the meat of the heathen table, whose slaughter and offering could not be trusted. The abstaining is a conscience guarding itself from defilement by association, not a verdict that the Torah's own clean food had changed.$n$,$n$And when we were carried away captives to Nineve, all my brothers and those that were of my kindred did eat of the bread of the Gentiles.$n$,false),
(12188,'T','apocrypha','tobit',14,6,14,6,$n$*And all nations shall turn, and fear Yahuah (God) truly, and shall bury their idols.* (Tobit 14:6). *It hath pleased them verily; and their debtors they are. For if the Gentiles have been made partakers of their spiritual things, their duty is also to minister unto them in carnal things.* (Romans 15:27). Paul is carrying a collection from the assemblies among the Gentiles to the poor saints at Jerusalem, and he calls it a debt: they received spiritual things from Jerusalem, so they owe carnal things back. The Gentiles Paul writes to are, for the most part, the scattered seed of Yashar'el (Israel) living as Gentiles among the nations, the people Tobit said would be gathered home. Tobit saw two things coming: his people gathered home, and the nations turning to fear Yahuah (God) truly and burying their idols, with his own people confessing him and being exalted in the next line. The collection belongs to the first of those. It is the gathering made tangible in one generation: the scattered children of Yashar'el (Israel), who had lived among the idols, sending back to Jerusalem what they owed for the spiritual things that came out of it, the idols left behind and the debt of love paid.$n$,$n$And all nations shall turn, and fear Yahuah (God) truly, and shall bury their idols.$n$,true),
(12213,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',7,25,7,25,$n$*For she is the breath of the power of Yahuah (God), and a pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty: therefore can no defiled thing fall into her.* (Wisdom of Solomon 7:25). *Who being the brightness of his glory, and the express image of his person, and upholding all things by the word of his power, when he had by himself purged our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high;* (Hebrews 1:3). A pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty: Wisdom describes something that comes out of the glory without lessening it. Hebrews puts a person where Wisdom put a picture. The Son is the brightness of his glory and the express image of his person, the way light shining is of the light and yet is not the lamp. He proceeds from the Father as brightness proceeds from light, the Formed shining out from the Formless: Elohim (God) who has a Father, the one who upholds all things by the word of his power and then sat down at the right hand of the one he came from.$n$,$n$For she is the breath of the power of Yahuah (God), and a pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty: therefore can no defiled thing fall into her.$n$,true),
(13139,'T','apocrypha','2-esdras',2,45,2,45,$n$*He answered and said to me, These be they that have put off the mortal clothing, and put on the immortal, and have confessed the name of Yahuah (God): now are they crowned, and receive palms.* (2 Esdras 2:45). The white-robed palm-bearers of Revelation 7 are named in the restored library: *now are they crowned, and receive palms.* The elder asks *what are these which are arrayed in white robes?* (Revelation 7:13) and answers *these are they which came out of great tribulation, and have washed their robes* (Revelation 7:14). Esdras tells the same: they that *put off the mortal clothing, and put on the immortal,* who *confessed the name of Yahuah (God)* — the same company, robed and palm-bearing, who came through tribulation into glory.$n$,$n$He answered and said to me, These be they that have put off the mortal clothing, and put on the immortal, and have confessed the name of Yahuah (God): now are they crowned, and receive palms.$n$,false),
(14230,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',10,20,10,20,$n$*Therefore the righteous spoiled the ungodly, and praised your holy name, O Yahuah (God), and magnified with one accord yours hand, that fought for them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 10:20). The Hebrew library remembers the same scene: the righteous *Brought them through the Red sea* (Wisdom of Solomon 10:18), their enemies drowned, and then *with one accord* they praise the holy name of the One *that fought for them.* This is exactly the posture of those who *had gotten the victory... having the harps of Elohim (God)* (Revelation 15:2), who *glorify thy name* (15:4). The redeemed praising with one accord after the sea is the song of Moses; the seer sees it sung again, the same hand that fought at the Red Sea fighting still.$n$,$n$Therefore the righteous spoiled the ungodly, and praised your holy name, O Yahuah (God), and magnified with one accord yours hand, that fought for them.$n$,false),
(15208,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,4,3,4,$n$*For though they be punished in the sight of men, yet is their hope full of immortality.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:4). The library tells what the eye of the ungodly cannot see: *the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God)... yet is their hope full of immortality* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1–4), a reward the ungodly *neither hoped... nor discerned* (Wisdom of Solomon 2:22). This is the unseen thing Paul means — *the things which Elohim (God) hath prepared for them that love him* (1 Corinthians 2:9), which *eye hath not seen.* The reward hidden from the world's wisdom is the immortal hope kept for the righteous, prepared before the world and disclosed by the Spirit.$n$,$n$For though they be punished in the sight of men, yet is their hope full of immortality.$n$,false),
(15943,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,1,3,1,$n$*But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1). *In the sight of the unwise they seemed to die... but they are in peace... yet is their hope full of immortality* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:2–4): the library holds that what looks like death and dishonour to the world is, for the righteous, the threshold of immortality. So Paul: the body *is sown in dishonour; it is raised in glory: it is sown in weakness; it is raised in power* (1 Corinthians 15:43). The seeming-death of the righteous is the sowing; the hope full of immortality is the raising.$n$,$n$But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.$n$,false),
(39109,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,19,23,19,$n$*Such a man only fears the eyes of men, and knoweth not that the eyes of Yahuah (God) are ten thousand times brighter than the sun, beholding all the ways of men, and considering the most secret parts.* (Ecclesiasticus 23:19). *For the ear of jealousy heareth all things: and the noise of murmurings is not hid.* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:10). Sirach describes the man who fears only the eyes of men and forgets that the eyes of Yahuah (God) are ten thousand times brighter than the sun, searching out the most secret places. The Wisdom of Solomon says the same of his hearing — the ear of jealousy hears everything, and murmuring is not hidden. Nothing done in the dark and nothing muttered under the breath escapes him. The two books together close every hiding place: he sees what the walls cover and hears what the voice keeps low.$n$,$n$Such a man only fears the eyes of men, and knoweth not that the eyes of Yahuah (God) are ten thousand times brighter than the sun, beholding all the ways of men, and considering the most secret parts.$n$,true),
(39194,'S','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',7,29,7,29,$n$John 1:5 — *And the light shineth in darkness; and the darkness comprehended it not.* Wisdom, who *being compared with the light, she is found before it*, and against whom *vice shall not prevail* (Wisdom of Solomon 7:29–30), is John's Light the darkness cannot overcome.$n$,$n$For she is more beautiful than the sun, and above all the order of stars: being compared with the light, she is found before it.$n$,false),
(39384,'S','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',10,6,10,6,$n$*When the ungodly perished, she delivered the righteous man, who fled from the fire which fell down upon the five cities.* (Wisdom of Solomon 10:6). *And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom.* (Jubilees 16:6). Jubilees goes on in the next verse to tell how Lot was saved because Elohim (God) remembered Abraham and sent him out of the midst of the overthrow; Wisdom names the same deliverance from the other side — the righteous man delivered from the fire that fell on the five cities. The judgment on Sodom and the rescue of the righteous out of it are one act, told in both books.$n$,$n$When the ungodly perished, she delivered the righteous man, who fled from the fire which fell down upon the five cities.$n$,false),
(39439,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',13,1,13,1,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 13:1 — *Surely vain are all men by nature, who are ignorant of Yahuah (God), and could not out of the good things that are seen know him that is: neither by considering the works did they acknowledge the workmaster;* the thesis that idolatry is *the beginning, the cause, and the end, of all evil* (Wisdom of Solomon 14:27) completes the polemic begun in 13:1, the Creator unknown though seen — the very pairing Romans 1:20 and 1:25 draw on.$n$,$n$Surely vain are all men by nature, who are ignorant of Yahuah (God), and could not out of the good things that are seen know him that is: neither by considering the works did they acknowledge the workmaster;$n$,false),
(39472,'S','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,21,16,21,$n$*For your sustenance declared your sweetness to your children, and serving to the appetite of the eater, tempered itself to every man’s liking.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:21). *For the bread of Elohim (God) is he which cometh down from heaven, and giveth life unto the world.* (John 6:33). The manna carried the Father's sweetness to his children and fitted itself to every one who ate it. John names the bread of Elohim (God) as a person — he who comes down from heaven and gives life to the world. The sweetness the wilderness tasted was a foretaste of the one who came down; the manna fed a generation, and the Bread who came down gives life.$n$,$n$For your sustenance declared your sweetness to your children, and serving to the appetite of the eater, tempered itself to every man’s liking.$n$,true),
(39501,'S','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',18,16,18,16,$n$*And brought your unfeigned commandment as a sharp sword, and standing up filled all things with death; and it touched the heaven, but it stood upon the earth.* (Wisdom of Solomon 18:16). *For the word of Elohim (God) is quick, and powerful, and sharper than any twoedged sword, piercing even to the dividing asunder of soul and spirit, and of the joints and marrow, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.* (Hebrews 4:12). In Egypt the word came as a sharp sword that filled the land with death, touching heaven and standing on the earth. Hebrews says the same word is still a sword, sharper than any two-edged blade, and it now cuts where no Egyptian saw it fall — between soul and spirit, into the thoughts and intents of the heart. The sword is the same; the field it works has moved inward. The one who judged Egypt in a night reads the heart of the one who reads him.$n$,$n$And brought your unfeigned commandment as a sharp sword, and standing up filled all things with death; and it touched the heaven, but it stood upon the earth.$n$,true),
(39532,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',1,29,1,29,$n$*Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men, and take good heed what you speak.* (Ecclesiasticus 1:29). *Even so ye also outwardly appear righteous unto men, but within ye are full of hypocrisy and iniquity.* (Matthew 23:28). Sirach gives the counsel; Yahusha (Jesus) delivers the indictment of the men who ignored it. To be a hypocrite in the sight of men is to appear righteous unto men while being something else within, which is exactly how Matthew 23:28 reads. The warning came first, in a book the Reformation later cut from the library, and the Messiah's woe assumes his hearers should have known better. The religious system of his day had built itself on appearance; Sirach had told Yashar'el (Israel) long before that appearance is not what Yahuah (God) is weighing.$n$,$n$Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men, and take good heed what you speak.$n$,true),
(39536,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',1,26,1,26,$n$*If you desire wisdom, keep the commandments, and Yahuah (God) shall give her to you.* (Ecclesiasticus 1:26). *Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: Fear Elohim (God), and keep his commandments: for this is the whole duty of man.* (Ecclesiastes 12:13). Sirach puts wisdom on the far side of the commandments: keep them, and Yahuah (God) gives her. Solomon ends his whole book in the same place, fear Elohim (God) and keep his commandments, and calls that the whole duty of man. Neither writer knows a wisdom that stands apart from the commandments or a fear of Yahuah (God) that outgrows them. The commandments are not the beginner's course a wise man leaves behind; they are the door wisdom comes through, and the conclusion the wisest man in Yashar'el (Israel) arrived at.$n$,$n$If you desire wisdom, keep the commandments, and Yahuah (God) shall give her to you.$n$,true),
(39553,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',2,5,2,5,$n$*For gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:5). *That the trial of your faith, being much more precious than of gold that perisheth, though it be tried with fire, might be found unto praise and honour and glory at the appearing of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ):* (1 Peter 1:7). Peter writes to the scattered strangers with Sirach's figure in his hand: faith tried with fire, more precious than gold that perishes. Sirach had said gold is tried in the fire and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity, and Peter, who had been through that furnace himself the night the cock crowed, hands the picture on to the scattered seed of Yashar'el (Israel) he is writing to. The trial is not a sign of being forsaken; it is the proving, and what is proved is found unto praise and honour and glory at the appearing of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ). The Apocrypha and the apostle are describing the same furnace.$n$,$n$For gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.$n$,true),
(39555,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',3,6,3,6,$n$*As gold in the furnace has he tried them, and received them as a burnt offering.* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:6). *For gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:5). Two wisdom books, one furnace. Sirach says gold is tried in the fire and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity; Wisdom says Yahuah (God) tried the righteous as gold in the furnace and received them as a burnt offering. The fire does not destroy the gold; it shows what the gold is. The righteous who were tormented and killed in Wisdom 2 were being refined, not discarded, and what came through the fire was received at the altar.$n$,$n$As gold in the furnace has he tried them, and received them as a burnt offering.$n$,true),
(39575,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *Water will quench a flaming fire; and alms makes an atonement for sins.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:30). Two books of the library say one thing in two pictures. Tobit's open hand keeps the face of Yahuah (God) turned toward the giver; Sirach says alms put out sin the way water puts out fire. Neither is buying anything. Alms is mercy, mercy is what the Torah asks of a man, and the one who walks in it walks in the light of the Father's face.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(39593,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',4,28,4,28,$n$*Strive for the truth to death, and Yahuah (God) shall fight for you.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:28). *Whosoever therefore shall confess me before men, him will I confess also before my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 10:32). Sirach tells his son to strive for the truth to death, and promises that Yahuah (God) will do the fighting. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the twelve that whoever confesses him before men will be confessed before the Father. Confessing him before men is the striving for the truth, carried all the way to death if it comes to that, and the confession before the Father is Yahuah (God) fighting for the one who did not let go. The promise in Sirach and the promise in Matthew are the same promise: the man who holds the truth in front of men is not left to hold it alone.$n$,$n$Strive for the truth to death, and Yahuah (God) shall fight for you.$n$,true),
(39600,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',4,13,4,13,$n$*He that holdeth her fast shall inherit glory; and wherever she entereth, Yahuah (God) will bless.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:13). *That I may cause those that love me to inherit substance; and I will fill their treasures.* (Proverbs 8:21). Wisdom speaks for herself in Proverbs: she causes those who love her to inherit, and she fills their treasures. Sirach describes the same inheritance from the receiving end: the one who holds her fast inherits glory, and wherever she enters, Yahuah (God) blesses. Both verses give wisdom an estate to leave, and both leave it to the ones who love her and will not let her go. Holding her fast is the inheritance's condition and its proof.$n$,$n$He that holdeth her fast shall inherit glory; and wherever she entereth, Yahuah (God) will bless.$n$,true),
(39646,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',6,6,6,6,$n$*Be in peace with many: nevertheless have but one counsellor of a thousand.* (Ecclesiasticus 6:6). *A man that hath friends must shew himself friendly: and there is a friend that sticketh closer than a brother.* (Proverbs 18:24). Solomon's proverb has two kinds of friend in it: the many a friendly man gathers, and the one who sticks closer than a brother. Sirach draws the same line and tells you how to count: be at peace with many, but take one counsellor out of a thousand. The crowd of friends is not the problem; letting the crowd counsel you is. Both writers are pointing at the rare one, the friend whose word you can build on, and both say you will not find him by being friendly to everyone but by proving him.$n$,$n$Be in peace with many: nevertheless have but one counsellor of a thousand.$n$,true),
(39654,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *And stretch yours hand to the poor, that your blessing may be perfected.* (Ecclesiasticus 7:32). Tobit tells his son to turn his face toward the poor; Sirach tells his to stretch out his hand to them so that his blessing may be perfected. The face and the hand together are the whole posture of the righteous man in the library: see the need and move toward it. The blessing is not finished until the hand is open.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(39657,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',7,31,7,31,$n$*Fear Yahuah (God), and honor the priest; and give him his portion, as it is commanded you; the firstfruits, and the trespass offering, and the gift of the shoulders, and the sacrifice of sanctification, and the firstfruits of the holy things.* (Ecclesiasticus 7:31). *Honour Yahuah (LORD) with thy substance, and with the firstfruits of all thine increase:* (Proverbs 3:9). Sirach puts the priest's portion under the fear of Yahuah (God) and says it is given as it is commanded — the firstfruits, the trespass offering, the shoulder, the holy things. Solomon had already said where that honor is going: the firstfruits of all the increase honor Yahuah (LORD) himself, and the priest is the hand that receives them for him. Neither verse treats the gift as a tax. It is the first and the best handed back to the one who gave the whole, and the Torah's instruction for the priest's due is where that honor lands in practice.$n$,$n$Fear Yahuah (God), and honor the priest; and give him his portion, as it is commanded you; the firstfruits, and the trespass offering, and the gift of the shoulders, and the sacrifice of sanctification, and the firstfruits of the holy things.$n$,true),
(39850,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',9,14,9,14,$n$*As near as you can, guess at your neighbour, and consult with the wise.* (Ecclesiasticus 9:14). *He that walketh with wise men shall be wise: but a companion of fools shall be destroyed.* (Proverbs 13:20). Sirach says to take the measure of your neighbour as best you can and to take counsel from the wise, and in the next verse he says to let all your communication be in the law of the Most High. Solomon had said the same about company: walk with the wise and you grow wise, keep company with fools and you share their ruin. Who sits at your table shapes who you become.$n$,$n$As near as you can, guess at your neighbour, and consult with the wise.$n$,true),
(39851,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',9,15,9,15,$n$*Let your talk be with the wise, and all your communication in the law of the Most High.* (Ecclesiasticus 9:15). *But his delight is in the law of Yahuah (LORD); and in his law doth he meditate day and night.* (Psalm 1:2). Sirach puts the whole of the wise man's conversation inside the law of the Most High. David's blessed man delights in the law of Yahuah (LORD) and meditates in it day and night. The one who delights in it will talk about it; the man whose speech is in the Torah is the man whose heart has been there first.$n$,$n$Let your talk be with the wise, and all your communication in the law of the Most High.$n$,true),
(39861,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',9,8,9,8,$n$*Turn away your eye from a beautiful woman, and look not upon another’s beauty; for many have been deceived by the beauty of a woman; for herewith love is kindled as a fire.* (Ecclesiasticus 9:8). *But I say unto you, That whosoever looketh on a woman to lust after her hath committed adultery with her already in his heart.* (Matthew 5:28). Sirach tells the eye to turn away before love is kindled as a fire, because the deception begins in the looking. Yahusha (Jesus) follows the same line to its root: the look that lusts has already committed the adultery in the heart. Neither is adding a new commandment to the seventh. Both are reading it where it was always meant to be read, at the eye and the heart, before the body ever moves.$n$,$n$Turn away your eye from a beautiful woman, and look not upon another’s beauty; for many have been deceived by the beauty of a woman; for herewith love is kindled as a fire.$n$,true),
(39949,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',14,11,14,11,$n$*My son, according to your ability do good to thyself, and give Yahuah (God) his due offering.* (Ecclesiasticus 14:11). *And said, Naked came I out of my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return thither: Yahuah (LORD) gave, and Yahuah (LORD) hath taken away; blessed be the name of Yahuah (LORD).* (Job 1:21). Sirach says to do good to yourself as you are able and to give Yahuah (God) his due offering, and his very next line is the reason: death will not be long in coming. Job said at the ash heap what Sirach is building on — a man comes naked and goes back naked, and what he had in between was Yahuah's (God's) to give and to take. Everything in the hand is on loan. Give the Giver his due while it is still in your hand to give.$n$,$n$My son, according to your ability do good to thyself, and give Yahuah (God) his due offering.$n$,true),
(39960,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',15,20,15,20,$n$James 1:15 — *Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death.* *He has commanded no man to do wickedly, neither has he given any man licence to sin* (Ecclesiasticus 15:20); sin is born of a man's own conceived lust, not of any divine warrant.$n$,$n$He has commanded no man to do wickedly, neither has he given any man licence to sin.$n$,false),
(39962,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',15,18,15,18,$n$*For the wisdom of Yahuah (God) is great, and he is mighty in power, and beholds all things:* (Ecclesiasticus 15:18). *The eyes of Yahuah (LORD) are in every place, beholding the evil and the good.* (Proverbs 15:3). Sirach says the wisdom of Yahuah (God) beholds all things and his power is great. Solomon put the same truth in one line: his eyes are in every place, on the evil and the good alike. The verses before this in Sirach say life and death are set before a man and he will be given what he chooses; the all-seeing eye is why the choice is weighed and not forgotten.$n$,$n$For the wisdom of Yahuah (God) is great, and he is mighty in power, and beholds all things:$n$,true),
(39982,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',10,13,10,13,$n$*For pride is the beginning of sin, and he that has it shall pour out abomination: and therefore Yahuah (God) brought upon them strange calamities, and overthrew them utterly.* (Ecclesiasticus 10:13). *Neither spared he the place where Lot sojourned, but abhorred them for their pride.* (Ecclesiasticus 16:8). Sirach's own proof case for pride comes later in his book: the place where Lot sojourned was not spared, and the reason he gives is their pride. The verse here says pride pours out abomination and brings strange calamities; the later verse names the city it happened to. Sodom is not a story about one sin. It is where pride ends when it is left to pour.$n$,$n$For pride is the beginning of sin, and he that has it shall pour out abomination: and therefore Yahuah (God) brought upon them strange calamities, and overthrew them utterly.$n$,true),
(40333,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,26,17,26,$n$*Turn again to the Most High, and turn away from iniquity: for he will lead you out of darkness into the light of health, and hate you abomination vehemently.* (Ecclesiasticus 17:26). *Therefore I will judge you, O house of Yashar'el (Israel), every one according to his ways, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD). Repent, and turn yourselves from all your transgressions; so iniquity shall not be your ruin.* (Ezekiel 18:30). Sirach says to turn again to the Most High and away from iniquity, and promises light at the end of it. Ezekiel says the same to the house of Yashar'el (Israel): repent, turn from your transgressions, so iniquity shall not be your ruin. The turn is what keeps iniquity from being the end of the story. Judgment according to a man's ways and the open call to turn stand in the same verse because they are the same mercy.$n$,$n$Turn again to the Most High, and turn away from iniquity: for he will lead you out of darkness into the light of health, and hate you abomination vehemently.$n$,true),
(40334,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,24,17,24,$n$*But to them that repent, he granted them return, and comforted those that failed in patience.* (Ecclesiasticus 17:24). *For I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD): wherefore turn yourselves, and live ye.* (Ezekiel 18:32). Sirach says return is granted to them that repent. Ezekiel heard the heart behind it: Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) has no pleasure in the death of the one who dies, and so he calls, turn yourselves, and live. The granting of return is not reluctant. It is what he wanted all along.$n$,$n$But to them that repent, he granted them return, and comforted those that failed in patience.$n$,true),
(40336,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',17,25,17,25,$n$*Return to Yahuah (God), and forsake your sins, make your prayer before his face, and offend less.* (Ecclesiasticus 17:25). *Draw nigh to Elohim (God), and he will draw nigh to you. Cleanse your hands, ye sinners; and purify your hearts, ye double minded.* (James 4:8). Sirach says to return, forsake sins, pray before his face, and offend less. James says to draw nigh to Elohim (God) and he will draw nigh to you, and he says it to sinners with unclean hands and double minds. Both put the turning first and the nearness after. The prayer before his face and the drawing nigh are one movement, and both expect the hands to be cleansed on the way.$n$,$n$Return to Yahuah (God), and forsake your sins, make your prayer before his face, and offend less.$n$,true),
(40341,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,30,18,30,$n$*Go not after your lusts, but refrain thyself from your appetites.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:30). *This I say then, Walk in the Spirit, and ye shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh.* (Galatians 5:16). Sirach tells his son to refrain himself from his appetites rather than follow his lusts. Paul tells the Galatians how that refraining is done: walk in the Spirit, and the lust of the flesh will not be fulfilled. The Spirit does not loosen the standard Sirach set; he supplies the strength to keep it. Walking in the Spirit is the way a man refrains himself, and the commandments are where that walk goes.$n$,$n$Go not after your lusts, but refrain thyself from your appetites.$n$,true),
(40346,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,19,18,19,$n$*Learn before you speak, and use physick or ever you be sick.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:19). *Wherefore, my beloved brethren, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath:* (James 1:19). Sirach puts learning before speaking the way a wise man takes his medicine before the sickness comes — the order matters. James says the same in three strokes: swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath. Both put the ear ahead of the mouth, and both treat it as wisdom you practice before the moment arrives, not after.$n$,$n$Learn before you speak, and use physick or ever you be sick.$n$,true),
(40348,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,22,18,22,$n$*Let nothing hinder you to pay your vow in due time, and defer not until death to be justified.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:22). *When thou vowest a vow unto Elohim (God), defer not to pay it; for he hath no pleasure in fools: pay that which thou hast vowed.* (Ecclesiastes 5:4). Solomon wrote the rule: a vow made to Elohim (God) is to be paid without delay, because he has no pleasure in fools. Sirach is holding that rule when he tells his son to let nothing hinder the paying of a vow in due time, and he presses it one step further — do not put off until death the matter of being justified before Yahuah (God). The vow deferred and the turning deferred are the same folly.$n$,$n$Let nothing hinder you to pay your vow in due time, and defer not until death to be justified.$n$,true),
(40349,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *My son, blemish not your good deeds, neither use uncomfortable words when you give any thing.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:15). Tobit warns against the envious eye when giving; Sirach warns against the sour word. Both know that a gift can be spoiled by the spirit it comes in. The alms Yahuah (God) looks on is given gladly, without a grudging eye and without a hard word to the one who receives it.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(40357,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,13,18,13,$n$*The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:13). *Yahuah (LORD) is good to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.* (Psalm 145:9). David sang it first: Yahuah (LORD) is good to all, and his tender mercies rest over everything he has made. Sirach sets the same truth beside the mercy of a man, which reaches only his neighbour, and shows how much wider the mercy of Yahuah (God) runs — over all flesh, as a shepherd over the whole flock. The psalm and the wisdom book are saying one thing in two voices.$n$,$n$The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.$n$,true),
(40359,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',18,9,18,9,$n$*The number of a man’s days at the most are an hundred years.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:9). *As for man, his days are as grass: as a flower of the field, so he flourisheth.* (Psalm 103:15). Sirach puts the limit on a man's days at a hundred years at the most, and in the next verse calls a thousand years a drop of water to the sea beside eternity. David says man's days are as grass and he flourishes as a flower of the field. A hundred years and a flower's season are the same length against eternity, and both writers say it so the reader will spend them rightly.$n$,$n$The number of a man’s days at the most are an hundred years.$n$,true),
(40364,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',19,29,19,29,$n$*A man may be known by his look, and one that has understanding by his countenance, when you meetest him.* (Ecclesiasticus 19:29). *Ye shall know them by their fruits. Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?* (Matthew 7:16). Sirach says a man can be known by his look, and a man of understanding by his countenance, before he has said a word. Yahusha (Jesus) gives the surer test: you will know them by their fruits, because thorns do not yield grapes. Both say the same thing about the inward man — what is in him comes out where it can be seen, whether in the face or in the fruit.$n$,$n$A man may be known by his look, and one that has understanding by his countenance, when you meetest him.$n$,true),
(40394,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',20,5,20,5,$n$*There is one that keepeth silence, and is found wise: and another by much babbling becometh hateful.* (Ecclesiasticus 20:5). *Even a fool, when he holdeth his peace, is counted wise: and he that shutteth his lips is esteemed a man of understanding.* (Proverbs 17:28). Solomon noticed that even a fool who holds his peace is counted wise. Sirach says it from the other side: one man keeps silence and is found wise, another babbles and makes himself hateful. Both are telling the same thing — the mouth gives a man away, and the one who can shut it is already halfway to understanding.$n$,$n$There is one that keepeth silence, and is found wise: and another by much babbling becometh hateful.$n$,true),
(40410,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',21,11,21,11,$n$*He that keepeth the law of Yahuah (God) getteth the understanding thereof: and the perfection of the fear of Yahuah (God) is wisdom.* (Ecclesiasticus 21:11). *The fear of Yahuah (LORD) is the beginning of wisdom: and the knowledge of the holy is understanding.* (Proverbs 9:10). Solomon set the foundation: the fear of Yahuah (LORD) is the beginning of wisdom. Sirach shows where it goes — the one who keeps the law of Yahuah (God) gets understanding of it, and the fear of Yahuah (God) brought to its perfection is wisdom itself. Fear is the beginning, keeping the law is the road, and wisdom is the crown of it. Understanding comes to the one who keeps, not to the one who only studies.$n$,$n$He that keepeth the law of Yahuah (God) getteth the understanding thereof: and the perfection of the fear of Yahuah (God) is wisdom.$n$,true),
(40411,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',21,1,21,1,$n$*My son, have you sinned? do so no more, but ask pardon for your former sins.* (Ecclesiasticus 21:1). *Forsake the foolish, and live; and go in the way of understanding.* (Proverbs 9:6). Wisdom's call in Proverbs is to forsake the foolish and live, and to walk in the way of understanding. Sirach opens his chapter with the same turn, put as a father puts it to a son — if you have sinned, do so no more, and ask pardon. Forsaking and turning are one motion; the way of understanding begins where the former sins are left behind.$n$,$n$My son, have you sinned? do so no more, but ask pardon for your former sins.$n$,true),
(40999,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,4,28,4,$n$*He sheweth no mercy to a man, which is like himself: and does he ask forgiveness of his own sins?* (Ecclesiasticus 28:4). *Then came Peter to him, and said, Adonai (Lord), how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?* (Matthew 18:21). Peter's question is about a brother, a man like himself, sinning against him over and over. Sirach had already asked what becomes of a man who shows no mercy to one made like himself and then asks forgiveness for his own sins. Peter is standing right at that question, counting toward a limit, and the King's answer in the next verse takes the limit away. The mercy owed to an equal is the mercy Sirach demands, and it is the mercy a disciple must give before he asks his own.$n$,$n$He sheweth no mercy to a man, which is like himself: and does he ask forgiveness of his own sins?$n$,true),
(41021,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',29,2,29,2,$n$*Lend to your neighbour in time of his need, and pay you your neighbour again in due season.* (Ecclesiasticus 29:2). *Give to him that asketh thee, and from him that would borrow of thee turn not thou away.* (Matthew 5:42). Sirach says lend to your neighbour when he needs it, and pay back your neighbour when the time comes. Yahusha (Jesus) on the mount says give to him who asks, and do not turn away the one who wants to borrow. The Sermon takes Sirach's line and opens the hand wider, but it is the same hand. Lending to a neighbour in his need was already the commandment in Yashar'el (Israel); the King was telling his disciples to keep it without flinching.$n$,$n$Lend to your neighbour in time of his need, and pay you your neighbour again in due season.$n$,true),
(41051,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',31,12,31,12,$n$*If you sit at a bountiful table, be not greedy upon it, and say not, There is much meat on it.* (Ecclesiasticus 31:12). *And put a knife to thy throat, if thou be a man given to appetite.* (Proverbs 23:2). Solomon tells the man given to appetite to put a knife to his own throat when he sits at a ruler's table; Sirach tells him not to be greedy at a bountiful table or to say, There is much meat here. It is the same instruction in two voices, generations apart. A full table is a test of the eye and the hand, and both wisdom writers lay the restraint on the guest, not on the host.$n$,$n$If you sit at a bountiful table, be not greedy upon it, and say not, There is much meat on it.$n$,true),
(41710,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',34,20,34,20,$n$*Whoso brings an offering of the goods of the poor doeth as one that killeth the son before his father’s eyes.* (Ecclesiasticus 34:20). *He that by usury and unjust gain increaseth his substance, he shall gather it for him that will pity the poor.* (Proverbs 28:8). Sirach says the man who brings an offering out of the goods of the poor is like one who kills a son in front of his father. Solomon says the man who grows rich by usury and unjust gain is only gathering it up for someone who will pity the poor. Both are talking about the same money. Taken from the poor and carried to the altar it is an abomination; held by the one who took it, it will not stay, for by the hand of Yahuah (God) it ends up with the merciful, and the one who gathered it is left with nothing but the blood on his hands.$n$,$n$Whoso brings an offering of the goods of the poor doeth as one that killeth the son before his father’s eyes.$n$,true),
(41847,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',40,1,40,1,$n$*Great travail is created for every man, and an heavy yoke is upon the sons of Adam, from the day that they go out of their mother’s womb, till the day that they return to the mother of all things.* (Ecclesiasticus 40:1). *Man that is born of a woman is of few days, and full of trouble.* (Job 14:1). Job and Sirach are looking at the same life. Job says the man born of a woman has few days and all of them full of trouble; Sirach says the heavy yoke lies on the sons of Adam from the day they leave the womb until the day they go back into the earth that is the mother of all things. Neither one is complaining about bad luck. They are describing the sentence laid on Adam in the garden, sorrow all his days and a return to the dust (Genesis 3:17–19), carried down the line to every son born of him.$n$,$n$Great travail is created for every man, and an heavy yoke is upon the sons of Adam, from the day that they go out of their mother’s womb, till the day that they return to the mother of all things.$n$,true),
(42674,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',44,20,44,20,$n$*Who kept the law of the Most High, and was in covenant with him: he established the covenant in his flesh; and when he was proved, he was found faithful.* (Ecclesiasticus 44:20). *And when Abram was ninety years old and nine, Yahuah (LORD) appeared to Abram, and said unto him, I am the El Shaddai (Almighty God); walk before me, and be thou perfect.* (Genesis 17:1). Sirach says Abraham kept the law of the Most High, and he says it of a man who lived centuries before Sinai. Genesis 17 shows what that law was: Yahuah (God) appears and commands him to walk before him and be perfect, and in the same chapter gives him the covenant in his flesh, the circumcision Sirach names (Genesis 17:10–11). When Yahuah (God) later speaks of Abraham he says he *obeyed my voice, and kept my charge, my commandments, my statutes, and my laws* (Genesis 26:5). The commandments did not begin with Moses; Abraham walked in them, and when he was proved on the mountain he was found faithful.$n$,$n$Who kept the law of the Most High, and was in covenant with him: he established the covenant in his flesh; and when he was proved, he was found faithful.$n$,true),
(42676,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',44,17,44,17,$n$*Noah was found perfect and righteous; in the time of wrath he was taken in exchange for the world; therefore was he left as a remnant to the earth, when the flood came.* (Ecclesiasticus 44:17). *By faith Noah, being warned of Elohim (God) of things not seen as yet, moved with fear, prepared an ark to the saving of his house; by the which he condemned the world, and became heir of the righteousness which is by faith.* (Hebrews 11:7). Sirach says Noah was found perfect and righteous and was left as a remnant to the earth when the flood came; Hebrews says Noah believed a warning about things not yet seen and built an ark, condemning the world by doing it. These are not two different Noahs. The righteousness Genesis saw in him, *Noah was a just man and perfect in his generations, and Noah walked with Elohim (God)* (Genesis 6:9), was faith with its hands on the timber. He is the first remnant in scripture, one household kept alive through the wrath while the world around it perished, and Sirach calls the trade by its name: he was taken in exchange for the world.$n$,$n$Noah was found perfect and righteous; in the time of wrath he was taken in exchange for the world; therefore was he left as a remnant to the earth, when the flood came.$n$,true),
(42684,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',44,7,44,7,$n$*All these were honoured in their generations, and were the glory of their times.* (Ecclesiasticus 44:7). *Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.* (Hebrews 11:1). Sirach's roll of honoured men and the roll of Hebrews 11 are the same men: Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Moses, the prophets. Sirach says they were the glory of their times; Hebrews says what made them so, they held things hoped for as already theirs and lived on the evidence of what they had not yet seen. Hebrews finishes the roll by saying they all died without receiving the promise, waiting for us so that they would not be made perfect without us (Hebrews 11:39–40). The glory of their generations was a faith that is still waiting for the resurrection to be paid out.$n$,$n$All these were honoured in their generations, and were the glory of their times.$n$,true),
(42695,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',45,7,45,7,$n$Hebrews 5:4 — *And no man taketh this honour unto himself, but he that is called of Elohim (God), as was Aaron.* *An everlasting covenant he made with him and gave him the priesthood among the people* (Ecclesiasticus 45:7) — the priesthood is the honour Hebrews says no man may seize but must be called to, as Aaron was.$n$,$n$An everlasting covenant he made with him and gave him the priesthood among the people; he beautified him with comely ornaments, and clothed him with a robe of glory.$n$,false),
(43706,'S','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',50,15,50,15,$n$*He stretched out his hand to the cup, and poured of the blood of the grape, he poured out at the foot of the altar a sweetsmelling savour to the most high King of all.* (Ecclesiasticus 50:15). *Neither by the blood of goats and calves, but by his own blood he entered in once into the holy place, having obtained eternal redemption for us.* (Hebrews 9:12). Simon the high priest finishes the altar service by pouring the blood of the grape at the foot of the altar, a sweet savour to the most high King. Yahusha (Jesus) took that same cup of the vine on his last night and said *this is my blood of the new testament* (Matthew 26:28). Hebrews says he then entered the holy place once, not with the blood of goats and calves but with his own. The wine poured at the altar's foot was showing in the fruit of the grape what the Messiah (Christ) would do in his own blood; Simon poured the picture, and the high priest after the order of Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) poured out the thing itself.$n$,$n$He stretched out his hand to the cup, and poured of the blood of the grape, he poured out at the foot of the altar a sweetsmelling savour to the most high King of all.$n$,true),
(43747,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *And the third I gave to them to whom it was meet, as Debora my father’s mother had commanded me, because I was left an orphan by my father.* (Tobit 1:8). Tobit 1:8 shows the old man doing as a young man what he later teaches: the third tithe given to those to whom it was meet, as his grandmother Debora had commanded him. Three generations carry it, Debora to Tobit, Tobit to Tobias. The alms he charges on his son in chapter 4 is a tithe he had been paying since he was an orphan.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(43759,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *But she replied upon me, It was given for a gift more than the wages. Howbeit I did not believe her, but told her render it to the owners: and I was abashed at her. But she replied upon me, Where are your alms and your righteous deeds? behold, you and all your works are known.* (Tobit 2:14). Blind and poor, Tobit is mocked by his own wife: where are your alms and your righteous deeds now? He does not answer her reproach by giving up the practice. Two chapters later he hands the same practice to his son as the way to keep the face of Yahuah (God) toward him. The reproach did not move him, because alms were never a trade for comfort; they were covenant life, kept in blindness as in sight.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(43801,'S','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *But rather give alms of such things as ye have; and, behold, all things are clean unto you.* (Luke 11:41). The Pharisees were careful about the outside of the cup; Yahusha (Jesus) told them the cleansing they needed was to give alms of what they had. Tobit had told his son the same long before: give of what you have, turn your face from no poor man, and the face of Yahuah (God) stays toward you. The King is not inventing a new rule; he is pointing a religious system back to the mercy the library had been teaching every son.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(43847,'S','apocrypha','tobit',8,7,8,7,$n$*And now, O Yahuah (God), I take not this my sister for lust but uprightly: therefore mercifully ordain that we may become aged together.* (Tobit 8:7). *And did not he make one? Yet had he the residue of the spirit. And wherefore one? That he might seek a godly seed. Therefore take heed to your spirit, and let none deal treacherously against the wife of his youth.* (Malachi 2:15). Tobias tells Yahuah (God) why he is taking Sarah: not for lust but uprightly, that they may grow old together. Malachi asks why Yahuah (God) made the two one and answers it — that he might seek a godly seed — and warns the man not to deal treacherously with the wife of his youth. Tobias's prayer is that purpose spoken aloud at the start of a marriage. The seed of promise is carried through marriages entered this way, and the prophet and the young bridegroom agree on what marriage is for.$n$,$n$And now, O Yahuah (God), I take not this my sister for lust but uprightly: therefore mercifully ordain that we may become aged together.$n$,true),
(44008,'S','apocrypha','judith',4,7,4,7,$n$*Charging them to keep the passages of the hill country: for by them there was an entrance into Judea, and it was easy to stop them that would come up, because the passage was straight, for two men at the most.* (Judith 4:7). *For the ships of Chittim shall come against him: therefore he shall be grieved, and return, and have indignation against the holy covenant: so shall he do; he shall even return, and have intelligence with them that forsake the holy covenant.* (Daniel 11:30). Judith's Yashar'el (Israel) hears that the invader is coming and does two things: they hold the narrow passes into Judea, and they fast and cry to Yahuah (God) (Judith 4:9–13). Daniel describes the king such a people will keep facing, one whose indignation is against the holy covenant itself and who has intelligence with those inside who forsake it. The Maccabees would meet that king in Antiochus, and the pattern does not end there. The war is never only about the land; it is a war on the covenant, with help from the covenant's own deserters, and the people who keep it answer at the passes and on their knees.$n$,$n$Charging them to keep the passages of the hill country: for by them there was an entrance into Judea, and it was easy to stop them that would come up, because the passage was straight, for two men at the most.$n$,true),
(44166,'S','apocrypha','judith',16,14,16,14,$n$*Let all creatures serve you: for you spakest, and they were made, you did send forth your spirit, and it created them, and there is none that can resist your voice.* (Judith 16:14). *Let them praise the name of Yahuah (LORD): for he commanded, and they were created.* (Psalm 148:5). Judith says the whole creation was made by speaking, you spoke and they were made, you sent forth your spirit and it created them. The psalm says the same of the heavens and the host of them: he commanded, and they were created, and that is why they are to praise his name. Moses wrote it as *Elohim (God) said, Let there be* with the Spirit moving on the waters (Genesis 1:2–3), and David wrote that *by the word of Yahuah (LORD) were the heavens made; and all the host of them by the breath of his mouth* (Psalm 33:6). John names that Word: all things were made by him (John 1:3). What was made by a voice cannot resist that voice, and Judith says so.$n$,$n$Let all creatures serve you: for you spakest, and they were made, you did send forth your spirit, and it created them, and there is none that can resist your voice.$n$,true),
(44173,'S','apocrypha','judith',16,6,16,6,$n$*But the Almighty Yahuah (God) has disappointed them by the hand of a woman.* (Judith 16:6). *But Elohim (God) hath chosen the foolish things of the world to confound the wise; and Elohim (God) hath chosen the weak things of the world to confound the things which are mighty;* (1 Corinthians 1:27). The Almighty Yahuah (God) brought a whole army to nothing by the hand of one widow, and Judith's song says so without apology. Paul tells the Corinthians this is how Elohim (God) works on purpose: he chooses the weak things to confound the mighty and the foolish things to confound the wise, so that no flesh can glory in his presence (1 Corinthians 1:29). Holofernes bragged of what he would do to Yashar'el (Israel); the next verse of the song says the mighty one did not fall by young men or giants but by a woman. The hand of a woman is the weak thing, and the weak thing is what he chose.$n$,$n$But the Almighty Yahuah (God) has disappointed them by the hand of a woman.$n$,true),
(44240,'S','apocrypha','the-rest-of-esther',14,6,14,6,$n$*And now we have sinned before you: therefore have you given us into the hands of our enemies,* (The Rest of Esther 14:6). *And that I also have walked contrary unto them, and have brought them into the land of their enemies; if then their uncircumcised hearts be humbled, and they then accept of the punishment of their iniquity:* (Leviticus 26:41). Esther's prayer follows the lines Moses laid down. Leviticus 26 said that if the people broke the covenant, Yahuah (God) himself would walk contrary to them and bring them into the land of their enemies, and that the way back would open when their uncircumcised hearts were humbled and they accepted the punishment as just. Esther does exactly that: we have sinned, you gave us into our enemies' hands, you are righteous. The enemy's hand is the covenant curse at work, not the covenant's end, and the next line in Leviticus is the promise, *then will I remember my covenant with Jacob* (Leviticus 26:42). Her humbled heart in Persia is the turn the Torah said would bring them home.$n$,$n$And now we have sinned before you: therefore have you given us into the hands of our enemies,$n$,true),
(44243,'S','apocrypha','the-rest-of-esther',14,5,14,5,$n$*From my youth up I have heard in the tribe of my family that you, O Yahuah (God), tookest Yashar'el (Israel) from among all people, and our fathers from all their predecessors, for a perpetual inheritance, and you have performed whatsoever you did promise them.* (The Rest of Esther 14:5). *For thou art an holy people unto Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God): Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath chosen thee to be a special people unto himself, above all people that are upon the face of the earth.* (Deuteronomy 7:6). Esther says she heard it in her youth, in the tribe of her family: Yahuah (God) took Yashar'el (Israel) from among all people for a perpetual inheritance. She is quoting Moses from memory in a Persian palace. Moses had said it to the whole people: chosen above all people on the face of the earth, a special people to himself. Esther is in exile, her people scattered under a foreign king and marked for slaughter, and nothing about that has cancelled what she heard in her tribe. A chosen people stays chosen in the lands of their scattering, and the inheritance is perpetual because the one who gave it does not take it back.$n$,$n$From my youth up I have heard in the tribe of my family that you, O Yahuah (God), tookest Yashar'el (Israel) from among all people, and our fathers from all their predecessors, for a perpetual inheritance, and you have performed whatsoever you did promise them.$n$,true),
(44245,'T','apocrypha','judith',9,11,9,11,$n$*For your power stands not in multitude nor your might in strong men: for you are a Yahuah (God) of the afflicted, an helper of the oppressed, an upholder of the weak, a protector of the forlorn, a saviour of them that are without hope.* (Judith 9:11). *But deliver us with yours hand, and help me that am desolate, and which have no other help but you.* (The Rest of Esther 14:14). Two women, two prayers, the same confession. Judith says Yahuah (God) is the helper of the oppressed and the saviour of those without hope, that his power never stood in numbers or strong men. Esther says it as a plea rather than a creed: deliver us with your hand, help me that am desolate, I have no other help but you. Each one stands alone in front of a power that means to wipe out her people, and each one knows the only hand that can turn it. The strength of Yashar'el (Israel) in both books is a woman on her knees who has run out of every other help.$n$,$n$For your power stands not in multitude nor your might in strong men: for you are a Yahuah (God) of the afflicted, an helper of the oppressed, an upholder of the weak, a protector of the forlorn, a saviour of them that are without hope.$n$,true),
(44297,'S','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',1,10,1,10,$n$*And they said, Behold, we have sent you money to buy you burnt offerings, and sin offerings, and incense, and prepare you manna, and offer upon the altar of Yahuah (God), our Elohim (God); And pray for the life of Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, and for the life of Balthasar his son, that their days may be upon earth as the days of heaven:* (Baruch 1:10–11). *That they may offer sacrifices of sweet savours unto the Elohim (God) of heaven, and pray for the life of the king, and of his sons.* (Ezra 6:10). The exiles in Babylon sent silver back to Jerusalem for burnt offerings and sin offerings and incense, and in the same breath asked that prayer be made for the life of Nebuchadnezzar and his son. Darius's decree in Ezra does the same two things together: it provides for the sacrifices to the Elohim (God) of heaven and asks that the priests pray for the life of the king and his sons. Offering and prayer for the ruler go together in both, under a Babylonian king and under a Persian one. Jeremiah had told the captives to seek the peace of the city they were carried to and to pray for it (Jeremiah 29:7), and here are the captives doing exactly that, with the altar at the center of it.$n$,$n$And they said, Behold, we have sent you money to buy you burnt offerings, and sin offerings, and incense, and prepare you manna, and offer upon the altar of Yahuah (God), our Elohim (God);$n$,true),
(44339,'S','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',4,36,4,36,$n$*O Jerusalem, look about you toward the east, and behold the joy that comes to you from Yahuah (God).* (Baruch 4:36). *Lift up thine eyes round about, and behold: all these gather themselves together, and come to thee. As I live, saith Yahuah (LORD), thou shalt surely clothe thee with them all, as with an ornament, and bind them on thee, as a bride doeth.* (Isaiah 49:18). Baruch tells Jerusalem to look east and behold the joy coming to her; Isaiah tells her to lift up her eyes round about and behold her children gathering and coming to her, so many she will wear them like a bride's ornaments. The same city is told to look up in both, and what she sees is the same: her own children coming back. Isaiah swears it by the life of Yahuah (LORD). Baruch, writing from the captivity, is holding Isaiah's promise up for the exiles to see.$n$,$n$O Jerusalem, look about you toward the east, and behold the joy that comes to you from Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(44345,'S','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',4,22,4,22,$n$*For my hope is in the Everlasting, that he will save you; and joy is come to me from the Holy One, because of the mercy which shall soon come to you from the Everlasting our Saviour.* (Baruch 4:22). *As one whom his mother comforteth, so will I comfort you; and ye shall be comforted in Jerusalem.* (Isaiah 66:13). Jerusalem speaks here as a mother whose children were carried off, and she tells them her hope: the Everlasting will save them, and joy has already come to her from the Holy One because of the mercy soon coming. Isaiah had promised that Yahuah (God) would comfort his people as a mother comforts, and that they would be comforted in Jerusalem. The mother's joy in Baruch and the mother's comfort in Isaiah are one promise, told from the city's side and from the side of Yahuah (God). The comfort is the children coming home; the chapter ends with them gathered from east to west (Baruch 4:37).$n$,$n$For my hope is in the Everlasting, that he will save you; and joy is come to me from the Holy One, because of the mercy which shall soon come to you from the Everlasting our Saviour.$n$,true),
(44355,'S','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',4,1,4,1,$n$*This is the book of the commandments of Yahuah (God), and the law that endureth for ever: all they that keep it shall come to life; but such as leave it shall die. Turn you, O Jacob, and take hold of it: walk in the presence of the light thereof, that you may be illuminated.* (Baruch 4:1–2). *But the word is very nigh unto thee, in thy mouth, and in thy heart, that thou mayest do it.* (Deuteronomy 30:14). Baruch sets the book of the commandments before Jacob and says take hold of it, walk in its light. Moses had said the same about the same book: the word is not up in heaven or across the sea, it is very near you, in your mouth and in your heart, so that you can do it (Deuteronomy 30:11–14). Both speak of the Torah as a thing within reach, not an impossible standard set up to show failure but a lamp handed to a people to walk by. Baruch's turn you, O Jacob, and take hold of it is Moses's that thou mayest do it, spoken again to the exiles.$n$,$n$This is the book of the commandments of Yahuah (God), and the law that endureth for ever: all they that keep it shall come to life; but such as leave it shall die.$n$,true),
(44383,'S','apocrypha','baruch-with-the-letter-of-jeremiah',6,51,6,51,$n$*And it shall manifestly appear to all nations and kings that they are no gods, but the works of men’s hands, and that there is no work of Yahuah (God) in them.* (Baruch 6:51). *And there ye shall serve gods, the work of men’s hands, wood and stone, which neither see, nor hear, nor eat, nor smell.* (Deuteronomy 4:28). Moses told Yashar'el (Israel) ahead of time what exile would look like: scattered among the nations, they would serve gods that were the work of men's hands, blind and deaf and lifeless. The letter in Baruch is written from inside that exile, in Babylon, where the gods are exactly what Moses described, wood overlaid with silver and gold. What Deuteronomy gave as the curse for forsaking the covenant, Baruch watches play out, and he points at the idols and says what Moses said: there is no work of Yahuah (God) in them. The exile was never only a change of address; it was being handed over to the very idols that cannot see or hear, until the people would know the difference and come home.$n$,$n$And it shall manifestly appear to all nations and kings that they are no gods, but the works of men’s hands, and that there is no work of Yahuah (God) in them.$n$,true),
(44509,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',1,63,1,63,$n$*Wherefore they chose rather to die, that they might not be defiled with meats, and that they might not profane the holy covenant: so then they died.* (1 Maccabees 1:63). *But said, Let us die all in our innocency: heaven and earth will testify for us, that you put us to death wrongfully.* (1 Maccabees 2:37). The two verses sit a chapter apart and describe the same resolve. In chapter 1 the faithful choose death over defilement so the holy covenant is not profaned. In chapter 2 a company hiding in the wilderness is attacked on the sabbath and answers, let us die all in our innocency, and calls heaven and earth to witness that they are being killed wrongfully. Neither group bargained with the covenant to buy a day of life. Their deaths are the measure of what the law of Yahuah (God) was worth to them, and the book records them so the reader will know what the covenant cost before the Maccabees ever drew a sword.$n$,$n$Wherefore they chose rather to die, that they might not be defiled with meats, and that they might not profane the holy covenant: so then they died.$n$,true),
(44573,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',6,54,6,54,$n$*Yet at the last, their vessels being without victuals, (for that it was the seventh year, and they in Judea that were delivered from the Gentiles, had eaten up the residue of the store;) There were but a few left in the sanctuary, because the famine did so prevail against them, that they were fain to disperse themselves, every man to his own place.* (1 Maccabees 6:53–54). *But the seventh year thou shalt let it rest and lie still; that the poor of thy people may eat: and what they leave the beasts of the field shall eat. In like manner thou shalt deal with thy vineyard, and with thy oliveyard.* (Exodus 23:11). The defenders in the sanctuary were not broken for want of courage but for want of bread, and the book says why: it was the seventh year, and the land had rested. Exodus commands that in the seventh year the land lie still so the poor may eat, and the people of Judea had kept it even with an army at the gates. The handful left in the sanctuary dispersed because there was nothing in the storehouse, and there was nothing in the storehouse because they had obeyed Yahuah (God). The book records the cost without apology, because the sabbath of the land was his commandment and they kept it.$n$,$n$There were but a few left in the sanctuary, because the famine did so prevail against them, that they were fain to disperse themselves, every man to his own place.$n$,true),
(44607,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',8,18,8,18,$n$*And to intreat them that they would take the yoke from them; for they saw that the kingdom of the Grecians did oppress Yashar'el (Israel) with servitude.* (1 Maccabees 8:18). *Woe to them that go down to Egypt for help; and stay on horses, and trust in chariots, because they are many; and in horsemen, because they are very strong; but they look not unto the Holy One of Yashar'el (Israel), neither seek Yahuah (LORD)!* (Isaiah 31:1). Judas sent to Rome asking them to take the Greek yoke off Yashar'el (Israel). Isaiah had pronounced woe on those who go down to Egypt for help and trust in horses and chariots because they are many, instead of looking to the Holy One of Yashar'el (Israel). Rome was many and very strong, and the league was made, and in time the yoke of Rome lay heavier on the land than the yoke of the Greeks ever had. The verse in 1 Maccabees does not condemn the embassy, but Isaiah does, and the reader who has both in front of him can see what the arm of flesh bought.$n$,$n$And to intreat them that they would take the yoke from them; for they saw that the kingdom of the Grecians did oppress Yashar'el (Israel) with servitude.$n$,true),
(44669,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,48,13,48,$n$Zechariah 14:21 — *Yea, every pot in Jerusalem and in Yahudah (Judah) shall be holiness unto Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts): and all they that sacrifice shall come and take of them, and seethe therein: and in that day there shall be no more the Canaanite in the house of Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).* Simon's purged stronghold of law-keepers in 1 Maccabees 13:48 foreshadows the day when all Jerusalem is holiness and no defiled thing remains.$n$,$n$Yea, he put all uncleanness out of it, and placed such men there as would keep the law, and made it stronger than it was before, and built in it a dwellingplace for himself.$n$,false),
(44670,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,48,13,48,$n$Isaiah 52:1 — *Awake, awake; put on thy strength, O Zion; put on thy beautiful garments, O Jerusalem, the holy city: for henceforth there shall no more come into thee the uncircumcised and the unclean.* Simon putting all uncleanness out and setting law-keepers there in 1 Maccabees 13:48 is the holy city into which the unclean shall come no more.$n$,$n$Yea, he put all uncleanness out of it, and placed such men there as would keep the law, and made it stronger than it was before, and built in it a dwellingplace for himself.$n$,false),
(44671,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,47,13,47,$n$Ezekiel 36:25 — *Then will I sprinkle clean water upon you, and ye shall be clean: from all your filthiness, and from all your idols, will I cleanse you.* Simon cleansing the houses of the idols in 1 Maccabees 13:47 enacts the promised cleansing of the covenant people from their idols.$n$,$n$So Simon was appeased toward them, and fought no more against them, but put them out of the city, and cleansed the houses in which the idols were, and so entered into it with songs and thanksgiving.$n$,false),
(44672,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,52,13,52,$n$John 10:22 — *And it was at Jerusalem the feast of the dedication, and it was winter.* Simon's ordaining the day of the cleansed citadel to be kept every year in 1 Maccabees 13:52 belongs to the same feast of dedication the Messiah Himself kept in Jerusalem.$n$,$n$He ordained also that that day should be kept every year with gladness. Moreover the hill of the temple that was by the tower he made stronger than it was, and there he dwelt himself with his company.$n$,false),
(44673,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,41,13,41,$n$Isaiah 14:25 — *That I will break the Assyrian in my land, and upon my mountains tread him under foot: then shall his yoke depart from off them, and his burden depart from off their shoulders.* Yahuah's pledge to break the oppressor and lift the burden from the shoulder is realized when the yoke departs from Israel in 1 Maccabees 13:41.$n$,$n$Thus the yoke of the heathen was taken away from Yashar'el (Israel) in the hundred and seventieth year.$n$,false),
(44674,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,41,13,41,$n$Isaiah 10:27 — *And it shall come to pass in that day, that his burden shall be taken away from off thy shoulder, and his yoke from off thy neck, and the yoke shall be destroyed because of the anointing.* The yoke destroyed because of the anointing answers the heathen yoke lifted from Israel in 1 Maccabees 13:41.$n$,$n$Thus the yoke of the heathen was taken away from Yashar'el (Israel) in the hundred and seventieth year.$n$,false),
(44675,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,41,13,41,$n$Isaiah 9:4 — *For thou hast broken the yoke of his burden, and the staff of his shoulder, the rod of his oppressor, as in the day of Midian.* The heathen yoke taken away in 1 Maccabees 13:41 is the broken yoke Isaiah sang of in the day of the Child to be born.$n$,$n$Thus the yoke of the heathen was taken away from Yashar'el (Israel) in the hundred and seventieth year.$n$,false),
(44676,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,42,13,42,$n$Leviticus 25:10 — *And ye shall hallow the fiftieth year, and proclaim liberty throughout all the land unto all the inhabitants thereof: it shall be a jubile unto you; and ye shall return every man unto his possession, and ye shall return every man unto his family.* Israel's new reckoning from the first year of Simon in 1 Maccabees 13:42 is the jubilee proclamation of liberty written into their dating itself.$n$,$n$Then the people of Yashar'el (Israel) began to write in their instruments and contracts, In the first year of Simon the high priest, the governor and leader of the Yahudim (Jews).$n$,false),
(44677,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,4,13,4,$n$Daniel 12:2 — *And many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt.* Daniel had already sealed the everlasting life that crowns the brothers fallen for Israel in 1 Maccabees 13:4.$n$,$n$By reason of which all my brothers are slain for Yashar'el (Israel)’s sake, and I am left alone.$n$,false),
(44678,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',7,9,7,9,$n$2 Maccabees 7:9 — *And when he was at the last gasp, he said, You like a fury takest us out of this present life, but the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.* The hope behind the brothers' deaths in 1 Maccabees 13:4 is voiced plainly by the martyr brother under the same persecution.$n$,$n$And when he was at the last gasp, he said, You like a fury takest us out of this present life, but the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.$n$,false),
(44679,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,4,13,4,$n$Hebrews 11:35 — *Women received their dead raised to life again: and others were tortured, not accepting deliverance; that they might obtain a better resurrection:* The brothers slain in 1 Maccabees 13:4 are among those tortured who looked past deliverance to the better resurrection.$n$,$n$By reason of which all my brothers are slain for Yashar'el (Israel)’s sake, and I am left alone.$n$,false),
(44680,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,4,13,4,$n$Numbers 25:11 — *Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron the priest, hath turned my wrath away from the children of Yashar'el (Israel), while he was zealous for my sake among them, that I consumed not the children of Yashar'el (Israel) in my jealousy.* The brothers slain for Israel's sake in 1 Maccabees 13:4 carry the priestly zeal of Phinehas, who stood for the covenant.$n$,$n$By reason of which all my brothers are slain for Yashar'el (Israel)’s sake, and I am left alone.$n$,false),
(44681,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',2,50,2,50,$n$1 Maccabees 2:50 — *Now therefore, my sons, be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.* Simon's reckoning in 1 Maccabees 13:3 of all the house has done for the laws and the sanctuary is the fulfilment of Mattathias' dying charge.$n$,$n$Now therefore, my sons, be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.$n$,false),
(44682,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,9,13,9,$n$Zechariah 9:13 — *When I have bent Yahudah (Judah) for me, filled the bow with Ephraim, and raised up thy sons, O Zion, against thy sons, O Greece, and made thee as the sword of a mighty man.* The people pledging Simon to fight their battles in 1 Maccabees 13:9 is the prophet's bow of Judah bent against the sons of Greece.$n$,$n$Fight you our battles, and whatsoever, you commandest us, that will we do.$n$,false),
(44683,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',13,5,13,5,$n$John 15:13 — *Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends.* Simon's refusal to spare his own life for the nation in 1 Maccabees 13:5 is the shape of the love the Messiah names and then perfects.$n$,$n$Now therefore be it far from me, that I should spare my own life in any time of trouble: for I am no better than my brothers.$n$,false),
(44684,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',9,30,9,30,$n$1 Maccabees 9:30 — *Now therefore we have chosen you this day to be our prince and captain in his stead, that you may fight our battles.* The same acclamation once given to Jonathan now passes to Simon in 1 Maccabees 13:8 — the remnant's leadership handed brother to brother, unbroken.$n$,$n$Now therefore we have chosen you this day to be our prince and captain in his stead, that you may fight our battles.$n$,false),
(44698,'S','apocrypha','1-maccabees',14,12,14,12,$n$*For every man sat under his vine and his fig tree, and there was none to fray them:* (1 Maccabees 14:12). *In that day, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), shall ye call every man his neighbour under the vine and under the fig tree.* (Zechariah 3:10). Under Simon the land had peace, and every man sat under his vine and his fig tree with no one to frighten him. Zechariah uses the same picture, but he fixes it to a day, in that day, and the verses before it name whose day it is: the BRANCH, the servant Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) will bring forth. Simon's peace lasted a few years and ended in blood at a banquet. The vine and the fig tree in 1 Maccabees 14 are a foretaste the people were given; the vine and the fig tree in Zechariah 3 are the rest the Messiah (Christ) brings, which no one will take away.$n$,$n$For every man sat under his vine and his fig tree, and there was none to fray them:$n$,true),
(44745,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,38,3,38,$n$2 Thessalonians 1:6 — *Seeing it is a righteous thing with Elohim (God) to recompense tribulation to them that trouble you;* The well-scourged spoiler of 2 Maccabees 3:38 shows the righteous recompense the apostle promises to all who trouble the covenant people — it ain't new.$n$,$n$If you have any enemy or traitor, send him thither, and you shall receive him well scourged, if he escape with his life: for in that place, no doubt, there is an especial power of Yahuah (God).$n$,false),
(44746,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,39,3,39,$n$Isaiah 31:5 — *As birds flying, so will Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) defend Jerusalem; defending also he will deliver it; and passing over he will preserve it.* The LORD who defends and preserves Jerusalem is the same that defendeth His place against all who would hurt it in 2 Maccabees 3:39.$n$,$n$For he that dwells in heaven has his eye on that place, and defendeth it; and he beateth and destroys them that come to hurt it.$n$,false),
(44747,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,39,3,39,$n$Zechariah 2:5 — *For I, saith Yahuah (LORD), will be unto her a wall of fire round about, and will be the glory in the midst of her.* The God who keeps His eye on the place and beats off its enemies in 2 Maccabees 3:39 is the wall of fire round His city.$n$,$n$For he that dwells in heaven has his eye on that place, and defendeth it; and he beateth and destroys them that come to hurt it.$n$,false),
(44748,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,39,3,39,$n$Psalm 127:1 — *Except Yahuah (LORD) build the house, they labour in vain that build it: except Yahuah (LORD) keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain.* Heliodorus confesses in 2 Maccabees 3:39 that He who dwells in heaven keeps the place no man's labour could defend.$n$,$n$For he that dwells in heaven has his eye on that place, and defendeth it; and he beateth and destroys them that come to hurt it.$n$,false),
(44749,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',9,5,9,5,$n$2 Maccabees 9:5 — *But Yahuah (God) Almighty, Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel), struck him with an incurable and invisible plague: or as soon as he had spoken these words, a pain of the bowels that was remediless came upon him, and sore torments of the inner parts;* The treasurer Heliodorus is felled and spared at a priest's prayer in 2 Maccabees 3:27, but the unrepentant Antiochus who likewise raged against the sanctuary is struck without remedy.$n$,$n$But Yahuah (God) Almighty, Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel), struck him with an incurable and invisible plague: or as soon as he had spoken these words, a pain of the bowels that was remediless came upon him, and sore torments of the inner parts;$n$,false),
(44750,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,29,3,29,$n$Psalm 76:7 — *Thou, even thou, art to be feared: and who may stand in thy sight when once thou art angry?* Heliodorus lies cast down and speechless in 2 Maccabees 3:29 because no man may stand before the wrath of Yahuah.$n$,$n$For he by the hand of Yahuah (God) was cast down, and lay speechless without all hope of life.$n$,false),
(44751,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,27,3,27,$n$Acts 9:4 — *And he fell to the earth, and heard a voice saying unto him, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?* Like Saul thrown to the earth and then made a herald, Heliodorus falls suddenly and is sent to declare the power he fought, in 2 Maccabees 3:27,34.$n$,$n$And Heliodorus fell suddenly to the ground, and was compassed with great darkness: but they that were with him took him up, and put him into a litter.$n$,false),
(44752,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,27,3,27,$n$Acts 9:3 — *And as he journeyed, he came near Damascus: and suddenly there shined round about him a light from heaven:* As light from heaven felled the persecutor Saul, the dread apparition compasses Heliodorus with great darkness and casts him down in 2 Maccabees 3:27.$n$,$n$And Heliodorus fell suddenly to the ground, and was compassed with great darkness: but they that were with him took him up, and put him into a litter.$n$,false),
(44753,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,25,3,25,$n$Psalm 35:5 — *Let them be as chaff before the wind: and let the angel of Yahuah (LORD) chase them.* The fiercely running rider of 2 Maccabees 3:25 is the chasing angel the psalmist summoned against the oppressors of the righteous.$n$,$n$For there appeared to them an horse with a terrible rider upon him, and adorned with a very fair covering, and he ran fiercely, and struck at Heliodorus with his forefeet, and it seemed that he that sat upon the horse had complete harness of gold.$n$,false),
(44754,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,24,3,24,$n$2 Chronicles 32:21 — *And Yahuah (LORD) sent an angel, which cut off all the mighty men of valour, and the leaders and captains in the camp of the king of Assyria. So he returned with shame of face to his own land...* As one angel cut off Sennacherib's captains, so the apparition routs the king's treasurer in 2 Maccabees 3:24.$n$,$n$Now as he was there present himself with his guard about the treasury, Yahuah (God) of spirits, and the Prince of all power, caused a great apparition, so that all that presumed to come in with him were astonished at the power of Yahuah (God), and fainted, and were sore afraid.$n$,false),
(44755,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,24,3,24,$n$2 Kings 19:35 — *And it came to pass that night, that the angel of Yahuah (LORD) went out, and smote in the camp of the Assyrians an hundred fourscore and five thousand: and when they arose early in the morning, behold, they were all dead corpses.* The same heaven that smote Assyria for besieging Jerusalem strikes Heliodorus' host in 2 Maccabees 3:24.$n$,$n$Now as he was there present himself with his guard about the treasury, Yahuah (God) of spirits, and the Prince of all power, caused a great apparition, so that all that presumed to come in with him were astonished at the power of Yahuah (God), and fainted, and were sore afraid.$n$,false),
(44756,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,24,3,24,$n$Joshua 5:14 — *And he said, Nay; but as captain of the host of Yahuah (LORD) am I now come. And Joshua fell on his face to the earth, and did worship, and said unto him, What saith my lord unto his servant?* The Prince of all power who causes the apparition in 2 Maccabees 3:24 is the captain of Yahuah's host come to defend His own.$n$,$n$Now as he was there present himself with his guard about the treasury, Yahuah (God) of spirits, and the Prince of all power, caused a great apparition, so that all that presumed to come in with him were astonished at the power of Yahuah (God), and fainted, and were sore afraid.$n$,false),
(44757,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,25,3,25,$n$*For there appeared to them an horse with a terrible rider upon him, and adorned with a very fair covering, and he ran fiercely, and struck at Heliodorus with his forefeet, and it seemed that he that sat upon the horse had complete harness of gold.* (2 Maccabees 3:25). *And it came to pass, when Joshua was by Jericho, that he lifted up his eyes and looked, and, behold, there stood a man over against him with his sword drawn in his hand: and Joshua went unto him, and said unto him, Art thou for us, or for our adversaries?* (Joshua 5:13). When Heliodorus pressed into the treasury, a rider in golden harness appeared and struck at him, and all who came in with him were astonished at the power of Yahuah (God). Joshua met the same kind of presence by Jericho: a man standing with his sword drawn, who named himself captain of the host of Yahuah (LORD) and told Joshua the ground he stood on was holy (Joshua 5:14–15). In both places an armed one stands up for the place and the people of Yahuah (God) when a stranger comes against them. The captain who stood for Jericho stood again in the treasury, and the defense of the temple was not left to men.$n$,$n$For there appeared to them an horse with a terrible rider upon him, and adorned with a very fair covering, and he ran fiercely, and struck at Heliodorus with his forefeet, and it seemed that he that sat upon the horse had complete harness of gold.$n$,true),
(44759,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,22,3,22,$n$Psalm 127:1 — *Except Yahuah (LORD) build the house, they labour in vain that build it: except Yahuah (LORD) keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain.* The people commit the keeping to the Almighty in 2 Maccabees 3:22 because no human watch can guard the house.$n$,$n$They then called upon the Almighty Yahuah (God) to keep the things committed of trust safe and sure for those that had committed them.$n$,false),
(44760,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,15,3,15,$n$Numbers 16:48 — *And he stood between the dead and the living; and the plague was stayed.* As Aaron's priestly standing turned back wrath, so the priests' supplication stands between the temple and the spoiler at 2 Maccabees 3:15.$n$,$n$But the priests, prostrating themselves before the altar in their priests’ vestments, called to heaven upon him that made a law concerning things given to be kept, that they should safely be preserved for such as had committed them to be kept.$n$,false),
(44761,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,15,3,15,$n$Joel 2:17 — *Let the priests, the ministers of Yahuah (LORD), weep between the porch and the altar, and let them say, Spare thy people, O Yahuah (LORD), and give not thine heritage to reproach, that the heathen should rule over them: wherefore should they say among the people, Where is their Elohim (God)?* The very prophetic liturgy the priests enact prostrate before the altar in 2 Maccabees 3:15.$n$,$n$But the priests, prostrating themselves before the altar in their priests’ vestments, called to heaven upon him that made a law concerning things given to be kept, that they should safely be preserved for such as had committed them to be kept.$n$,false),
(44762,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *Then the high priest told him that there was such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children:* (2 Maccabees 3:10). When Heliodorus came to seize the temple treasury, the high priest told him the money was laid up for widows and fatherless children. The temple was keeping what Tobit charged his son to give: the poor had a store in the house of Yahuah (God). The library shows alms as a private duty in Tobit and a public trust in Maccabees, and the same mercy stands behind both.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(44765,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,10,3,10,$n$Deuteronomy 10:18 — *He doth execute the judgment of the fatherless and widow, and loveth the stranger, in giving him food and raiment.* Yahuah Himself defends the cause whose relief was laid up in the house at 2 Maccabees 3:10.$n$,$n$Then the high priest told him that there was such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children:$n$,false),
(44766,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,10,3,10,$n$*Then the high priest told him that there was such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children:* (2 Maccabees 3:10). *Ye shall not afflict any widow, or fatherless child.* (Exodus 22:22). Heliodorus came to Jerusalem to take the temple treasury for the king, and the high priest told him what the money was: provision laid up for widows and the fatherless. The Torah had already spoken over that money. Yahuah (God) commanded that no widow or fatherless child be afflicted, and the verses that follow promise that his wrath will wax hot against any who do it (Exodus 22:23–24). What looked to Heliodorus like a king's revenue was the very thing the Torah guards, and the rest of the chapter shows the guard was real.$n$,$n$Then the high priest told him that there was such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children:$n$,true),
(44777,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',4,14,4,14,$n$*That the priests had no courage to serve any more at the altar, but despising the temple, and neglecting the sacrifices, hastened to be partakers of the unlawful allowance in the place of exercise, after the game of Discus called them forth;* (2 Maccabees 4:14). *But ye are departed out of the way; ye have caused many to stumble at the law; ye have corrupted the covenant of Levi, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).* (Malachi 2:8). Under Jason the priests lost their stomach for the altar. They despised the temple, neglected the sacrifices, and ran to the Greek gymnasium when the discus called, taking their share of what the Torah forbade. Malachi had already said what that is: priests departed out of the way, causing many to stumble at the law, corrupting the covenant of Levi. The threat to the temple in these chapters did not begin with Antiochus; it began at the altar, with the men set apart to serve there. The covenant of Levi was broken from the inside before any king broke in.$n$,$n$That the priests had no courage to serve any more at the altar, but despising the temple, and neglecting the sacrifices, hastened to be partakers of the unlawful allowance in the place of exercise, after the game of Discus called them forth;$n$,true),
(44786,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',4,8,4,8,$n$*Promising to the king by intercession three hundred and threescore talents of silver, and of another revenue eighty talents:* (2 Maccabees 4:8). *The heads thereof judge for reward, and the priests thereof teach for hire, and the prophets thereof divine for money: yet will they lean upon Yahuah (LORD), and say, Is not Yahuah (LORD) among us? none evil can come upon us.* (Micah 3:11). Jason did not inherit the high priesthood; he bought it. He went to Antiochus behind his brother Onias and promised three hundred and sixty talents of silver, with eighty more from another revenue, to be made high priest. Micah had already named that transaction: heads who judge for reward, priests who teach for hire, prophets who divine for money, all of them still saying Yahuah (LORD) is among us and no evil can come. The office was turned into a purchase, and the chapters that follow show how much evil did come. A priesthood bought for silver is the thing the prophet condemned, in whatever age it appears.$n$,$n$Promising to the king by intercession three hundred and threescore talents of silver, and of another revenue eighty talents:$n$,true),
(44862,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,30,10,30,$n$Exodus 15:3 — *Yahuah (LORD) is a man of war: Yahuah (LORD) is his name.* The heavenly riders who shoot arrows and lightnings to deliver Maccabeus in 2 Maccabees 10:30 make plain that the battle belongs to Yahuah the man of war.$n$,$n$And took Maccabeus between them, and covered him on every side weapons, and kept him safe, but shot arrows and lightnings against the enemies: so that being confounded with blindness, and full of trouble, they were killed.$n$,false),
(44863,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,38,10,38,$n$Exodus 15:1 — *Then sang Moses and the children of Yashar'el (Israel) this song unto Yahuah (LORD), and spake, saying, I will sing unto Yahuah (LORD), for he hath triumphed gloriously: the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea.* The psalms and thanksgiving with which the people praise Yahuah for the victory in 2 Maccabees 10:38 reprise the song Moses sang when the LORD overthrew horse and rider.$n$,$n$When this was done, they praised Yahuah (God) with psalms and thanksgiving, who had done so great things for Yashar'el (Israel), and given them the victory.$n$,false),
(44864,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,29,10,29,$n$2 Kings 6:17 — *And Elisha prayed, and said, Yahuah (LORD), I pray thee, open his eyes, that he may see. And Yahuah (LORD) opened the eyes of the young man; and he saw: and, behold, the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire round about Elisha.* The five comely horsemen from heaven who shield Maccabeus in 2 Maccabees 10:29 are the same heavenly cavalry Elisha's servant was given eyes to see.$n$,$n$But when the battle waxed strong, there appeared to the enemies from heaven five comely men upon horses, with bridles of gold, and two of them led the Yahudim (Jews),$n$,false),
(44865,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,36,10,36,$n$Numbers 25:13 — *And he shall have it, and his seed after him, even the covenant of an everlasting priesthood; because he was zealous for his Elohim (God), and made an atonement for the children of Yashar'el (Israel).* The everlasting covenant given for Phinehas' zeal is the inheritance the zealous Maccabean house claims as it burns out the blasphemers in 2 Maccabees 10:36.$n$,$n$Others likewise ascending after them, whiles they were busied with them that were within, burnt the towers, and kindling fires burnt the blasphemers alive; and others broke open the gates, and, having received in the rest of the army, took the city,$n$,false),
(44866,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,35,10,35,$n$*Nevertheless upon the fifth day early twenty young men of Maccabeus’ company, inflamed with anger because of the blasphemies, assaulted the wall manly, and with a fierce courage killed all that they met withal.* (2 Maccabees 10:35). *Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron the priest, hath turned my wrath away from the children of Yashar'el (Israel), while he was zealous for my sake among them, that I consumed not the children of Yashar'el (Israel) in my jealousy.* (Numbers 25:11). The men inside the city trusted their walls and blasphemed from them, and on the fifth day twenty of Judas's young men, inflamed by the blasphemies, went up the wall. The book names the cause of their anger: not the siege, not the plunder, but the blasphemies. That is the zeal of Phinehas. Yahuah (God) said Phinehas turned his wrath away from Yashar'el (Israel) because he was zealous for his sake, and for that zeal gave him a covenant of peace (Numbers 25:12–13). Zeal for the name of Yahuah (God) when his name is mocked is the thread that runs from Phinehas's javelin to Judas's twenty.$n$,$n$Nevertheless upon the fifth day early twenty young men of Maccabeus’ company, inflamed with anger because of the blasphemies, assaulted the wall manly, and with a fierce courage killed all that they met withal.$n$,true),
(44867,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,4,10,4,$n$Hebrews 8:12 — *For I will be merciful to their unrighteousness, and their sins and their iniquities will I remember no more.* The very mercy the Maccabees ask to be chastened with in 2 Maccabees 10:4 is the covenant promise — corrected in mercy, their sins not held against them.$n$,$n$When that was done, they fell flat down, and besought Yahuah (God) that they might come no more into such troubles; but if they sinned any more against him, that he himself would chasten them with mercy, and that they might not be delivered to the blasphemous and barbarous nations.$n$,false),
(44868,'T','apocrypha','tobit',13,5,13,5,$n$Tobit 13:5 — *And he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us.* Tobit voices the same covenant confidence the Maccabees pray in 2 Maccabees 10:4 — the chastening of the scattered people ends in mercy and regathering, not abandonment.$n$,$n$And he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us.$n$,false),
(44869,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,4,10,4,$n$2 Samuel 24:14 — *And David said unto Gad, I am in a great strait: let us fall now into the hand of Yahuah (the LORD); for his mercies are great: and let me not fall into the hand of man.* David's choice to fall into Yahuah's merciful hand rather than man's is precisely the prayer of 2 Maccabees 10:4 — chasten us, but spare us the blasphemous nations.$n$,$n$When that was done, they fell flat down, and besought Yahuah (God) that they might come no more into such troubles; but if they sinned any more against him, that he himself would chasten them with mercy, and that they might not be delivered to the blasphemous and barbarous nations.$n$,false),
(44870,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,1,10,1,$n$Matthew 24:15 — *When ye therefore shall see the abomination of desolation, spoken of by Daniel the prophet, stand in the holy place, (whoso readeth, let him understand:)* The recovery of the defiled temple in 2 Maccabees 10:1 is the historical first fulfillment of the abomination the Messiah warns will yet stand in the holy place.$n$,$n$Now Maccabeus and his company, Yahuah (God) guiding them, recovered the temple and the city:$n$,false),
(44871,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,5,10,5,$n$Daniel 8:13 — *Then I heard one saint speaking, and another saint said unto that certain saint which spake, How long shall be the vision concerning the daily sacrifice, and the transgression of desolation, to give both the sanctuary and the host to be trodden under foot?* The cleansing on the very day of the profanation in 2 Maccabees 10:5 answers the angel's 'how long' over the trodden sanctuary.$n$,$n$Now upon the same day that the strangers profaned the temple, on the very same day it was cleansed again, even the five and twentieth day of the same month, which is Casleu.$n$,false),
(44872,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,2,10,2,$n$Daniel 11:31 — *And arms shall stand on his part, and they shall pollute the sanctuary of strength, and shall take away the daily sacrifice, and they shall place the abomination that maketh desolate.* The heathen altars and chapels the Maccabees pull down in 2 Maccabees 10:2 are exactly the abomination Daniel foresaw Antiochus placing in the polluted sanctuary.$n$,$n$But the altars which the heathen had built in the open street, and also the chapels, they pulled down.$n$,false),
(44873,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,7,10,7,$n$Zechariah 14:16 — *And it shall come to pass, that every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), and to keep the feast of tabernacles.* The tabernacles-keeping with palms and psalms in 2 Maccabees 10:7 prefigures the day all nations go up yearly to keep that same feast before the King.$n$,$n$Therefore they bare branches, and fair boughs, and palms also, and sang psalms to him that had given them good success in cleansing his place.$n$,false),
(44874,'T','apocrypha','1-maccabees',4,59,4,59,$n$1 Maccabees 4:59 — *Moreover Judas and his brothers with the whole congregation of Yashar'el (Israel) ordained, that the days of the dedication of the altar should be kept in their season from year to year by the space of eight days, from the five and twentieth day of the month Casleu, with mirth and gladness.* The brother book records the same eight-day statute on the twenty-fifth of Casleu that 2 Maccabees 10:8 decrees for the whole nation.$n$,$n$Moreover Judas and his brothers with the whole congregation of Yashar'el (Israel) ordained, that the days of the dedication of the altar should be kept in their season from year to year by the space of eight days, from the five and twentieth day of the month Casleu, with mirth and gladness.$n$,false),
(44875,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,8,10,8,$n$John 10:22 — *And it was at Jerusalem the feast of the dedication, and it was winter.* The yearly winter feast the whole nation ordained in 2 Maccabees 10:8 is the very Feast of Dedication the Messiah kept in the temple — He honoured the Maccabean rededication.$n$,$n$They ordained also by a common statute and decree, That every year those days should be kept of the whole nation of the Yahudim (Jews).$n$,false),
(44876,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,6,10,6,$n$Nehemiah 8:15 — *And that they should publish and proclaim in all their cities, and in Jerusalem, saying, Go forth unto the mount, and fetch olive branches, and pine branches, and myrtle branches, and palm branches, and branches of thick trees, to make booths, as it is written.* As the returned remnant under Ezra rediscovered the booths-feast and proclaimed it, so the Maccabees in 2 Maccabees 10:6 keep tabernacles as a people lately delivered out of the wilderness of dens and beasts.$n$,$n$And they kept the eight days with gladness, as in the feast of the tabernacles, remembering that not long afore they had held the feast of the tabernacles, when as they wandered in the mountains and dens like beasts.$n$,false),
(44877,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',10,7,10,7,$n$Leviticus 23:40 — *And ye shall take you on the first day the boughs of goodly trees, branches of palm trees, and the boughs of thick trees, and willows of the brook; and ye shall rejoice before Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God) seven days.* The branches, boughs, and palms the Maccabees bore in 2 Maccabees 10:7 are the prescribed Sukkot rite of Torah, kept out of season because they had been hunted in the mountains.$n$,$n$Therefore they bare branches, and fair boughs, and palms also, and sang psalms to him that had given them good success in cleansing his place.$n$,false),
(44878,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,25,11,25,$n$John 10:22 — *And it was at Jerusalem the feast of the dedication, and it was winter.* The temple restored to the Jews in 2 Maccabees 11:25 is the house whose rededication became the very feast the Messiah Himself kept at Jerusalem.$n$,$n$Wherefore our mind is, that this nation shall be in rest, and we have determined to restore them their temple, that they may live according to the customs of their forefathers.$n$,false),
(44879,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,25,11,25,$n$Ezra 7:25 — *And thou, Ezra, after the wisdom of thy Elohim (God), that is in thine hand, set magistrates and judges, which may judge all the people that are beyond the river, all such as know the laws of thy Elohim (God); and teach ye them that know them not.* As Persia once decreed the restoration of Yahuah's law over His people, so the king's letter in 2 Maccabees 11:25 is forced to restore the temple and the customs of the forefathers.$n$,$n$Wherefore our mind is, that this nation shall be in rest, and we have determined to restore them their temple, that they may live according to the customs of their forefathers.$n$,false),
(44880,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,31,11,31,$n$Hebrews 11:35 — *Women received their dead raised to life again: and others were tortured, not accepting deliverance; that they might obtain a better resurrection.* The Maccabean refusal to abandon their own meats and laws in 2 Maccabees 11:31 is the very faith Hebrews crowns with the better resurrection.$n$,$n$And the Yahudim (Jews) shall use their own kind of meats and laws, as before; and none of them any manner of ways shall be molested for things ignorantly done.$n$,false),
(44881,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',7,9,7,9,$n$2 Maccabees 7:9 — *And when he was at the last gasp, he said, You like a fury takest us out of this present life, but the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.* The laws Antiochus is forced to restore in 2 Maccabees 11:24 are the very laws the seven brothers died for, in the hope of resurrection.$n$,$n$And when he was at the last gasp, he said, You like a fury takest us out of this present life, but the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.$n$,false),
(44882,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,24,11,24,$n$Psalm 119:44 — *So shall I keep thy law continually for ever and ever.* The manner of living the Jews would not surrender in 2 Maccabees 11:24 is exactly this enduring delight to keep Yahuah's law for ever.$n$,$n$We understand also that the Yahudim (Jews) would not consent to our father, for to be brought to the custom of the Gentiles, but had rather keep their own manner of living: for the which cause they require of us, that we should suffer them to live after their own laws.$n$,false),
(44883,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,13,11,13,$n$Revelation 19:14 — *And the armies which were in heaven followed him upon white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and clean.* The Almighty who could not be resisted in 2 Maccabees 11:13 is He whose heavenly armies ride behind Him in white at the last.$n$,$n$Who, as he was a man of understanding, casting with himself what loss he had had, and considering that the Hebrews could not be overcome, because the Almighty Yahuah (God) helped them, he sent to them,$n$,false),
(44884,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,13,11,13,$n$2 Kings 6:16 — *And he answered, Fear not: for they that be with us are more than they that be with them.* Lysias' own conclusion in 2 Maccabees 11:13 that the Hebrews could not be overcome is the enemy's-eye proof of Elisha's word — heaven's host outnumbers every army of men.$n$,$n$Who, as he was a man of understanding, casting with himself what loss he had had, and considering that the Hebrews could not be overcome, because the Almighty Yahuah (God) helped them, he sent to them,$n$,false),
(44885,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,10,11,10,$n$2 Kings 6:17 — *And Elisha prayed, and said, Yahuah (LORD), I pray thee, open his eyes, that he may see. And Yahuah (LORD) opened the eyes of the young man; and he saw: and, behold, the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire round about Elisha.* The helper from heaven seen marching with Israel in 2 Maccabees 11:10 is that same unseen host of fiery horses and chariots.$n$,$n$Thus they marched forward in their armour, having an helper from heaven: for Yahuah (God) was merciful to them$n$,false),
(44886,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,8,11,8,$n$Revelation 19:11 — *And I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse; and he that sat upon him was called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he doth judge and make war.* The white horseman shaking his golden armour in 2 Maccabees 11:8 foreshadows the Rider Faithful and True who leads the armies of heaven.$n$,$n$And as they were at Jerusalem, there appeared before them on horseback one in white clothing, shaking his armour of gold.$n$,false),
(44887,'T','apocrypha','2-maccabees',3,25,3,25,$n$2 Maccabees 3:25 — *For there appeared to them an horse with a terrible rider upon him, and adorned with a very fair covering, and he ran fiercely, and struck at Heliodorus with his forefeet, and it seemed that he that sat upon the horse had complete harness of gold.* The golden-harnessed rider who smote Heliodorus at the temple is the same heavenly horseman who now leads Israel in 2 Maccabees 11:8.$n$,$n$For there appeared to them an horse with a terrible rider upon him, and adorned with a very fair covering, and he ran fiercely, and struck at Heliodorus with his forefeet, and it seemed that he that sat upon the horse had complete harness of gold.$n$,false),
(44888,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,8,11,8,$n$Joshua 5:14 — *And he said, Nay; but as captain of the host of Yahuah (LORD) am I now come. And Joshua fell on his face to the earth, and did worship, and said unto him, What saith my lord unto his servant?* The white-clad horseman of 2 Maccabees 11:8 is that Captain of the host of Yahuah, come to lead Israel's battle.$n$,$n$And as they were at Jerusalem, there appeared before them on horseback one in white clothing, shaking his armour of gold.$n$,false),
(44889,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,8,11,8,$n$Joshua 5:13 — *And it came to pass, when Joshua was by Jericho, that he lifted up his eyes and looked, and, behold, there stood a man over against him with his sword drawn in his hand: and Joshua went unto him, and said unto him, Art thou for us, or for our adversaries?* The same drawn-armed Figure that met Joshua at Jericho rides before the Maccabees at Jerusalem in 2 Maccabees 11:8.$n$,$n$And as they were at Jerusalem, there appeared before them on horseback one in white clothing, shaking his armour of gold.$n$,false),
(44890,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,6,11,6,$n$Exodus 23:22 — *But if thou shalt indeed obey his voice, and do all that I speak; then I will be an enemy unto thine enemies, and an adversary unto thine adversaries.* The condition of that heavenly help is covenant obedience — the very Torah-faithfulness for which the men of 2 Maccabees 11:6 are fighting.$n$,$n$Now when they that were with Maccabeus heard that he besieged the holds, they and all the people with lamentation and tears besought Yahuah (God) that he would send a good angel to deliver Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,false),
(44891,'S','apocrypha','2-maccabees',11,6,11,6,$n$Exodus 23:20 — *Behold, I send an Angel before thee, to keep thee in the way, and to bring thee into the place which I have prepared.* The good angel the Maccabees beg for in 2 Maccabees 11:6 is the very Angel Yahuah pledged to send before His covenant people.$n$,$n$Now when they that were with Maccabeus heard that he besieged the holds, they and all the people with lamentation and tears besought Yahuah (God) that he would send a good angel to deliver Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,false),
(45038,'S','apocrypha','1-esdras',7,14,7,14,$n$*And they kept the feast of unleavened bread seven days, making merry before Yahuah (God),* (1 Esdras 7:14). *Purge out therefore the old leaven, that ye may be a new lump, as ye are unleavened. For even Messiah (Christ) our passover is sacrificed for us:* (1 Corinthians 5:7). The exiles had come up out of Babylon, and the first thing they did after the Passover was keep the seven days of unleavened bread, glad before Yahuah (God). Paul writes to an assembly far from Jerusalem and tells them to purge out the old leaven for the same reason, because Messiah (Christ) our Passover has been slain, and the next line says it plainly, let us keep the feast. The lamb being slain did not end the feast; it is the reason the feast is kept. The returned remnant and the assembly at Corinth are keeping the same appointed days, and the one the days point to is Yahusha (Jesus).$n$,$n$And they kept the feast of unleavened bread seven days, making merry before Yahuah (God),$n$,true),
(45051,'S','apocrypha','1-esdras',8,89,8,89,$n$*O Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel), you are true: for we are left a root this day.* (1 Esdras 8:89). *O Yahuah Elohim (LORD God) of Yashar'el (Israel), thou art righteous: for we remain yet escaped, as it is this day: behold, we are before thee in our trespasses: for we cannot stand before thee because of this.* (Ezra 9:15). These are the same prayer told twice, once by Ezra and once by Esdras. In both, the man kneels with torn garments and confesses that Yahuah (God) is righteous and the people are not, that they are a root left this day, a remnant yet escaped, and that they cannot stand before him because of what they have done. The confession does not plead the people's merit; it pleads his faithfulness, you are true. A root left and a remnant escaped is how Yahuah (God) keeps his word to the fathers when the sons have broken theirs.$n$,$n$O Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel), you are true: for we are left a root this day.$n$,true),
(45054,'S','apocrypha','1-esdras',8,70,8,70,$n$*For both they and their sons have married with their daughters, and the holy seed is mixed with the strange people of the land; and from the beginning of this matter the rulers and the great men have been partakers of this iniquity.* (1 Esdras 8:70). *But yet in it shall be a tenth, and it shall return, and shall be eaten: as a teil tree, and as an oak, whose substance is in them, when they cast their leaves: so the holy seed shall be the substance thereof.* (Isaiah 6:13). Both verses call the people the holy seed, and both mean a bloodline. Esdras rends his garment because the seed of Yashar'el (Israel) has been mixed with the strange people of the land, and the rulers led the way in it. Isaiah had already said what would become of that seed: the tree is cut down to a stump, a tenth returns, and the holy seed is what remains alive in the stump when everything else is eaten away. The grief in Esdras and the promise in Isaiah are about the same seed, and the promise is that Yahuah (God) keeps it through the cutting.$n$,$n$For both they and their sons have married with their daughters, and the holy seed is mixed with the strange people of the land; and from the beginning of this matter the rulers and the great men have been partakers of this iniquity.$n$,true),
(45078,'S','apocrypha','1-esdras',9,4,9,4,$n$*And that whoever met not there within two or three days according as the elders that bare rule appointed, their cattle should be seized to the use of the temple, and himself cast out from them that were of the captivity.* (1 Esdras 9:4). *And that whosoever would not come within three days, according to the counsel of the princes and the elders, all his substance should be forfeited, and himself separated from the congregation of those that had been carried away.* (Ezra 10:8). Ezra and 1 Esdras record the same proclamation. Everyone of the captivity was to gather at Jerusalem within three days, and whoever stayed away forfeited his goods and was cut off from the congregation of the returned. The matter was the strange wives, and the remedy began with the whole remnant standing together in one place before anything was decided. The two accounts agree on the terms, the goods seized for the temple and the man put out, so the reader can watch one event through two witnesses.$n$,$n$And that whoever met not there within two or three days according as the elders that bare rule appointed, their cattle should be seized to the use of the temple, and himself cast out from them that were of the captivity.$n$,true),
(45181,'S','apocrypha','2-esdras',6,20,6,20,$n$*And when the world, that shall begin to vanish away, shall be finished, then will I shew these tokens: the books shall be opened before the firmament, and they shall see all together:* (2 Esdras 6:20). *And I saw the dead, small and great, stand before Elohim (God); and the books were opened: and another book was opened, which is the book of life: and the dead were judged out of those things which were written in the books, according to their works.* (Revelation 20:12). Ezra is told the order of the end: when this vanishing world is finished, the books are opened before the firmament and all see together. John sees the same moment from the other side of it, the dead small and great standing before Elohim (God), the books opened, and another book opened with them, the book of life. Both witnesses put the opening of the books after the world's course is run, not before it. What Ezra was told would be shown as a token, John watched being done.$n$,$n$And when the world, that shall begin to vanish away, shall be finished, then will I shew these tokens: the books shall be opened before the firmament, and they shall see all together:$n$,true),
(45212,'S','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,49,8,49,$n$*In that you have humbled thyself, as it becometh you, and have not judged thyself worthy to be much glorified among the righteous.* (2 Esdras 8:49). *I tell you, this man went down to his house justified rather than the other: for every one that exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.* (Luke 18:14). The Most High tells Ezra that what is marvellous in him is that he humbled himself and did not judge himself worthy to be much glorified among the righteous. The publican in the temple does the same, standing afar off and asking for mercy, and he goes home justified while the one who exalted himself does not. The rule Yahusha (Jesus) states, that the humble are exalted, is the rule by which Ezra is commended. Ezra's not counting himself worthy is exactly what the Most High counts worthy.$n$,$n$In that you have humbled thyself, as it becometh you, and have not judged thyself worthy to be much glorified among the righteous.$n$,true),
(45221,'S','apocrypha','2-esdras',8,1,8,1,$n$*And he answered me, saying, The most High has made this world for many, but the world to come for few.* (2 Esdras 8:1). *Enter ye in at the strait gate: for wide is the gate, and broad is the way, that leadeth to destruction, and many there be which go in thereat:* (Matthew 7:13). Ezra is told that this world was made for many and the world to come for few. Yahusha (Jesus) draws the same two ways on the ground: a wide gate and a broad way crowded with the many, and a strait gate that few find (Matthew 7:14). The many of the broad way are the many of this world; the few of the narrow way are the few of the world to come. The numbers match because the two men are describing the same thing.$n$,$n$And he answered me, saying, The most High has made this world for many, but the world to come for few.$n$,true),
(49190,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you. If you have abundance give alms accordingly: if you have but a little, be not afraid to give according to that little:* (Tobit 4:7–8). *Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for Elohim (God) loveth a cheerful giver.* (2 Corinthians 9:7). Tobit's son is told to give without an envious eye, out of abundance or out of a little. Paul says the same to Corinth: not grudgingly, not of necessity, each as he purposes in his heart, for Elohim (God) loves a cheerful giver. The heart that gives gladly is the heart the library had been shaping for generations before the letter. Paul was not writing a new ethic for a new people; he was writing Tobit's charge to the scattered children of Yashar'el (Israel) who had grown up among the nations and forgotten it.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(52030,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',7,25,7,25,$n$*For she is the breath of the power of Yahuah (God), and a pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty: therefore can no defiled thing fall into her.* (Wisdom of Solomon 7:25). *Who, being in the form of Elohim (God), thought it not robbery to be equal with Elohim (God):* (Philippians 2:6). The Hebrew library saw Wisdom as the breath of the power of Yahuah (God), a pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty. Brought forth from him, carrying his glory, and not a second god standing beside him. Paul describes the same one as being in the form of Elohim (God) and counting it no robbery to be equal with Elohim (God), and then in the next breath emptying himself. What flows from the glory is of the glory, so the equality is no theft; and what flows has a source, so he has a Father and takes the lower place freely. The Formed is not a rival to the Formless; he is what the Formless breathed out.$n$,$n$For she is the breath of the power of Yahuah (God), and a pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty: therefore can no defiled thing fall into her.$n$,true),
(55059,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,9,24,9,$n$*He created me from the beginning before the world, and I shall never fail.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:9). *For by him were all things created, that are in heaven, and that are in earth, visible and invisible, whether they be thrones, or dominions, or principalities, or powers: all things were created by him, and for him:* (Colossians 1:16). Wisdom says she was brought forth from the beginning, before the world, and that she will never fail. Paul says of the Son that all things in heaven and earth were created by him and for him — he is before all things, and everything holds together in him. These are one and the same: the Wisdom beside the Maker before the world is the Formed one through whom the all things were made, the Son who has a Father and is before every creature. Not a second co-equal Elohim (God) beside the Father, but the Word the Father brought forth, who came in flesh as Yahusha (Jesus).$n$,$n$He created me from the beginning before the world, and I shall never fail.$n$,true),
(56814,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *If any man or woman that believeth have widows, let them relieve them, and let not the church be charged; that it may relieve them that are widows indeed.* (1 Timothy 5:16). Tobit's charge is to turn his face from no poor man. Paul orders the believing household to relieve its own widows, so that the assembly's care is kept for the widows who have no one. Both put the first duty of mercy at home, in the household, before it goes out the door. The face never turned from the poor begins with the poor who are nearest.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(56890,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,23,24,23,$n$*All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:23). *Moreover by them is thy servant warned: and in keeping of them there is great reward.* (Psalm 19:11). David spent a whole psalm praising the law of Yahuah (God), sweeter than honey, warning his servant, holding great reward for the one who keeps it. Sirach names what David was praising: the Wisdom whose memorial is sweeter than honey is *the law which Moses commanded for an heritage*. The Torah is the inheritance of the congregations of Jacob, the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God). It is the heritage, not the curse, and the one who keeps it finds the great reward the psalmist promised.$n$,$n$All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.$n$,true),
(57479,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *And let ours also learn to maintain good works for necessary uses, that they be not unfruitful.* (Titus 3:14). Paul tells Titus to teach the believers a settled habit of usefulness: maintain good works for necessary uses, and so bear fruit. Tobit's charge is one of those works named exactly — give alms of what you have, turn your face from no poor man. This is the doing of mercy the Torah commands and the new heart loves, and the library had been teaching it to sons in exile long before Paul wrote to Crete.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(57480,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,9,4,9,$n$*For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* (Tobit 4:9). *This is a faithful saying, and these things I will that thou affirm constantly, that they which have believed in Elohim (God) might be careful to maintain good works. These things are good and profitable unto men.* (Titus 3:8). Tobit's son is told to give alms as treasure laid up against the day of need, out of a heart that fears Yahuah (God) and keeps his commandments (4:5). Paul tells Titus to affirm constantly that those who have believed be careful to maintain good works, good and profitable to men. The two are one posture: believing and doing are not rivals, and the open hand is what a believing heart does. The library had been teaching that to sons in exile long before the letter.$n$,$n$For you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.$n$,true),
(58679,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,23,24,23,$n$*All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:23). *He sheweth his word unto Jacob, his statutes and his judgments unto Yashar'el (Israel).* (Psalm 147:19). Sirach says it without a veil: Wisdom is the book of the covenant, the law Moses commanded as the heritage of Jacob. The psalmist had already sung that Yahuah (God) shows his word to Jacob and his statutes and judgments to Yashar'el (Israel), and the next verse of that psalm says he did not deal so with any nation. The Torah is an inheritance handed to one family, the congregations of Jacob, and an inheritance is a gift to a son, not a burden laid on a slave. The law is the heritage of Yashar'el (Israel), never the curse.$n$,$n$All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.$n$,true),
(58818,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',23,9,23,9,$n$*Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.* (Sirach 23:9). The Hebrew wisdom-library says it almost in James's own words: *Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One* (Sirach 23:9), for *he that sweareth and nameth Yahuah (God) continually shall not be faultless* (Sirach 23:10). This is the reverence James commands — *swear not... but let your yea be yea* (James 5:12). Not the abolition of the lawful oath, but the refusal to make the Holy Name a habit of the careless tongue.$n$,$n$Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.$n$,false),
(58822,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',38,9,38,9,$n$*My son, in your sickness be not negligent: but pray to Yahuah (God), and he will make you whole.* (Sirach 38:9). The Hebrew wisdom-library already directed the sick to prayer: *in your sickness be not negligent: but pray to Yahuah (God), and he will make you whole,* and *Leave off from sin, and order yours hands aright, and cleanse your heart from all wickedness* (Sirach 38:9–10). James says the same — *Is any sick among you? let him... pray... and if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him* (James 5:14-15). Prayer to Yahuah and the leaving of sin are the wisdom-library's medicine of the soul, which James lays on the assembly.$n$,$n$My son, in your sickness be not negligent: but pray to Yahuah (God), and he will make you whole.$n$,false),
(60259,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,7,28,7,$n$*Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour: remember the covenant of the Highest, and wink at ignorance.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:7). *But if he be shameless and persisteth in his wrong-doing, even so forgive him from the heart, and leave to Elohim (God) the avenging.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 100:7). Sirach says remember the commandments and bear no malice to your neighbour, and overlook what was done in ignorance. The patriarch's testament goes further down the same road: even if the man is shameless and keeps on in his wrong, forgive him from the heart and leave the avenging to Elohim (God). Both are the commandment of Leviticus 19:18 lived out, no vengeance and no grudge. Forgiving the shameless is simply bearing no malice when it costs the most.$n$,$n$Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour: remember the covenant of the Highest, and wink at ignorance.$n$,true),
(65423,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',2,6,2,6,$n$*Believe in him, and he will help you; order your way aright, and trust in him.* (Ecclesiasticus 2:6). *Trust in Yahuah (LORD), and do good; so shalt thou dwell in the land, and verily thou shalt be fed.* (Psalm 37:3). Sirach is repeating David. Believe in him, order your way aright, trust in him, is the same counsel as trust in Yahuah (LORD) and do good, the trusting and the doing bound together in one breath. Neither verse lets a man trust Yahuah (God) while walking however he pleases, and neither lets him order his way as if his own ordering were what held him up. The whole life leans on his help, and the leaning shows up as a way set aright.$n$,$n$Believe in him, and he will help you; order your way aright, and trust in him.$n$,true),
(71513,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',35,8,35,8,$n$*Give Yahuah (God) his honour with a good eye, and diminish not the firstfruits of yours hands.* (Ecclesiasticus 35:8). *Honour Yahuah (LORD) with thy substance, and with the firstfruits of all thine increase:* (Proverbs 3:9). Sirach is repeating Solomon almost word for word. Honour Yahuah (LORD) with what you have and with your firstfruits, Solomon says; give Yahuah (God) his honour and do not diminish the firstfruits of your hands, says Sirach, and he adds the good eye, the ungrudging eye, as the manner of it. The first and the best belong to Yahuah (God), and they are not to be cut short. The proverb came down whole, and Sirach handed it on whole.$n$,$n$Give Yahuah (God) his honour with a good eye, and diminish not the firstfruits of yours hands.$n$,true),
(71554,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',9,9,9,9,$n$*And wisdom was with you: which knoweth your works, and was present when you madest the world, and knew what was acceptable in your sight, and right in your commandments.* (Wisdom of Solomon 9:9). When Wisdom says *When he prepared the heavens, I was there* (Proverbs 8:27), the restored witness agrees: she *was present when you madest the world*. The Formed Son was there at the making — present at the founding, the workman who knew the Father's works because he wrought them with him.$n$,$n$And wisdom was with you: which knoweth your works, and was present when you madest the world, and knew what was acceptable in your sight, and right in your commandments.$n$,false),
(71934,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,19,24,19,$n$*Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.* (Ecclesiasticus 24:19). *Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters, and he that hath no money; come ye, buy, and eat; yea, come, buy wine and milk without money and without price.* (Isaiah 55:1). Wisdom calls everyone who wants her to come and fill themselves; the prophet calls everyone who thirsts to come to the waters and eat without money. Both invitations go out to the desirous and the thirsty, and both offer what satisfies for nothing but the coming. There is no price at the door in either verse, only the call to come and be filled.$n$,$n$Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.$n$,true),
(90159,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',10,14,10,14,$n$*And left him not in bonds, till she brought him the sceptre of the kingdom, and power against those that oppressed him: as for them that had accused him, she shewed them to be liars, and gave him perpetual glory.* (Wisdom of Solomon 10:14). *He made him lord of his house, and ruler of all his substance:* (Psalm 105:21). Joseph was sold, went down into the pit, and was not left in bonds. Wisdom says she brought him the sceptre of the kingdom and power against those that oppressed him, showed his accusers to be liars, and gave him perpetual glory. The psalm tells it as history: Pharaoh made him lord of his house and ruler of all he had, and the next verse adds that he bound Pharaoh's princes at his pleasure and taught his senators wisdom (Psalm 105:22). The bound man binds the princes; the man accused as a liar is the one who ends up teaching the court. Wisdom went down into the pit with him and came up with him to the throne.$n$,$n$And left him not in bonds, till she brought him the sceptre of the kingdom, and power against those that oppressed him: as for them that had accused him, she shewed them to be liars, and gave him perpetual glory.$n$,true),
(103290,'T','apocrypha','tobit',13,14,13,14,$n$*O blessed are they which love you, for they shall rejoice in your peace: blessed are they which have been sorrowful for all your scourges; for they shall rejoice for you, when they have seen all your glory, and shall be glad for ever.* (Tobit 13:14). *Pray for the peace of Jerusalem: they shall prosper that love thee* (Psalm 122:6): Tobit, praying from captivity, sings the same blessing — they that love Jerusalem rejoice in her peace; the prosperity promised to her lovers in the psalm is the everlasting gladness Tobit foresees when her glory is restored.$n$,$n$O blessed are they which love you, for they shall rejoice in your peace: blessed are they which have been sorrowful for all your scourges; for they shall rejoice for you, when they have seen all your glory, and shall be glad for ever.$n$,false),
(108669,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *Thou shalt neither vex a stranger, nor oppress him: for ye were strangers in the land of Egypt.* (Exodus 22:21). Tobit lives in Nineveh, a stranger among the nations, and he teaches his son the open hand to the poor. The Torah had told Yashar'el (Israel) not to vex or oppress the stranger, because they had been strangers in Egypt. Tobit is now the stranger, and he keeps the commandment from the other side of it, showing mercy where he might have asked for it. The promise mirrors the warning: turn not your face from the poor, and Yahuah (God) turns not his from you.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(119997,'T','apocrypha','tobit',4,7,4,7,$n$*Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). *Take heed that ye do not your alms before men, to be seen of them: otherwise ye have no reward of your Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 6:1). Tobit ties alms to one face, the face of Yahuah (God) turned toward the giver. Yahusha (Jesus) warns against alms done to be seen of men, because a gift aimed at men's eyes has already had its reward and the Father's is lost. Both are saying whose face the giver should be looking for. Tobit never mentions secrecy, but he sets the giver before the Father's face and no one else's, and that is the whole of the warning in Matthew.$n$,$n$Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.$n$,true),
(122689,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',16,26,16,26,$n$*That your children, O Yahuah (God), whom you lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:26). *Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing? and one of them shall not fall on the ground without your Father* (Matthew 10:29). The same Father who numbers the hairs and marks the sparrow's fall preserves His own — not the world's provision but His word keeps them.$n$,$n$That your children, O Yahuah (God), whom you lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.$n$,false),
(122693,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',7,27,7,27,$n$*Honour your father with your whole heart, and forget not the sorrows of your mother. Remember that you were begotten of them; and how can you recompense them the things that they have done for you?* (Ecclesiasticus 7:27–28). The honour owed to father and mother is never abolished — the Master who says *He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me* (Matthew 10:37) still upholds the commandment in full. It is an ordering of loves, not a cancelling of the fifth word.$n$,$n$Honour your father with your whole heart, and forget not the sorrows of your mother.$n$,false),
(135262,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',27,1,27,1,$n$*Many have sinned for a small matter; and he that seeks for abundance will turn his eyes away.* (Ecclesiasticus 27:1). *And Ephraim said, Yet I am become rich, I have found me out substance: in all my labours they shall find none iniquity in me that were sin.* (Hosea 12:8). Ephraim had grown rich and could find no sin in any of his labours, and Sirach tells you how that happens: the one who is chasing abundance turns his eyes away from the small matters he sinned in to get it. The gain does not remove the sin. It hides the sin from the man who did it, until he can stand in the prophet's hearing and swear his hands are clean. That blindness is what Hosea is prophesying against, and it is what Sirach is warning the reader of before it sets in.$n$,$n$Many have sinned for a small matter; and he that seeks for abundance will turn his eyes away.$n$,true),
(135977,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',24,23,24,23,$n$*All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.* (Sirach 24:23). The wisdom that was *created me from the beginning before the world* (Sirach 24:9) is the very Torah Moses gave — the living testimony Yahusha points to: *Search the scriptures... they testify of me.* The Torah is the heritage, not the discarded thing.$n$,$n$All these things are the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.$n$,false),
(137924,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',5,8,5,8,$n$*Set not yours heart upon goods unjustly gotten, for they shall not profit you in the day of calamity.* (Ecclesiasticus 5:8). They who feast and stretch themselves *go captive with the first that go captive* (Amos 6:7) — for goods unjustly gotten *shall not profit you in the day of calamity*. The banquet is removed when the calamity comes.$n$,$n$Set not yours heart upon goods unjustly gotten, for they shall not profit you in the day of calamity.$n$,false),
(139531,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',34,22,34,22,$n$*He that takes away his neighbour’s living slayeth him; and he that defraudeth the labourer of his hire is a bloodshedder.* (Ecclesiasticus 34:22). To *build up Zion with blood* (Micah 3:10) is precisely this — the bloodshedder who *defraudeth the labourer of his hire*. The judges who *judge for reward* and priests who *teach for hire* (Micah 3:11) build a city whose every stone is another man's stolen life.$n$,$n$He that takes away his neighbour’s living slayeth him; and he that defraudeth the labourer of his hire is a bloodshedder.$n$,false),
(140754,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',47,17,47,17,$n$*The countries marvelled at you for your songs, and proverbs, and parables, and interpretations.* (Ecclesiasticus 47:17). The library remembers how Solomon's wisdom drew the nations from afar — the very fame that brought the queen of the south; yet *a greater than Solomon is here* (Luke 11:31).$n$,$n$The countries marvelled at you for your songs, and proverbs, and parables, and interpretations.$n$,false),
(142047,'T','apocrypha','the-wisdom-of-solomon',18,3,18,3,$n$*Instead of which you gavest them a burning pillar of fire, both to be a guide of the unknown journey, and an harmless sun to entertain them honourably.* (Wisdom of Solomon 18:3). The guiding Moses recalls — Yahuah (God) *went in the way before you, to search you out a place to pitch your tents in, in fire by night, to shew you by what way ye should go, and in a cloud by day* (Deuteronomy 1:33) — is witnessed in the restored book: *a burning pillar of fire, both to be a guide of the unknown journey*. The fire by night that led the son through the wilderness.$n$,$n$Instead of which you gavest them a burning pillar of fire, both to be a guide of the unknown journey, and an harmless sun to entertain them honourably.$n$,false),
(146283,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',4,10,4,10,$n$*Be as a father to the fatherless, and instead of an husband to their mother: so shall you be as the son of the Most High, and he shall love you more than your mother does.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:10). The wisdom tradition names exactly Job's deed — *I was a father to the poor* (Job 29:16) — and weighs it as the mark of a son of the Most High: covenant mercy is the fruit by which the righteous are known.$n$,$n$Be as a father to the fatherless, and instead of an husband to their mother: so shall you be as the son of the Most High, and he shall love you more than your mother does.$n$,false),
(147791,'T','apocrypha','ecclesiasticus',28,1,28,1,$n$*He that revengeth shall find vengeance from Yahuah (God), and he will surely keep his sins in remembrance.* (Ecclesiasticus 28:1). *Now therefore, my lord, as Yahuah (LORD) liveth, and as thy soul liveth, seeing Yahuah (LORD) hath withholden thee from coming to shed blood, and from avenging thyself with thine own hand, now let thine enemies, and they that seek evil to my lord, be as Nabal.* (1 Samuel 25:26). Abigail met David on the road while he was riding to shed Nabal's blood, and she told him plainly that Yahuah (LORD) had held him back from avenging himself with his own hand. Sirach says why that mattered: the man who takes his own vengeance finds vengeance from Yahuah (God), and his sins are kept in remembrance. David was one hour from writing that sentence against himself, and a wise woman turned him from it. He blessed her for it, and Nabal died by the hand of Yahuah (God) ten days later without David lifting a finger.$n$,$n$He that revengeth shall find vengeance from Yahuah (God), and he will surely keep his sins in remembrance.$n$,true),
(176192,'T','apocrypha','the-prayer-of-manasseh',1,11,1,11,$n$*Now therefore I bow the knee of my heart, beseeching you of grace. I have sinned, O Yahuah (God), I have sinned, and I acknowledge my iniquities:* (Prayer of Manasseh 1:11–12). *And prayed unto him: and he was intreated of him, and heard his supplication, and brought him again to Jerusalem into his kingdom. Then Manasseh knew that Yahuah (LORD) he was Elohim (God).* (2 Chronicles 33:13). The restored library preserves the very prayer attributed to him, and it goes on to call Yahuah (God) the God of them that repent. It is the inside of the humbling the Chronicler records — the bowed knee of the heart that the worst king found Yahuah (God) would not refuse.$n$,$n$Now therefore I bow the knee of my heart, beseeching you of grace.$n$,false),
(177372,'S','pseudepigrapha','3-maccabees',1,11,1,11,$n$*And when they told him that this was not permissible, none of the nation, no, nor even the priests in general, but only the supreme high priest of all, and he only once in a year, being allowed to go in, he would by no means give way.* (3 Maccabees 1:11). *Therefore thou and thy sons with thee shall keep your priest’s office for every thing of the altar, and within the vail; and ye shall serve: I have given your priest’s office unto you as a service of gift: and the stranger that cometh nigh shall be put to death.* (Numbers 18:7). Ptolemy saw the temple, admired it, and decided he would walk into the sanctuary itself. The priests told him the rule: no one of the nation, not even the priests, only the high priest, and he only once a year. The king called that an honour he was being denied; the Torah calls it a death sentence for anyone else who comes near. Yahuah (God) gave the priesthood to Aaron and his sons as a gift, within the vail as well as at the altar, and said the stranger that cometh nigh shall be put to death. The high priest's one entry a year on the Day of Atonement (Leviticus 16:2) was not a privilege to be extended; it was the boundary Yahuah (God) set on his own dwelling, and Ptolemy was pressing against the boundary and its penalty.$n$,$n$And when they told him that this was not permissible, none of the nation, no, nor even the priests in general, but only the supreme high priest of all, and he only once in a year, being allowed to go in, he would by no means give way.$n$,true),
(177390,'T','apocrypha','the-rest-of-esther',13,6,13,6,$n$The Rest of Esther 13:6 — *Therefore have we commanded, that all they that are signified in writing to you by Aman, who is ordained over the affairs, and is next to us, shall all, with their wives and children, be utterly destroyed by the sword of their enemies, without all mercy and pity, the fourteenth day of the twelfth month Adar of this present year:* The Greek text of Artaxerxes' decree matches 3 Macc 3:25 phrase for phrase — total destruction of the people *with wives and children*.$n$,$n$Therefore have we commanded, that all they that are signified in writing to you by Aman, who is ordained over the affairs, and is next to us, shall all, with their wives and children, be utterly destroyed by the sword of their enemies, without all mercy and pity, the fourteenth day of the twelfth month Adar of this present year:$n$,false),
(177395,'T','apocrypha','the-rest-of-esther',13,4,13,4,$n$The Rest of Esther 13:4 — *Declared to us, that in all nations throughout the world there was scattered a certain malicious people, that had laws contrary to all nations, and continually despised the commandments of kings, so as the uniting of our kingdoms, honourably intended by us cannot go forward.* The Greek decree of Artaxerxes frames the same accusation as 3 Macc 3:7 — a people whose laws set them apart is named an enemy of the realm.$n$,$n$Declared to us, that in all nations throughout the world there was scattered a certain malicious people, that had laws contrary to all nations, and continually despised the commandments of kings, so as the uniting of our kingdoms, honourably intended by us cannot go forward.$n$,false),
(177475,'S','apocrypha','2-esdras',7,30,7,30,$n$*And the world shall be turned into the old silence seven days, like as in the former judgments: so that no man shall remain.* (2 Esdras 7:30). *I beheld the earth, and, lo, it was without form, and void; and the heavens, and they had no light.* (Jeremiah 4:23). Esdras is told the world returns to the old silence for seven days, no man remaining. Jeremiah was shown the like: the earth *without form, and void,* the heavens without light, and, as he goes on, *lo, there was no man* (Jeremiah 4:25). These are the words of the earth before the first day, when *the earth was without form, and void; and darkness was upon the face of the deep* (Genesis 1:2); the old silence is the earth returned to that first stillness, no man remaining, before *the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her* (2 Esdras 7:32).$n$,$n$And the world shall be turned into the old silence seven days, like as in the former judgments: so that no man shall remain.$n$,false);
INSERT INTO _s463cm VALUES
(10742,45038,$n$1 Esdras 7:14 — *they kept the feast of unleavened bread seven days, making merry before Yahuah (God).* Messiah (Christ) our Passover is the reason the feast is kept, not the end of it (1 Corinthians 5:7).$n$),
(10747,45054,$n$1 Esdras 8:70 — *the holy seed is mixed with the strange people of the land.* Isaiah 6:13 names the same holy seed as what remains alive in the stump.$n$),
(10748,45051,$n$1 Esdras 8:89 — *you are true: for we are left a root this day.* Ezra 9:15 is the same confession: he is righteous, and a remnant yet escaped.$n$),
(10750,45078,$n$1 Esdras 9:4 — *their cattle should be seized to the use of the temple, and himself cast out.* Ezra 10:8 records the same three-day summons and the same forfeit.$n$),
(412,4855,$n$1 Maccabees 1:63 — *that they might not profane the holy covenant: so then they died.* The blood of the new covenant (Matthew 26:28) seals the same covenant they died for.$n$),
(10572,44509,$n$1 Maccabees 1:63 — *they chose rather to die, that they might not be defiled with meats.* The same resolve as those who said let us die all in our innocency (1 Maccabees 2:37).$n$),
(183,4495,$n$1 Maccabees 2:41 — *Whoever shall come to make battle with us on the sabbath day, we will fight against him.* Preserving life on the sabbath is doing well on it (Matthew 12:12).$n$),
(412,4856,$n$1 Maccabees 2:50 — *be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.* The King gave his life for that covenant; his blood of the new covenant is shed for many (Matthew 26:28).$n$),
(630,7872,$n$1 Maccabees 2:50 — *give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.* Mark 14:24, the King's blood of the new covenant shed for many, is a life given for that covenant.$n$),
(119,4465,$n$1 Maccabees 2:58 — *Elias for being zealous and fervent for the law was taken up into heaven.* John came in that same zeal; this is Elias which was for to come (Matthew 11:14).$n$),
(662,7980,$n$1 Maccabees 4:36 — *let us go up to cleanse and dedicate the sanctuary.* The King cleansed the same house: make not my Father's house an house of merchandise (John 2:16).$n$),
(387,4746,$n$1 Maccabees 4:43 — *cleansed the sanctuary, and bare out the defiled stones into an unclean place.* The King cast out what defiled the same house (Matthew 21:12).$n$),
(387,4749,$n$1 Maccabees 4:46 — *until there should come a prophet to shew what should be done with them.* The prophet came and spoke for the house: my house shall be called the house of prayer (Matthew 21:13).$n$),
(10594,44573,$n$1 Maccabees 6:54 — *There were but a few left in the sanctuary, because the famine did so prevail against them.* The famine came because they kept the seventh-year rest of Exodus 23:11.$n$),
(10602,44607,$n$1 Maccabees 8:18 — *to intreat them that they would take the yoke from them.* The embassy to Rome is the going down to Egypt for help that Isaiah 31:1 warns against.$n$),
(10626,44698,$n$1 Maccabees 14:12 — *every man sat under his vine and his fig tree, and there was none to fray them.* A foretaste of the day of the BRANCH in Zechariah 3:10.$n$),
(205,4563,$n$2 Esdras 1:19 — *Then had I pity upon your mournings, and gave you manna to eat.* The same pity fed the multitude in the desert place of Matthew 14:13.$n$),
(664,7994,$n$2 Esdras 1:19 — *so you did eat angels’ bread.* The bread of Elohim (God) that comes down from heaven is the one the manna pointed to (John 6:33).$n$),
(400,4803,$n$2 Esdras 1:30 — *I gathered you together, as a hen gathers her chickens under her wings.* The King speaks the same words in the same voice over Jerusalem (Matthew 23:37).$n$),
(390,4759,$n$2 Esdras 1:32 — *I sent to you my servants the prophets, whom you have taken and slain.* The husbandmen who beat, killed, and stoned the servants (Matthew 21:35) are the same story.$n$),
(400,4804,$n$2 Esdras 1:32 — *whose blood I will require of your hands, says Yahuah (God).* The same sender says, behold, I send unto you prophets, and some of them ye shall kill (Matthew 23:34).$n$),
(390,4760,$n$2 Esdras 1:33 — *Your house is desolate, I will cast you out as the wind does stubble.* The sentence falls when the husbandmen cast out the son and slay him (Matthew 21:39).$n$),
(400,4805,$n$2 Esdras 1:33 — *Thus says the Almighty Yahuah (God), Your house is desolate.* The King said it in the temple and walked out: your house is left unto you desolate (Matthew 23:38).$n$),
(646,7925,$n$2 Esdras 2:31 — *Remember your children that sleep, for I shall bring them out of the sides of the earth.* The son who was dead and is alive again (Luke 15:24) is that mercy in a parable.$n$),
(391,4763,$n$2 Esdras 2:38 — *behold the number of those that be sealed in the feast of Yahuah (God).* The servants sent into the highways gather them to the marriage (Matthew 22:9).$n$),
(391,4764,$n$2 Esdras 2:42 — *a great people, whom I could not number, and they all praised Yahuah (God) with songs.* The wedding furnished with guests (Matthew 22:10) is the same gathered crowd.$n$),
(391,4765,$n$2 Esdras 2:43 — *upon every one of their heads he set crowns, and was more exalted.* The king came in to see the guests and found one without a wedding garment (Matthew 22:11).$n$),
(196,4533,$n$2 Esdras 3:21 — *the first Adam bearing a wicked heart transgressed, and was overcome.* The field of Adam's sons is where the enemy sowed tares while men slept (Matthew 13:25).$n$),
(196,4532,$n$2 Esdras 4:30 — *the grain of evil seed has been sown in the heart of Adam from the beginning.* The parable of the good seed sown in the field (Matthew 13:24) runs to the same threshing.$n$),
(620,7837,$n$2 Esdras 4:30 — *how much ungodliness has it brought up to this time?.* The same heart is the ground where Satan takes away the word that was sown (Mark 4:15).$n$),
(117,4458,$n$2 Esdras 4:33 — *How, and when shall these things come to pass? wherefore are our years few and evil?.* John's question from prison (Matthew 11:3) is the same question from the same distress.$n$),
(117,4459,$n$2 Esdras 4:34 — *Do not you hasten above the most Highest: for your haste is in vain to be above him.* Blessed is he who is not offended in the waiting (Matthew 11:6).$n$),
(104,4429,$n$2 Esdras 4:35 — *How long shall I hope on this fashion? when comes the fruit of the floor of our reward?.* The living are sent into the harvest the sleeping righteous wait for (Matthew 9:37).$n$),
(117,4460,$n$2 Esdras 4:35 — *Did not the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers.* The one who waits without being offended is blessed (Matthew 11:6).$n$),
(227,4614,$n$2 Esdras 4:35 — *the souls also of the righteous ask question of these things in their chambers.* The chambers hold the righteous until the resurrection; the gates of hell shall not prevail (Matthew 16:18).$n$),
(104,4430,$n$2 Esdras 4:36 — *Even when the number of seeds is filled in you: for he has weighed the world in the balance.* The labourers sent into the harvest (Matthew 9:38) are how the number is filled.$n$),
(403,4815,$n$2 Esdras 5:1 — *the way of truth shall be hidden, and the land shall be barren of faith.* The same sign: many offended, betraying and hating one another (Matthew 24:10).$n$),
(403,4816,$n$2 Esdras 5:4 — *the sun shall suddenly shine again in the night, and the moon thrice in the day.* The signs in the heavens come after the tribulation of those days (Matthew 24:29).$n$),
(194,4527,$n$2 Esdras 5:9 — *then shall wit hide itself, and understanding withdraw itself into his secret chamber.* To some it is given to know the mysteries; to others it is not (Matthew 13:11).$n$),
(403,4817,$n$2 Esdras 5:9 — *all friends shall destroy one another.* Many shall betray one another, and shall hate one another (Matthew 24:10).$n$),
(676,8029,$n$2 Esdras 5:18 — *forsake us not, as the shepherd that leaveth his flock in the hands of cruel wolves.* Peter, who forsook three times, is charged three times to feed the flock (John 21:15).$n$),
(381,4724,$n$2 Esdras 5:23 — *of every wood of the earth, and of all the trees thereof, you have chosen you one only vine.* The householder's vineyard at Matthew 20:1 is that one chosen vine, and the labourers are hired into it.$n$),
(626,7863,$n$2 Esdras 5:23 — *you have chosen you one only vine.* The vineyard let out to husbandmen at Mark 12:1 was the owner's choice, never the keepers'.$n$),
(651,7960,$n$2 Esdras 5:24 — *of all lands of the whole world you have chosen you one pit.* At Luke 20:16 the keeping passes to others; the chosen vine stays the Most High's own.$n$),
(626,7862,$n$2 Esdras 5:27 — *among all the multitudes of people you have gotten you one people.* The lord of the vineyard at Mark 12:9 takes back the keeping of a people that was always his.$n$),
(651,7961,$n$2 Esdras 5:27 — *to this people, whom you lovedst, you gavest a law that is approved of all.* The vineyard let out at Luke 20:9 is this people, and the fruit sought is the law kept.$n$),
(626,7861,$n$2 Esdras 5:29 — *they which did gainsay your promises, and believed not your covenants, have trodden them down.* The husbandmen of Mark 12:9 carry the same unbelief inside the hedge, and meet the same owner.$n$),
(10784,45181,$n$2 Esdras 6:20 — *the books shall be opened before the firmament, and they shall see all together.* John sees the same books opened before the throne at Revelation 20:12.$n$),
(200,4547,$n$2 Esdras 6:26 — *the heart of the inhabitants shall be changed, and turned into another meaning.* The parables of Matthew 13:35 open what was kept secret to a heart turned toward their meaning.$n$),
(101,4423,$n$2 Esdras 7:28 — *my son the Messiah shall be revealed with those that be with him.* The blind men of Matthew 9:27 confess as present the one Ezra was told would be revealed.$n$),
(419,4881,$n$2 Esdras 7:30 — *the world shall be turned into the old silence seven days.* The three hours of darkness at Matthew 27:45 were a small showing of that silence.$n$),
(228,4618,$n$2 Esdras 7:32 — *the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her.* Resurrection first, then reward according to works, as the Son of Adam says at Matthew 16:27.$n$),
(419,4883,$n$2 Esdras 7:32 — *the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence.* The graves opened and the sleeping saints arose at Matthew 27:52, a first showing of it.$n$),
(648,7945,$n$2 Esdras 7:32 — *the secret places shall deliver those souls that were committed to them.* The one who might rise from the dead at Luke 16:31 would come from that same holding place.$n$),
(668,8009,$n$2 Esdras 7:32 — *the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her.* Martha's resurrection at the last day (John 11:24) is this promise, and Lazarus is its first sign.$n$),
(419,4882,$n$2 Esdras 7:33 — *the Most High shall appear upon the seat of judgment, and misery shall pass away.* The veil rent and the earth quaking at Matthew 27:51 carry the marks of that day.$n$),
(228,4619,$n$2 Esdras 7:35 — *the work shall follow, and the reward shall be shewed.* At Matthew 16:27 the Son of Adam rewards every man according to his works.$n$),
(408,4838,$n$2 Esdras 7:35 — *the good deeds shall be of force, and wicked deeds shall bear no rule.* The unprofitable servant of Matthew 25:30 was of the house, and his work did not follow him.$n$),
(192,4520,$n$2 Esdras 7:48 — *though it was you that sinned, you are not fallen alone, but we all that come of you.* The swept but empty house of Matthew 12:45 is a son of Adam without a new heart.$n$),
(382,4728,$n$2 Esdras 8:1 — *The most High has made this world for many, but the world to come for few.* Many called, few chosen (Matthew 20:16) is the same count.$n$),
(408,4836,$n$2 Esdras 8:1 — *this world for many, but the world to come for few.* The shut door of Matthew 25:10 divides the many who set out from the few found ready.$n$),
(10795,45221,$n$2 Esdras 8:1 — *made this world for many, but the world to come for few.* The broad way and the strait gate of Matthew 7:13 carry the same many and few.$n$),
(202,4553,$n$2 Esdras 8:3 — *There be many created, but few shall be saved.* The angels sever the wicked from among the just at Matthew 13:49; the few are what remains.$n$),
(382,4729,$n$2 Esdras 8:3 — *There be many created, but few shall be saved.* Many called, few chosen (Matthew 20:16); the few are the sheep who hear and keep.$n$),
(408,4837,$n$2 Esdras 8:3 — *There be many created, but few shall be saved.* The bridegroom's I know you not at Matthew 25:12 is the other end of the same sentence.$n$),
(196,4534,$n$2 Esdras 8:6 — *give us seed to our heart, and culture to our understanding, that there may come fruit of it.* The sower of Matthew 13:24 is the answer to this prayer.$n$),
(637,7910,$n$2 Esdras 8:41 — *neither does all that is planted take root.* The rock hearers of Luke 8:13 spring up with no root and fall away.$n$),
(10798,45212,$n$2 Esdras 8:49 — *have not judged thyself worthy to be much glorified among the righteous.* He that humbleth himself shall be exalted (Luke 18:14) is the rule Ezra is commended by.$n$),
(389,4758,$n$2 Esdras 9:11 — *when as yet place of repentance was open to them, understood not, but despised it.* The chief priests who would not repent when John came (Matthew 21:32) walked past that open place.$n$),
(381,4725,$n$2 Esdras 9:22 — *let my grape be kept, and my plant; for with great labour have I made it perfect.* The grape kept out of the cluster is the few chosen of Matthew 20:16.$n$),
(620,7835,$n$2 Esdras 9:31 — *I sow my law in you, and it shall bring fruit in you.* The good ground of Mark 4:20 brings forth the fruit the sown law was given to bring.$n$),
(637,7911,$n$2 Esdras 9:31 — *I sow my law in you, and it shall bring fruit in you.* The seed is the word of Elohim (God) at Luke 8:11, and the word sown is the law.$n$),
(637,7909,$n$2 Esdras 9:33 — *they that received it perished, because they kept not the thing that was sown in them.* The good ground of Luke 8:15 hears the word and keeps it; the fruit is the keeping.$n$),
(101,4420,$n$2 Esdras 12:31 — *the lion, whom you sawest rising up out of the wood, and roaring.* The lion named as the anointed is the Son of David the blind men cry to at Matthew 9:27.$n$),
(101,4421,$n$2 Esdras 12:32 — *This is the anointed, which the Highest has kept for them.* The anointed kept to the end is the Son of David met on the road at Matthew 9:27.$n$),
(101,4422,$n$2 Esdras 12:34 — *the rest of my people shall he deliver with mercy, those that have been pressed upon my borders.* The blind men ask the Son of David for that same mercy at Matthew 9:27.$n$),
(403,4818,$n$2 Esdras 13:32 — *then shall my Son be declared, whom you sawest as a man ascending.* The Son of Adam seen coming in the clouds at Matthew 24:30 is the Son declared after the signs.$n$),
(667,8010,$n$2 Esdras 13:33 — *when all the people hear his voice, every man shall in their own land leave the battle.* My sheep hear my voice (John 10:27); the hearing shows who they were.$n$),
(403,4819,$n$2 Esdras 13:39 — *he gathered another peaceable multitude to him.* The elect gathered from the four winds at Matthew 24:31 are the ten tribes the next verse names.$n$),
(628,7885,$n$2 Esdras 13:39 — *he gathered another peaceable multitude to him.* The elect gathered from the four winds at Mark 13:27 are the ten tribes the next verse names.$n$),
(106,4433,$n$2 Esdras 13:40 — *Those are the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land.* The lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) the twelve are sent to at Matthew 10:6.$n$),
(221,4587,$n$2 Esdras 13:40 — *whom Salmanasar the king of Assyria led away captive.* The lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) the King says he was sent to (Matthew 15:24).$n$),
(424,4903,$n$2 Esdras 13:40 — *he carried them over the waters, and so came they into another land.* The scattered house lives among the nations, which is why the word goes to all nations (Matthew 28:19).$n$),
(646,7926,$n$2 Esdras 13:40 — *carried away prisoners out of their own land.* The lost sheep whose finding the shepherd rejoices over at Luke 15:6.$n$),
(106,4434,$n$2 Esdras 13:41 — *they would leave the multitude of the heathen, and go forth into a further country.* The remnant went out to keep the statutes; the twelve are sent after them (Matthew 10:6).$n$),
(106,4435,$n$2 Esdras 13:47 — *The Highest shall stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through.* The way home opens at the latter time; the mission runs till the Son of Adam comes (Matthew 10:23).$n$),
(424,4904,$n$2 Esdras 13:47 — *stay the springs of the stream again, that they may go through.* The Father brings the tribes back; the disciples go to all nations because that is where they were carried (Matthew 28:19).$n$),
(628,7883,$n$2 Esdras 13:47 — *therefore sawest you the multitude with peace.* The act of the Most High, not the scattered's own contriving; Mark 13:27's elect gathered from the four winds.$n$),
(221,4588,$n$2 Esdras 13:48 — *those that be left behind of your people are they that are found within my borders.* The remnant that stayed; the King's mission reaches them and the scattered alike (Matthew 15:24).$n$),
(10648,44766,$n$2 Maccabees 3:10 — *such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children.* The treasury Heliodorus came for was the Torah's protected ones (Exodus 22:22).$n$),
(10650,44757,$n$2 Maccabees 3:25 — *an horse with a terrible rider upon him, and adorned with a very fair covering.* The armed one standing for the place of Yahuah (God), as the captain stood by Jericho (Joshua 5:13).$n$),
(10653,44786,$n$2 Maccabees 4:8 — *Promising to the king by intercession three hundred and threescore talents of silver.* The priesthood bought for silver; priests that teach for hire (Micah 3:11).$n$),
(10655,44777,$n$2 Maccabees 4:14 — *the priests had no courage to serve any more at the altar, but despising the temple.* The covenant of Levi corrupted from within (Malachi 2:8).$n$),
(393,4771,$n$2 Maccabees 7:9 — *the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.* The resurrection confessed in blood; the Sadducees erred, not knowing the power of Elohim (God) (Matthew 22:29).$n$),
(393,4772,$n$2 Maccabees 7:23 — *will also of his own mercy give you breath and life again.* The Creator gives breath again; Elohim (God) of the living, not of the dead (Matthew 22:32).$n$),
(412,4857,$n$2 Maccabees 7:36 — *are dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life.* The covenant the martyrs died under is sealed in the King's blood (Matthew 26:28).$n$),
(630,7871,$n$2 Maccabees 7:36 — *are dead under the covenant of Yahuah (God) of everlasting life.* Mark 14:24's blood of the new covenant seals the covenant of everlasting life the martyrs died under.$n$),
(383,4733,$n$2 Maccabees 7:37 — *offer up my body and life for the laws of our fathers.* The cup the sons of Zebedee said they could drink (Matthew 20:22).$n$),
(387,4747,$n$2 Maccabees 10:3 — *having cleansed the temple they made another altar.* The temple cleansed of a defilement from outside; the King cleansed it from inside (Matthew 21:12).$n$),
(386,4744,$n$2 Maccabees 10:7 — *they bare branches, and fair boughs, and palms also, and sang psalms.* Branches and psalms before a cleansed temple, as at the entry (Matthew 21:8).$n$),
(10686,44866,$n$2 Maccabees 10:35 — *inflamed with anger because of the blasphemies.* The zeal of Phinehas for the name of Yahuah (God) (Numbers 25:11).$n$),
(43175,177372,$n$3 Maccabees 1:11 — *only the supreme high priest of all, and he only once in a year.* The boundary of Numbers 18:7: the stranger that cometh nigh shall be put to death.$n$),
(10507,44297,$n$Baruch 1:10 — *we have sent you money to buy you burnt offerings, and sin offerings, and incense.* Offerings and prayer for the king together, as in Darius's decree (Ezra 6:10).$n$),
(660,7987,$n$Baruch 3:29 — *Who has gone up into heaven, and taken her, and brought her down from the clouds.* The question John 3:13 answers: the Son of Adam came down.$n$),
(660,7986,$n$Baruch 3:30 — *Who has gone over the sea, and found her, and will bring her for pure gold.* Wisdom past man's reach; the Son of Adam came down from where she dwells (John 3:13).$n$),
(122,4473,$n$Baruch 3:36 — *has given it to Jacob his servant, and to Yashar'el (Israel) his beloved.* Wisdom's children at Matthew 11:19 are the people she was given to.$n$),
(660,7985,$n$Baruch 3:37 — *Afterward did he shew himself upon earth, and conversed with men.* The descent John 3:13 names: the Son of Adam who came down from heaven.$n$),
(122,4474,$n$Baruch 4:1 — *the law that endureth for ever: all they that keep it shall come to life.* Wisdom is the Torah; her children keep it (Matthew 11:19).$n$),
(10522,44355,$n$Baruch 4:1 — *This is the book of the commandments of Yahuah (God), and the law that endureth for ever.* Near and keepable, as Deuteronomy 30:14 says of the same word.$n$),
(10525,44345,$n$Baruch 4:22 — *joy is come to me from the Holy One, because of the mercy which shall soon come.* The mother's comfort of Isaiah 66:13, told from Jerusalem's side.$n$),
(632,7890,$n$Baruch 4:36 — *look about you toward the east, and behold the joy that comes to you from Yahuah (God).* The gathering that keeps the oath to Abraham and his seed for ever (Luke 1:55).$n$),
(10527,44339,$n$Baruch 4:36 — *behold the joy that comes to you from Yahuah (God).* Isaiah's lift up thine eyes upon the gathering children (Isaiah 49:18).$n$),
(632,7891,$n$Baruch 4:37 — *your sons come, whom you sentest away, they come gathered together from the east to the west.* Abraham's seed for ever, gathered home (Luke 1:55).$n$),
(669,8006,$n$Baruch 4:37 — *they come gathered together from the east to the west by the word of the Holy One.* The scattered children of Elohim (God) gathered into one (John 11:52).$n$),
(628,7881,$n$Baruch 5:5 — *behold your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One.* With 4:37 the gathering comes from every direction; the elect from the four winds (Mark 13:27).$n$),
(645,7929,$n$Baruch 5:5 — *Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and look about toward the east.* Jerusalem's own children brought home as children of the kingdom; they come from east and west to sit down in it (Luke 13:29).$n$),
(669,8005,$n$Baruch 5:5 — *your children gathered from the west to the east by the word of the Holy One.* The scattered children gathered into one (John 11:52).$n$),
(634,7895,$n$Baruch 5:7 — *every high hill, and banks of long continuance, should be cast down, and valleys filled up.* The leveled road is for Yashar'el (Israel) to walk home; John opened it (Luke 3:5).$n$),
(634,7896,$n$Baruch 5:9 — *Yahuah (God) shall lead Yashar'el (Israel) with joy in the light of his glory.* Where the road ends; all flesh sees the salvation of Elohim (God) (Luke 3:6).$n$),
(669,8004,$n$Baruch 5:9 — *with the mercy and righteousness that comes from him.* Yashar'el (Israel) led home with joy is the scattered children gathered into one (John 11:52).$n$),
(10536,44383,$n$Baruch 6:51 — *they are no gods, but the works of men’s hands.* The exile curse Moses named in Deuteronomy 4:28, watched from inside Babylon.$n$),
(9141,39536,$n$Ecclesiasticus 1:26 — *If you desire wisdom, keep the commandments.* Solomon's conclusion of the whole matter, in one line (Ecclesiastes 12:13).$n$),
(389,4755,$n$Ecclesiasticus 1:28 — *come not to him with a double heart.* The second son's *I go, sir: and went not* (Matthew 21:30).$n$),
(399,4796,$n$Ecclesiasticus 1:28 — *come not to him with a double heart.* The clean cup with the foul inside at Matthew 23:25.$n$),
(389,4756,$n$Ecclesiasticus 1:29 — *Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men.* The second son's public yes and absent obedience at Matthew 21:30.$n$),
(399,4797,$n$Ecclesiasticus 1:29 — *Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men.* Righteousness outward unto men, hypocrisy within, at Matthew 23:28.$n$),
(9142,39532,$n$Ecclesiasticus 1:29 — *Be not an hypocrite in the sight of men, and take good heed what you speak.* The Messiah's woe at Matthew 23:28 indicts exactly this man.$n$),
(109,4442,$n$Ecclesiasticus 2:1 — *if you come to serve Yahuah (God), prepare your soul for temptation.* The endurance to the end Yahusha (Jesus) asks of the twelve at Matthew 10:22.$n$),
(415,4867,$n$Ecclesiasticus 2:1 — *My son, if you come to serve Yahuah (God), prepare your soul for temptation.* The watch and pray the sleeping disciples did not keep at Matthew 26:41.$n$),
(109,4443,$n$Ecclesiasticus 2:5 — *gold is tried in the fire, and acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.* The furnace the twelve are sent into at Matthew 10:22.$n$),
(415,4868,$n$Ecclesiasticus 2:5 — *acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.* Peter's denial and bitter weeping at Matthew 26:75 is the furnace doing its work.$n$),
(9143,39553,$n$Ecclesiasticus 2:5 — *gold is tried in the fire.* Peter's faith tried with fire at 1 Peter 1:7 is Sirach's figure handed on.$n$),
(16263,65423,$n$Ecclesiasticus 2:6 — *Believe in him, and he will help you; order your way aright, and trust in him.* David's *Trust in Yahuah (LORD), and do good* (Psalm 37:3), repeated.$n$),
(109,4444,$n$Ecclesiasticus 2:18 — *We will fall into the hands of Yahuah (God), and not into the hands of men.* The fear moved off men and onto Yahuah (God) at Matthew 10:28.$n$),
(219,4576,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:1 — *Hear me your father, O children, and do thereafter, that you may be safe.* The fifth commandment Yahusha (Jesus) quotes against the tradition at Matthew 15:4.$n$),
(377,4704,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:1 — *Hear me your father, O children, and do thereafter.* The children who come when called, of whom the kingdom is, at Matthew 19:14.$n$),
(622,7851,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:7 — *He that fears Yahuah (God) will honour his father.* The commandment the corban tradition severs from the fear of Yahuah (God) at Mark 7:10.$n$),
(219,4577,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:8 — *Honour your father and mother both in word and deed.* The word without the deed the corban tradition allowed, against Matthew 15:4.$n$),
(30909,4577,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:8 — *Honour your father and mother both in word and deed.* The word without the deed the corban tradition allowed, against Matthew 15:4.$n$),
(622,7850,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:8 — *both in word and deed, that a blessing may come upon you from them.* The deed the tradition forbids at Mark 7:12.$n$),
(622,7849,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:9 — *the blessing of the father establishes the houses of children.* The foundation the tradition of Mark 7:13 roots out.$n$),
(219,4579,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:16 — *He that forsaketh his father is as a blasphemer.* The weight behind the voided commandment at Matthew 15:6.$n$),
(126,4485,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:18 — *The greater you are, the more humble thyself.* Revealed unto babes, hidden from the wise and prudent (Matthew 11:25).$n$),
(375,4696,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:18 — *The greater you are, the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).* The humbling that is the kingdom's entry at Matthew 18:3.$n$),
(384,4738,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:18 — *the more humble thyself, and you shall find favour before Yahuah (God).* Greatness as service, against the domination of the princes, at Matthew 20:26.$n$),
(396,4786,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:18 — *The greater you are, the more humble thyself.* He that is greatest shall be your servant (Matthew 23:11).$n$),
(126,4486,$n$Ecclesiasticus 3:19 — *mysteries are revealed to the meek.* Hid from the wise and prudent, revealed unto babes (Matthew 11:25).$n$),
(187,4505,$n$Ecclesiasticus 4:1 — *defraud not the poor of his living, and make not the needy eyes to wait long.* The bruised reed not broken at Matthew 12:20, asked of the son first.$n$),
(409,4843,$n$Ecclesiasticus 4:1 — *make not the needy eyes to wait long.* What the sheep are found to have done at Matthew 25:35.$n$),
(187,4506,$n$Ecclesiasticus 4:4 — *Reject not the supplication of the afflicted; neither turn away your face from a poor man.* The face the Messiah keeps toward the bruised reed at Matthew 12:20.$n$),
(193,4524,$n$Ecclesiasticus 4:10 — *Be as a father to the fatherless, and instead of an husband to their mother.* The doing of the Father's will by which the family is known at Matthew 12:50.$n$),
(9157,39600,$n$Ecclesiasticus 4:13 — *He that holdeth her fast shall inherit glory.* Wisdom's inheritance for those who love her, Proverbs 8:21.$n$),
(9158,39593,$n$Ecclesiasticus 4:28 — *Strive for the truth to death, and Yahuah (God) shall fight for you.* The confession before men that is confessed before the Father (Matthew 10:32).$n$),
(415,4864,$n$Ecclesiasticus 6:1 — *Instead of a friend become not an enemy.* Judas's *Master, is it I?* at Matthew 26:25 with the bargain already made.$n$),
(9166,39646,$n$Ecclesiasticus 6:6 — *have but one counsellor of a thousand.* The friend that sticketh closer than a brother of Proverbs 18:24.$n$),
(201,4549,$n$Ecclesiasticus 6:14 — *he that has found such an one has found a treasure.* The one pearl worth all the merchant had, Matthew 13:45.$n$),
(127,4491,$n$Ecclesiasticus 6:28 — *at the last you shall find her rest, and that shall be turned to your joy.* The rest found under the yoke at Matthew 11:29.$n$),
(396,4787,$n$Ecclesiasticus 7:4 — *Seek not of Yahuah (God) preeminence, neither of the king the seat of honour.* The chief seats loved at Matthew 23:6.$n$),
(377,4703,$n$Ecclesiasticus 7:23 — *Have you children? instruct them, and bow down their neck from their youth.* The parents bringing their children to Yahusha (Jesus) at Matthew 19:13.$n$),
(219,4578,$n$Ecclesiasticus 7:27 — *Honour your father with your whole heart.* The whole heart the corban tradition divided, against Matthew 15:4.$n$),
(394,4775,$n$Ecclesiasticus 7:30 — *Love him that made you with all your strength.* The Shema the Messiah quotes at Matthew 22:37, carried forward by Sirach.$n$),
(9175,39657,$n$Ecclesiasticus 7:31 — *give him his portion, as it is commanded you; the firstfruits.* The firstfruits that honor Yahuah (LORD) in Proverbs 3:9 reach him through the priest's hand.$n$),
(409,4841,$n$Ecclesiasticus 7:32 — *stretch yours hand to the poor, that your blessing may be perfected.* The hand stretched to the poor is the measure the King uses at Matthew 25:36.$n$),
(9232,39861,$n$Ecclesiasticus 9:8 — *Turn away your eye from a beautiful woman.* The eye turned away in Sirach is the heart Yahusha (Jesus) reads at Matthew 5:28.$n$),
(98,4414,$n$Ecclesiasticus 9:10 — *a new friend is as new wine; when it is old, you shall drink it with pleasure.* Sirach's new wine that ages well sits beside the new wine and new bottles of Matthew 9:17.$n$),
(9235,39850,$n$Ecclesiasticus 9:14 — *guess at your neighbour, and consult with the wise.* Sirach's consult with the wise is Solomon's walk with wise men at Proverbs 13:20.$n$),
(9235,39851,$n$Ecclesiasticus 9:15 — *all your communication in the law of the Most High.* Talk in the law of the Most High is the delight in the law of Psalm 1:2 spoken aloud.$n$),
(375,4698,$n$Ecclesiasticus 10:7 — *Pride is hateful before Yahuah (God) and man.* The pride Sirach names is the disposition behind who is the greatest at Matthew 18:1.$n$),
(642,7920,$n$Ecclesiasticus 10:12 — *The beginning of pride is when one departs from Yahuah (God).* The heart turned from its Maker is the lawyer lading burdens at Luke 11:46.$n$),
(642,7921,$n$Ecclesiasticus 10:13 — *pride is the beginning of sin, and he that has it shall pour out abomination.* The pride that begins sin is the love of the uppermost seats at Luke 11:43.$n$),
(9270,39982,$n$Ecclesiasticus 10:13 — *Yahuah (God) brought upon them strange calamities, and overthrew them utterly.* The strange calamities of pride land on the place where Lot sojourned at Ecclesiasticus 16:8.$n$),
(375,4697,$n$Ecclesiasticus 10:14 — *cast down the thrones of proud princes, and set up the meek in their stead.* The meek set up in the proud's stead is the child who is greatest at Matthew 18:4.$n$),
(396,4785,$n$Ecclesiasticus 10:14 — *cast down the thrones of proud princes, and set up the meek in their stead.* The thrones changing hands is the rule of Matthew 23:12 written as history.$n$),
(60,4364,$n$Ecclesiasticus 11:14 — *Prosperity and adversity, life and death, poverty and riches, come of Yahuah (God).* Because both come of Yahuah (God), the citizen can seek first the kingdom at Matthew 6:33.$n$),
(643,7936,$n$Ecclesiasticus 11:18 — *waxeth rich by his wariness and pinching, and this is the portion of his reward.* The hoarder's whole reward is the heap left behind when the soul is required at Luke 12:20.$n$),
(643,7937,$n$Ecclesiasticus 11:19 — *I have found rest, and now will eat continually of my goods.* Sirach's rich man speaks the rich fool's line of Luke 12:19 centuries early.$n$),
(407,4832,$n$Ecclesiasticus 11:21 — *trust in Yahuah (God), and abide in your labour.* Abiding in the labour is what earns well done, thou good and faithful servant at Matthew 25:21.$n$),
(404,4820,$n$Ecclesiasticus 11:26 — *in the day of death to reward a man according to his ways.* Reward according to his ways is the servant found so doing at Matthew 24:46.$n$),
(95,4406,$n$Ecclesiasticus 12:4 — *Give to the godly man, and help not a sinner.* Sirach guards the hand from strengthening the ungodly; the King's table at Matthew 9:10 calls sinners to turn.$n$),
(95,4407,$n$Ecclesiasticus 12:5 — *hold back your bread, and give it not to him, lest he overmaster you thereby.* Bread held back from the one who would overmaster you is not the table the Pharisees question at Matthew 9:11.$n$),
(95,4408,$n$Ecclesiasticus 12:7 — *Give to the good, and help not the sinner.* Help not the sinner and call sinners to repentance (Matthew 9:13) meet at the word repentance.$n$),
(415,4865,$n$Ecclesiasticus 12:16 — *An enemy speaks sweetly with his lips, but in his heart he imagineth how to throw you into a pit.* The enemy with sweet lips is Judas with Hail, master, and a kiss at Matthew 26:49.$n$),
(58,4356,$n$Ecclesiasticus 14:8 — *The envious man has a wicked eye.* Sirach's wicked eye of envy is the evil eye that darkens the body at Matthew 6:23.$n$),
(58,4357,$n$Ecclesiasticus 14:9 — *A covetous man’s eye is not satisfied with his portion.* The unsatisfied eye that dries the soul is the evil eye of Matthew 6:23.$n$),
(58,4358,$n$Ecclesiasticus 14:10 — *A wicked eye envies his bread, and he is a niggard at his table.* The niggard's table is the darkness of Matthew 6:23 with bread on it.$n$),
(9257,39949,$n$Ecclesiasticus 14:11 — *according to your ability do good to thyself, and give Yahuah (God) his due offering.* Give Yahuah (God) his due while it is in your hand; Job 1:21 says who gave it and who takes it.$n$),
(220,4581,$n$Ecclesiasticus 15:11 — *Say not you, It is through Yahuah (God) that I fell away.* The fall is not from Yahuah (God), and the defilement is not from the food (Matthew 15:11); both come from the man.$n$),
(9264,39962,$n$Ecclesiasticus 15:18 — *he is mighty in power, and beholds all things.* Sirach's all-beholding Yahuah (God) is the eyes in every place of Proverbs 15:3.$n$),
(220,4582,$n$Ecclesiasticus 15:20 — *He has commanded no man to do wickedly, neither has he given any man licence to sin.* No licence to sin from Yahuah (God); the sin comes out of the heart at Matthew 15:18.$n$),
(228,4615,$n$Ecclesiasticus 16:12 — *he judges a man according to his works.* Judged according to his works is the Son of Adam's reward at Matthew 16:27.$n$),
(228,4616,$n$Ecclesiasticus 16:14 — *Make way for every work of mercy: for every man shall find according to his works.* Every man finds according to his works when the Son of Adam comes at Matthew 16:27.$n$),
(404,4821,$n$Ecclesiasticus 16:14 — *every man shall find according to his works.* Finding according to works is the servant made ruler over all his goods at Matthew 24:47.$n$),
(392,4767,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:3 — *made them according to his image.* The image stamped on man is the image the King asks about at Matthew 22:20.$n$),
(378,4706,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:11 — *the law of life for an heritage.* The law of life is the keep the commandments that enters into life at Matthew 19:17.$n$),
(392,4768,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:11 — *he gave them knowledge, and the law of life for an heritage.* The law of life given as heritage is among the things that are Elohim's (God's) at Matthew 22:21.$n$),
(394,4776,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:14 — *he gave every man commandment concerning his neighbour.* The commandment concerning the neighbour is the second great commandment of Matthew 22:39.$n$),
(389,4754,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:24 — *to them that repent, he granted them return.* Return granted to them that repent is the son who afterward repented, and went at Matthew 21:29.$n$),
(9363,40334,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:24 — *to them that repent, he granted them return, and comforted those that failed in patience.* Return granted to the penitent is the no pleasure in the death of him that dieth of Ezekiel 18:32.$n$),
(9363,40336,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:25 — *make your prayer before his face, and offend less.* Prayer before his face and offending less is James's draw nigh and cleanse your hands at James 4:8.$n$),
(9363,40333,$n$Ecclesiasticus 17:26 — *Turn again to the Most High, and turn away from iniquity.* Turn away from iniquity is Ezekiel's so iniquity shall not be your ruin at Ezekiel 18:30.$n$),
(9365,40359,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:9 — *The number of a man’s days at the most are an hundred years.* A hundred years at the most is the flower of the field of Psalm 103:15.$n$),
(646,7927,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:13 — *he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.* The bringing again is the lost sheep carried home on the shepherd's shoulders (Luke 15:5).$n$),
(676,8030,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:13 — *he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.* The Father's own shepherding, handed to the restored Peter in *Feed my sheep* (John 21:16).$n$),
(9366,40357,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:13 — *the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh.* The tender mercies over all his works in Psalm 145:9, said again.$n$),
(676,8028,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:14 — *He has mercy on them that receive discipline.* Peter, grieved at the third asking, receives the discipline and the mercy lands as *Feed my sheep* (John 21:17).$n$),
(9368,40346,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:19 — *Learn before you speak, and use physick or ever you be sick.* Learning before speaking is James' *swift to hear, slow to speak* (James 1:19).$n$),
(9368,40348,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:22 — *Let nothing hinder you to pay your vow in due time.* The rule of Ecclesiastes 5:4, *defer not to pay it*, carried into Sirach's counsel.$n$),
(9369,40341,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:30 — *Go not after your lusts, but refrain thyself from your appetites.* The refraining Sirach commands is done by walking in the Spirit (Galatians 5:16).$n$),
(373,4687,$n$Ecclesiasticus 19:13 — *Admonish a friend, it may be he has not done it.* The private first step of Matthew 18:15 — go to him alone, and he may be gained.$n$),
(373,4689,$n$Ecclesiasticus 19:15 — *for many times it is a slander, and believe not every tale.* The two or three witnesses of Matthew 18:16 guard against the slander Sirach warns of.$n$),
(373,4688,$n$Ecclesiasticus 19:17 — *Admonish your neighbour before you threaten him.* The order and the temper of the private word in Matthew 18:15 — admonish first, and not in anger.$n$),
(9374,40364,$n$Ecclesiasticus 19:29 — *A man may be known by his look.* What is inward shows outwardly; *Ye shall know them by their fruits* (Matthew 7:16).$n$),
(373,4690,$n$Ecclesiasticus 20:2 — *It is much better to reprove, than to be angry secretly.* The open reproof before the assembly in Matthew 18:17 — the alternative to secret anger that restores no one.$n$),
(9375,40394,$n$Ecclesiasticus 20:5 — *There is one that keepeth silence, and is found wise.* The same paradox as Proverbs 17:28 — the fool who holds his peace is counted wise.$n$),
(201,4548,$n$Ecclesiasticus 20:30 — *Wisdom that is hid, and treasure that is hoarded up, what profit is in them both.* The treasure hid in the field is found and bought with everything (Matthew 13:44).$n$),
(389,4757,$n$Ecclesiasticus 21:1 — *have you sinned? do so no more, but ask pardon for your former sins.* The repentance John preached — the publicans and harlots did it, the office refused it (Matthew 21:32).$n$),
(9380,40411,$n$Ecclesiasticus 21:1 — *do so no more, but ask pardon for your former sins.* The turn Wisdom calls for in Proverbs 9:6 — forsake the foolish, and live.$n$),
(9381,40410,$n$Ecclesiasticus 21:11 — *He that keepeth the law of Yahuah (God) getteth the understanding thereof.* The fear of Yahuah (LORD) that begins wisdom in Proverbs 9:10 is made perfect in keeping the law.$n$),
(642,7919,$n$Ecclesiasticus 21:14 — *The inner parts of a fool are like a broken vessel.* The lawyers who took away the key of knowledge held none themselves (Luke 11:52).$n$),
(642,7918,$n$Ecclesiasticus 21:15 — *it displeaseth him, and he casteth it behind his back.* The word cast behind the back, then the key of knowledge taken away from those entering (Luke 11:52).$n$),
(407,4833,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:1 — *A slothful man is compared to a filthy stone.* The *wicked and slothful servant* of Matthew 25:26 — the work left undone is the disgrace.$n$),
(64,4372,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:7 — *Whoso teaches a fool is as one that glueth a potsherd together.* The futility of teaching the one who cannot receive; *neither cast ye your pearls before swine* (Matthew 7:6).$n$),
(64,4373,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:8 — *He that telleth a tale to a fool speaks to one in a slumber.* Discernment of the hearer, made a kingdom rule in Matthew 7:6.$n$),
(68,4381,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:16 — *so the heart that is stablished by advised counsel shall fear at no time.* The hearer and doer who built on the rock (Matthew 7:24) has the heart Sirach describes.$n$),
(68,7913,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:16 — *As timber girt and bound together in a building cannot be loosed with shaking.* Luke 6:48's builder *digged deep*, and the stream *could not shake* the house.$n$),
(68,4382,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:17 — *A heart settled upon a thought of understanding.* The settled heart is the hearer and doer of Matthew 7:24.$n$),
(68,4383,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:18 — *so a fearful heart in the imagination of a fool cannot stand against any fear.* The wind of Matthew 7:25 that cannot move the house on the rock blows the pales flat.$n$),
(68,7912,$n$Ecclesiasticus 22:18 — *Pales set on an high place will never stand against the wind.* The hearer who does not do, *without a foundation*, falls and the ruin is great (Luke 6:49).$n$),
(52,4336,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:9 — *Accustom not your mouth to swearing.* The casual oath Sirach warns against is what Matthew 5:33 begins to cut away.$n$),
(398,4793,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:9 — *neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.* Every oath counts; the graded oaths of Matthew 23:16 are the evasion Sirach's counsel never allowed.$n$),
(52,4337,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:10 — *he that sweareth and nameth Yahuah (God) continually shall not be faultless.* The mark habitual swearing leaves; *Swear not at all; neither by heaven* (Matthew 5:34).$n$),
(52,4338,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:11 — *A man that useth much swearing shall be filled with iniquity.* The iniquity that fills the swearer is the evil Matthew 5:37 names at the root.$n$),
(398,4794,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:11 — *and if he swear in vain, he shall not be innocent.* No oath is empty — to swear by heaven is to swear by the throne (Matthew 23:22).$n$),
(9006,39109,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:19 — *the eyes of Yahuah (God) are ten thousand times brighter than the sun.* The eyes that see the secret parts and the ear that hears all murmuring (Wisdom of Solomon 1:10).$n$),
(388,4752,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:25 — *Her children shall not take root, and her branches shall bring forth no fruit.* Leaves without fruit; the fig tree told *Let no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever* (Matthew 21:19).$n$),
(207,4571,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:3 — *I came out of the mouth of the Most High, and covered the earth as a cloud.* The Word from the Father's mouth is the one on the water saying *it is I* (Matthew 14:27).$n$),
(381,4721,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:8 — *Let your dwelling be in Jacob, and your inheritance in Yashar'el (Israel).* Wisdom planted in Yashar'el (Israel), the vineyard the householder hires labourers into (Matthew 20:1).$n$),
(13316,55059,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:9 — *He created me from the beginning before the world, and I shall never fail.* Wisdom before the world is the Son by whom all things were created (Colossians 1:16).$n$),
(381,4722,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:12 — *I took root in an honourable people.* The honourable people is the vineyard of Matthew 20:1 — Yashar'el (Israel), the Father's own inheritance.$n$),
(672,8021,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:17 — *As the vine brought I forth pleasant savour.* The vine of Wisdom planted in Jacob is the true vine of John 15:1.$n$),
(122,4472,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:19 — *Come to me, all you that be desirous of me.* Wisdom's invitation; *wisdom is justified of her children* who came (Matthew 11:19).$n$),
(127,4492,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:19 — *Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.* The same call Yahusha (Jesus) gives at Matthew 11:28, *come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden*; Wisdom speaking before she came in flesh.$n$),
(205,4566,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:19 — *all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.* In the desert place *they did all eat, and were filled* (Matthew 14:20); Wisdom feeding her children, with twelve baskets left for the twelve tribes.$n$),
(18046,71934,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:19 — *Come to me, all you that be desirous of me.* Wisdom's invitation stands beside Isaiah 55:1, *come ye, buy, and eat*; the same free call to come and be filled.$n$),
(661,7983,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:21 — *they that drink me shall yet be thirsty.* The thirst that keeps a man coming back to Wisdom; at John 4:14 that same Wisdom in flesh sets the well inside him, *a well of water springing up into everlasting life*.$n$),
(672,8020,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:23 — *the book of the covenant of the most high Yahuah (God), even the law which Moses commanded.* Wisdom's vine named as the Torah itself; to abide in the true vine of John 15:5 is to abide in the Word the Father spoke.$n$),
(13792,56890,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:23 — *the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.* The Wisdom sweeter than honey is the Torah, the inheritance whose keeping holds *great reward* (Psalm 19:11).$n$),
(14356,58679,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:23 — *the law which Moses commanded for an heritage to the congregations of Jacob.* Wisdom named as the Torah, the heritage of Jacob; the word, statutes and judgments shown to Yashar'el (Israel) at Psalm 147:19.$n$),
(33848,135262,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:1 — *he that seeks for abundance will turn his eyes away.* The blindness of Ephraim's boast at Hosea 12:8, *they shall find none iniquity in me*; the pursuit of gain hides the sin from the sinner.$n$),
(404,4822,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:4 — *As when one sifteth with a sieve, the refuse remains.* The sifting of the household: the trusted servant's talk and conduct show what he is, and at Matthew 24:51 he is *cut asunder* and given *his portion with the hypocrites*.$n$),
(67,4379,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:6 — *The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed.* The fruit shows the tree, the speech shows the heart; the same test Yahusha (Jesus) gives at Matthew 7:16, *ye shall know them by their fruits*.$n$),
(190,4514,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:6 — *so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.* Words as the fruit of the heart; *out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh* (Matthew 12:34).$n$),
(220,4583,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:6 — *so is the utterance of a conceit in the heart of man.* The heart's conceit comes out in the mouth; *those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart* (Matthew 15:18).$n$),
(67,7915,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:6 — *The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed.* Good tree, good fruit; corrupt tree, corrupt fruit (Luke 6:43), and the mouth is the heart's fruit.$n$),
(67,4380,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:7 — *Praise no man before you hear him speak.* The man is tried in his speaking; *by their fruits ye shall know them* (Matthew 7:20), and a teacher's teaching is his fruit.$n$),
(190,4515,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:7 — *Praise no man before you hear him speak; for this is the trial of men.* Speech as the trial of a man; at Matthew 12:36 that trial reaches the day of judgment, where *every idle word* is given account of.$n$),
(398,4795,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:14 — *The talk of him that sweareth much makes the hair stand upright.* The much swearing Sirach warns of; at Matthew 23:18 it is a whole system of oaths by the altar and oaths by the gift, binding and not binding.$n$),
(63,4369,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:30 — *Malice and wrath, even these are abominations.* The heart condition that has to go before a brother is weighed; *judge not, that ye be not judged* (Matthew 7:1).$n$),
(374,4691,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:30 — *even these are abominations; and the sinful man shall have them both.* Malice and wrath as the sinful man's portion; Peter's *till seven times?* (Matthew 18:21) is still counting, and the King's answer leaves the count behind.$n$),
(63,4370,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:1 — *He that revengeth shall find vengeance from Yahuah (God).* Measure for measure: *with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you again* (Matthew 7:2).$n$),
(36511,147791,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:1 — *He that revengeth shall find vengeance from Yahuah (God).* The snare Abigail turned David from at 1 Samuel 25:26, *avenging thyself with thine own hand*.$n$),
(56,4347,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:2 — *so shall your sins also be forgiven when you prayest.* Forgiveness asked for and forgiveness given in one prayer; *forgive us our debts, as we forgive our debtors* (Matthew 6:12).$n$),
(374,4692,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:2 — *Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you.* The one line behind the King's *seventy times seven* (Matthew 18:22) and the parable that follows it: forgive, and be forgiven.$n$),
(394,4777,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:2 — *Forgive your neighbour the hurt that he has done to you.* Forgiving the hurt is love of neighbour in practice; *thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself* (Matthew 22:39) begins in Leviticus 19:18 with no vengeance and no grudge.$n$),
(374,4693,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:3 — *One man bears hatred against another, and does he seek pardon from Yahuah (God)?.* Sirach's question is the lord's question at Matthew 18:33, *shouldest not thou also have had compassion on thy fellowservant*; the pardon that was not passed on was taken back.$n$),
(56,4349,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:4 — *He sheweth no mercy to a man, which is like himself.* No mercy to the man like yourself, no forgiveness to ask; *if ye forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive* (Matthew 6:15).$n$),
(9542,40999,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:4 — *He sheweth no mercy to a man, which is like himself.* Peter's *how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him?* (Matthew 18:21) stands right at Sirach's question about mercy to the man like yourself.$n$),
(63,4371,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:5 — *If he that is but flesh nourish hatred, who will intreat for pardon of his sins?.* The beam that comes out first (Matthew 7:5); hatred nursed in the heart leaves no one to plead for the man's own pardon.$n$),
(374,4694,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:7 — *Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour.* Torah remembered and malice laid down as one thing; at Matthew 18:35 the Father deals the same way with the one who will not forgive *from your hearts*.$n$),
(394,4778,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:7 — *remember the covenant of the Highest, and wink at ignorance.* Commandments and covenant held together with no malice; at Matthew 22:40 the law and the prophets hang on love, which gathers them up rather than shrinking them.$n$),
(14820,60259,$n$Ecclesiasticus 28:7 — *Remember the commandments, and bear no malice to your neighbour.* No malice rooted in the commandments; the Testaments say *even so forgive him from the heart, and leave to Elohim (God) the avenging*.$n$),
(378,4707,$n$Ecclesiasticus 29:1 — *he that strengtheneth his hand keepeth the commandments.* Mercy to the neighbour as commandment keeping; *if thou wilt enter into life, keep the commandments* (Matthew 19:17), and the test that followed was the poor.$n$),
(9547,41021,$n$Ecclesiasticus 29:2 — *Lend to your neighbour in time of his need.* The open hand of Matthew 5:42, *from him that would borrow of thee turn not thou away*, is Sirach's lending to the neighbour in his need.$n$),
(409,4842,$n$Ecclesiasticus 29:9 — *Help the poor for the commandment’s sake, and turn him not away.* Mercy as the commandment kept; at Matthew 25:40 what was done *unto one of the least of these my brethren* was done to the King.$n$),
(57,4352,$n$Ecclesiasticus 29:11 — *Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High.* The treasure laid up by the open hand of the commandment keeper is the *treasures in heaven* of Matthew 6:20.$n$),
(378,4708,$n$Ecclesiasticus 29:11 — *and it shall bring you more profit than gold.* The treasure laid up by the commandments is the *treasure in heaven* the King offers the young man at Matthew 19:21.$n$),
(644,7933,$n$Ecclesiasticus 29:11 — *Lay up your treasure according to the commandments of the Most High.* The commandment keeper's treasure is the *treasure in the heavens that faileth not* of Luke 12:33, laid up by alms.$n$),
(59,4359,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:5 — *He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption.* The gold-lover cannot stand justified because *ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon* (Matthew 6:24).$n$),
(625,7866,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:5 — *He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption.* The one Sirach says will not be justified is the one who *hardly* enters the kingdom at Mark 10:23.$n$),
(647,7951,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:5 — *he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof.* Gold-love disqualifies; at Luke 16:13 the King closes the steward parable with *ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon*.$n$),
(59,4360,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:6 — *Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present.* Where gold leads; the choice between Elohim (God) and mammon at Matthew 6:24 is the fork before that ruin.$n$),
(625,7865,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:6 — *Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present..* The ruin is in the trusting; *how hard is it for them that trust in riches* (Mark 10:24).$n$),
(647,7950,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:6 — *Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present..* The servant of mammon in Luke 16:13 walks into the ruin Sirach names.$n$),
(59,4361,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:7 — *It is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it.* Gold as a master men sacrifice to; *Ye cannot serve Elohim (God) and mammon* (Matthew 6:24).$n$),
(625,7864,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:7 — *every fool shall be taken with it.* The one taken by his gold cannot pass the needle's eye of Mark 10:25.$n$),
(647,7949,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:7 — *them that sacrifice to it, and every fool shall be taken with it.* To serve mammon (Luke 16:13) is to sacrifice to the stumblingblock.$n$),
(625,7867,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:8 — *Blessed is the rich that is found without blemish, and has not gone after gold..* The rich young ruler of Mark 10:21 was offered that blessing and went away grieved.$n$),
(9559,41051,$n$Ecclesiasticus 31:12 — *If you sit at a bountiful table, be not greedy upon it.* Solomon's knife to the throat (Proverbs 23:2) is the same restraint at the same table.$n$),
(396,4788,$n$Ecclesiasticus 32:1 — *lift not thyself up, but be among them as one of the rest.* The uppermost rooms at feasts (Matthew 23:6) are the opposite of Sirach's master of the feast.$n$),
(381,4723,$n$Ecclesiasticus 33:13 — *As the clay is in the potter’s hand, to fashion it at his pleasure.* The householder's right over his own (Matthew 20:15) is the potter's right over the clay.$n$),
(407,4834,$n$Ecclesiasticus 33:28 — *Set him to work, as is fit for him: if he be not obedient, put on more heavy fetters..* The servant of Matthew 25:27 was judged for doing nothing with his charge.$n$),
(184,4497,$n$Ecclesiasticus 34:18 — *the gifts of unjust men are not accepted.* Sacrifice while condemning the guiltless (Matthew 12:7) is the gift Yahuah (God) does not take.$n$),
(9744,41710,$n$Ecclesiasticus 34:20 — *Whoso brings an offering of the goods of the poor.* Unjust gain only gathers for the merciful (Proverbs 28:8); offered at the altar it is bloodshed.$n$),
(97,4413,$n$Ecclesiasticus 34:26 — *a man that fasteth for his sins, and goes again, and doeth the same: who will hear his prayer?.* Fasting that turns is heard; *then shall they fast* (Matthew 9:15) is fasting with the loss truly felt.$n$),
(93,4399,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:1 — *He that keepeth the law brings offerings enough.* Mercy and not sacrifice (Matthew 9:13) is the commandment kept, which is the offering.$n$),
(30589,4399,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:1 — *He that keepeth the law brings offerings enough.* Mercy and not sacrifice (Matthew 9:13) is the commandment kept, which is the offering.$n$),
(184,4496,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:1 — *he that takes heed to the commandment offers a peace offering.* Heeding the commandment is the offering; mercy over sacrifice at Matthew 12:7.$n$),
(30604,4496,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:1 — *he that takes heed to the commandment offers a peace offering.* Heeding the commandment is the offering; mercy over sacrifice at Matthew 12:7.$n$),
(93,4400,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:2 — *he that gives alms sacrificeth praise.* Alms as sacrifice; *I will have mercy, and not sacrifice* (Matthew 9:13).$n$),
(93,4401,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:3 — *to forsake unrighteousness is a propitiation.* The turning is the offering; Matthew 9:13 calls sinners to that repentance.$n$),
(55,4345,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:6 — *the sweet savour thereof is before the Most High.* Alms in secret (Matthew 6:4) reach the same Most High who receives the righteous offering.$n$),
(55,4346,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:7 — *the memorial thereof shall never be forgotten.* The Father who sees in secret (Matthew 6:4) keeps the memorial men never saw.$n$),
(58,4355,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:8 — *Give Yahuah (God) his honour with a good eye.* The good eye in giving is the single eye of Matthew 6:22.$n$),
(17935,71513,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:8 — *diminish not the firstfruits of yours hands.* Proverbs 3:9's firstfruits, kept whole and given with a good eye.$n$),
(387,4748,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:12 — *Do not think to corrupt with gifts; for such he will not receive.* The den of thieves (Matthew 21:13) was gifts and sacrifices turned to trade, and the judge will not take them.$n$),
(187,4507,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:14 — *He will not despise the supplication of the fatherless; nor the widow.* The bruised reed not broken (Matthew 12:20) is the same mercy toward the ones who cry.$n$),
(228,4617,$n$Ecclesiasticus 35:19 — *Till he have rendered to every man according to his deeds.* Reward according to works at the Son of Adam's coming (Matthew 16:27).$n$),
(221,4585,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:1 — *Have mercy upon us, O Yahuah (God) of all, and behold us.* The prayer for mercy that the mission to the lost sheep (Matthew 15:24) answers.$n$),
(69,4384,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *Gather all the tribes of Jacob together, and inherit you them, as from the beginning..* The many from east and west (Matthew 8:11) are the tribes coming home to their fathers' table.$n$),
(221,4586,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *inherit you them, as from the beginning.* The lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) in Matthew 15:24 are the tribes Sirach prayed home.$n$),
(380,4718,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *Gather all the tribes of Jacob together.* Twelve thrones for twelve tribes (Matthew 19:28); the tribes gathered whole.$n$),
(390,4762,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *inherit you them, as from the beginning.* The one nation bringing forth fruit (Matthew 21:43) is the gathered tribes of Jacob, not a replacement.$n$),
(628,7880,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *Gather all the tribes of Jacob together.* The elect gathered from the four winds (Mark 13:27) is this prayer answered.$n$),
(632,7888,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *inherit you them, as from the beginning.* Mary's *he hath holpen his servant Yashar'el (Israel)* (Luke 1:54) is this prayer beginning to be answered.$n$),
(645,7928,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *Gather all the tribes of Jacob together.* From east, west, north and south (Luke 13:29) the scattered tribes sit down at home.$n$),
(650,7940,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *inherit you them, as from the beginning.* To seek and save the lost (Luke 19:10) is to gather the tribes Sirach prayed for.$n$),
(669,8003,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:11 — *Gather all the tribes of Jacob together.* The children of Elohim (God) scattered abroad (John 11:52) are the tribes this prayer asks home.$n$),
(69,4385,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:12 — *Yashar'el (Israel), whom you have named your firstborn.* The firstborn's scattered children sit down with the fathers in Matthew 8:11.$n$),
(632,7889,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:12 — *have mercy upon the people that is called by your name.* Mercy remembered toward his servant Yashar'el (Israel) in Luke 1:54.$n$),
(650,7939,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:12 — *whom you have named your firstborn.* The lost whom the Son of Adam seeks (Luke 19:10) is the firstborn scattered.$n$),
(386,4745,$n$Ecclesiasticus 36:14 — *Fill Sion with your unspeakable oracles, and your people with your glory.* The glory entered the city at Matthew 21:9 to the cry of Hosanna.$n$),
(415,4866,$n$Ecclesiasticus 37:2 — *when a companion and friend is turned to an enemy.* *Friend, wherefore art thou come?* (Matthew 26:50) is this grief spoken in the garden.$n$),
(99,4415,$n$Ecclesiasticus 38:9 — *pray to Yahuah (God), and he will make you whole.* The woman who touched the hem (Matthew 9:20) made that prayer with her hand.$n$),
(99,4416,$n$Ecclesiasticus 38:12 — *give place to the physician, for Yahuah (God) has created him.* The bleeding woman gave place to physicians for twelve years; at Matthew 9:22 she reached past them to the one who made them, and her faith made her whole.$n$),
(99,4417,$n$Ecclesiasticus 38:14 — *they shall also pray to Yahuah (God), that he would prosper that, which they give.* The physician prays that his remedy would prosper; at Matthew 9:25 the one he prays to takes the dead girl by the hand himself.$n$),
(194,4529,$n$Ecclesiasticus 39:6 — *he shall be filled with the spirit of understanding: he shall pour out wise sentences.* Understanding is given when Yahuah (God) wills; the blessed eyes and ears of Matthew 13:16 are given the same way.$n$),
(9772,41847,$n$Ecclesiasticus 40:1 — *an heavy yoke is upon the sons of Adam, from the day that they go out of their mother’s womb.* Job 14:1 says the same of the man born of a woman: few days, full of trouble, the yoke laid on Adam carried down his line.$n$),
(206,4567,$n$Ecclesiasticus 43:23 — *By his counsel he appeaseth the deep, and planteth islands in it.* The one who stills the deep by his counsel walks on it at Matthew 14:25; Job 9:8 said he alone treads upon the waves of the sea.$n$),
(206,4568,$n$Ecclesiasticus 43:25 — *in it be strange and wondrous works, variety of all kinds of beasts and whales created.* The sea is the Creator's storehouse of wonders; at Matthew 14:25 the Creator walks on it and tells them, it is I.$n$),
(10004,42684,$n$Ecclesiasticus 44:7 — *All these were honoured in their generations, and were the glory of their times.* The honoured men of Sirach 44 are the roll of Hebrews 11; the glory of their times was faith in things not yet seen.$n$),
(10007,42676,$n$Ecclesiasticus 44:17 — *Noah was found perfect and righteous; in the time of wrath he was taken in exchange for the world.* Hebrews 11:7 shows the faith behind the righteousness: Noah believed what was not yet seen and built, and was left as a remnant.$n$),
(10008,42674,$n$Ecclesiasticus 44:20 — *Who kept the law of the Most High, and was in covenant with him.* The law Abraham kept is the walk Yahuah (God) commanded at Genesis 17:1, and the covenant in his flesh is the circumcision of the same chapter.$n$),
(38,4287,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:1 — *Then stood up Elias the prophet as fire, and his word burned like a lamp.* John comes preaching in the wilderness at Matthew 3:1 with the same fire in his word; he is the Elijah who was to come.$n$),
(204,4559,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:1 — *his word burned like a lamp.* Elijah's fire at Ahab's court is John's word to Herod at Matthew 14:3–4; the king answers the prophet by binding him.$n$),
(204,4560,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:2 — *by his zeal he diminished their number.* Elijah's zeal against Ahab's court brought famine; John's zeal against Herod's court brought his imprisonment at Matthew 14:3.$n$),
(38,4288,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:10 — *to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.* The Elijah errand ends in the restoring of the tribes of Jacob; the voice in the wilderness at Matthew 3:3 is its beginning.$n$),
(30411,4288,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:10 — *to turn the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.* The Elijah errand ends in the restoring of the tribes of Jacob; the voice in the wilderness at Matthew 3:3 is its beginning.$n$),
(119,4464,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:10 — *to restore the tribes of Jacob.* Yahusha (Jesus) names John as Elias at Matthew 11:14, and Sirach says what Elijah comes to do: restore the tribes of Jacob, all twelve.$n$),
(30600,4464,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:10 — *to restore the tribes of Jacob.* Yahusha (Jesus) names John as Elias at Matthew 11:14, and Sirach says what Elijah comes to do: restore the tribes of Jacob, all twelve.$n$),
(204,4561,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:10 — *Who were ordained for reproofs in their times, to pacify the wrath of the judgment of Yahuah (God).* The multitude at Matthew 14:5 counted John a prophet; Sirach describes which prophet, the Elijah ordained for reproofs and the restoring of Jacob.$n$),
(204,4562,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:11 — *Blessed are they that saw you, and slept in love; for we shall surely live.* John's disciples at Matthew 14:12 bury the Elijah of their day and go to Yahusha (Jesus), the one in whom we shall surely live.$n$),
(38,4290,$n$Ecclesiasticus 48:12 — *Elias it was, who was covered with a whirlwind: and Eliseus was filled with his spirit.* The spirit passed from Elijah to Elisha at the Jordan; at Matthew 3:13 Yahusha (Jesus) comes to the same river and the Spirit descends.$n$),
(10311,43706,$n$Ecclesiasticus 50:15 — *poured of the blood of the grape, he poured out at the foot of the altar a sweetsmelling savour.* The blood of the grape at the altar's foot pictures what Hebrews 9:12 says the Messiah (Christ) did with his own blood, once.$n$),
(127,4488,$n$Ecclesiasticus 51:23 — *Draw near to me, you unlearned, and dwell in the house of learning.* Sirach's closing invitation is the one Yahusha (Jesus) gives at Matthew 11:28; come near, and find the rest in the house of learning.$n$),
(127,4489,$n$Ecclesiasticus 51:26 — *Put your neck under the yoke, and let your soul receive instruction.* The yoke is the instruction and the rest is found under it, not away from it; Matthew 11:29 says take my yoke, learn of me.$n$),
(30603,4489,$n$Ecclesiasticus 51:26 — *Put your neck under the yoke, and let your soul receive instruction.* The yoke is the instruction and the rest is found under it, not away from it; Matthew 11:29 says take my yoke, learn of me.$n$),
(127,4490,$n$Ecclesiasticus 51:27 — *I have but little labour, and have gotten to me much rest.* Sirach's little labour and much rest under the yoke is Matthew 11:30, my yoke is easy and my burden is light.$n$),
(407,4835,$n$Ecclesiasticus 51:30 — *Work your work betimes, and in his time he will give you your reward.* The reward comes in his time; at Matthew 25:23 the faithful servant receives it when the master returns, not before.$n$),
(10406,44008,$n$Judith 4:7 — *it was easy to stop them that would come up, because the passage was straight.* The invader Judith's people brace for at the passes is the king of Daniel 11:30, whose indignation is against the holy covenant.$n$),
(10491,44245,$n$Judith 9:11 — *a protector of the forlorn, a saviour of them that are without hope.* Esther prays the same thing at The Rest of Esther 14:14, no other help but you; two women alone before a power bent on their people.$n$),
(10469,44173,$n$Judith 16:6 — *the Almighty Yahuah (God) has disappointed them by the hand of a woman.* Yahuah (God) bringing an army to nothing by the hand of a widow is the weak confounding the mighty of 1 Corinthians 1:27.$n$),
(10471,44166,$n$Judith 16:14 — *for you spakest, and they were made, you did send forth your spirit, and it created them.* Creation by the spoken word; Psalm 148:5 calls all of it to praise the one who commanded, and they were created.$n$),
(10492,44243,$n$The Rest of Esther 14:5 — *tookest Yashar'el (Israel) from among all people, and our fathers from all their predecessors, for a perpetual inheritance.* Esther in exile recites what Moses said at Deuteronomy 7:6, a people chosen from among all people, and the choosing holds in the scattering.$n$),
(10493,44240,$n$The Rest of Esther 14:6 — *we have sinned before you: therefore have you given us into the hands of our enemies.* Esther prays inside Leviticus 26:41, the sin named, the enemy's hand owned as just, the humbled heart that turns the curse toward home.$n$),
(194,4528,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 1:4 — *into a malicious soul wisdom shall not enter.* Wisdom does not enter a soul set against her; at Matthew 13:13 the parables meet eyes that have closed themselves.$n$),
(190,4516,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 1:11 — *there is no word so secret, that shall go for nought.* No word goes for nothing; Matthew 12:36 says every idle word is accounted for in the day of judgment.$n$),
(220,4584,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 1:11 — *the mouth that belieth slayeth the soul.* The lying mouth of Wisdom 1:11 and the false witness of Matthew 15:19 come from the same place, the heart.$n$),
(227,4611,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 1:13 — *Yahuah (God) made not death: neither has he pleasure in the destruction of the living.* Death is not of Yahuah's (God's) making, so the gates of the grave at Matthew 16:18 have no lasting claim on the assembly he builds.$n$),
(70,4387,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:12 — *let us lie in wait for the righteous; because he is not for our turn.* The righteous one of Wisdom 2 is Isaiah's servant; at Matthew 8:17 he bears our sicknesses while the ungodly lie in wait for him.$n$),
(121,4469,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:12 — *he is clean contrary to our doings: he upbraideth us with our offending the law.* The contradictory complaints of Matthew 11:19 hide the real grievance Wisdom 2:12 names: the righteous one reproves their lawbreaking.$n$),
(208,4573,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:12 — *he upbraideth us with our offending the law.* John upbraids Herod with the law at Matthew 14:3–4 and is bound for it, the ungodly lying in wait for the righteous.$n$),
(226,4606,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:12 — *let us lie in wait for the righteous.* The plot of Wisdom 2 ends in a shameful death; at Matthew 16:21 Yahusha (Jesus) names the same end and adds the third day.$n$),
(675,8036,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:12 — *Therefore let us lie in wait for the righteous.* The band with lanterns and torches at John 18:3 is the lying in wait of the ungodly for the righteous one who reproves them.$n$),
(46,4318,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:13 — *he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).* The ungodly test the righteous one's claim to be the child of Yahuah (God); the tempter at Matthew 4:3 says if thou be the Son.$n$),
(193,4522,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:13 — *He professeth to have the knowledge of Yahuah (God): and he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).* The child of Yahuah (God) is known by a life of another fashion; Matthew 12:50 says the same, whoever does the will of my Father.$n$),
(675,8035,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:13 — *he calls himself the child of Yahuah (God).* The capital charge of John 19:7, he made himself the Son of Elohim (God), is the complaint Wisdom 2:13 gives the ungodly.$n$),
(121,4470,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:15 — *his life is not like other men’s, his ways are of another fashion.* The life of another fashion is what the slander of Matthew 11:19 was aimed at — a glutton and winebibber for sitting with sinners.$n$),
(46,4319,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:16 — *makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.* The boast the ungodly resent is the claim the tempter dares him to prove at Matthew 4:6.$n$),
(226,4607,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:16 — *We are esteemed of him as counterfeits: he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness.* The grievance of the religious seat, which is why he must suffer of the elders and chief priests and scribes (Matthew 16:21).$n$),
(675,8034,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:16 — *he pronounceth the end of the just to be blessed, and makes his boast that Yahuah (God) is his father.* Pilate found no fault at all (John 18:38); the only charge was the one Wisdom named.$n$),
(46,4320,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:18 — *if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him.* The ungodly's test is the devil's dare at Matthew 4:6, down to the cut quotation of Psalm 91.$n$),
(70,4388,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:18 — *he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.* The help came as bearing, not escape — *Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses* (Matthew 8:17).$n$),
(193,4523,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:18 — *For if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him.* The ungodly test sonship by rescue; Yahusha (Jesus) marks his family by doing the Father's will (Matthew 12:50).$n$),
(224,4601,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:18 — *if the just man be the son of Yahuah (God), he will help him.* Said in mockery here; confessed by Peter at Matthew 16:16 as *the Son of the living Elohim (God).*$n$),
(675,8031,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:18 — *he will help him, and deliver him from the hand of his enemies.* The deliverance came through *It is finished* (John 19:30) and the third day, not by coming down from the cross.$n$),
(208,4574,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:19 — *Let us examine him with despitefulness and torture, that we may know his meekness.* Herod's heart toward John, held back only by fear of the multitude (Matthew 14:5).$n$),
(675,8033,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:19 — *that we may know his meekness, and prove his patience.* The scourging and the crown of thorns at John 19:1–5 are this test carried out, and *Behold the man* is the answer.$n$),
(46,4321,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:20 — *Let us condemn him with a shameful death.* The plot's end; Matthew 4:11's *Then the devil leaveth him* is only the end of its first round.$n$),
(121,4471,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:20 — *Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* The end of the road whose first step is the slander of Matthew 11:19.$n$),
(208,4575,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:20 — *Let us condemn him with a shameful death.* John beheaded in the prison on a birthday oath (Matthew 14:10) is the death Wisdom describes.$n$),
(226,4608,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:20 — *Let us condemn him with a shameful death: for by his own saying he shall be respected.* The shameful death is the cross Yahusha (Jesus) said he must die at Matthew 16:21, hung on the tree of Deuteronomy 21:23.$n$),
(654,7953,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:20 — *Let us condemn him with a shameful death.* The suffering half the Emmaus disciples could not read — *Ought not Messiah (Christ) to have suffered these things* (Luke 24:26).$n$),
(675,8032,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:20 — *Let us condemn him with a shameful death.* The resolve that became *Crucify him, crucify him* at John 19:6.$n$),
(392,4769,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:23 — *made him to be an image of his own eternity.* Caesar's image is on the coin (Matthew 22:20); the Father's image is on the man who holds it.$n$),
(203,4556,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:24 — *through envy of the devil came death into the world: and they that do hold of his side.* The enemy who sowed the tares (Matthew 13:39) is the one whose envy let death in.$n$),
(641,7922,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:24 — *through envy of the devil came death into the world.* The one who let death in is the one Yahusha (Jesus) saw fall like lightning (Luke 10:18).$n$),
(48,4326,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:1 — *the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* Where the persecuted of Matthew 5:10 end up — theirs is the kingdom.$n$),
(110,4445,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:1 — *there shall no torment touch them.* The persecutor kills the body but cannot kill the soul held in the Father's hand (Matthew 10:28).$n$),
(226,4609,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:1 — *But the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God).* The other half of the shameful death — killed, *and be raised again the third day* (Matthew 16:21).$n$),
(383,4730,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:1 — *the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God).* What held the sons of Zebedee while they drank the cup they said they could drink (Matthew 20:22).$n$),
(393,4774,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:1 — *the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* Kept and asleep, not in heaven now — the living Abraham, Isaac and Jacob of Matthew 22:32, held for the resurrection.$n$),
(627,7860,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:1 — *the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God).* Why the fathers are living to the Father — *He is not the Elohim (God) of the dead, but the Elohim (God) of the living* (Mark 12:27).$n$),
(627,7859,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:4 — *though they be punished in the sight of men, yet is their hope full of immortality.* The hope the bush bears witness to at Mark 12:26 — the Elohim (God) of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, long after their burial.$n$),
(110,4446,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:5 — *Yahuah (God) proved them, and found them worthy for himself.* The proving is the hatred of all men; *he that endureth to the end shall be saved* (Matthew 10:22).$n$),
(9143,39555,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:6 — *As gold in the furnace has he tried them.* Sirach says the same at Ecclesiasticus 2:5 — *acceptable men in the furnace of adversity.*$n$),
(199,4543,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:7 — *in the time of their visitation they shall shine, and run to and fro like sparks among the stubble.* The harvest scene of Matthew 13:43 — tares to the fire, *then shall the righteous shine forth as the sun.*$n$),
(404,4823,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:7 — *in the time of their visitation they shall shine.* The visitation is the lord's coming at Matthew 24:46, and the servant found so doing is the one who shines.$n$),
(406,4828,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:7 — *in the time of their visitation they shall shine.* The visitation is the Son of Adam coming in his glory at Matthew 25:31.$n$),
(421,4890,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:7 — *in the time of their visitation they shall shine.* The empty tomb at Matthew 28:6 is the firstfruits of that visitation; the rest sleep and wait.$n$),
(202,4555,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:8 — *They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people.* After the angels sever the wicked from among the just (Matthew 13:49), the just are the ones who judge.$n$),
(380,4719,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:8 — *They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people.* The outer ring of the kingdom whose inner ring is the twelve thrones over the twelve tribes (Matthew 19:28).$n$),
(406,4829,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:8 — *They shall judge the nations, and have dominion over the people.* The reign that follows the gathering and sorting of Matthew 25:32 — sheep from goats first, then the saints possess the kingdom.$n$),
(421,4891,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:8 — *their Elohim (God) shall reign for ever.* The reign of risen saints on the earth, which began with *he is risen* at Matthew 28:6.$n$),
(48,4327,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:9 — *grace and mercy is to his saints, and he has care for his elect.* The reward laid up for the persecuted at Matthew 5:12, who join the line of the prophets.$n$),
(376,4702,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:14 — *to him shall be given the special gift of faith, and an inheritance in the temple of Yahuah (God).* The gift behind Matthew 19:12's *he that is able to receive it, let him receive it.*$n$),
(388,4750,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 4:4 — *though they flourish in branches for a time; yet standing not last.* Leaves without fruit at Matthew 21:19 is the same show of life with nothing under it.$n$),
(388,4751,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 4:5 — *The imperfect branches shall be broken off, their fruit unprofitable.* The fig tree with leaves and no fruit (Matthew 21:19) is the leadership, not the people; the branch that bears nothing is broken off.$n$),
(110,4447,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:1 — *the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him.* The one who confesses the King before his afflicters (Matthew 10:32) is the one who stands in boldness before them on that day.$n$),
(185,4499,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:1 — *Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him.* The council to destroy him (Matthew 12:14) is the verdict this age renders; the day of judgment reverses it in front of them.$n$),
(226,4610,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:1 — *stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him, and made no account of his labours.* Killed and raised the third day (Matthew 16:21) is where the righteous man's standing before his afflicters begins.$n$),
(383,4731,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:1 — *the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him.* The cup the sons of Zebedee will drink (Matthew 20:23) comes first; the standing in boldness before the afflicters comes after.$n$),
(406,4831,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:1 — *Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him.* The day the righteous stand in boldness is the day of Matthew 25:41; the fire was prepared for the devil and his angels, and the goats are the unfruitful among the gathered.$n$),
(654,7952,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:1 — *Then shall the righteous man stand in great boldness before the face of such as have afflicted him.* The glory the Messiah (Christ) enters after suffering (Luke 24:26) is this standing in boldness before those who afflicted him.$n$),
(185,4500,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:4 — *We fools accounted his life madness, and his end to be without honour.* The council to destroy him (Matthew 12:14) is the judgment of fools that the day of judgment turns into their own confession.$n$),
(406,4830,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:5 — *How is he numbered among the children of Yahuah (God), and his lot is among the saints.* The lot among the saints is the kingdom prepared from the foundation of the world (Matthew 25:34), revealed on the day, not assigned on it.$n$),
(399,4800,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:8 — *What has pride profited us? or what good has riches with our vaunting brought us.* The inside of the cup full of extortion and excess (Matthew 23:25) is what the day exposes, and pride will have profited nothing.$n$),
(60,4362,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:15 — *the care of them is with the Most High.* The Father who feeds the birds (Matthew 6:26) is the Most High whose care the righteous are already in.$n$),
(383,4732,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:15 — *their reward also is with Yahuah (God).* The seats that are the Father's to give (Matthew 20:23) are the reward kept with Yahuah (God) for the righteous.$n$),
(421,4892,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:15 — *But the righteous live for evermore; their reward also is with Yahuah (God).* The risen body the women held by the feet (Matthew 28:9) is the firstfruits of the righteous living for evermore.$n$),
(60,4363,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:16 — *Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God).* Seek the kingdom first (Matthew 6:33); the kingdom and the crown come from the Father's hand, and all the rest is added.$n$),
(391,4766,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:16 — *Therefore shall they receive a glorious kingdom, and a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God).* The man without a wedding garment (Matthew 22:12) has no crown to receive; the kingdom and the crown go to those clothed for the feast.$n$),
(421,4893,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:16 — *a beautiful crown from the hand of Yahuah (God): for with his right hand shall he cover them.* The risen King the women worshipped (Matthew 28:9) is the first of the righteous to receive the kingdom and the crown from the Father's hand.$n$),
(397,4792,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:4 — *being ministers of his kingdom, you have not judged aright, nor kept the law.* Ministers who have not kept the law are the ones who shut up the kingdom against men (Matthew 23:13).$n$),
(123,4475,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:5 — *a sharp judgment shall be to them that be in high places.* Chorazin and Bethsaida (Matthew 11:21) saw the mighty works and did not repent; the more shown, the sharper the judgment.$n$),
(123,4476,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:6 — *mercy will soon pardon the meanest: but mighty men shall be mightily tormented.* More tolerable for Tyre and Sidon (Matthew 11:22): the judgment is real and weighed by what each was shown.$n$),
(123,4477,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:8 — *But a sore trial shall come upon the mighty.* Capernaum, exalted unto heaven and brought down to the grave (Matthew 11:23), is the mighty under the sore trial.$n$),
(65,4374,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:12 — *she is easily seen of them that love her, and found of such as seek her.* Seek and ye shall find (Matthew 7:7) is the promise Solomon wrote about wisdom first.$n$),
(30025,4374,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:12 — *she is easily seen of them that love her, and found of such as seek her.* Seek and ye shall find (Matthew 7:7) is the promise Solomon wrote about wisdom first.$n$),
(65,4375,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:13 — *She preventeth them that desire her, in making herself first known to them.* Wisdom goes before those who desire her; the Father answers ask, seek, knock (Matthew 7:7) because he came out to meet the seeker first.$n$),
(394,4779,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:18 — *love is the keeping of her laws; and the giving heed to her laws is the assurance of incorruption.* The two commandments all the law hangs on (Matthew 22:40) do not replace the law; love is the keeping of it.$n$),
(200,4546,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 6:22 — *I will tell you, and will not hide mysteries from you.* Things kept secret from the foundation of the world uttered in parables (Matthew 13:35) is the same refusal to hide mysteries.$n$),
(126,4487,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 7:7 — *I prayed, and understanding was given me: I called upon Yahuah (God), and the spirit of wisdom came to me.* Understanding is given to the one who prays; the Father reveals to babes what the wise cannot reach (Matthew 11:25).$n$),
(201,4550,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 7:8 — *I preferred her before sceptres and thrones, and esteemed riches nothing in comparison of her.* The merchant who sells all for one pearl (Matthew 13:45) is doing Solomon's arithmetic: sceptres and riches are nothing beside wisdom.$n$),
(201,4551,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 7:14 — *she is a treasure to men that never fails: which they that use become the friends of Yahuah (God).* The treasure hid in a field worth selling all for (Matthew 13:44) is the treasure that never fails and makes friends of Yahuah (God).$n$),
(1711,12213,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 7:25 — *a pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty.* What flows out from the glory without lessening it is a person in Hebrews 1:3, the brightness of his glory.$n$),
(12597,52030,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 7:25 — *the breath of the power of Yahuah (God), and a pure influence flowing from the glory of the Almighty.* The breath of the power flowing from the glory is the one in the form of Elohim (God) (Philippians 2:6), brought forth, not a rival.$n$),
(207,4572,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 9:1 — *who have made all things with your word.* The Word all things were made with walks on the sea and says it is I (Matthew 14:27).$n$),
(392,4770,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 9:1 — *who have made all things with your word.* Render unto Elohim (God) the things that are his (Matthew 22:21): all things, since he made them all with his word.$n$),
(124,4480,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 10:7 — *a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an unbelieving soul.* Sodom, the standing monument of unbelief, is the floor the King measures Capernaum against (Matthew 11:24).$n$),
(22645,90159,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 10:14 — *left him not in bonds, till she brought him the sceptre of the kingdom.* Joseph, not left in bonds but made lord of Pharaoh's house (Psalm 105:21), is the sold man raised to the sceptre.$n$),
(189,4513,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 12:10 — *their malice was bred in them, and that their cogitation would never be changed.* The cogitation that would never be changed is the heart behind the word against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) that is not forgiven (Matthew 12:32).$n$),
(54,4342,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 12:19 — *by such works have you taught your people that the just man should be merciful.* Love your enemies (Matthew 5:44) is the lesson the Father taught by his own mercy on enemies.$n$),
(54,4343,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 12:20 — *giving them time and place, by which they might be delivered from their malice.* Sun on the evil and rain on the unjust (Matthew 5:45) is the time and place the Father gives enemies to turn.$n$),
(72,4393,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 14:3 — *your providence, O Father, governeth it: for you have made a way in the sea.* The one who rebuked the winds and the sea (Matthew 8:26) is the one whose providence governs it.$n$),
(206,4569,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 14:3 — *you have made a way in the sea, and a safe path in the waves.* The King walking on the sea in the fourth watch (Matthew 14:25) is walking the way the Father made in the sea.$n$),
(72,4394,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 14:4 — *Shewing that you can save from all danger.* Lord, save us, we perish (Matthew 8:25) is answered by the one who can save from all danger at sea (Matthew 8:26).$n$),
(100,4418,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:13 — *you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.* The maid not dead but sleeping (Matthew 9:24) is raised by the one who leads to the grave and brings up again.$n$),
(191,4517,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:13 — *you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.* The sign of Jonah walks that road: three days and three nights in the heart of the earth, then up (Matthew 12:40).$n$),
(227,4612,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:13 — *you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again.* The gates that could not hold the raised King will not prevail against his assembly (Matthew 16:18).$n$),
(100,4419,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:14 — *the spirit, when it is gone forth, returns not.* What no man can do, the King does with his hand on a dead girl (Matthew 9:25).$n$),
(205,4564,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:20 — *did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour.* Bread without labour again, blessed and broken in a desert place and handed out by the disciples (Matthew 14:19).$n$),
(30906,4564,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:20 — *did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour.* Bread without labour again, blessed and broken in a desert place and handed out by the disciples (Matthew 14:19).$n$),
(664,7996,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:20 — *you feddest your own people with angels’ food.* The crowd cites the same wilderness bread, *He gave them bread from heaven to eat* (John 6:31).$n$),
(9119,39472,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:21 — *your sustenance declared your sweetness to your children.* The sweetness the wilderness tasted is the Bread who comes down and gives life to the world (John 6:33).$n$),
(205,4565,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:26 — *it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word.* The loaves blessed and broken point past themselves to the word that nourishes (Matthew 14:19).$n$),
(664,7995,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:26 — *it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.* The word that preserved the wilderness is the true bread the Father now gives (John 6:32).$n$),
(207,4570,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 18:15 — *Your Almighty word leaped down from heaven out of your royal throne.* The Word who came down on Passover night speaks across the dark water, *it is I; be not afraid* (Matthew 14:27).$n$),
(9128,39501,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 18:16 — *brought your unfeigned commandment as a sharp sword.* The same sword, now sharper than any twoedged sword and turned on the heart (Hebrews 4:12).$n$),
(219,4580,$n$Tobit 4:3 — *despise not your mother, but honour her all the days of your life.* The commandment to honour father and mother, kept to the end of her days, which the gift tradition voided (Matthew 15:4).$n$),
(377,4705,$n$Tobit 4:3 — *My son, when I am dead, bury me; and despise not your mother.* A father handing covenant life to his son; the King laying hands on the children brought to him (Matthew 19:15).$n$),
(94,4402,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* The mercy Hosea asked for in place of sacrifice, which the Pharisees at the table had forgotten (Matthew 9:13).$n$),
(409,4844,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor.* The face not turned from the poor meets the King in the least of his brethren (Matthew 25:40).$n$),
(9153,39575,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor.* Sirach's picture of the same mercy: *alms makes an atonement for sins* as water quenches fire (Ecclesiasticus 3:30).$n$),
(9176,39654,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *neither turn your face from any poor.* Sirach adds the hand to the face: *stretch yours hand to the poor, that your blessing may be perfected* (Ecclesiasticus 7:32).$n$),
(9367,40349,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *when you give alms, let not your eye be envious.* Sirach's twin warning, not to blemish the good deed with uncomfortable words (Ecclesiasticus 18:15).$n$),
(10322,43747,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor.* The charge to his son is the third tithe Tobit himself paid from his orphan years (Tobit 1:8).$n$),
(10329,43759,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* The alms Anna threw in his face are the alms he charges on his son (Tobit 2:14).$n$),
(10336,43801,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor.* The alms that make all things clean (Luke 11:41) are the alms Tobit charged on his son.$n$),
(10648,44762,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *neither turn your face from any poor.* The temple treasury kept money for widows and the fatherless, the same alms held in public trust (2 Maccabees 3:10).$n$),
(11926,49190,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *when you give alms, let not your eye be envious.* Give of abundance or of little, without the envious eye; *not grudgingly, or of necessity* (2 Corinthians 9:7).$n$),
(13768,56814,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *neither turn your face from any poor.* The household relieves its own widows first (1 Timothy 5:16), the face never turned from the nearest poor.$n$),
(13966,57479,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor.* The good work for necessary uses that keeps the believer from being unfruitful (Titus 3:14).$n$),
(27300,108669,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* The stranger in Nineveh keeps the mercy Torah commanded toward the stranger (Exodus 22:21).$n$),
(30016,119997,$n$Tobit 4:7 — *the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* Alms given for the Father's face, not for men's eyes (Matthew 6:1).$n$),
(94,4403,$n$Tobit 4:9 — *you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* The treasure laid up by mercy, the thing Hosea asked for in place of sacrifice (Matthew 9:13).$n$),
(378,4709,$n$Tobit 4:9 — *a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* Give to the poor and have treasure in heaven (Matthew 19:21), the treasure Tobit told his son to lay up.$n$),
(644,7935,$n$Tobit 4:9 — *a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* The alms given now are the bags that wax not old, the treasure that faileth not (Luke 12:33).$n$),
(13966,57480,$n$Tobit 4:9 — *you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity.* Alms as good works laid up, the fruit of a believing heart careful to maintain them (Titus 3:8).$n$),
(57,4351,$n$Tobit 4:10 — *alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* The deliverance the open hand brings, stored where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt (Matthew 6:20).$n$),
(94,4404,$n$Tobit 4:10 — *alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* Mercy is what delivers, and mercy is what Hosea asked for in place of sacrifice (Matthew 9:13).$n$),
(184,4498,$n$Tobit 4:10 — *alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* Mercy carries eternal weight; the Sabbath condemnation showed a system that had lost it (Matthew 12:7).$n$),
(644,7934,$n$Tobit 4:10 — *alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* The open hand delivers from death; its treasure is in the heavens where no thief approacheth (Luke 12:33).$n$),
(62,4368,$n$Tobit 4:15 — *Do that to no man which you hatest.* The golden rule in negative form, generations before *this is the law and the prophets* (Matthew 7:12).$n$),
(30026,4368,$n$Tobit 4:15 — *Do that to no man which you hatest.* The golden rule in negative form, generations before *this is the law and the prophets* (Matthew 7:12).$n$),
(409,4840,$n$Tobit 4:16 — *Give of your bread to the hungry, and of your garments to them that are naked.* Two of the King's own measures at the judgment, *I was an hungred, and ye gave me meat* (Matthew 25:35).$n$),
(376,4701,$n$Tobit 8:6 — *You madest Adam, and gavest him Eve his wife for an helper and stay.* The creation prayed over a marriage bed; *What therefore Elohim (God) hath joined together, let not man put asunder* (Matthew 19:6).$n$),
(10358,43847,$n$Tobit 8:7 — *I take not this my sister for lust but uprightly.* Marriage entered uprightly, for the godly seed Malachi says it was made for (Malachi 2:15).$n$),
(97,4412,$n$Tobit 12:8 — *Prayer is good with fasting and alms and righteousness.* Fasting belongs with prayer, alms, and righteousness, not alone; the King keeps it in that place (Matthew 9:14).$n$),
(94,4405,$n$Tobit 12:9 — *alms does deliver from death, and shall purge away all sin.* Mercy with the weight of life and death, the mercy Hosea asked for in place of sacrifice (Matthew 9:13).$n$),
(107,4437,$n$Tobit 13:3 — *Confess him before the Gentiles, you children of Yashar'el (Israel): for he has scattered us among them.* The scattered confess him where they were scattered, and the King confesses them before his Father (Matthew 10:32).$n$),
(107,4436,$n$Tobit 13:5 — *he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations.* The gathering Tobit prayed for is the mission to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) (Matthew 10:6).$n$),
(221,4589,$n$Tobit 13:5 — *will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us.* The lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) Yahusha (Jesus) was sent to are the scattered Tobit said would be gathered (Matthew 15:24).$n$),
(107,4438,$n$Tobit 14:5 — *afterward they shall return from all places of their captivity, and build up Jerusalem gloriously.* Two returns, the second still to come; the mission runs till the Son of Adam comes (Matthew 10:23).$n$),
(1707,12188,$n$Tobit 14:6 — *all nations shall turn, and fear Yahuah (God) truly, and shall bury their idols.* The collection for Jerusalem is the turning made tangible, carnal things paid back for spiritual things received (Romans 15:27).$n$),
(1695,12154,$n$Tobit 1:10 — *all my brothers and those that were of my kindred did eat of the bread of the Gentiles* the captive's case behind the weak who eateth herbs: the heathen table refused (Romans 14:2).$n$),
(9037,39194,$n$John 1:5 — *And the light shineth in darkness; and the darkness comprehended it not.* Wisdom, who *being compared with the light, she is found before it*, and against whom *vice shall not prevail* (Wisdom of Solomon 7:29–30), is John's Light the darkness cannot overcome.$n$),
(9089,39384,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 10:6 — *she delivered the righteous man, who fled from the fire which fell down upon the five cities.* Jubilees 16:6–7 tells the same judgment of Sodom and the saving of Lot for Abraham's sake.$n$),
(9110,39439,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 13:1 — *Surely vain are all men by nature, who are ignorant of Yahuah (God), and could not out of the good things that are seen know him that is: neither by considering the works did they acknowledge the workmaster;* the thesis that idolatry is *the beginning, the cause, and the end, of all evil* (Wisdom of Solomon 14:27) completes the polemic begun in 13:1, the Creator unknown though seen — the very pairing Romans 1:20 and 1:25 draw on.$n$),
(9264,39960,$n$James 1:15 — *Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death.* *He has commanded no man to do wickedly, neither has he given any man licence to sin* (Ecclesiasticus 15:20); sin is born of a man's own conceived lust, not of any divine warrant.$n$),
(10014,42695,$n$Hebrews 5:4 — *And no man taketh this honour unto himself, but he that is called of Elohim (God), as was Aaron.* *An everlasting covenant he made with him and gave him the priesthood among the people* (Ecclesiasticus 45:7) — the priesthood is the honour Hebrews says no man may seize but must be called to, as Aaron was.$n$),
(17942,71554,$n$*was present when you madest the world* (Wisdom of Solomon 9:9) — *When he prepared the heavens, I was there* (Proverbs 8:27): the Formed Son present at the making.$n$),
(26059,103290,$n$★ *O blessed are they which love you, for they shall rejoice in your peace* (Tobit 13:14) — Tobit, praying from captivity, sings the same blessing on Jerusalem's lovers that Psalm 122:6 pronounces.$n$),
(30596,122689,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:26 — *it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you.* The Father who marks the sparrow preserves His own (Matthew 10:29).$n$),
(30597,122693,$n$Ecclesiasticus 7:27–28 — *Honour your father with your whole heart, and forget not the sorrows of your mother.* The fifth word is never abolished; loving the Master more is an ordering of loves, not a cancelling (Matthew 10:37).$n$),
(34391,137924,$n$★ *Set not yours heart upon goods unjustly gotten, for they shall not profit you in the day of calamity* (Ecclesiasticus 5:8) — the banquet *removed* (Amos 6:7) when the calamity comes.$n$),
(34702,139531,$n$★ *he that defraudeth the labourer of his hire is a bloodshedder* (Ecclesiasticus 34:22) — to *build up Zion with blood* (Micah 3:10) is exactly this; the city raised on judges for reward and priests for hire is built of stolen lives.$n$),
(34943,140754,$n$Ecclesiasticus 47:17 — *The countries marvelled at you for your songs, and proverbs, and parables, and interpretations.* The library remembers the fame that drew the nations to Solomon; yet a greater than Solomon is here (Luke 11:31).$n$),
(35198,142047,$n$★ *you gavest them a burning pillar of fire, both to be a guide of the unknown journey* (Wisdom of Solomon 18:3) — the restored witness to the *fire by night* that led the son (Deuteronomy 1:33).$n$),
(36188,146283,$n$*Be as a father to the fatherless, and instead of an husband to their mother: so shall you be as the son of the Most High.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:10) — the wisdom tradition names Job's deed as the mark of a true son (Job 29:16).$n$),
(42953,176192,$n$Prayer of Manasseh 1:11 — *Now therefore I bow the knee of my heart, beseeching you of grace.* The very prayer attributed to him, the inside of the humbling by which Yahuah (God) was intreated (2 Chronicles 33:13).$n$),
(393,4773,$n$2 Esdras 7:32 — *the earth shall restore those that are asleep in her, and so shall the dust those that dwell in silence.* The dead sleep and wait for the earth to give them back, the resurrection Yahusha (Jesus) defends against the Sadducees (Matthew 22:31).$n$),
(665,7992,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:9 — *He created me from the beginning before the world, and I shall never fail.* Wisdom's own testimony from before the world, dwelling in Jacob and Yashar'el (Israel); *Before Abraham was, I am* (John 8:58) names the same eternity in the Formed Word who came to tabernacle among his own.$n$),
(1507,11562,$n$2 Esdras 3:7 — *immediately you appointedst death in him and in his generations, of whom came nations, tribes, people, and kindreds, out of number.* The reign of death from Adam over all his seed (Romans 5:14).$n$),
(2442,15195,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 2:21 — *and were deceived: for their own wickedness has blinded them.* The root of the false wisdom Elohim (God) *made foolish* (1 Corinthians 1:20).$n$),
(2445,15208,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:4 — *yet is their hope full of immortality.* The reward of the righteous the ungodly neither hoped for nor discerned (Wisdom of Solomon 2:22); the unseen *things which Elohim (God) hath prepared for them that love him* (1 Corinthians 2:9).$n$),
(2652,15937,$n$2 Esdras 7:29 — *shall my son Messiah (Christ) die, and all men that have life.* The library's universal death, then the raising; *as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive* (1 Corinthians 15:22).$n$),
(2655,15943,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 3:1 — *the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them.* The seeming death is the sowing and the raising the harvest; *sown in corruption; it is raised in incorruption* (1 Corinthians 15:42).$n$),
(14385,58818,$n$Ecclesiasticus 23:9 — *Accustom not your mouth to swearing; neither use thyself to the naming of the Holy One.* The wisdom library almost in James's words; not the oath abolished but the Name honored (James 5:12).$n$),
(14386,58822,$n$Ecclesiasticus 38:9 — *in your sickness be not negligent: but pray to Yahuah (God), and he will make you whole.* The wisdom library's medicine of the soul; *Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him* (James 5:14).$n$),
(14388,58833,$n$Tobit 12:9 — *alms does deliver from death, and shall purge away all sin.* Righteous love that delivers from death; *shall save a soul from death* (James 5:20).$n$),
(17320,69244,$n$★ *delivered me, according to the multitude of your mercies and greatness of your name* (Ecclesiasticus 51:3) — delivered by the Name (Psalm 54:1) from those who *seek after my soul* (Psalm 54:3).$n$),
(19383,77279,$n$★ *the souls of the righteous are in the hand of Yahuah (God), and there shall no torment touch them* (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1) — the end seen in the sanctuary (Psalm 73:17): the righteous held safe, only seeming to die.$n$),
(19863,79231,$n$*A faithfull friend is a strong defence: and he that has found such an one has found a treasure* (Ecclesiasticus 6:14) — the restored witness echoes Proverbs 18:24; the true friend is a fortress and a treasure found.$n$),
(20129,80393,$n$★ *you feddest your own people with angels’ food, and did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:20) — the restored wisdom sings the very phrase of Psalm 78:25.$n$),
(20776,82784,$n$★ *they thought to destroy the generation of Jacob that was among them* (1 Maccabees 5:2) — the very plot of Psalm 83:4 to cut the people off from being a nation.$n$),
(30189,120781,$n$★ *So shall all nations praise Yahuah (God), and his people shall confess Yahuah (God), and Yahuah (God) shall exalt his people* (Tobit 14:7) — the nations take hold of the skirt of a Yahudi (Jew) to go with him (Zechariah 8:23); his people exalted, not replaced.$n$),
(30190,120788,$n$★ *with thanksgiving, and branches of palm trees, and with harps, and cymbals, and with viols, and hymns, and songs* (1 Maccabees 13:51) — the palm-branch entry into Jerusalem; the rejoicing the daughter of Zion would pour on her true King (Zechariah 9:9).$n$),
(34722,139622,$n$★ *will gather us out of all nations, among whom he has scattered us* (Tobit 13:5) — the regathering *from sea to sea* (Micah 7:12) is the two-house people brought home.$n$),
(34945,140767,$n$★ *Judas fought against the children of Esau in Idumea at Arabattine* (1 Maccabees 5:3) — he gave them a great overthrow; the recompense returns on the brother-line, the deed done to Jacob repaid (Obadiah 1:10).$n$),
(38196,155336,$n$*So shall all nations praise Yahuah (God), and his people shall confess Yahuah (God), and Yahuah (God) shall exalt his people* (Tobit 14:7) — the stranger who prays toward the house is heard, and the people are exalted, not displaced (1 Kings 8:43).$n$),
(42094,172334,$n$*send her out of your holy heavens, and from the throne of your glory* (Wisdom of Solomon 9:10) — wisdom comes down from the throne of Yahuah (God) to labour with the king who asked for it (2 Chronicles 1:10).$n$),
(43064,176786,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 16:20 — *you feddest your own people with angels’ food, and did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour*: the restored witness names the manna (Nehemiah 9:20).$n$),
(43065,176796,$n$Ecclesiasticus 18:11 — *Therefore is Yahuah (God) patient with them, and poureth forth his mercy upon them.* The restored wisdom witnesses the Elohim (God) ready to pardon, slow to anger (Nehemiah 9:17).$n$),
(67,7914,$n$Ecclesiasticus 27:6 — *The fruit declareth if the tree have been dressed.* The tree is known by what it brings forth and the heart by what its mouth speaks; *every tree is known by his own fruit* (Luke 6:44).$n$),
(628,7884,$n$2 Esdras 13:40 — *the ten tribes, which were carried away prisoners out of their own land.* The tribes named by the history of their scattering are the elect the angels gather *from the uttermost part of the earth* (Mark 13:27).$n$),
(645,7931,$n$Baruch 4:36 — *look about you toward the east, and behold the joy that comes to you from Yahuah (God).* Jerusalem's face turned east toward the coming; the scattered children of Yashar'el (Israel) *shall come from the east, and from the west* to sit down in the kingdom (Luke 13:29).$n$),
(645,7930,$n$Baruch 4:37 — *Lo, your sons come, whom you sentest away, they come gathered together from the east to the west.* The sent-away sons coming home, not strangers added; *they shall come from the east, and from the west* (Luke 13:29).$n$),
(661,7982,$n$Ecclesiasticus 24:19 — *Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits.* Wisdom's call to come and be filled is the offer at the well, *he would have given thee living water* (John 4:10).$n$),
(666,8016,$n$Tobit 11:11 — *he strake of the gall on his fathers’ eyes, saying, Be of good hope, my father.* Sight restored by the means appointed; the man born blind *washed, and came seeing* (John 9:7).$n$),
(666,8014,$n$Tobit 11:14 — *Blessed art you, O Yahuah (God), and blessed is your name for ever.* The name blessed the moment sight returns; the man whose eyes were opened said *Lord, I believe. And he worshipped him* (John 9:38).$n$),
(10622,44684,$n$1 Maccabees 9:30 — *we have chosen you this day to be our prince and captain in his stead.* The acclamation given to Jonathan passes to Simon, *You shall be our leader instead of Judas and Jonathan your brother* (1 Maccabees 13:8).$n$),
(10622,44683,$n$1 Maccabees 13:5 — *be it far from me, that I should spare my own life in any time of trouble.* Simon's refusal to spare himself for the nation is the shape of the love the Messiah (Christ) names, *that a man lay down his life for his friends* (John 15:13).$n$),
(10622,44682,$n$1 Maccabees 13:9 — *Fight you our battles, and whatsoever, you commandest us, that will we do.* The people pledging Simon to fight is the bow of Yahudah (Judah) bent against the sons of Greece (Zechariah 9:13).$n$),
(10623,44681,$n$1 Maccabees 2:50 — *be you zealous for the law, and give your lives for the covenant of your fathers.* Mattathias' dying charge, which Simon reckons fulfilled in all his house has done *for the laws and the sanctuary* (1 Maccabees 13:3).$n$),
(10623,44680,$n$1 Maccabees 13:4 — *all my brothers are slain for Yashar'el (Israel)’s sake, and I am left alone.* The brothers who died for the covenant carry the zeal of Phinehas, who turned the wrath away (Numbers 25:11).$n$),
(10623,44679,$n$1 Maccabees 13:4 — *all my brothers are slain for Yashar'el (Israel)’s sake, and I am left alone.* The slain brothers are among those who were tortured, not accepting deliverance, *that they might obtain a better resurrection* (Hebrews 11:35).$n$),
(10623,44678,$n$2 Maccabees 7:9 — *the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.* The hope behind the deaths of Simon's brothers, spoken plainly by the martyr under the same persecution (1 Maccabees 13:4).$n$),
(10623,44677,$n$1 Maccabees 13:4 — *all my brothers are slain for Yashar'el (Israel)’s sake, and I am left alone.* Daniel had already sealed the everlasting life that crowns them, *many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake* (Daniel 12:2).$n$),
(10624,44676,$n$1 Maccabees 13:42 — *In the first year of Simon the high priest, the governor and leader of the Yahudim (Jews).* Yashar'el (Israel) writing a new reckoning into its contracts is liberty proclaimed in the land, the pattern of the jubile (Leviticus 25:10).$n$),
(10624,44675,$n$1 Maccabees 13:41 — *the yoke of the heathen was taken away from Yashar'el (Israel) in the hundred and seventieth year.* The yoke lifted is the one Isaiah sang broken, *the yoke of his burden, and the staff of his shoulder* (Isaiah 9:4).$n$),
(10624,44674,$n$1 Maccabees 13:41 — *the yoke of the heathen was taken away from Yashar'el (Israel).* The yoke lifted answers *the yoke shall be destroyed because of the anointing* (Isaiah 10:27).$n$),
(10624,44673,$n$1 Maccabees 13:41 — *Thus the yoke of the heathen was taken away from Yashar'el (Israel).* The pledge to break the oppressor so that *his yoke depart from off them* is seen kept (Isaiah 14:25).$n$),
(10625,44672,$n$1 Maccabees 13:52 — *He ordained also that that day should be kept every year with gladness.* The cleansed citadel's yearly day belongs with *the feast of the dedication* the Messiah (Christ) kept in Jerusalem (John 10:22).$n$),
(10625,44671,$n$1 Maccabees 13:47 — *cleansed the houses in which the idols were, and so entered into it with songs and thanksgiving.* Simon's cleansing enacts the promise, *from all your idols, will I cleanse you* (Ezekiel 36:25).$n$),
(10625,44670,$n$1 Maccabees 13:48 — *he put all uncleanness out of it, and placed such men there as would keep the law.* The purged stronghold of law-keepers is the holy city into which *there shall no more come into thee the uncircumcised and the unclean* (Isaiah 52:1).$n$),
(10625,44669,$n$1 Maccabees 13:48 — *he put all uncleanness out of it, and placed such men there as would keep the law.* A foreshadow of the day when *every pot in Jerusalem and in Yahudah (Judah) shall be holiness unto Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts)* (Zechariah 14:21).$n$),
(10648,44765,$n$2 Maccabees 3:10 — *there was such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children.* The cause whose relief lay in the house is the cause Yahuah (God) himself takes up, *the judgment of the fatherless and widow* (Deuteronomy 10:18).$n$),
(10649,44761,$n$2 Maccabees 3:15 — *the priests, prostrating themselves before the altar in their priests’ vestments, called to heaven.* The very liturgy the prophet gave, *weep between the porch and the altar* (Joel 2:17).$n$),
(10649,44760,$n$2 Maccabees 3:15 — *the priests, prostrating themselves before the altar in their priests’ vestments, called to heaven.* As Aaron *stood between the dead and the living* (Numbers 16:48), the priests' supplication stands between the house and the spoiler.$n$),
(10649,44759,$n$2 Maccabees 3:22 — *called upon the Almighty Yahuah (God) to keep the things committed of trust safe and sure.* No human watch can guard the house; *except Yahuah (LORD) keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain* (Psalm 127:1).$n$),
(10650,44756,$n$2 Maccabees 3:24 — *the Prince of all power, caused a great apparition.* All who presumed to come in with Heliodorus were astonished; the Prince of all power is the *captain of the host of Yahuah (LORD)* who met Joshua (Joshua 5:14).$n$),
(10650,44755,$n$2 Maccabees 3:24 — *astonished at the power of Yahuah (God), and fainted, and were sore afraid.* The same heaven that smote the Assyrian camp before Jerusalem strikes Heliodorus' guard (2 Kings 19:35).$n$),
(10650,44754,$n$2 Maccabees 3:24 — *astonished at the power of Yahuah (God), and fainted, and were sore afraid.* As one angel cut off Sennacherib's captains, the apparition routs the king's treasurer (2 Chronicles 32:21).$n$),
(10650,44753,$n$2 Maccabees 3:25 — *he ran fiercely, and struck at Heliodorus with his forefeet.* The fiercely running rider is the chasing angel the psalmist summoned, *let the angel of Yahuah (LORD) chase them* (Psalm 35:5).$n$),
(10651,44752,$n$2 Maccabees 3:27 — *Heliodorus fell suddenly to the ground, and was compassed with great darkness.* As light from heaven felled Saul on the Damascus road, darkness fells the spoiler of the house (Acts 9:3).$n$),
(10651,44751,$n$2 Maccabees 3:27 — *Heliodorus fell suddenly to the ground, and was compassed with great darkness.* Like Saul thrown to the earth and then made a herald, Heliodorus falls and is sent to declare the power he fought (Acts 9:4).$n$),
(10651,44750,$n$2 Maccabees 3:29 — *he by the hand of Yahuah (God) was cast down, and lay speechless without all hope of life.* No man may stand before his anger; *who may stand in thy sight when once thou art angry?* (Psalm 76:7).$n$),
(10651,44749,$n$2 Maccabees 9:5 — *struck him with an incurable and invisible plague.* Heliodorus was felled and spared at a priest's prayer (2 Maccabees 3:27); the unrepentant Antiochus who raged against the sanctuary is struck without remedy.$n$),
(10652,44748,$n$2 Maccabees 3:39 — *he that dwells in heaven has his eye on that place, and defendeth it.* Heliodorus confesses what the psalm sings, *except Yahuah (LORD) keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain* (Psalm 127:1).$n$),
(10652,44747,$n$2 Maccabees 3:39 — *he beateth and destroys them that come to hurt it.* The one who keeps his eye on the place is *a wall of fire round about* his city (Zechariah 2:5).$n$),
(10652,44746,$n$2 Maccabees 3:39 — *he that dwells in heaven has his eye on that place, and defendeth it.* The same Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) who will *defend Jerusalem; defending also he will deliver it* (Isaiah 31:5).$n$),
(10652,44745,$n$2 Maccabees 3:38 — *send him thither, and you shall receive him well scourged, if he escape with his life.* The well-scourged spoiler shows the recompense the apostle promises, *a righteous thing with Elohim (God) to recompense tribulation to them that trouble you* (2 Thessalonians 1:6).$n$),
(10683,44877,$n$2 Maccabees 10:7 — *they bare branches, and fair boughs, and palms also, and sang psalms.* The tabernacles rite of Torah, *branches of palm trees, and the boughs of thick trees* (Leviticus 23:40), kept out of season because they had been hunted in the mountains.$n$),
(10683,44876,$n$2 Maccabees 10:6 — *they kept the eight days with gladness, as in the feast of the tabernacles.* As the returned remnant under Ezra proclaimed the booths again (Nehemiah 8:15), the Maccabees keep tabernacles as a people lately out of the dens.$n$),
(10683,44875,$n$2 Maccabees 10:8 — *every year those days should be kept of the whole nation of the Yahudim (Jews).* The yearly winter feast the nation ordained is *the feast of the dedication* the Messiah (Christ) kept at Jerusalem (John 10:22).$n$),
(10683,44874,$n$1 Maccabees 4:59 — *the days of the dedication of the altar should be kept in their season from year to year.* The brother book records the same eight-day statute from the twenty-fifth of Casleu (2 Maccabees 10:8).$n$),
(10683,44873,$n$2 Maccabees 10:7 — *they bare branches, and fair boughs, and palms also, and sang psalms.* Tabernacles kept with palms and psalms, before the day all that are left of the nations go up yearly *to keep the feast of tabernacles* (Zechariah 14:16).$n$),
(10684,44872,$n$2 Maccabees 10:2 — *the altars which the heathen had built in the open street, and also the chapels, they pulled down.* The heathen altars pulled down are *the abomination that maketh desolate* Daniel saw placed in the polluted sanctuary (Daniel 11:31).$n$),
(10684,44871,$n$2 Maccabees 10:5 — *upon the same day that the strangers profaned the temple, on the very same day it was cleansed again.* The cleansing on the day of the profaning answers the saint's question, *How long shall be the vision* (Daniel 8:13).$n$),
(10684,44870,$n$2 Maccabees 10:1 — *Maccabeus and his company, Yahuah (God) guiding them, recovered the temple and the city.* The temple Maccabeus recovers is the one Antiochus had defiled — the first standing of *the abomination of desolation, spoken of by Daniel the prophet*, which the Messiah (Christ) warns will yet stand again in the holy place (Matthew 24:15).$n$),
(10685,44869,$n$2 Maccabees 10:4 — *if they sinned any more against him, that he himself would chasten them with mercy.* David chose the hand of Yahuah (LORD) over the hand of man, *let us fall now into the hand of Yahuah (LORD); for his mercies are great* (2 Samuel 24:14), and the Maccabees pray the same.$n$),
(10685,44868,$n$Tobit 13:5 — *he will scourge us for our iniquities, and will have mercy again, and will gather us out of all nations.* The same covenant confidence the Maccabees pray, that chastening ends in mercy and not in abandonment (2 Maccabees 10:4).$n$),
(10685,44867,$n$2 Maccabees 10:4 — *if they sinned any more against him, that he himself would chasten them with mercy.* The mercy they ask to be chastened with is the covenant promise, *I will be merciful to their unrighteousness* (Hebrews 8:12).$n$),
(10686,44865,$n$2 Maccabees 10:36 — *burnt the towers, and kindling fires burnt the blasphemers alive.* The zeal that burns out the blasphemers claims the covenant given for Phinehas' zeal, *the covenant of an everlasting priesthood* (Numbers 25:13).$n$),
(10687,44864,$n$2 Maccabees 10:29 — *there appeared to the enemies from heaven five comely men upon horses, with bridles of gold.* The heavenly cavalry that shields Maccabeus is the host Elisha's servant was given eyes to see, *horses and chariots of fire* (2 Kings 6:17).$n$),
(10687,44863,$n$2 Maccabees 10:38 — *they praised Yahuah (God) with psalms and thanksgiving, who had done so great things for Yashar'el (Israel).* The victory song reprises the song at the sea, *the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea* (Exodus 15:1).$n$),
(10687,44862,$n$2 Maccabees 10:30 — *shot arrows and lightnings against the enemies: so that being confounded with blindness, and full of trouble, they were killed.* The riders who deliver Maccabeus make plain whose battle it is, *Yahuah (LORD) is a man of war* (Exodus 15:3).$n$),
(10688,44891,$n$2 Maccabees 11:6 — *besought Yahuah (God) that he would send a good angel to deliver Yashar'el (Israel).* The good angel they beg for is the Angel pledged to go before the people, *Behold, I send an Angel before thee* (Exodus 23:20).$n$),
(10688,44890,$n$2 Maccabees 11:6 — *with lamentation and tears besought Yahuah (God) that he would send a good angel.* The help is promised to obedience, *if thou shalt indeed obey his voice* (Exodus 23:22), the Torah faithfulness these men are fighting for.$n$),
(10689,44889,$n$2 Maccabees 11:8 — *there appeared before them on horseback one in white clothing, shaking his armour of gold.* The same armed figure who met Joshua at Jericho *with his sword drawn in his hand* (Joshua 5:13) rides before the Maccabees.$n$),
(10689,44888,$n$2 Maccabees 11:8 — *there appeared before them on horseback one in white clothing, shaking his armour of gold.* The white-clad horseman is the *captain of the host of Yahuah (LORD)* come to lead the battle (Joshua 5:14).$n$),
(10689,44887,$n$2 Maccabees 3:25 — *it seemed that he that sat upon the horse had complete harness of gold.* The golden-harnessed rider who smote Heliodorus at the temple is the horseman who now leads Yashar'el (Israel) (2 Maccabees 11:8).$n$),
(10689,44886,$n$2 Maccabees 11:8 — *one in white clothing, shaking his armour of gold.* The white horseman foreshadows the Rider *called Faithful and True* who judges and makes war (Revelation 19:11).$n$),
(10690,44885,$n$2 Maccabees 11:10 — *they marched forward in their armour, having an helper from heaven.* The helper from heaven is the unseen host, *horses and chariots of fire round about Elisha* (2 Kings 6:17).$n$),
(10690,44884,$n$2 Maccabees 11:13 — *the Hebrews could not be overcome, because the Almighty Yahuah (God) helped them.* The enemy's own conclusion proves Elisha's word, *they that be with us are more than they that be with them* (2 Kings 6:16).$n$),
(10690,44883,$n$2 Maccabees 11:13 — *the Hebrews could not be overcome, because the Almighty Yahuah (God) helped them.* The Almighty who cannot be resisted is the one *the armies which were in heaven followed* at the last (Revelation 19:14).$n$),
(10691,44882,$n$2 Maccabees 11:24 — *had rather keep their own manner of living.* The manner of living the Yahudim (Jews) would not surrender is the delight of the psalm, *So shall I keep thy law continually for ever and ever* (Psalm 119:44).$n$),
(10691,44881,$n$2 Maccabees 7:9 — *the King of the world shall raise us up, who have died for his laws, to everlasting life.* The laws the king is forced to let them keep (2 Maccabees 11:24) are the laws the seven brothers died for, in hope of the resurrection.$n$),
(10691,44880,$n$2 Maccabees 11:31 — *the Yahudim (Jews) shall use their own kind of meats and laws, as before.* The refusal to abandon their meats and laws is the faith Hebrews crowns, *that they might obtain a better resurrection* (Hebrews 11:35).$n$),
(10692,44879,$n$2 Maccabees 11:25 — *restore them their temple, that they may live according to the customs of their forefathers.* As Persia once decreed the law of Elohim (God) set over his people (Ezra 7:25), the king's letter is forced to restore the temple and the fathers' customs.$n$),
(10692,44878,$n$2 Maccabees 11:25 — *we have determined to restore them their temple.* The restored house is the one whose rededication became *the feast of the dedication* the Messiah (Christ) kept (John 10:22).$n$),
(13707,56573,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 5:18 — *He shall put on righteousness as a breastplate, and true judgment instead of an helmet.* The armour the righteous Deliverer wears is what the children of the day put on, *the breastplate of faith and love; and for an helmet, the hope of salvation* (1 Thessalonians 5:8).$n$),
(15982,64446,$n$Tobit 13:2 — *he leadeth down to hell, and brings up again: neither is there any that can avoid his hand.* The library's thanksgiving prays the same motion as David, *thou hast brought up my soul from the grave* (Psalm 30:3).$n$),
(15996,64489,$n$Wisdom of Solomon 9:1 — *O Yahuah (God) of my fathers, and Yahuah (God) of mercy, who have made all things with your word.* Solomon's prayer confesses the psalm's own creation by the Word, *By the word of Yahuah (LORD) were the heavens made* (Psalm 33:6).$n$),
(43184,177395,$n$The Rest of Esther 13:4 — *there was scattered a certain malicious people, that had laws contrary to all nations.* Artaxerxes' decree frames the same accusation as 3 Maccabees 3:7, a people set apart by their laws named an enemy of the realm.$n$),
(43186,177390,$n$The Rest of Esther 13:6 — *with their wives and children, be utterly destroyed by the sword of their enemies, without all mercy and pity.* The decree matches 3 Maccabees 3:25 phrase for phrase, total destruction *with wives and children*.$n$),
(14387,58828,$n$Sirach 48:1–3 — *Elias the prophet as fire... he shut up the heaven* the Hebrew library's memory of Elias; the prophet whose earnest prayer governed the rain (James 5:17).$n$);
INSERT INTO _s463cs VALUES
(9007,$n$Here is the heart of the chapter, and a verse cited again and again in the live apparatus: *For Yahuah (God) made not death: neither has he pleasure in the destruction of the living* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:13), *For he created all things, that they might have their being: and the generations of the world were healthful; and there is no poison of destruction in them, nor the kingdom of death upon the earth* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:14), *(For righteousness is immortal:)* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:15). It ain't new — at the first, *Elohim (God) saw every thing that he had made, and, behold, it was very good. And the evening and the morning were the sixth day* (Genesis 1:31): no death was woven into it. And Yahuah Himself swears He takes no joy in dying men: *For I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD): wherefore turn yourselves, and live ye* (Ezekiel 18:32), *As I live, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD), I have no pleasure in the death of the wicked; but that the wicked turn from his way and live* (Ezekiel 33:11). Death came not from Elohim but by transgression of the one word — *for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die* (Genesis 2:17).$n$,$n$Here is the heart of the chapter: *For Yahuah (God) made not death: neither has he pleasure in the destruction of the living* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:13), *For he created all things, that they might have their being: and the generations of the world were healthful; and there is no poison of destruction in them, nor the kingdom of death upon the earth* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:14), *(For righteousness is immortal:)* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:15). It ain't new — at the first, *Elohim (God) saw every thing that he had made, and, behold, it was very good. And the evening and the morning were the sixth day* (Genesis 1:31): no death was woven into it. And Yahuah (God) Himself swears He takes no joy in dying men: *For I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD): wherefore turn yourselves, and live ye* (Ezekiel 18:32), *As I live, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD), I have no pleasure in the death of the wicked; but that the wicked turn from his way and live* (Ezekiel 33:11). Death came not from Elohim (God) but by transgression of the one word — *for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die* (Genesis 2:17).$n$),
(9093,$n$Wisdom rehearses the wilderness mercy: *When they were thirsty, they called upon you, and water was given them out of the flinty rock, and their thirst was quenched out of the hard stone.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:4). This is the Horeb miracle remembered: *Behold, I will stand before thee there upon the rock in Horeb; and thou shalt smite the rock, and there shall come water out of it, that the people may drink. And Moses did so in the sight of the elders of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Exodus 17:6) — and again at Meribah, *And Moses lifted up his hand, and with his rod he smote the rock twice: and the water came out abundantly, and the congregation drank, and their beasts also.* (Numbers 20:11). It ain't new: Paul reads the same rock messianically, *And did all drink the same spiritual drink: for they drank of that spiritual Rock that followed them: and that Rock was Messiah (Christ).* (1 Corinthians 10:4). Wisdom's wilderness draught and the smitten stone are one witness — the provision that followed Yashar'el was the Anointed.$n$,$n$Wisdom rehearses the wilderness mercy: *When they were thirsty, they called upon you, and water was given them out of the flinty rock, and their thirst was quenched out of the hard stone.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:4). This is the Horeb miracle remembered: *Behold, I will stand before thee there upon the rock in Horeb; and thou shalt smite the rock, and there shall come water out of it, that the people may drink. And Moses did so in the sight of the elders of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Exodus 17:6) — and again at Meribah, *And Moses lifted up his hand, and with his rod he smote the rock twice: and the water came out abundantly, and the congregation drank, and their beasts also.* (Numbers 20:11). It ain't new: Paul reads the same rock messianically, *And did all drink the same spiritual drink: for they drank of that spiritual Rock that followed them: and that Rock was Messiah (Christ).* (1 Corinthians 10:4). Wisdom's wilderness draught and the smitten stone are one witness — the provision that followed Yashar'el (Israel) was the Anointed, Messiah (Christ).$n$),
(9094,$n$Wisdom states the law of measured judgment plainly: *For by what things their enemies were punished, by the same they in their need were benefited.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:5) — *For instead of a perpetual running river troubled with foul blood* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:6), Israel was given clean water. The fouled river is the first plague: *And Moses and Aaron did so, as Yahuah (LORD) commanded; and he lifted up the rod, and smote the waters that were in the river, in the sight of Pharaoh, and in the sight of his servants; and all the waters that were in the river were turned to blood.* (Exodus 7:20). Wisdom distills the principle into a maxim, *That they might know, that wherewithal a man sinneth, by the same also shall he be punished.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:16) — the bloodied Nile that drowned the infants becomes the measure of Egypt's reproof while Israel drinks from the rock.$n$,$n$Wisdom states the law of measured judgment plainly: *For by what things their enemies were punished, by the same they in their need were benefited.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:5) — *For instead of a perpetual running river troubled with foul blood* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:6), Yashar'el (Israel) was given clean water. The fouled river is the first plague: *And Moses and Aaron did so, as Yahuah (LORD) commanded; and he lifted up the rod, and smote the waters that were in the river, in the sight of Pharaoh, and in the sight of his servants; and all the waters that were in the river were turned to blood.* (Exodus 7:20). Wisdom distills the principle into a maxim, *That they might know, that wherewithal a man sinneth, by the same also shall he be punished.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:16) — the bloodied Nile that drowned the infants becomes the measure of Egypt's reproof while Yashar'el (Israel) drinks from the rock.$n$),
(9095,$n$Wisdom closes the plague-meditation with the great creation maxim: *but you have ordered all things in measure and number and weight.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:20). This is the Creator who weighs creation: *To make the weight for the winds; and he weigheth the waters by measure.* (Job 28:25), and the One whom Isaiah praises, *Who hath measured the waters in the hollow of his hand, and meted out heaven with the span, and comprehended the dust of the earth in a measure, and weighed the mountains in scales, and the hills in a balance?* (Isaiah 40:12). The judgments on Egypt are no caprice but the same exact ordering by which the world was framed — measure, number, weight — the wisdom that lays out the wind and the sea.$n$,$n$Wisdom closes the plague-meditation with the great creation maxim: *but you have ordered all things in measure and number and weight.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:20). This is the Creator who weighs creation: *To make the weight for the winds; and he weigheth the waters by measure.* (Job 28:25), and the One whom Isaiah praises, *Who hath measured the waters in the hollow of his hand, and meted out heaven with the span, and comprehended the dust of the earth in a measure, and weighed the mountains in scales, and the hills in a balance?* (Isaiah 40:12). The judgments on Egypt are no caprice but the same exact ordering by which the world was framed — measure, number, weight — the wisdom that lays out the wind and the sea.$n$),
(9096,$n$Against the measured power, Wisdom sets the smallness of creation before Yahuah: *For the whole world before you is as a little grain of the balance, yes, as a drop of the morning dew that falls down upon the earth.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:22). The same scale runs in the wisdom of Yeshua ben Sira: *As a drop of water to the sea, and a gravelstone in comparison of the sand; so are a thousand years to the days of eternity.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:10) — and there too it is the ground of patience, *Therefore is Yahuah (God) patient with them, and poureth forth his mercy upon them.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:11). It ain't new: the deuterocanon speaks with one voice — because the world weighs nothing against Him, His strength is never threatened, and so He can afford to be merciful.$n$,$n$Against the measured power, Wisdom sets the smallness of creation before Yahuah (God): *For the whole world before you is as a little grain of the balance, yes, as a drop of the morning dew that falls down upon the earth.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:22). The same scale runs in the wisdom of Yeshua ben Sira: *As a drop of water to the sea, and a gravelstone in comparison of the sand; so are a thousand years to the days of eternity.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:10) — and there too it is the ground of patience, *Therefore is Yahuah (God) patient with them, and poureth forth his mercy upon them.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:11). It ain't new: the two books speak with one voice — because the world weighs nothing against Him, His strength is never threatened, and so He can afford to be merciful.$n$),
(9097,$n$Wisdom ends with one of Scripture's tenderest confessions of divine mercy: *But you have mercy upon all; for you can do all things, and winkest at the sins of men, because they should amend.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:23) — *For you lovest all the things that are, and abhorrest nothing which you have made: for never wouldest you have made any thing, if you had hated it.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:24) — *But you sparest all: for they are yours, O Yahuah (God), you lover of souls.* (the-wisdom-of-solomon 11:26). This is the David of the Psalms: *Yahuah (LORD) is good to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.* (Psalms 145:9), and the Yahuah who reasons with Jonah, *And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?* (Jonah 4:11), the One who swears, *For I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD): wherefore turn yourselves, and live ye.* (Ezekiel 18:32). It ain't new — Ben Sira sang it already: *The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:13). Judgment is measured; mercy is over all His works.$n$,$n$Wisdom ends with one of Scripture's tenderest confessions of divine mercy: *But you have mercy upon all; for you can do all things, and winkest at the sins of men, because they should amend.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:23) — *For you lovest all the things that are, and abhorrest nothing which you have made: for never wouldest you have made any thing, if you had hated it.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:24) — *But you sparest all: for they are yours, O Yahuah (God), you lover of souls.* (Wisdom of Solomon 11:26). This is the David of the Psalms: *Yahuah (LORD) is good to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.* (Psalm 145:9), and the Yahuah (God) who reasons with Jonah, *And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?* (Jonah 4:11), the One who swears, *For I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD): wherefore turn yourselves, and live ye.* (Ezekiel 18:32). It ain't new — Ben Sira sang it already: *The mercy of man is toward his neighbour; but the mercy of Yahuah (God) is upon all flesh: he reproveth, and nurtureth, and teaches and brings again, as a shepherd his flock.* (Ecclesiasticus 18:13). Judgment is measured; mercy is over all His works.$n$),
(9117,$n$When the serpents stung Yashar'el in the wilderness, the cure was not the bronze on the pole but the One it pointed to: *But they were troubled for a small season, that they might be admonished, having a sign of salvation, to put them in remembrance of the commandment of thy law* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:6), *For he that turned himself toward it was not saved by the thing that he saw, but by thee, that art the Saviour of all* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:7). Wisdom reads Moses' serpent exactly as the Tanakh sets it — *And the LORD said unto Moses, Make thee a fiery serpent, and set it upon a pole: and it shall come to pass, that every one that is bitten, when he looketh upon it, shall live* (Numbers 21:8) — and Yahusha makes the type explicit: *And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up* (John 3:14). The look of faith toward the lifted-up One is the sign of salvation; it ain't new.$n$,$n$When the serpents stung Yashar'el (Israel) in the wilderness, the cure was not the bronze on the pole but the One it pointed to: *But they were troubled for a small season, that they might be admonished, having a sign of salvation, to put them in remembrance of the commandment of your law* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:6), *For he that turned himself toward it was not saved by the thing that he saw, but by you, that are the Saviour of all* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:7). Wisdom reads Moses' serpent exactly as the Tanakh sets it — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Moses, Make thee a fiery serpent, and set it upon a pole: and it shall come to pass, that every one that is bitten, when he looketh upon it, shall live* (Numbers 21:8) — and Yahusha (Jesus) makes the type explicit: *And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Adam be lifted up* (John 3:14). The look of faith toward the lifted-up One is the sign of salvation; it ain't new.$n$),
(9118,$n$Wisdom presses past the means to the Healer Himself: *For it was neither herb, nor mollifying plaister, that restored them to health: but thy word, O Yahuah (God), which healeth all things* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:12). This is the Psalmist's confession — *He sent his word, and healed them, and delivered them from their destructions* (Psalm 107:20) — the spoken word of the Maker as the true medicine. And the next breath of Wisdom presses to the deepest power: *For thou hast power of life and death: thou leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:13), echoing the song of Moses — *I kill, and I make alive; I wound, and I heal: neither is there any that can deliver out of my hand* (Deuteronomy 32:39) — and Hannah's — *The LORD killeth, and maketh alive: he bringeth down to the grave, and bringeth up* (1 Samuel 2:6). The Word that heals and raises is the same Word made flesh.$n$,$n$Wisdom presses past the means to the Healer Himself: *For it was neither herb, nor mollifying plaister, that restored them to health: but your word, O Yahuah (God), which healeth all things* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:12). This is the Psalmist's confession — *He sent his word, and healed them, and delivered them from their destructions* (Psalm 107:20) — the spoken word of the Maker as the true medicine. And the next breath of Wisdom presses to the deepest power: *For you have power of life and death: you leadest to the gates of hell, and bringest up again* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:13), echoing the song of Moses — *I kill, and I make alive; I wound, and I heal: neither is there any that can deliver out of my hand* (Deuteronomy 32:39) — and Hannah's — *Yahuah (LORD) killeth, and maketh alive: he bringeth down to the grave, and bringeth up* (1 Samuel 2:6). The Word that heals and raises is the same Word made flesh.$n$),
(9119,$n$Against Egypt's plagued harvest, Yahuah fed His people the bread of heaven: *Instead of which thou feddest thine own people with angels' food, and didst send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour, able to content every man's delight, and agreeing to every taste* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:20); *For thy sustenance declared thy sweetness unto thy children, and serving to the appetite of the eater, tempered itself to every man's liking* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:21). This is the manna of Exodus — *Then said the LORD unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you* (Exodus 16:4) — sung in the Psalms as *the corn of heaven* and *angels' food* — *And had rained down manna upon them to eat, and had given them of the corn of heaven. Man did eat angels' food: he sent them meat to the full* (Psalm 78:24-25). Yahusha gathers the whole figure: *Moses gave you not that bread from heaven; but my Father giveth you the true bread from heaven* (John 6:32). The manna was a shadow; the Bread of Life is the substance.$n$,$n$Against Egypt's plagued harvest, Yahuah (God) fed His people the bread of heaven: *Instead of which you feddest your own people with angels’ food, and did send them from heaven bread prepared without their labour, able to content every man’s delight, and agreeing to every taste* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:20); *For your sustenance declared your sweetness to your children, and serving to the appetite of the eater, tempered itself to every man’s liking* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:21). This is the manna of Exodus — *Then said Yahuah (LORD) unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you* (Exodus 16:4) — sung in the Psalms as *the corn of heaven* and *angels' food* — *And had rained down manna upon them to eat, and had given them of the corn of heaven. Man did eat angels' food: he sent them meat to the full* (Psalm 78:24-25). Yahusha (Jesus) gathers the whole figure: *Moses gave you not that bread from heaven; but my Father giveth you the true bread from heaven* (John 6:32). The manna was the shadow; the Bread of Life is the true bread it pointed to.$n$),
(9120,$n$Wisdom draws the manna to its lesson: *That thy children, O Yahuah (God), whom thou lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourisheth man: but that it is thy word, which preserveth them that put their trust in thee* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:26). This is Moses' own teaching from the manna — *And he humbled thee, and suffered thee to hunger, and fed thee with manna... that he might make thee know that man doth not live by bread only, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of the LORD doth man live* (Deuteronomy 8:3) — and it is the sword Yahusha drew in the wilderness against the tempter: *But he answered and said, It is written, Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of God* (Matthew 4:4). The bread feeds the body for a day; the word of Yahuah preserves the trusting forever.$n$,$n$Wisdom draws the manna to its lesson: *That your children, O Yahuah (God), whom you lovest, might know, that it is not the growing of fruits that nourishes man: but that it is your word, which preserves them that put their trust in you* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:26). This is Moses' own teaching from the manna — *And he humbled thee, and suffered thee to hunger, and fed thee with manna... that he might make thee know that man doth not live by bread only, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of Yahuah (LORD) doth man live* (Deuteronomy 8:3) — and it is the sword Yahusha (Jesus) drew in the wilderness against the tempter: *But he answered and said, It is written, Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of Elohim (God)* (Matthew 4:4). The bread feeds the body for a day; the word of Yahuah (God) preserves the trusting forever.$n$),
(9121,$n$Wisdom contrasts the two wilderness tables: mercy gave Yashar'el quails, while the venomous creatures that stung them were turned to a discipline, not a death — *Instead of which punishment, dealing graciously with thine own people, thou preparedst for them meat of a strange taste, even quails to stir up their appetite* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:2); *But thy sons not the very teeth of venomous dragons overcame: for thy mercy was ever by them, and healed them* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:10). The quails are from Numbers — *And there went forth a wind from the LORD, and brought quails from the sea, and let them fall by the camp* (Numbers 11:31) — and the serpents from the same wandering — *And the LORD sent fiery serpents among the people, and they bit the people; and much people of Israel died* (Numbers 21:6). The same wilderness held both the gift and the rod; mercy ran through the judgment.$n$,$n$Wisdom contrasts the two wilderness tables: mercy gave Yashar'el (Israel) quails, while the venomous creatures that stung them were turned to a discipline, not a death — *Instead of which punishment, dealing graciously with your own people, you preparedst for them meat of a strange taste, even quails to stir up their appetite* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:2); *But your sons not the very teeth of venomous dragons overcame: for your mercy was ever by them, and healed them* (Wisdom of Solomon 16:10). The quails are from Numbers — *And there went forth a wind from Yahuah (LORD), and brought quails from the sea, and let them fall by the camp* (Numbers 11:31) — and the serpents from the same wandering — *And Yahuah (LORD) sent fiery serpents among the people, and they bit the people; and much people of Yashar'el (Israel) died* (Numbers 21:6). The same wilderness held both the gift and the rod; mercy ran through the judgment.$n$),
(9149,$n$Ben Sira opens chapter 3 by taking the fifth word of the Decalogue and unfolding it as wisdom: *For Yahuah (God) has given the father honour over the children, and has confirmed the authority of the mother over the sons.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:2). To honour is no light thing — *Whoso honoureth his father makes an atonement for his sins* (Ecclesiasticus 3:3), and *Whoso honoureth his father shall have joy of his own children; and when he makes his prayer, he shall be heard* (Ecclesiasticus 3:5). This is the very commandment graven at Sinai: *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* (Exodus 20:12), repeated in Moses' rehearsal — *Honour thy father and thy mother, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee; that thy days may be prolonged, and that it may go well with thee* (Deuteronomy 5:16). Sha'ul knows it as the hinge of the household: *Honour thy father and mother; (which is the first commandment with promise;)* (Ephesians 6:2). It ain't new — the apparatus Ben Sira raises on the commandment is the same covenant honour, the same long-life promise, the same blessing that *establishes the houses of children* (Ecclesiasticus 3:9).$n$,$n$Ben Sira opens chapter 3 by taking the fifth word of the Decalogue and unfolding it as wisdom: *For Yahuah (God) has given the father honour over the children, and has confirmed the authority of the mother over the sons.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:2). To honour is no light thing — *Whoso honoureth his father makes an atonement for his sins* (Ecclesiasticus 3:3), and *Whoso honoureth his father shall have joy of his own children; and when he makes his prayer, he shall be heard* (Ecclesiasticus 3:5). This is the very commandment graven at Sinai: *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* (Exodus 20:12), repeated in Moses' rehearsal — *Honour thy father and thy mother, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee; that thy days may be prolonged, and that it may go well with thee* (Deuteronomy 5:16). Sha'ul (Paul) knows it as the hinge of the household: *Honour thy father and mother; (which is the first commandment with promise;)* (Ephesians 6:2). It ain't new — what Ben Sira builds on the commandment is the same covenant honour, the same long-life promise, the same blessing that *establishes the houses of children* (Ecclesiasticus 3:9).$n$),
(9153,$n$Ben Sira ends the chapter on the heart that will not bow: *A stubborn heart shall fare evil at the last; and he that loves danger shall perish in it.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:26), and *In the punishment of the proud there is no remedy; for the plant of wickedness has taken root in him.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:28). The Tanakh says it plainly — *Pride goeth before destruction, and an haughty spirit before a fall.* (Proverbs 16:18) — and Nebuchadnezzar learned it on his knees: *those that walk in pride he is able to abase* (Daniel 4:37). Against the proud heart Ben Sira sets the humble deed: *Water will quench a flaming fire; and alms makes an atonement for sins.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:30). This is Tobit's gospel of mercy — *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor* (Tobit 4:7) — and the Sermon on the Mount, where the Messiah hides the almsdeed from human praise: *That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.* (Matthew 6:4). It ain't new — the proud heart has no remedy, but the lowly hand that gives finds atonement and a stay.$n$,$n$Ben Sira ends the chapter on the heart that will not bow: *A stubborn heart shall fare evil at the last; and he that loves danger shall perish in it.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:26), and *In the punishment of the proud there is no remedy; for the plant of wickedness has taken root in him.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:28). The Tanakh says it plainly — *Pride goeth before destruction, and an haughty spirit before a fall.* (Proverbs 16:18) — and Nebuchadnezzar learned it on his knees: *those that walk in pride he is able to abase* (Daniel 4:37). Against the proud heart Ben Sira sets the humble deed: *Water will quench a flaming fire; and alms makes an atonement for sins.* (Ecclesiasticus 3:30). This is Tobit's counsel of mercy — *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor* (Tobit 4:7) — and the Sermon on the Mount, where the Messiah (Christ) hides the almsdeed from human praise: *That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.* (Matthew 6:4). It ain't new — the proud heart has no remedy, but the lowly hand that gives finds atonement and a stay.$n$),
(9157,$n$Sirach turns from mercy to the lady Wisdom herself: *Wisdom exalteth her children, and layeth hold of them that seek her.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:11), *He that loves her loves life; and they that seek to her early shall be filled with joy.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:12), *He that holdeth her fast shall inherit glory; and wherever she entereth, Yahuah (God) will bless.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:13). This is Proverbs 8 made plain — the formed Wisdom of Yahuah declaring *I love them that love me; and those that seek me early shall find me.* (Proverbs 8:17), whose gift is *Riches and honour are with me; yea, durable riches and righteousness.* (Proverbs 8:18), and *That I may cause those that love me to inherit substance; and I will fill their treasures.* (Proverbs 8:21). Proverbs 4 commands the same fast embrace: *Forsake her not, and she shall preserve thee: love her, and she shall keep thee.* (Proverbs 4:6). And the live Wisdom of Solomon, Sirach's apocryphal sister, echoes it: *Wisdom is glorious, and never fadeth away: yes, she is easily seen of them that love her, and found of such as seek her.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:12).$n$,$n$Sirach turns from mercy to the lady Wisdom herself: *Wisdom exalteth her children, and layeth hold of them that seek her.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:11), *He that loves her loves life; and they that seek to her early shall be filled with joy.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:12), *He that holdeth her fast shall inherit glory; and wherever she entereth, Yahuah (God) will bless.* (Ecclesiasticus 4:13). This is Proverbs 8 made plain — the Wisdom Yahuah (God) formed beside himself, declaring *I love them that love me; and those that seek me early shall find me.* (Proverbs 8:17), whose gift is *Riches and honour are with me; yea, durable riches and righteousness.* (Proverbs 8:18), and *That I may cause those that love me to inherit substance; and I will fill their treasures.* (Proverbs 8:21). Proverbs 4 commands the same fast embrace: *Forsake her not, and she shall preserve thee: love her, and she shall keep thee.* (Proverbs 4:6). And the Wisdom of Solomon, Sirach's sister book, echoes it: *Wisdom is glorious, and never fadeth away: yes, she is easily seen of them that love her, and found of such as seek her.* (Wisdom of Solomon 6:12).$n$),
(9160,$n$*And say not, I have sinned, and what harm has happened to me? for Yahuah (God) is longsuffering, he will in no wise let you go.* (Ecclesiasticus 5:4) — the sinner reads the silence of God as license. It ain't new: the Preacher diagnosed the same self-deceit, *Because sentence against an evil work is not executed speedily, therefore the heart of the sons of men is fully set in them to do evil.* (Ecclesiastes 8:11). And Sha'ul presses it into a warning — the delay is goodness meant to lead to repentance, not a verdict of acquittal: *Or despisest thou the riches of his goodness and forbearance and longsuffering; not knowing that the goodness of Elohim (God) leadeth thee to repentance?* (Romans 2:4), *But after thy hardness and impenitent heart treasurest up unto thyself wrath against the day of wrath and revelation of the righteous judgment of Elohim (God);* (Romans 2:5). The longsuffering Ben Sira names is the same forbearance that the impenitent abuse.$n$,$n$*Say not, I have sinned, and what harm has happened to me? for Yahuah (God) is longsuffering, he will in no wise let you go.* (Ecclesiasticus 5:4) — the sinner reads the silence of Elohim (God) as license. It ain't new: the Preacher diagnosed the same self-deceit, *Because sentence against an evil work is not executed speedily, therefore the heart of the sons of men is fully set in them to do evil.* (Ecclesiastes 8:11). And Sha'ul (Paul) presses it into a warning — the delay is goodness meant to lead to repentance, not a verdict of acquittal: *Or despisest thou the riches of his goodness and forbearance and longsuffering; not knowing that the goodness of Elohim (God) leadeth thee to repentance?* (Romans 2:4), *But after thy hardness and impenitent heart treasurest up unto thyself wrath against the day of wrath and revelation of the righteous judgment of Elohim (God);* (Romans 2:5). The longsuffering Ben Sira names is the same forbearance that the impenitent abuse.$n$),
(9176,$n$The chapter's mercy-cluster: *And stretch yours hand to the poor, that your blessing may be perfected* (Ecclesiasticus 7:32), *Fail not to be with them that weep, and mourn with them that mourn* (Ecclesiasticus 7:34), *Be not slow to visit the sick: for that shall make you to be beloved* (Ecclesiasticus 7:35). It ain't new — this is the Proverb the NT folds into the gospel: *He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto Yahuah (LORD); and that which he hath given will he pay him again* (Proverbs 19:17), and it is precisely the works the King names at the judgment: *Naked, and ye clothed me: I was sick, and ye visited me: I was in prison, and ye came unto me* (Matthew 25:36). And the restored Tobit, ben Sira's apocryphal sibling, sounds the same note: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you* (Tobit 4:7). Almsgiving perfects the blessing, library to library.$n$,$n$The chapter's mercy-cluster: *And stretch yours hand to the poor, that your blessing may be perfected* (Ecclesiasticus 7:32), *Fail not to be with them that weep, and mourn with them that mourn* (Ecclesiasticus 7:34), *Be not slow to visit the sick: for that shall make you to be beloved* (Ecclesiasticus 7:35). It ain't new — this is the Proverb the New Testament folds into the gospel: *He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto Yahuah (LORD); and that which he hath given will he pay him again* (Proverbs 19:17), and it is precisely the works the King names at the judgment: *Naked, and ye clothed me: I was sick, and ye visited me: I was in prison, and ye came unto me* (Matthew 25:36). And Tobit sounds the same note: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you* (Tobit 4:7). Almsgiving perfects the blessing, in every book of the library.$n$),
(9259,$n$The chapter closes with a beatitude on the seeker of wisdom, drawn in the figure of a hunter who camps at her gates and at last dwells beneath her: *Blessed is the man that does meditate good things in wisdom, and that reasoneth of holy things by his understanding* (Ecclesiasticus 14:20), *He that does lodge near her house shall also fasten a pin in her walls* (Ecclesiasticus 14:24), *He shall set his children under her shelter, and shall lodge under her branches* (Ecclesiasticus 14:26). The Psalter's blessed man meditates the same way — *But his delight is in the law of Yahuah (LORD); and in his law doth he meditate day and night* (Psalm 1:2). Solomon's personified Wisdom blesses the watcher at her doors — *Blessed is the man that heareth me, watching daily at my gates, waiting at the posts of my doors* (Proverbs 8:34) — and calls her a tree of life: *She is a tree of life to them that lay hold upon her: and happy is every one that retaineth her* (Proverbs 3:18). Ben Sira himself, later in his own book, will have this same Wisdom invite the seeker home — *Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits* (Ecclesiasticus 24:19) — the dwelling-under-her-branches of Sirach 14 fulfilled at her table.$n$,$n$The chapter closes with a beatitude on the seeker of wisdom, drawn in the figure of a hunter who camps at her gates and at last dwells beneath her: *Blessed is the man that does meditate good things in wisdom, and that reasoneth of holy things by his understanding* (Ecclesiasticus 14:20), *He that does lodge near her house shall also fasten a pin in her walls* (Ecclesiasticus 14:24), *He shall set his children under her shelter, and shall lodge under her branches* (Ecclesiasticus 14:26). The Psalter's blessed man meditates the same way — *But his delight is in the law of Yahuah (LORD); and in his law doth he meditate day and night* (Psalm 1:2). Solomon's personified Wisdom blesses the watcher at her doors — *Blessed is the man that heareth me, watching daily at my gates, waiting at the posts of my doors* (Proverbs 8:34) — and calls her a tree of life: *She is a tree of life to them that lay hold upon her: and happy is every one that retaineth her* (Proverbs 3:18). Ben Sira himself, later in his own book, will have this same Wisdom invite the seeker home — *Come to me, all you that be desirous of me, and fill yourselves with my fruits* (Ecclesiasticus 24:19) — and the lodging under her branches in chapter 14 is fulfilled at her table.$n$),
(9260,$n$Ben Sira opens the chapter binding wisdom to Torah: *He that fears Yahuah (God) will do good, and he that has the knowledge of the law shall obtain her* (Ecclesiasticus 15:1). Wisdom is not abstract — she comes to the law-keeper as a nursing mother: *And as a mother shall she meet him, and receive him as a wife married of a virgin* (Ecclesiasticus 15:2), feeding him *the bread of understanding* and *the water of wisdom* (Ecclesiasticus 15:3). This is the proverb's own doctrine: *The fear of Yahuah (LORD) is the beginning of knowledge* (Proverbs 1:7) and *The fear of Yahuah (LORD) is the beginning of wisdom: and the knowledge of the holy is understanding* (Proverbs 9:10). Whoso fears Yahuah is *stayed upon her, and shall not be moved* (Ecclesiasticus 15:4) — *Happy is the man that findeth wisdom, and the man that getteth understanding* (Proverbs 3:13). And the door stands open to the one who simply asks: *If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of Elohim (God), that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him* (James 1:5). It ain't new — the same wisdom-out-of-Torah witness.$n$,$n$Ben Sira opens the chapter binding wisdom to Torah: *He that fears Yahuah (God) will do good, and he that has the knowledge of the law shall obtain her* (Ecclesiasticus 15:1). Wisdom is not abstract — she comes to the law-keeper as a nursing mother: *And as a mother shall she meet him, and receive him as a wife married of a virgin* (Ecclesiasticus 15:2), feeding him *the bread of understanding* and *the water of wisdom* (Ecclesiasticus 15:3). This is the proverb's own doctrine: *The fear of Yahuah (LORD) is the beginning of knowledge* (Proverbs 1:7) and *The fear of Yahuah (LORD) is the beginning of wisdom: and the knowledge of the holy is understanding* (Proverbs 9:10). Whoso fears Yahuah (God) is *stayed upon her, and shall not be moved* (Ecclesiasticus 15:4) — *Happy is the man that findeth wisdom, and the man that getteth understanding* (Proverbs 3:13). And the door stands open to the one who simply asks: *If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of Elohim (God), that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him* (James 1:5). It ain't new — in every one of these witnesses wisdom grows out of Torah.$n$),
(9262,$n$Here is the heart of the chapter — the ★ no-author-of-evil verse: *Say not you, It is through Yahuah (God) that I fell away: for you oughtest not to do the things that he hates* (Ecclesiasticus 15:11), *Say not you, He has caused me to err: for he has no need of the sinful man* (Ecclesiasticus 15:12). Ben Sira slams the door on every excuse that lays sin at the Creator's feet. James, Sirach's closest NT sibling, repeats it almost verbatim: *Let no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of Elohim (God): for Elohim (God) cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man* (James 1:13) — *But every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed* (James 1:14). Wisdom of Solomon confirms it from the other restored shelf: *For Yahuah (God) made not death: neither has he pleasure in the destruction of the living* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:13). Yahuah *hates all abomination* (Ecclesiasticus 15:13) — he is no tempter, no cause of the fall. It ain't new: the same anti-fatalism, the same clean Elohim.$n$,$n$Here is the heart of the chapter — the verse that refuses to make Elohim (God) the author of evil: *Say not you, It is through Yahuah (God) that I fell away: for you oughtest not to do the things that he hates* (Ecclesiasticus 15:11), *Say not you, He has caused me to err: for he has no need of the sinful man* (Ecclesiasticus 15:12). Ben Sira slams the door on every excuse that lays sin at the Creator's feet. James, Sirach's closest New Testament kin, repeats it almost verbatim: *Let no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of Elohim (God): for Elohim (God) cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man* (James 1:13) — *But every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed* (James 1:14). Wisdom of Solomon confirms it from its sister book: *For Yahuah (God) made not death: neither has he pleasure in the destruction of the living* (Wisdom of Solomon 1:13). Yahuah (God) *hates all abomination* (Ecclesiasticus 15:13) — he is no tempter, no cause of the fall. It ain't new: the same refusal to blame the Maker, the same Elohim (God) whose hands are clean.$n$),
(9367,$n$Sira's wisdom on giving anticipates the Sermon: *My son, blemish not your good deeds, neither use uncomfortable words when you give any thing* (Ecclesiasticus 18:15), for *Lo, is not a word better than a gift? but both are with a gracious man* (Ecclesiasticus 18:17). Yahusha would say the alms that is trumpeted is blemished: *Therefore when thou doest thine alms, do not sound a trumpet before thee, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, that they may have glory of men* (Matthew 6:2) — *But when thou doest alms, let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth* (Matthew 6:3). And a word better than a gift is the tongue's life-power Mishle weighs: *Death and life are in the power of the tongue: and they that love it shall eat the fruit thereof* (Proverbs 18:21). The same almsgiving-wisdom runs through the restored library — Tobit teaches his son: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you* (Tobit 4:7).$n$,$n$Sira's wisdom on giving anticipates the Sermon: *My son, blemish not your good deeds, neither use uncomfortable words when you give any thing* (Ecclesiasticus 18:15), for *Lo, is not a word better than a gift? but both are with a gracious man* (Ecclesiasticus 18:17). Yahusha (Jesus) would say the alms that is trumpeted is blemished: *Therefore when thou doest thine alms, do not sound a trumpet before thee, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, that they may have glory of men* (Matthew 6:2) — *But when thou doest alms, let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth* (Matthew 6:3). And a word better than a gift is the tongue's life-power Proverbs weighs: *Death and life are in the power of the tongue: and they that love it shall eat the fruit thereof* (Proverbs 18:21). The same wisdom on almsgiving runs through the whole library — Tobit teaches his son: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you* (Tobit 4:7).$n$),
(9557,$n$Ben Sira opens the chapter with the sleepless burden of wealth: *Watching for riches consumeth the flesh, and the care thereof drives away sleep* (Ecclesiasticus 31:1), and he names its peril plainly — *He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof* (Ecclesiasticus 31:5), for *Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present* (Ecclesiasticus 31:6). It ain't new — this is Paul to Timothy: *But they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition* (1 Timothy 6:9), and *For the love of money is the root of all evil: which while some coveted after, they have erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows* (1 Timothy 6:10). The Preacher already knew the empty chase — *He that loveth silver shall not be satisfied with silver; nor he that loveth abundance with increase: this is also vanity* (Ecclesiastes 5:10) — and the sleeplessness Sira describes — *the abundance of the rich will not suffer him to sleep* (Ecclesiastes 5:12). Solomon warned against the same vanishing prize, and James saw the rich man fade like grass. Ben Sira himself doubles the witness elsewhere: *Be not at variance with a rich man, lest he overweigh you: for gold has destroyed many, and perverted the hearts of kings* (Ecclesiasticus 8:2). The wisdom-stream is one.$n$,$n$Ben Sira opens the chapter with the sleepless burden of wealth: *Watching for riches consumeth the flesh, and the care thereof drives away sleep* (Ecclesiasticus 31:1), and he names its peril plainly — *He that loves gold shall not be justified, and he that followeth corruption shall have enough thereof* (Ecclesiasticus 31:5), for *Gold has been the ruin of many, and their destruction was present* (Ecclesiasticus 31:6). It ain't new — this is Paul to Timothy: *But they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition* (1 Timothy 6:9), and *For the love of money is the root of all evil: which while some coveted after, they have erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows* (1 Timothy 6:10). The Preacher already knew the empty chase — *He that loveth silver shall not be satisfied with silver; nor he that loveth abundance with increase: this is also vanity* (Ecclesiastes 5:10) — and the sleeplessness Sira describes — *the abundance of the rich will not suffer him to sleep* (Ecclesiastes 5:12). Solomon warned against the same vanishing prize, and James saw the rich man fade like grass. Ben Sira himself doubles the witness elsewhere: *Be not at variance with a rich man, lest he overweigh you: for gold has destroyed many, and perverted the hearts of kings* (Ecclesiasticus 8:2). The witness is one.$n$),
(9744,$n$Ben Sira presses the injustice to its root — robbing the poor of bread is murder: *Whoso brings an offering of the goods of the poor doeth as one that killeth the son before his father's eyes* (Ecclesiasticus 34:20); *The bread of the needy is their life: he that defraudeth him thereof is a man of blood* (Ecclesiasticus 34:21); *He that takes away his neighbour's living slayeth him; and he that defraudeth the labourer of his hire is a bloodshedder* (Ecclesiasticus 34:22). It ain't new. The Torah commands the wage be paid at sundown: *Thou shalt not oppress an hired servant that is poor and needy* (Deuteronomy 24:14), *At his day thou shalt give him his hire, neither shall the sun go down upon it; for he is poor* (Deuteronomy 24:15). And James, in pure Sirach idiom, makes the withheld wage cry out to heaven: *Behold, the hire of the labourers who have reaped down your fields, which is of you kept back by fraud, crieth: and the cries of them which have reaped are entered into the ears of Yahuah (Lord) of sabaoth* (James 5:4). The unjust gain only gathers wrath: *He that by usury and unjust gain increaseth his substance, he shall gather it for him that will pity the poor* (Proverbs 28:8).$n$,$n$Ben Sira presses the injustice to its root — robbing the poor of bread is murder: *Whoso brings an offering of the goods of the poor doeth as one that killeth the son before his father’s eyes* (Ecclesiasticus 34:20); *The bread of the needy is their life: he that defraudeth him thereof is a man of blood* (Ecclesiasticus 34:21); *He that takes away his neighbour's living slayeth him; and he that defraudeth the labourer of his hire is a bloodshedder* (Ecclesiasticus 34:22). It ain't new. The Torah commands the wage be paid at sundown: *Thou shalt not oppress an hired servant that is poor and needy* (Deuteronomy 24:14), *At his day thou shalt give him his hire, neither shall the sun go down upon it; for he is poor* (Deuteronomy 24:15). And James, in pure Sirach idiom, makes the withheld wage cry out to heaven: *Behold, the hire of the labourers who have reaped down your fields, which is of you kept back by fraud, crieth: and the cries of them which have reaped are entered into the ears of Yahuah (Lord) of sabaoth* (James 5:4). The unjust gain only gathers wrath: *He that by usury and unjust gain increaseth his substance, he shall gather it for him that will pity the poor* (Proverbs 28:8).$n$),
(9772,$n$Ben Sira opens the human lot with the weight that lies on every child of Adam: *Great travail is created for every man, and an heavy yoke is upon the sons of Adam, from the day that they go out of their mother’s womb, till the day that they return to the mother of all things* (Ecclesiasticus 40:1). It ain't new — this is the Edenic sentence read out over the whole race. Yahuah said to the man, *cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life* (Genesis 3:17), and *In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground; for out of it wast thou taken: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return* (Genesis 3:19) — the womb-to-dust arc Ben Sira traces. Job names the same brief, burdened span: *Man that is born of a woman is of few days, and full of trouble* (Job 14:1), and confesses the going-out-and-returning that Sirach echoes — *Naked came I out of my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return thither: Yahuah (LORD) gave, and Yahuah (LORD) hath taken away; blessed be the name of Yahuah (LORD)* (Job 1:21).$n$,$n$Ben Sira opens the human lot with the weight that lies on every child of Adam: *Great travail is created for every man, and an heavy yoke is upon the sons of Adam, from the day that they go out of their mother’s womb, till the day that they return to the mother of all things* (Ecclesiasticus 40:1). It ain't new — this is the Edenic sentence read out over the whole race. Yahuah (God) said to the man, *cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life* (Genesis 3:17), and *In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground; for out of it wast thou taken: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return* (Genesis 3:19) — the same arc from the womb to the dust that Ben Sira traces. Job names the same brief, burdened span: *Man that is born of a woman is of few days, and full of trouble* (Job 14:1), and confesses the same going out and returning that Sirach echoes — *Naked came I out of my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return thither: Yahuah (LORD) gave, and Yahuah (LORD) hath taken away; blessed be the name of Yahuah (LORD)* (Job 1:21).$n$),
(10006,$n$*Enoch pleased Yahuah (God), and was translated, being an example of repentance to all generations* (Ecclesiasticus 44:16). The Tanakh records the taking in a single line: *And Enoch walked with Elohim (God): and he was not; for Elohim (God) took him* (Genesis 5:24), and Hebrews makes the walk a walk of faith: *By faith Enoch was translated that he should not see death; and was not found, because Elohim (God) had translated him: for before his translation he had this testimony, that he pleased Elohim (God)* (Hebrews 11:5). Jubilees tells the same translation, that he was *taken from amongst the children of men, and we conducted him into the Garden of Eden in majesty and honour* (Jubilees 4:23) — the same Enoch whose own witness, the live apparatus, testifies to all generations.$n$,$n$*Enoch pleased Yahuah (God), and was translated, being an example of repentance to all generations* (Ecclesiasticus 44:16). The Tanakh records the taking in a single line: *And Enoch walked with Elohim (God): and he was not; for Elohim (God) took him* (Genesis 5:24), and Hebrews makes the walk a walk of faith: *By faith Enoch was translated that he should not see death; and was not found, because Elohim (God) had translated him: for before his translation he had this testimony, that he pleased Elohim (God)* (Hebrews 11:5). Jubilees tells the same translation, that he was *taken from amongst the children of men, and we conducted him into the Garden of Eden in majesty and honour* (Jubilees 4:23) — the same Enoch whose own book still testifies to all generations.$n$),
(10302,$n$Of the prophet who saw the holy city burn, Ben Sira writes: *They burnt the chosen city of the sanctuary, and made the streets desolate, according to the prophecy of Jeremias* (Sirach 49:6), *For they entreated him evil, who nevertheless was a prophet, sanctified in his mother's womb, that he might root out, and afflict, and destroy; and that he might build up also, and plant* (Sirach 49:7). This is no embellishment — it is Jeremiah's own commissioning, word for word in substance: *Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee; and before thou camest forth out of the womb I sanctified thee, and I ordained thee a prophet unto the nations* (Jeremiah 1:5), and the very fourfold charge Sirach echoes: *See, I have this day set thee over the nations and over the kingdoms, to root out, and to pull down, and to destroy, and to throw down, to build, and to plant* (Jeremiah 1:10). The deuterocanon carries the same prophetic witness it received from the Tanakh.$n$,$n$Of the prophet who saw the holy city burn, Ben Sira writes: *They burnt the chosen city of the sanctuary, and made the streets desolate, according to the prophecy of Jeremias* (Ecclesiasticus 49:6), *For they entreated him evil, who nevertheless was a prophet, sanctified in his mother's womb, that he might root out, and afflict, and destroy; and that he might build up also, and plant* (Ecclesiasticus 49:7). This is no embellishment — it is Jeremiah's own commissioning, in nearly the same words: *Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee; and before thou camest forth out of the womb I sanctified thee, and I ordained thee a prophet unto the nations* (Jeremiah 1:5), and the very fourfold charge Sirach echoes: *See, I have this day set thee over the nations and over the kingdoms, to root out, and to pull down, and to destroy, and to throw down, to build, and to plant* (Jeremiah 1:10). Ecclesiasticus carries the same prophetic witness it received from the Tanakh.$n$),
(10309,$n$*He was as the morning star in the midst of a cloud, and as the moon at the full: As the sun shining upon the temple of the Most High, and as the rainbow giving light in the bright clouds* (Ecclesiasticus 50:6-7). The high priest in his glory at the altar is sung in the light-imagery of heaven — morning star, full moon, sun, rainbow. The rainbow round about glory is the throne-sign of the covenant: *I do set my bow in the cloud, and it shall be for a token of a covenant between me and the earth* (Genesis 9:13), and John saw it about the very throne — *there was a rainbow round about the throne, in sight like unto an emerald* (Revelation 4:3). The Sun that shines upon the house of Yahuah points beyond Simon to the One the prophet named, *unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings* (Malachi 4:2), and the day star Peter says shall *arise in your hearts* (2 Peter 1:19). Do not collapse Simon into the Messiah; the radiance of the priest at the altar is the shadow, and the Sun of righteousness is the substance.$n$,$n$*He was as the morning star in the midst of a cloud, and as the moon at the full: As the sun shining upon the temple of the Most High, and as the rainbow giving light in the bright clouds* (Ecclesiasticus 50:6-7). The high priest in his glory at the altar is sung in the light-imagery of heaven — morning star, full moon, sun, rainbow. The rainbow round about glory is the throne-sign of the covenant: *I do set my bow in the cloud, and it shall be for a token of a covenant between me and the earth* (Genesis 9:13), and John saw it about the very throne — *there was a rainbow round about the throne, in sight like unto an emerald* (Revelation 4:3). The Sun that shines upon the house of Yahuah (God) points beyond Simon to the One the prophet named, *unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings* (Malachi 4:2), and the day star Peter says shall *arise in your hearts* (2 Peter 1:19). Do not collapse Simon into the Messiah (Christ); the radiance of the priest at the altar is the shadow, and the Sun of righteousness is the One it foreshadows.$n$),
(10312,$n$*So were all the sons of Aaron in their glory, and the oblations of Yahuah (God) in their hands, before all the congregation of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Ecclesiasticus 50:13). The sons of Aaron stand in their glory with the offerings in their hands before the whole assembly — the Levitical priesthood at its height. Yet the writer of Hebrews shows the limit honestly: *If therefore perfection were by the Levitical priesthood, (for under it the people received the law,) what further need was there that another priest should rise after the order of Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek), and not be called after the order of Aaron?* (Hebrews 7:11). The Aaronic priests *truly were many priests, because they were not suffered to continue by reason of death* (Hebrews 7:23) — Simon and his sons in their glory are the shadow, and the One who *continueth ever, hath an unchangeable priesthood* (Hebrews 7:24) is the substance toward which it all points. It ain't new: the glory of Aaron's sons is real, and it is a shadow.$n$,$n$*So were all the sons of Aaron in their glory, and the oblations of Yahuah (God) in their hands, before all the congregation of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Ecclesiasticus 50:13). The sons of Aaron stand in their glory with the offerings in their hands before the whole assembly — the Levitical priesthood at its height. Yet the writer of Hebrews shows the limit honestly: *If therefore perfection were by the Levitical priesthood, (for under it the people received the law,) what further need was there that another priest should rise after the order of Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek), and not be called after the order of Aaron?* (Hebrews 7:11). The Aaronic priests *truly were many priests, because they were not suffered to continue by reason of death* (Hebrews 7:23) — Simon and his sons in their glory are the shadow, and the One who *continueth ever, hath an unchangeable priesthood* (Hebrews 7:24) is the One toward whom it all points. It ain't new: the glory of Aaron's sons is real, and it is a shadow.$n$),
(10327,$n$The man who feared Yahuah and buried the dead is rewarded with calamity: *and the sparrows muted warm dung into my eyes, and a whiteness came in my eyes: and I went to the physicians, but they helped me not* (Tobit 2:10). Here is the riddle of Job set in a new key — the upright man stripped of sight as Job was stripped of all. For Job too was named perfect: *There was a man in the land of Uz, whose name was Job; and that man was perfect and upright, and one that feared Elohim (God), and eschewed evil.* (Job 1:1) And the affliction fell upon his very body: *So went Satan forth from the presence of Yahuah (LORD), and smote Job with sore boils from the sole of his foot unto his crown.* (Job 2:7) The blindness of Tobit, like the boils of Job, is the suffering of the just — not the wages of sin, but a trial within the hand of Yahuah, who will yet heal by His angel.$n$,$n$The man who feared Yahuah (God) and buried the dead is rewarded with calamity: *the sparrows muted warm dung into my eyes, and a whiteness came in my eyes: and I went to the physicians, but they helped me not* (Tobit 2:10). Here is the riddle of Job set in a new key — the upright man stripped of sight as Job was stripped of all. For Job too was named perfect: *There was a man in the land of Uz, whose name was Job; and that man was perfect and upright, and one that feared Elohim (God), and eschewed evil.* (Job 1:1) And the affliction fell upon his very body: *So went Satan forth from the presence of Yahuah (LORD), and smote Job with sore boils from the sole of his foot unto his crown.* (Job 2:7) The blindness of Tobit, like the boils of Job, is the suffering of the just — not the wages of sin, but a trial within the hand of Yahuah (God), who will yet heal by His angel.$n$),
(10329,$n$Anna means her words as a taunt — *Where are your alms and your righteous deeds?* (Tobit 2:14) — but they name the very life Tobit has lived and will counsel his son to live: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7) The alms are no empty works; they are treasure laid up: *Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10) The Master taught the same hidden reward, not paraded before men: *That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.* (Matthew 6:4) Tobit's righteous deeds are known — to Yahuah, and to the angel who stood beside him — and they will not go unrewarded.$n$,$n$Anna means her words as a taunt — *Where are your alms and your righteous deeds?* (Tobit 2:14) — but they name the very life Tobit has lived and will counsel his son to live: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7) The alms are no empty gesture; they are treasure laid up: *Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10) Yahusha (Jesus) taught the same hidden reward, not paraded before men: *That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.* (Matthew 6:4) Tobit's righteous deeds are known — to Yahuah (God), and to the angel who stood beside him — and they will not go unrewarded.$n$),
(10335,$n$Tobit's deathbed wisdom turns on a hinge the whole library shares: *Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10) — give *of your substance*, *if you have abundance give alms accordingly: if you have but a little, be not afraid to give according to that little* (Tobit 4:8), *for you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity* (Tobit 4:9). It is no new saying. The Proverbs already twice cut the same channel — *Treasures of wickedness profit nothing: but righteousness delivereth from death.* (Proverbs 10:2) and *Riches profit not in the day of wrath: but righteousness delivereth from death.* (Proverbs 11:4). Daniel pressed it on a king's conscience — *Wherefore, O king, let my counsel be acceptable unto thee, and break off thy sins by righteousness, and thine iniquities by shewing mercy to the poor; if it may be a lengthening of thy tranquillity.* (Daniel 4:27). And Yahusha lays up the very treasure Tobit names: *Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.* (Luke 12:33). One covenant ethic, exile to Sermon.$n$,$n$Tobit's deathbed wisdom turns on a hinge the whole library shares: *Because that alms do deliver from death, and suffers not to come into darkness.* (Tobit 4:10) — give *of your substance* — *If you have abundance give alms accordingly: if you have but a little, be not afraid to give according to that little* (Tobit 4:8), *for you layest up a good treasure for thyself against the day of necessity* (Tobit 4:9). It is no new saying. The Proverbs already twice cut the same channel — *Treasures of wickedness profit nothing: but righteousness delivereth from death.* (Proverbs 10:2) and *Riches profit not in the day of wrath: but righteousness delivereth from death.* (Proverbs 11:4). Daniel pressed it on a king's conscience — *Wherefore, O king, let my counsel be acceptable unto thee, and break off thy sins by righteousness, and thine iniquities by shewing mercy to the poor; if it may be a lengthening of thy tranquillity.* (Daniel 4:27). And Yahusha (Jesus) lays up the very treasure Tobit names: *Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.* (Luke 12:33). One covenant ethic, exile to Sermon.$n$),
(10336,$n$Tobit measures the gift by the heart, not the purse: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7) — *Give of your bread to the hungry, and of your garments to them that are naked; and according to your abundance give alms: and let not your eye be envious, when you give alms.* (Tobit 4:16). Yahusha makes the cleansing turn on exactly this — *But rather give alms of such things as ye have; and, behold, all things are clean unto you.* (Luke 11:41) — and binds the heart to the treasure: *For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.* (Luke 12:34). The 'unenvious eye' Tobit commands is the Sermon's 'single eye' that fills the body with light.$n$,$n$Tobit measures the gift by the heart, not the purse: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7) — *Give of your bread to the hungry, and of your garments to them that are naked; and according to your abundance give alms: and let not your eye be envious, when you give alms.* (Tobit 4:16). Yahusha (Jesus) makes the cleansing turn on exactly this — *But rather give alms of such things as ye have; and, behold, all things are clean unto you.* (Luke 11:41) — and binds the heart to the treasure: *For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.* (Luke 12:34). The 'unenvious eye' Tobit commands is the Sermon's 'single eye' that fills the body with light.$n$),
(10445,$n$Judith stakes everything on Yahuah accomplishing the work through her: *if you will follow the words of yours handmaid, Yahuah (God) will bring the thing perfectly to pass by you* (Judith 11:6), and *Yahuah (God) has sent me to work things with you, whereat all the earth shall be astonished* (Judith 11:16). The astonishing thing is the oldest promise — that the seed of the woman crushes the head of the enemy: *And I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel* (Genesis 3:15). Jael had already prefigured it, the woman's hand driving the spike into the sleeping general's skull: *Then Jael Heber's wife took a nail of the tent, and took an hammer in her hand, and went softly unto him, and smote the nail into his temples, and fastened it into the ground* (Judges 4:21). And Miriam sings the meaning of every such victory — the proud cast down by the arm of Yahuah through the lowly: *He hath shewed strength with his arm; he hath scattered the proud in the imagination of their hearts* (Luke 1:51). The weak-confound-the-mighty pattern runs straight from Eden to the Magnificat; Judith stands in that line without being made the Messiah of it.$n$,$n$Judith stakes everything on Yahuah (God) accomplishing the work through her: *if you will follow the words of yours handmaid, Yahuah (God) will bring the thing perfectly to pass by you* (Judith 11:6), and *Yahuah (God) has sent me to work things with you, whereat all the earth shall be astonished* (Judith 11:16). The astonishing thing is the oldest promise — that the seed of the woman crushes the head of the enemy: *And I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel* (Genesis 3:15). Jael had already prefigured it, the woman's hand driving the spike into the sleeping general's skull: *Then Jael Heber's wife took a nail of the tent, and took an hammer in her hand, and went softly unto him, and smote the nail into his temples, and fastened it into the ground* (Judges 4:21). And Miriam (Mary) sings the meaning of every such victory — the proud cast down by the arm of Yahuah (God) through the lowly: *He hath shewed strength with his arm; he hath scattered the proud in the imagination of their hearts* (Luke 1:51). The pattern of the weak confounding the mighty runs straight from Eden to the Magnificat; Judith stands in that line without being made the Messiah (Christ) of it.$n$),
(10447,$n$Holiness in the tyrant's tent is not only diet but devotion. Judith rises before dawn — *and she arose when it was toward the morning watch* (Judith 12:5) — and asks leave to go out to prayer: *And when she came out, she besought Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel) to direct her way to the raising up of the children of her people* (Judith 12:8), then *So she came in clean, and remained in the tent* (Judith 12:9). It ain't new: David fixed the same hour for his prayer — *My voice shalt thou hear in the morning, O Yahuah (LORD); in the morning will I direct my prayer unto thee, and will look up* (Psalm 5:3) — the very "direct my way / direct my prayer" of the morning watch. And her washing-and-coming-in-clean is the worshipper's approach: *But as for me, I will come into thy house in the multitude of thy mercy: and in thy fear will I worship toward thy holy temple* (Psalm 5:7). She lives by the sunset-to-sunset reckoning too, eating *at evening* (Judith 12:9) — the appointed times kept even behind enemy lines.$n$,$n$Holiness in the tyrant's tent is not only diet but devotion. Judith rises before dawn — *and she arose when it was toward the morning watch* (Judith 12:5) — and asks leave to go out to prayer: *And when she came out, she besought Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel) to direct her way to the raising up of the children of her people* (Judith 12:8), then *So she came in clean, and remained in the tent* (Judith 12:9). It ain't new: David fixed the same hour for his prayer — *My voice shalt thou hear in the morning, O Yahuah (LORD); in the morning will I direct my prayer unto thee, and will look up* (Psalm 5:3) — she asks him to direct her way, David asks him to direct his prayer, both at the morning watch. And her washing and coming in clean is the worshipper's approach: *But as for me, I will come into thy house in the multitude of thy mercy: and in thy fear will I worship toward thy holy temple* (Psalm 5:7). She lives by the reckoning from sunset to sunset too, eating *at evening* (Judith 12:9) — the appointed times kept even behind enemy lines.$n$),
(10469,$n$The decisive line of the whole book: *But the Almighty Yahuah (God) has disappointed them by the hand of a woman. For the mighty one did not fall by the young men, neither did the sons of the Titans smite him, nor high giants set upon him: but Judith the daughter of Merari weakened him with the beauty of her countenance.* (Judith 16:6-7) This is heaven's settled pattern — not many mighty, but the weak chosen to shame the strong: *But Elohim (God) hath chosen the foolish things of the world to confound the wise; and Elohim (God) hath chosen the weak things of the world to confound the things which are mighty;* (1 Corinthians 1:27) Hannah sang it before her: *The bows of the mighty men are broken, and they that stumbled are girded with strength.* (1 Samuel 2:4) And the head taken by a woman's hand reaches back to the first promise — the woman's seed that bruises the head of the enemy: *And I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel.* (Genesis 3:15) Honestly read: Judith is no Messiah, but her hand-of-a-woman victory echoes the Edenic word that the proud serpent-head falls.$n$,$n$The decisive line of the whole book: *But the Almighty Yahuah (God) has disappointed them by the hand of a woman. For the mighty one did not fall by the young men, neither did the sons of the Titans smite him, nor high giants set upon him: but Judith the daughter of Merari weakened him with the beauty of her countenance.* (Judith 16:6-7) This is heaven's settled pattern — not many mighty, but the weak chosen to shame the strong: *But Elohim (God) hath chosen the foolish things of the world to confound the wise; and Elohim (God) hath chosen the weak things of the world to confound the things which are mighty;* (1 Corinthians 1:27) Hannah sang it before her: *The bows of the mighty men are broken, and they that stumbled are girded with strength.* (1 Samuel 2:4) And the head taken by a woman's hand reaches back to the first promise — the woman's seed that bruises the head of the enemy: *And I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel.* (Genesis 3:15) Honestly read: Judith is no Messiah (Christ), but her victory by the hand of a woman echoes the Edenic word that the proud head of the serpent falls.$n$),
(10471,$n$The song turns to a new psalm of the whole creation's obedience to its Maker: *I will sing to Yahuah (God) a new song: O Yahuah (God), you are great and glorious, wonderful in strength, and invincible. Let all creatures serve you: for you spakest, and they were made, you did send forth your spirit, and it created them, and there is none that can resist your voice.* (Judith 16:13-14) It ain't new — it is the new song of the Psalter: *O sing unto Yahuah (LORD) a new song: sing unto Yahuah (LORD), all the earth.* (Psalm 96:1) And the creation-by-the-word the Psalm of praise declares: *Let them praise the name of Yahuah (LORD): for he commanded, and they were created.* (Psalm 148:5) Yet the same song carries the dread end of those who transgress — fire and the worm that dieth not: *Woe to the nations that rise up against my kindred! Yahuah (God) Almighty will take vengeance of them in the day of judgment, in putting fire and worms in their flesh; and they shall feel them, and weep for ever.* (Judith 16:17) — which Isaiah seals as the abhorring of all flesh: *And they shall go forth, and look upon the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me: for their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched; and they shall be an abhorring unto all flesh.* (Isaiah 66:24)$n$,$n$The song turns to a new psalm of the whole creation's obedience to its Maker: *I will sing to Yahuah (God) a new song: O Yahuah (God), you are great and glorious, wonderful in strength, and invincible. Let all creatures serve you: for you spakest, and they were made, you did send forth your spirit, and it created them, and there is none that can resist your voice.* (Judith 16:13-14) It ain't new — it is the new song of the Psalter: *O sing unto Yahuah (LORD) a new song: sing unto Yahuah (LORD), all the earth.* (Psalm 96:1) And the creation by the word that the Psalm of praise declares: *Let them praise the name of Yahuah (LORD): for he commanded, and they were created.* (Psalm 148:5) Yet the same song carries the dread end of the nations that rise against the seed — fire and the worm: *Woe to the nations that rise up against my kindred! Yahuah (God) Almighty will take vengeance of them in the day of judgment, in putting fire and worms in their flesh; and they shall feel them, and weep for ever.* (Judith 16:17) — which Isaiah seals as carcases abhorred by all flesh: *And they shall go forth, and look upon the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me: for their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched; and they shall be an abhorring unto all flesh.* (Isaiah 66:24)$n$),
(10490,$n$Before she dares the king's gate, the queen strips off the crown's splendour and goes down into mourning: *And laid away her glorious apparel, and put on the garments of anguish and mourning: and instead of precious ointments, she covered her head with ashes and dung, and she humbled her body greatly, and all the places of her joy she filled with her torn hair.* (the-rest-of-esther 14:2). It ain't new — this is the prophets' own liturgy of repentance, *Therefore also now, saith Yahuah (LORD), turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning* (Joel 2:12), the heart rent and not the garment. Her sister-deliverer Judith does the same before her own peril: *Judith fell upon her face, and put ashes upon her head, and uncovered the sackcloth with which she was clothed* (Judith 9:1). And the Messiah seals that the fast is for the Father and not for show: *But thou, when thou fastest, anoint thine head, and wash thy face* (Matthew 6:17).$n$,$n$Before she dares the king's gate, the queen strips off the crown's splendour and goes down into mourning: *And laid away her glorious apparel, and put on the garments of anguish and mourning: and instead of precious ointments, she covered her head with ashes and dung, and she humbled her body greatly, and all the places of her joy she filled with her torn hair.* (The Rest of Esther 14:2). It ain't new — this is the prophets' own liturgy of repentance, *Therefore also now, saith Yahuah (LORD), turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning* (Joel 2:12), the heart rent and not the garment. Her sister-deliverer Judith does the same before her own peril: *Judith fell upon her face, and put ashes upon her head, and uncovered the sackcloth with which she was clothed* (Judith 9:1). And the Messiah (Christ) seals that the fast is for the Father and not for show: *But thou, when thou fastest, anoint thine head, and wash thy face* (Matthew 6:17).$n$),
(10491,$n$She names herself by her need: *And she prayed to Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel), saying, O my Elohim (God), you only art our King: help me, desolate woman, which have no helper but you* (the-rest-of-esther 14:3), and again *help me that am desolate, and which have no other help but you* (the-rest-of-esther 14:14). This is the cry of the forsaken Psalter — *Be not far from me; for trouble is near; for there is none to help* (Psalm 22:11) — and *Turn thee unto me, and have mercy upon me; for I am desolate and afflicted* (Psalm 25:16). Judith names the same God: *you are a Yahuah (God) of the afflicted, an helper of the oppressed, an upholder of the weak, a protector of the forlorn, a saviour of them that are without hope* (Judith 9:11).$n$,$n$She names herself by her need: *And she prayed to Yahuah (God) of Yashar'el (Israel), saying, O my Elohim (God), you only art our King: help me, desolate woman, which have no helper but you* (The Rest of Esther 14:3), and again *help me that am desolate, and which have no other help but you* (The Rest of Esther 14:14). This is the cry of the forsaken Psalter — *Be not far from me; for trouble is near; for there is none to help* (Psalm 22:11) — and *Turn thee unto me, and have mercy upon me; for I am desolate and afflicted* (Psalm 25:16). Judith names the same God: *you are a Yahuah (God) of the afflicted, an helper of the oppressed, an upholder of the weak, a protector of the forlorn, a saviour of them that are without hope* (Judith 9:11).$n$),
(10492,$n$Her plea is grounded in election: *From my youth up I have heard in the tribe of my family that you, O Yahuah (God), tookest Yashar'el (Israel) from among all people, and our fathers from all their predecessors, for a perpetual inheritance, and you have performed whatsoever you did promise them* (the-rest-of-esther 14:5). It ain't new — this is Sinai's own word, *if ye will obey my voice indeed, and keep my covenant, then ye shall be a peculiar treasure unto me above all people* (Exodus 19:5), and *Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath chosen thee to be a special people unto himself, above all people that are upon the face of the earth* (Deuteronomy 7:6). The election stands; the seed is kept.$n$,$n$Her plea is grounded in election: *From my youth up I have heard in the tribe of my family that you, O Yahuah (God), tookest Yashar'el (Israel) from among all people, and our fathers from all their predecessors, for a perpetual inheritance, and you have performed whatsoever you did promise them* (The Rest of Esther 14:5). It ain't new — this is Sinai's own word, *if ye will obey my voice indeed, and keep my covenant, then ye shall be a peculiar treasure unto me above all people* (Exodus 19:5), and *Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath chosen thee to be a special people unto himself, above all people that are upon the face of the earth* (Deuteronomy 7:6). The election stands; the seed is kept.$n$),
(10493,$n$Like every true intercessor she confesses for her people before she asks: *And now we have sinned before you: therefore have you given us into the hands of our enemies, Because we worshipped their gods: O Yahuah (God), you are righteous* (the-rest-of-esther 14:6-7). This is Daniel's prayer breath for breath — *We have sinned, and have committed iniquity, and have done wickedly, and have rebelled, even by departing from thy precepts and from thy judgments* (Daniel 9:5) — and Nehemiah's, *Howbeit thou art just in all that is brought upon us; for thou hast done right, but we have done wickedly* (Nehemiah 9:33). The Torah named the captivity beforehand, healed by the humbled heart: *if then their uncircumcised hearts be humbled, and they then accept of the punishment of their iniquity* (Leviticus 26:41). Mercy is real, never cheap.$n$,$n$Like every true intercessor she confesses for her people before she asks: *And now we have sinned before you: therefore have you given us into the hands of our enemies, Because we worshipped their gods: O Yahuah (God), you are righteous* (The Rest of Esther 14:6-7). This is Daniel's prayer breath for breath — *We have sinned, and have committed iniquity, and have done wickedly, and have rebelled, even by departing from thy precepts and from thy judgments* (Daniel 9:5) — and Nehemiah's, *Howbeit thou art just in all that is brought upon us; for thou hast done right, but we have done wickedly* (Nehemiah 9:33). The Torah named the captivity beforehand, healed by the humbled heart: *if then their uncircumcised hearts be humbled, and they then accept of the punishment of their iniquity* (Leviticus 26:41). Mercy is real, never cheap.$n$),
(10494,$n$She wars not against flesh but against the system of dead gods: *O Yahuah (God), give not your sceptre to them that be nothing, and let them not laugh at our fall* (the-rest-of-esther 14:11), pleading to the *King of the nations, and Yahuah (God) of all power* (14:12) and asking that the king's heart be turned: *Give me eloquent speech in my mouth before the lion: turn his heart to hate him that fighteth against us* (14:13). The idols 'that be nothing' are the mouthless, eyeless work of hands — *Their idols are silver and gold, the work of men's hands. They have mouths, but they speak not* (Psalm 115:4-5) — over against *Yahuah (LORD) is the true Elohim (God), he is the living Elohim (God), and an everlasting king* (Jeremiah 10:10). And the heathen lion's heart is His to turn: *The king's heart is in the hand of Yahuah (LORD), as the rivers of water: he turneth it whithersoever he will* (Proverbs 21:1).$n$,$n$She wars not against flesh but against the system of dead gods: *O Yahuah (God), give not your sceptre to them that be nothing, and let them not laugh at our fall* (The Rest of Esther 14:11), pleading to the *King of the nations, and Yahuah (God) of all power* (14:12) and asking that the king's heart be turned: *Give me eloquent speech in my mouth before the lion: turn his heart to hate him that fighteth against us* (14:13). The idols 'that be nothing' are the mouthless, eyeless work of hands — *Their idols are silver and gold, the work of men's hands. They have mouths, but they speak not* (Psalm 115:4-5) — over against *Yahuah (LORD) is the true Elohim (God), he is the living Elohim (God), and an everlasting king* (Jeremiah 10:10). And the heathen lion's heart is His to turn: *The king's heart is in the hand of Yahuah (LORD), as the rivers of water: he turneth it whithersoever he will* (Proverbs 21:1).$n$),
(10495,$n$Her hidden Torah-faithfulness is laid bare: *you know that I hate the glory of the unrighteous, and abhor the bed of the uncircumcised, and of all the heathen* (the-rest-of-esther 14:15); she abhors the diadem itself, *I abhor it as a menstruous rag* (14:16), and she keeps the dietary line in the heart of the palace — *yours handmaid has not eaten at Aman's table, and that I have not greatly esteemed the king's feast, nor drunk the wine of the drink offerings* (14:17). This is Daniel in Babylon: *Daniel purposed in his heart that he would not defile himself with the portion of the king's meat, nor with the wine which he drank* (Daniel 1:8). And it is the unashamed confession before kings, *I will speak of thy testimonies also before kings, and will not be ashamed* (Psalm 119:46). The crown abhorred, the heart's joy only in *Yahuah (God) of Abraham* (14:18).$n$,$n$Her hidden Torah-faithfulness is laid bare: *you know that I hate the glory of the unrighteous, and abhor the bed of the uncircumcised, and of all the heathen* (The Rest of Esther 14:15); she abhors the diadem itself, *I abhor it as a menstruous rag* (14:16), and she keeps the dietary line in the heart of the palace — *yours handmaid has not eaten at Aman's table, and that I have not greatly esteemed the king's feast, nor drunk the wine of the drink offerings* (14:17). This is Daniel in Babylon: *Daniel purposed in his heart that he would not defile himself with the portion of the king's meat, nor with the wine which he drank* (Daniel 1:8). And it is the unashamed confession before kings, *I will speak of thy testimonies also before kings, and will not be ashamed* (Psalm 119:46). The crown abhorred, the heart's joy only in *Yahuah (God) of Abraham* (14:18).$n$),
(10526,$n$Patient endurance is promised vindication, and repentance is real turning: *My children, suffer patiently the wrath that is come upon you from Yahuah (God): for your enemy has persecuted you; but shortly you shall see his destruction, and shall tread upon his neck.* (Baruch 4:25) — *For as it was your mind to go astray from Yahuah (God): so, being returned, seek him ten times more.* (Baruch 4:28). The seek-him-and-return is Moses' own promise of restored captivity: *And shalt return unto Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God), and shalt obey his voice according to all that I command thee this day, thou and thy children, with all thine heart, and with all thy soul;* (Deuteronomy 30:2). The cup of fury passes to the persecutor: *But I will put it into the hand of them that afflict thee; which have said to thy soul, Bow down, that we may go over: and thou hast laid thy body as the ground, and as the street, to them that went over.* (Isaiah 51:23) — the trodden become the treaders, *shall tread upon his neck*. The mercy is never cheap; the turning is wholehearted.$n$,$n$Patient endurance is promised vindication, and repentance is real turning: *My children, suffer patiently the wrath that is come upon you from Yahuah (God): for your enemy has persecuted you; but shortly you shall see his destruction, and shall tread upon his neck.* (Baruch 4:25) — *For as it was your mind to go astray from Yahuah (God): so, being returned, seek him ten times more.* (Baruch 4:28). The returning and seeking is Moses' own promise of restored captivity: *And shalt return unto Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God), and shalt obey his voice according to all that I command thee this day, thou and thy children, with all thine heart, and with all thy soul;* (Deuteronomy 30:2). The cup of fury passes to the persecutor: *But I will put it into the hand of them that afflict thee; which have said to thy soul, Bow down, that we may go over: and thou hast laid thy body as the ground, and as the street, to them that went over.* (Isaiah 51:23) — the trodden become the treaders, *shall tread upon his neck*. The mercy is never cheap; the turning is wholehearted.$n$),
(10550,$n$Condemned and led toward death, Susanna does not curse but commits herself to the Judge who sees in secret: *And she weeping looked up toward heaven: for her heart trusted in Yahuah* (Susanna 1:35), and she prays *O everlasting Yahuah, that knowest the secrets, and knowest all things before they be* (Susanna 1:42). This is the wicked-watch-the-righteous pattern, and its promised deliverance: *The wicked watcheth the righteous, and seeketh to slay him* (Psalm 37:32) — the elders watching her every day in 1:8 — *Yahuah will not leave him in his hand, nor condemn him when he is judged* (Psalm 37:33). It is the Apocrypha's own confidence that *Yahuah knoweth how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be punished* (2 Peter 2:9). She looks up where the elders refused to look; the eyes turned heavenward are heard.$n$,$n$Condemned and led toward death, Susanna does not curse but commits herself to the Judge who sees in secret: *And she weeping looked up toward heaven: for her heart trusted in Yahuah (God)* (Susanna 1:35), and she prays *O everlasting Yahuah (God), that knowest the secrets, and knowest all things before they be* (Susanna 1:42). This is the pattern of the wicked watching the righteous, and its promised deliverance: *The wicked watcheth the righteous, and seeketh to slay him* (Psalm 37:32) — the elders watching her every day in 1:8 — *Yahuah (LORD) will not leave him in his hand, nor condemn him when he is judged* (Psalm 37:33). It is the same confidence Peter writes down, that *Yahuah (Lord) knoweth how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be punished* (2 Peter 2:9). She looks up where the elders refused to look; the eyes turned heavenward are heard.$n$),
(10602,$n$The chapter's pivot is a turning of the eyes — from heaven, which had already given the victories, to a distant arm of flesh: *In consideration of these things, Judas chose Eupolemus the son of John, the son of Accos, and Jason the son of Eleazar, and sent them to Rome, to make a league of amity and confederacy with them,* (1 Maccabees 8:17) — *And to intreat them that they would take the yoke from them; for they saw that the kingdom of the Grecians did oppress Yashar'el (Israel) with servitude.* (1 Maccabees 8:18). The text invites the old caution, and we weave it honestly. *Thus saith Yahuah (LORD); Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm, and whose heart departeth from Yahuah (LORD).* (Jeremiah 17:5) — to make Rome the arm is the very thing the prophet names. *Put not your trust in princes, nor in the son of Adam, in whom there is no help.* (Psalm 146:3) — the Roman senate is princes, and their help is the help of dust. *There is no king saved by the multitude of an host: a mighty man is not delivered by much strength.* (Psalm 33:16) — Rome's hundred-and-twenty elephants and great army do not save. *Woe to them that go down to Egypt for help; and stay on horses, and trust in chariots, because they are many; and in horsemen, because they are very strong; but they look not unto the Holy One of Yashar'el (Israel), neither seek Yahuah (LORD)!* (Isaiah 31:1) — the embassy to Rome is the new going-down-to-Egypt. This is not to condemn Judas, whose zeal for the covenant was true; it is the reader's quiet warning, that even the faithful are tempted to lean where they should kneel.$n$,$n$The chapter's pivot is a turning of the eyes — from heaven, which had already given the victories, to a distant arm of flesh: *In consideration of these things, Judas chose Eupolemus the son of John, the son of Accos, and Jason the son of Eleazar, and sent them to Rome, to make a league of amity and confederacy with them,* (1 Maccabees 8:17) — *And to intreat them that they would take the yoke from them; for they saw that the kingdom of the Grecians did oppress Yashar'el (Israel) with servitude.* (1 Maccabees 8:18). The text invites the old caution, and it is worth hearing plainly. *Thus saith Yahuah (LORD); Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm, and whose heart departeth from Yahuah (LORD).* (Jeremiah 17:5) — to make Rome the arm is the very thing the prophet names. *Put not your trust in princes, nor in the son of Adam, in whom there is no help.* (Psalm 146:3) — the Roman senate is princes, and their help is the help of dust. *There is no king saved by the multitude of an host: a mighty man is not delivered by much strength.* (Psalm 33:16) — Rome's hundred and twenty elephants and great army do not save. *Woe to them that go down to Egypt for help; and stay on horses, and trust in chariots, because they are many; and in horsemen, because they are very strong; but they look not unto the Holy One of Yashar'el (Israel), neither seek Yahuah (LORD)!* (Isaiah 31:1) — the embassy to Rome is a new going down to Egypt for help. This is not to condemn Judas, whose zeal for the covenant was true; it is the reader's quiet warning, that even the faithful are tempted to lean where they should kneel.$n$),
(10648,$n$When Heliodorus comes to plunder the temple, the high priest tells him what the treasury truly holds: *Then the high priest told him that there was such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children:* (2 Maccabees 3:10) — the king's man covets gold, but it is the substance of the helpless. The Torah set a fence around exactly this: *Ye shall not afflict any widow, or fatherless child.* (Exodus 22:22), for of Yahuah it is said *He doth execute the judgment of the fatherless and widow, and loveth the stranger, in giving him food and raiment.* (Deuteronomy 10:18) and *A father of the fatherless, and a judge of the widows, is Elohim (God) in his holy habitation.* (Psalm 68:5). The same covenant heart runs straight into the apostolic age — *Pure religion and undefiled before Elohim (God) and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep himself unspotted from the world.* (James 1:27) — it ain't new. And the restored Tobit names the very almsgiving the temple safeguarded: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). To rob this house is to rob the poor of the covenant.$n$,$n$When Heliodorus comes to plunder the temple, the high priest tells him what the treasury truly holds: *Then the high priest told him that there was such money laid up for the relief of widows and fatherless children:* (2 Maccabees 3:10) — the king's man covets gold, but it is the living of the helpless. The Torah set a fence around exactly this: *Ye shall not afflict any widow, or fatherless child.* (Exodus 22:22), for of Yahuah (God) it is said *He doth execute the judgment of the fatherless and widow, and loveth the stranger, in giving him food and raiment.* (Deuteronomy 10:18) and *A father of the fatherless, and a judge of the widows, is Elohim (God) in his holy habitation.* (Psalm 68:5). The same covenant heart runs straight into the apostolic age — *Pure religion and undefiled before Elohim (God) and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep himself unspotted from the world.* (James 1:27) — it ain't new. And Tobit names the very almsgiving the temple safeguarded: *Give alms of your substance; and when you give alms, let not your eye be envious, neither turn your face from any poor, and the face of Yahuah (God) shall not be turned away from you.* (Tobit 4:7). To rob this house is to rob the poor of the covenant.$n$),
(10713,$n$Josiah speaks to the Levites: *that they should hallow themselves to Yahuah (God), to set the holy ark of Yahuah (God) in the house that king Solomon the son of David had built:* (1 Esdras 1:3), *You shall no more bear the ark upon your shoulders: now therefore serve Yahuah (God), your Elohim (God), and minister to his people Yashar'el (Israel)* (1 Esdras 1:4). It is the Chronicler's charge word for word in substance — *Put the holy ark in the house which Solomon the son of David king of Yashar'el (Israel) did build; it shall not be a burden upon your shoulders: serve now Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God), and his people Yashar'el (Israel),* (2 Chronicles 35:3) — and the keeping done *according to the word of Yahuah (LORD) by the hand of Moses.* (2 Chronicles 35:6). Election and ordinance precede the feast: the courses David set, the law Moses gave, the ark Solomon housed — the same covenant witness, unbroken.$n$,$n$Josiah speaks to the Levites: *that they should hallow themselves to Yahuah (God), to set the holy ark of Yahuah (God) in the house that king Solomon the son of David had built:* (1 Esdras 1:3), *You shall no more bear the ark upon your shoulders: now therefore serve Yahuah (God), your Elohim (God), and minister to his people Yashar'el (Israel)* (1 Esdras 1:4). It is the Chronicler's charge in nearly the same words — *Put the holy ark in the house which Solomon the son of David king of Yashar'el (Israel) did build; it shall not be a burden upon your shoulders: serve now Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God), and his people Yashar'el (Israel),* (2 Chronicles 35:3) — and the keeping done *according to the word of Yahuah (LORD) by the hand of Moses.* (2 Chronicles 35:6). Election and ordinance precede the feast: the courses David set, the law Moses gave, the ark Solomon housed — the same covenant witness, unbroken.$n$),
(10750,$n$Esdras' account of the gathered remnant is Ezra 10 told over again, word for word in substance: *And in three days were all they of the tribe of Yahudah (Judah) and Benjamin gathered together at Jerusalem the twentieth day of the ninth month. And all the multitude sat trembling in the broad court of the temple because of the present foul weather* (1 Esdras 9:5-6). The Hebrew canon records the same hour: *Then all the men of Yahudah (Judah) and Benjamin gathered themselves together unto Jerusalem within three days. It was the ninth month, on the twentieth day of the month; and all the people sat in the street of the house of Elohim (God), trembling because of this matter, and for the great rain* (Ezra 10:9). It ain't new — 1 Esdras is the same testimony, the same returned remnant trembling in the rain before the rebuilt house.$n$,$n$Esdras' account of the gathered remnant is Ezra 10 told over again, in nearly the same words: *And in three days were all they of the tribe of Yahudah (Judah) and Benjamin gathered together at Jerusalem the twentieth day of the ninth month. And all the multitude sat trembling in the broad court of the temple because of the present foul weather* (1 Esdras 9:5-6). The Hebrew canon records the same hour: *Then all the men of Yahudah (Judah) and Benjamin gathered themselves together unto Jerusalem within three days. It was the ninth month, on the twentieth day of the month; and all the people sat in the street of the house of Elohim (God), trembling because of this matter, and for the great rain* (Ezra 10:9). It ain't new — 1 Esdras is the same testimony, the same returned remnant trembling in the rain before the rebuilt house.$n$),
(10756,$n$The mercies are itemized: *Then had I pity upon your mournings, and gave you manna to eat; so you did eat angels’ bread* (2 Esdras 1:19), and *When you were thirsty, did I not cleave the rock, and waters flowed out to your fill?* (2 Esdras 1:20). The source is the wilderness record: *Then said Yahuah (LORD) unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you; and the people shall go out and gather a certain rate every day, that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no* (Exodus 16:4). The Psalmist names it the very phrase Esdras uses — *Man did eat angels’ food: he sent them meat to the full* (Psalm 78:25). And it ain't new: the manna and the rock were always reaching toward the Messiah. Yahusha takes up the manna and reveals its substance — *Verily, verily, I say unto you, Moses gave you not that bread from heaven; but my Father giveth you the true bread from heaven* (John 6:32). The rock Esdras says was cleaved Sha'ul names outright: *And did all drink the same spiritual drink: for they drank of that spiritual Rock that followed them: and that Rock was Messiah (Christ)* (1 Corinthians 10:4). The bread and the water were Him all along.$n$,$n$The mercies are itemized: *Then had I pity upon your mournings, and gave you manna to eat; so you did eat angels’ bread* (2 Esdras 1:19), and *When you were thirsty, did I not cleave the rock, and waters flowed out to your fill?* (2 Esdras 1:20). The source is the wilderness record: *Then said Yahuah (LORD) unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you; and the people shall go out and gather a certain rate every day, that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no* (Exodus 16:4). The Psalmist names it the very phrase Esdras uses — *Man did eat angels’ food: he sent them meat to the full* (Psalm 78:25). And it ain't new: the manna and the rock were always reaching toward the Messiah (Christ). Yahusha (Jesus) takes up the manna and reveals what it pointed to — *Verily, verily, I say unto you, Moses gave you not that bread from heaven; but my Father giveth you the true bread from heaven* (John 6:32). The rock Esdras says was cleaved Sha'ul (Paul) names outright: *And did all drink the same spiritual drink: for they drank of that spiritual Rock that followed them: and that Rock was Messiah (Christ)* (1 Corinthians 10:4). The bread and the water were Him all along.$n$),
(10761,$n$The prepared kingdom is handed to the faithful with the tree of life and the everlasting dwellings: *Thus says Yahuah to Esdras, Tell my people that I will give them the kingdom of Jerusalem, which I would have given to Yashar'el. Their glory also will I take to me, and give these the everlasting tabernacles, which I had prepared for them.* (2 Esdras 2:10-11) and *They shall have the tree of life for an ointment of sweet savour; they shall neither labour, nor be weary.* (2 Esdras 2:12). This is the close of the Apocalypse: the river and the tree restored in the city — *In the midst of the street of it, and on either side of the river, was there the tree of life, which bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month: and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.* (Revelation 22:2) — promised to the overcomer — *To him that overcometh will I give to eat of the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of Elohim.* (Revelation 2:7). The Esdras vision and the live Revelation apparatus see the same prepared inheritance.$n$,$n$The prepared kingdom is handed to the faithful with the tree of life and the everlasting dwellings: *Thus says Yahuah (God) to Esdras, Tell my people that I will give them the kingdom of Jerusalem, which I would have given to Yashar'el (Israel). Their glory also will I take to me, and give these the everlasting tabernacles, which I had prepared for them.* (2 Esdras 2:10-11) and *They shall have the tree of life for an ointment of sweet savour; they shall neither labour, nor be weary.* (2 Esdras 2:12). The kingdom passes from the unfaithful generation to the faithful of the same seed — not to another people in the place of Yashar'el (Israel). *I say then, Hath Elohim (God) cast away his people? Elohim (God) forbid. For I also am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin.* (Romans 11:1). This is the close of the Apocalypse: the river and the tree restored in the city — *In the midst of the street of it, and on either side of the river, was there the tree of life, which bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month: and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.* (Revelation 22:2) — promised to the overcomer — *To him that overcometh will I give to eat of the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of Elohim (God).* (Revelation 2:7). The Esdras vision and John's Revelation see the same prepared inheritance.$n$),
(10766,$n$Esdras sees the climactic vision — an unnumbered people on mount Sion, crowned and palm-bearing, set by the Son of Elohim himself: *I Esdras saw upon the mount Sion a great people, whom I could not number, and they all praised Yahuah with songs.* (2 Esdras 2:42) and *He answered and said to me, These be they that have put off the mortal clothing, and put on the immortal, and have confessed the name of Yahuah: now are they crowned, and receive palms.* (2 Esdras 2:45) and *So he answered and said to me, It is the Son of Yahuah, whom they have confessed in the world.* (2 Esdras 2:47). This is the throne-vision of the Apocalypse, the very self-link of the live Revelation apparatus: the innumerable palm-bearing multitude — *After this I beheld, and, lo, a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues, stood before the throne, and before the Lamb, clothed with white robes, and palms in their hands;* (Revelation 7:9) — the Lamb on mount Sion with the Name in their foreheads — *And I looked, and, lo, a Lamb stood on the mount Sion, and with him an hundred forty and four thousand, having his Father's name written in their foreheads.* (Revelation 14:1) — and the crown the faithful-unto-death receive — *be thou faithful unto death, and I will give thee a crown of life.* (Revelation 2:10). The messianic Son of Elohim who crowns them is genuinely the Messiah: the Son confessed in the world is Yahusha, and Esdras commends those who stand stiffly for his Name.$n$,$n$Esdras sees the climactic vision — an unnumbered people on mount Sion, crowned and palm-bearing, set by the Son of Elohim (God) himself: *I Esdras saw upon the mount Sion a great people, whom I could not number, and they all praised Yahuah (God) with songs.* (2 Esdras 2:42) and *He answered and said to me, These be they that have put off the mortal clothing, and put on the immortal, and have confessed the name of Yahuah (God): now are they crowned, and receive palms.* (2 Esdras 2:45) and *So he answered and said to me, It is the Son of Yahuah (God), whom they have confessed in the world.* (2 Esdras 2:47). This is the throne-vision of the Apocalypse: the innumerable palm-bearing multitude — *After this I beheld, and, lo, a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues, stood before the throne, and before the Lamb, clothed with white robes, and palms in their hands;* (Revelation 7:9) — the Lamb on mount Sion with the Name in their foreheads — *And I looked, and, lo, a Lamb stood on the mount Sion, and with him an hundred forty and four thousand, having his Father's name written in their foreheads.* (Revelation 14:1) — and the crown the faithful-unto-death receive — *be thou faithful unto death, and I will give thee a crown of life.* (Revelation 2:10). The Son of Elohim (God) who crowns them is genuinely the Messiah (Christ): the Son confessed in the world is Yahusha (Jesus), and Esdras commends those who stand stiffly for his Name.$n$),
(10780,$n$Esdras grieves over the scattering: *And now, O Yahuah (God), why have you given this one people over to many? and upon the one root have you prepared others, and why have you scattered your only one people among many?* (2 Esdras 5:28) — and Uriel turns the grief, *You are sore troubled in mind for Yashar'el (Israel)'s sake: lovest you that people better than he that made them?* (2 Esdras 5:33). The scattering is never a casting-off. Sha'ul answers the very question outright: *I say then, Hath Elohim (God) cast away his people? Elohim (God) forbid. For I also am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin.* (Romans 11:1). The election holds for the fathers' sakes — *As concerning the gospel, they are enemies for your sakes: but as touching the election, they are beloved for the fathers' sakes.* (Romans 11:28) — and it does not fail, *For the gifts and calling of Elohim (God) are without repentance.* (Romans 11:29). The unfaithful generation forfeits; the faithful remnant of both houses keeps the inheritance. No people-that-shall-come replaces Yashar'el — election precedes confession, and the gathering is the two sticks made one.$n$,$n$Esdras grieves over the scattering: *And now, O Yahuah (God), why have you given this one people over to many? and upon the one root have you prepared others, and why have you scattered your only one people among many?* (2 Esdras 5:28) — and Uriel turns the grief, *You are sore troubled in mind for Yashar'el (Israel)'s sake: lovest you that people better than he that made them?* (2 Esdras 5:33). The scattering is never a casting off. Sha'ul (Paul) answers the very question outright: *I say then, Hath Elohim (God) cast away his people? Elohim (God) forbid. For I also am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin.* (Romans 11:1). The election holds for the fathers' sakes — *As concerning the gospel, they are enemies for your sakes: but as touching the election, they are beloved for the fathers' sakes.* (Romans 11:28) — and it does not fail, *For the gifts and calling of Elohim (God) are without repentance.* (Romans 11:29). The unfaithful generation forfeits; the faithful remnant of both houses keeps the inheritance. No other people that shall come replaces Yashar'el (Israel) — election precedes confession, and the gathering is the two sticks made one.$n$),
(10781,$n$Uriel answers the why-not-all-at-once with the womb and the seasons: *And he said to me, I will liken my judgment to a ring: like as there is no slackness of the last, even so there is no swiftness of the first.* (2 Esdras 5:42) — *And he said to me, Ask the womb of a woman, and say to her, If you bring forth children, why do you it not together, but one after another?* (2 Esdras 5:46) — *Then said he to me, Even so have I given the womb of the earth to those that be sown in it in their times.* (2 Esdras 5:48). The Preacher set it first: *To every thing there is a season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven:* (Ecclesiastes 3:1). The fulness of the time is the Father's own — when the womb of the earth had run its appointed measure, *But when the fulness of the time was come, Elohim (God) sent forth his Son, made of a woman, made under the law,* (Galatians 4:4). The times and seasons are reserved to Him alone — *And he said unto them, It is not for you to know the times or the seasons, which the Father hath put in his own power.* (Acts 1:7) — even the day and hour, *But of that day and that hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels which are in heaven, neither the Son, but the Father.* (Mark 13:32). The creature may not haste above the Maker.$n$,$n$Uriel answers the question why not all at once with the womb and the seasons: *And he said to me, I will liken my judgment to a ring: like as there is no slackness of the last, even so there is no swiftness of the first.* (2 Esdras 5:42) — *And he said to me, Ask the womb of a woman, and say to her, If you bring forth children, why do you it not together, but one after another?* (2 Esdras 5:46) — *Then said he to me, Even so have I given the womb of the earth to those that be sown in it in their times.* (2 Esdras 5:48). The Preacher set it first: *To every thing there is a season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven:* (Ecclesiastes 3:1). The fulness of the time is the Father's own — when the womb of the earth had run its appointed measure, *But when the fulness of the time was come, Elohim (God) sent forth his Son, made of a woman, made under the law,* (Galatians 4:4). The times and seasons are reserved to Him alone — *And he said unto them, It is not for you to know the times or the seasons, which the Father hath put in his own power.* (Acts 1:7) — even the day and hour, *But of that day and that hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels which are in heaven, neither the Son, but the Father.* (Mark 13:32). The creature may not haste above the Maker.$n$),
(10838,$n$The deliverance is rehearsed in judgment's key: *I will bring them with a mighty hand and a stretched out arm, and smite Egypt with plagues, as before, and will destroy all the land thereof* (2 Esdras 15:11). The mighty-hand-and-stretched-arm is the Exodus formula — *Or hath Elohim (God) assayed to go and take him a nation from the midst of another nation... by a mighty hand, and by a stretched out arm... according to all that Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God) did for you in Egypt before your eyes?* (Deuteronomy 4:34). And the renewed sword upon Egypt is Ezekiel's oracle — *And the sword shall come upon Egypt, and great pain shall be in Ethiopia, when the slain shall fall in Egypt* (Ezekiel 30:4). It ain't new: the Arm that broke Egypt once breaks the proud world-system again.$n$,$n$The deliverance is rehearsed in judgment's key: *I will bring them with a mighty hand and a stretched out arm, and smite Egypt with plagues, as before, and will destroy all the land thereof* (2 Esdras 15:11). The mighty hand and the stretched out arm are the Exodus formula — *Or hath Elohim (God) assayed to go and take him a nation from the midst of another nation... by a mighty hand, and by a stretched out arm... according to all that Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God) did for you in Egypt before your eyes?* (Deuteronomy 4:34). And the renewed sword upon Egypt is Ezekiel's oracle — *And the sword shall come upon Egypt, and great pain shall be in Ethiopia, when the slain shall fall in Egypt* (Ezekiel 30:4). It ain't new: the Arm that broke Egypt once breaks the proud world system again.$n$),
(43184,$n$The charge against the covenant people is their fidelity to the Torah. The slanderers complain *who said much of the exclusiveness of the Yahudim (Jews) with regard to their worship and meats; they alleged that they were men unsociable, hostile to the king's interests, refusing to associate with him or his troops. By this way of speaking, they brought much odium upon them* (3 Maccabees 3:7) — yet the real ground was that *they worshipped Elohim (God), and observed his law, they made certain distinctions, and avoided certain things* (3 Maccabees 3:4). It ain't new: this is Haman's identical libel before Ahasuerus — *There is a certain people scattered abroad and dispersed among the people in all the provinces of thy kingdom; and their laws are diverse from all people; neither keep they the king's laws: therefore it is not for the king's profit to suffer them* (Esther 3:8). The very same words echo in the lost portions of that book — *that in all nations throughout the world there was scattered a certain malicious people, that had laws contrary to all nations, and continually despised the commandments of kings* (the-rest-of-esther 13:4). The world has always read covenant obedience as treason; the distinctions of clean and unclean, the set-apart worship, are not abolished but criminalized by the kingdoms of men.$n$,$n$The charge against the covenant people is their fidelity to the Torah. The slanderers complain *who said much of the exclusiveness of the Yahudim (Jews) with regard to their worship and meats; they alleged that they were men unsociable, hostile to the king's interests, refusing to associate with him or his troops. By this way of speaking, they brought much odium upon them* (3 Maccabees 3:7) — yet the real ground was that *they worshipped Elohim (God), and observed his law, they made certain distinctions, and avoided certain things* (3 Maccabees 3:4). It ain't new: this is Haman's identical libel before Ahasuerus — *There is a certain people scattered abroad and dispersed among the people in all the provinces of thy kingdom; and their laws are diverse from all people; neither keep they the king's laws: therefore it is not for the king's profit to suffer them* (Esther 3:8). The very same words echo in the lost portions of that book — *that in all nations throughout the world there was scattered a certain malicious people, that had laws contrary to all nations, and continually despised the commandments of kings* (The Rest of Esther 13:4). The world has always read covenant obedience as treason; the distinctions of clean and unclean, the set-apart worship, are not abolished but criminalized by the kingdoms of men.$n$),
(43186,$n$The letter ends in a sentence of extermination: *in that same hour we order those Yahudim (Jews) who dwell among you, with wives and children, to be sent to us, vilified and abused, in chains of iron, to undergo a death, cruel and ignominious, suitable to men disaffected* (3 Maccabees 3:25), reasoning that *by the punishment of them in one body... we have found the only means of establishing our affairs* (3 Maccabees 3:26). It ain't new: the same genocidal post went out under Ahasuerus — *And the letters were sent by posts into all the king's provinces, to destroy, to kill, and to cause to perish, all Yahudim (Jews), both young and old, little children and women, in one day* (Esther 3:13). The Greek decree spells out the identical sentence — *all they that are signified in writing to you by Aman... shall all, with their wives and children, be utterly destroyed by the sword of their enemies, without all mercy and pity* (the-rest-of-esther 13:6). One decree, one adversary, one seed marked for destruction across the ages — and a covenant-keeping Deliverer who will not let the seed be cut off.$n$,$n$The letter ends in a sentence of extermination: *in that same hour we order those Yahudim (Jews) who dwell among you, with wives and children, to be sent to us, vilified and abused, in chains of iron, to undergo a death, cruel and ignominious, suitable to men disaffected* (3 Maccabees 3:25), reasoning that *by the punishment of them in one body... we have found the only means of establishing our affairs* (3 Maccabees 3:26). It ain't new: the same genocidal post went out under Ahasuerus — *And the letters were sent by posts into all the king's provinces, to destroy, to kill, and to cause to perish, all Yahudim (Jews), both young and old, little children and women, in one day* (Esther 3:13). The Greek decree spells out the identical sentence — *all they that are signified in writing to you by Aman... shall all, with their wives and children, be utterly destroyed by the sword of their enemies, without all mercy and pity* (The Rest of Esther 13:6). One decree, one adversary, one seed marked for destruction across the ages — and a covenant-keeping Deliverer who will not let the seed be cut off.$n$);
